ELECTRIC SCOOTERS)
OF THE OAK LAWN VILLAGE CODE RELATIVE TO PENALTIES FOR THE UNLAWFUL OPERATION
OF BICYCLES/TRICYCLES, LOW-SPEED ELECTRIC BICYCLES AND ELECTRIC SCOOTERS WITHIN
THE VILLAGE OF OAK LAWN
 
which Ordinance was passed by the Board of Trustees of the Village of Oak Lawn
at a Regular Village Board Meeting on the 14th day of October, 2025, at which
meeting a quorum was present, and approved by the President of the Village of
Oak Lawn on the 14th day of October, 2025.
 
   I further certify that the vote on the question of the passage of said
Ordinance by the Board of Trustees of the Village of Oak Lawn was taken by Ayes
and Nays and recorded in the minutes of the Board of Trustees of the Village of
Oak Lawn, and that the result of said vote was as follows, to-wit:
 
   AYES: Trustees Olejniczak, Mallo, Stalker, and Soch
   NAYS:    
   ABSENT: Trustees Pembroke and Desmond
 
   I do further certify that the original Ordinance, of which the foregoing is
a true copy, is entrusted to my care for safekeeping, and that I am the lawful
keeper of the same.
 
   IN WITNESS WHEREOF, I have hereunto set my hand and affixed the seal of the
Village of Oak Lawn, this 14th day of October, 2025.
 
 
                        _____________________________
                        Claire Henning, Village Clerk
 
 
[SEAL]
 
TITLE 1
ADMINISTRATIVE
CHAPTER 1
OFFICIAL VILLAGE CODE
SECTION:
1-1-1: Title
1-1-2: Acceptance
1-1-3: Amendments
1-1-4: Code Alterations
1-1-5: Interpretation and Application
1-1-6: Historical Citation Procedure
1-1-7: Illinois Compiled Statutes
1-1-1: TITLE:
Upon adoption by the Board of Trustees, this Village Code is hereby declared to
be and shall hereafter constitute the official Village Code of Oak Lawn. This
Village Code of ordinances shall be known and cited as the OAK LAWN VILLAGE
CODE, and it is hereby published by authority of the Board of Trustees and
shall be kept up to date as provided in Section
1-1-3 of this Chapter under the direction of the Village Attorney, acting for
said Village Board of Trustees. Any reference to the number of any section
contained herein shall be understood to refer to the position of the same
number, its appropriate chapter and title heading, and to the general penalty
clause relating thereto, as well as to the section itself, when reference is
made to this Village Code by title in any legal document. (1951 Code 19-1-1;
1985 Code)
1-1-2: ACCEPTANCE:
This Village Code, as hereby presented in printed form, shall hereafter be
received without further proof in all courts and in all administrative
tribunals of this State as the ordinances of the Village of general and
permanent effect, except the excluded ordinances enumerated in Section
1-2-1 of this Code. (1985 Code)
1-1-3: AMENDMENTS:
Any ordinance amending this Village Code shall set forth the title, chapter and
section number of the section or sections to be amended, and this shall
constitute a sufficient compliance with any statutory requirement pertaining to
the amendment or revision by ordinance of any part of this Village Code. All
such amendments or revisions by ordinance shall be immediately forwarded to the
codifiers and the said ordinance material shall be prepared for insertion in
its proper place in each copy of this Village Code. Each such replacement page
shall be properly identified and shall be inserted in each individual copy of
this Village Code. (1951 Code 19-1-3; 1985 Code)
1-1-4: CODE ALTERATIONS:
It shall be deemed unlawful for any person to alter, change, replace or deface
in any way any section or any page of this Village Code in such a manner that
the meaning of any phrase or order may be changed or omitted. Replacement pages
may be inserted according to the official instructions when so authorized by
the Village Board. The Clerk shall see that the replacement pages are properly
inserted in the official copies maintained in the office of the Clerk. Any
person having in his custody an official copy of this Village Code shall make
every effort to maintain this Code in an up to date and efficient manner. He
shall see to the immediate insertion of new or replacement pages when such are
delivered to him or made available to him through the office of the Village
Clerk. Said Code books, while in actual possession of officials and other
interested persons, shall be and remain the property of the Village and shall
be returned to the office of the Clerk when directed so to do by order of the
Village Board. (1951 Code 19-1-3; 1985 Code)
1-1-5: INTERPRETATION AND APPLICATION:
In the interpretation and application of any provision of this Code, said
provisions shall be held to be the minimum requirements adopted for the
promotion of the public health, safety, comfort, convenience and general
welfare. Where any provision of the Code imposes greater restrictions upon the
subject matter than the general provision imposed by the Code, the provision
imposing the greater restriction or regulation shall be deemed to be
controlling. (1951 Code 19-1-2)
1-1-6: HISTORICAL CITATION PROCEDURE:
The following is an example of the method used in citing the various provisions
of the 1951 Municipal Code as said provisions are found in this Code. The
appropriate amending ordinances are listed in numerical order following the
1951 Code citation: (1951 Code 2-9-6; Ords. 487, 76-7-12). In the 1951 Code
reference, the first number indicates the Chapter, the second number indicates
the Article and the third number indicates the section; also, the ordinance
citations show only the ordinance number and not the date and section of the
ordinance. (1985 Code)
1-1-7: ILLINOIS COMPILED STATUTES:
Pursuant to Public Act 87-1005, the Illinois Revised Statutes were recodified
as the Illinois Compiled Statutes. As such, all references contained in this
Code to the Illinois Revised Statutes are hereby revised to read as if said
reference is to the corresponding section of the Illinois Compiled Statutes, as
such correspondence is shown on the official cross-reference published by the
Illinois Legislative Reference Bureau, as more fully set forth in the Tables
Volume of the State Bar Association Edition of the Illinois Compiled Statutes
published by West Publishing Co. A copy of said cross-reference publication is
on file with the Village Clerk. (Ord. 93-12-43)
CHAPTER 2
SAVING CLAUSE
SECTION:
1-2-1: Repeal of General Ordinances
1-2-2: Public Utility Ordinances
1-2-3: Court Proceedings
1-2-4: Severability Clause
1-2-1: REPEAL OF GENERAL ORDINANCES:
All general ordinances of the Village passed prior to the adoption of this
Village Code are hereby repealed, except such as are included in this Village
Code or are by necessary implication herein reserved from repeal (subject to
the saving clauses contained in the following sections), and excluding the
following ordinances which are not hereby repealed: tax levy ordinances;
appropriation ordinances; ordinances relating to boundaries and annexations;
franchise ordinances and other ordinances granting special rights to persons or
corporations; contract ordinances and ordinances authorizing the execution of a
contract or the issuance of warrants; salary ordinances; ordinances
establishing, naming or vacating streets, alleys or other public places;
improvement ordinances; bond ordinances; ordinances relating to elections;
ordinances relating to the transfer or acceptance of real estate by or from the
Village; and all special ordinances. (1985 Code)
1-2-2: PUBLIC UTILITY ORDINANCES:
No ordinance relating to railroads or railroad crossings with streets and other
public ways, or relating to the conduct, duties, service or rates of public
utilities shall be replaced by virtue of the preceding section, excepting as
this Village Code may contain provisions for such matters, in which case this
Village Code shall be considered as amending such ordinance or ordinances in
respect to such provisions only. (1985 Code)
1-2-3: COURT PROCEEDINGS:
No new ordinance shall be construed or held to repeal a former ordinance,
whether such former ordinance is expressly repealed or not, as to any offense
committed against such former ordinance or as to any act done, any penalty,
forfeiture or punishment so incurred, or any right accrued or claim arising
under the former ordinance, or in any way whatever to affect any such offense
or act so committed or so done, or any penalty, forfeiture or punishment so
incurred or any right accrued or claim arising before the new ordinance takes
effect, save only that proceedings thereafter shall conform to the ordinance in
force at the time of such proceeding, so far as practicable. If any penalty,
forfeiture or punishment be mitigated by any provision of a new ordinance, such
provisions may be, by the consent of the party affected, applied to any
judgment announced after the new ordinance takes effect.
This Section shall extend to all repeals, either by express words or
implication, whether the repeal is in the ordinance making any new provisions
upon the same subject or in any other ordinance.
Nothing contained in this Chapter shall be construed as abating any action now
pending under or by virtue of any general ordinance of the Village herein
repealed and the provisions of all general ordinances contained in this Code
shall be deemed to be continuing provisions and not a new enactment of the same
provision; nor shall this Chapter be deemed as discontinuing, abating,
modifying or altering any penalty accrued or to accrue, or as affecting the
liability of any person, firm or corporation, or as waiving any right of the
Village under any ordinance or provision thereof in force at the time of the
adoption of this Village Code. (1951 Code 19-1-3; 1985 Code)
1-2-4: SEVERABILITY CLAUSE:
If any section, subsection, subdivision, paragraph, sentence, clause or phrase
of this Village Code or any part thereof is for any reason held to be
unconstitutional or invalid or ineffective by any court of competent
jurisdiction, such decision shall not affect the validity or effectiveness of
the remaining portions of this Code, or any part thereof. The Village Board of
Trustees hereby declares that it would have passed each section, subsection,
subdivision, paragraph, sentence, clause or phrase thereof irrespective of the
fact that any one or more sections, subsections, subdivisions, paragraphs,
sentences, clauses or phrases be declared unconstitutional, invalid or
ineffective. (1951 Code 19-1-3; 1985 Code)
CHAPTER 3
DEFINITIONS
SECTION:
1-3-1: Construction of Words
1-3-2: Definitions
1-3-3: Catchlines
1-3-1: CONSTRUCTION OF WORDS:
Whenever any word in any section of this Village Code importing the plural
number is used in describing or referring to any matters, parties or persons,
any single matter, party or person shall be deemed to be included, although
distributive words may not have been used. When any subject matter, party or
person is referred to in this Village Code by words importing the singular
number only, or the masculine gender, several matters, parties or persons and
females as well as males and bodies corporate shall be applied to any section
of this Village Code which contains any express provision excluding such
construction or where the subject matter or content may be repugnant thereto.
All general provisions, terms, phrases and expressions contained in his Code
shall be liberally construed in order that the true intent and meaning of the
Board of Trustees may be fully carried out.
   A.   Ordinance: The word "ordinance" contained in the ordinances of the
Village has been changed in the content of this Village Code to "title",
"chapter", "section" and/or "subsection" or words of like import for
organizational and clarification purposes only. Such change to the Village's
ordinances is not meant to amend the passage and effective dates of such
original ordinances.
   B.   Computation of Time: Whenever a notice is required to be given or an
act to be done a certain length of time before any proceeding shall be had, the
day on which such notice is given, or such act is done, shall be counted in
computing the time, but the day on which such proceeding is to be held shall
not be counted. Time shall mean Central Standard Time, except when the State of
Illinois is on Daylight Saving Time and then it shall mean Central Daylight
Saving Time.
   C.   Delegation of Authority: Whenever a provision appears requiring the
head of a department or some other Village officer to do some act or perform
some duty, it is to be construed to authorize the head of the department or
other officer to designate, delegate and authorize subordinates to perform the
required act or perform the duty unless the terms of the provision or section
specify otherwise.
   D.   Joint Authority: All words giving a joint authority to three (3) or
more persons or officers shall be construed as giving such authority to a
majority of such persons or officers.
   E.   Nontechnical and Technical Words: Words and phrases shall be construed
according to the common and approved usage of the language, but technical words
and phrases and such others as may have acquired peculiar and appropriate
meanings in law shall be construed and understood according to such meanings.
   F.   Shall: The word "shall" is mandatory. (1951 Code 19-1-2; 1985 Code)
1-3-2: DEFINITIONS:
Whenever the following words or terms are used in
this Code, they shall have the meanings herein ascribed to them, unless the
context makes such meaning repugnant thereto:
AGENT: A person on behalf of another.
BOARD: Unless otherwise indicated, the Board of Trustees of the Village.
CODE: The Village Code of Oak Lawn, Illinois and amendments thereto.
CORPORATE or VILLAGE LIMITS: The legal boundaries of the Village of Oak Lawn.
COUNTY: The County of Cook, State of Illinois.
EMPLOYEES: Whenever reference is made in this Code to a Village employee by
title only, this shall be construed as though followed by the words, "of the
Village of Oak Lawn".
FEE: A sum of money charged by the Village for the carrying on of a business,
profession or occupation.
FISCAL YEAR: The fiscal year of the Village shall be the same as the calendar
year.
LICENSE: The permission granted for the carrying on of a business, profession
or occupation.
MAYOR: See Village President.
MISDEMEANOR: Any offense for which a sentence to a term of imprisonment in
other than a penitentiary for less than one year may be imposed.
MONTH: A calendar month.
NUISANCE: Anything offensive or obnoxious to the health and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; or any act or thing repugnant to, or creating a
hazard to, or having a detrimental effect on the property of another person or
to the community.
OATH: Shall be construed to include an affirmation in all cases in which, by
law, an affirmation may be substituted for an oath, and in such cases the words
"swear" and "sworn" shall be equivalent to the words "affirm" and "affirmed".
OCCUPANT: As applied to a building or land, this term shall include any person
who occupies the whole or any part of such building or land, whether alone or
with others.
OFFENSE: Any act forbidden by any provision of this Code or the omission of any
act required by the provisions of this Code.
OFFICERS: Whenever reference is made in this Code to a Village officer by title
only, this shall be construed as though followed by the words "of the Village
of Oak Lawn".
OPERATOR: The person who is in charge of any operation, business or profession.
OWNER: As applied to a building or land, this term shall include any part
owner, joint owner, tenant in common, joint tenant or lessee of the whole or a
part of such building or land.
PERSON: Any public or private corporation, firm, partnership, association,
organization, government or any other group acting as a unit, as well as a
natural person.
PERSONAL PROPERTY: Every description of money, goods, chattels, effects,
evidence of rights in action and all written instruments by which any pecuniary
obligation, right or title to property is created, acknowledged, transferred,
increased, defeated, discharged or diminished and every right of interest
therein.
PRECEDING, FOLLOWING: Next before and next after, respectively.
PROPERTY: Includes real and personal property.
REAL PROPERTY: Includes lands, tenements and hereditaments.
RETAILER: Unless otherwise specifically defined, this term shall be understood
to relate to the sale of goods, merchandise, articles or things in small
quantities direct to the consumer.
SIDEWALK: Any portion of a street between the curb line and the adjacent
property line, intended for the use of pedestrians, excluding parkways.
SIGNATURE OR SUBSCRIPTION: Includes a mark when the person cannot write.
STATE: The State of Illinois.
STREET: This term shall include alleys, lanes, courts, boulevards, public
squares, public places and sidewalks.
TENANT: As applied to a building or land, this term shall include any person
who occupies the whole or any part of such building or land, whether alone or
with others.
VILLAGE: The Village of Oak Lawn, County of Cook, State of Illinois.
VILLAGE PRESIDENT: The President of the Village may be referred to as "Mayor"
or "President" of the Village.
WHOLESALER AND WHOLESALE DEALER: Unless otherwise specifically defined, such
terms shall be understood to relate to the sale of goods, merchandise, articles
or things in quantity to persons who purchase for the purpose of resale.
WRITTEN; IN WRITING: May include printing and any other mode of representing
words and letters, but when the written signature of any person is required by
law to any official or public writing or bond required by law, it shall be in
the proper handwriting of such person, or in case he is unable to write, by his
proper mark.
YEAR: A calendar year. (1951 Code 2-8-2, 19-1-2; Ord. 68-26-72; 1985 Code)
1-3-3: CATCHLINES:
The catchlines of the several sections of this Code are intended as mere
catchwords to indicate the content of the section and shall not be deemed or
taken to be titles of such sections, nor be deemed to govern, limit, modify or
in any manner affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of any
division or section hereof, nor unless expressly so provided, shall they be so
deemed when any of such sections, including the catchlines, are amended or re-
enacted. (1951 Code 19-1-3; 1985 Code)
CHAPTER 4
GENERAL PENALTY
SECTION:
1-4-1: General Penalty
1-4-1-1: Imprisonment Fine Surcharge
1-4-2: Citation Procedure (Rep. by Ord. 08-16-41)
1-4-3: Application Of Provisions
1-4-4: Liability Of Officers
1-4-5: Alternative Penalties; Compliance Tickets (Rep. by Ord. 08-16-41)
1-4-6: Release Of Liens; Fees
1-4-7: Community Service
1-4-8: Repeat Offenders
1-4-1: GENERAL PENALTY:
Except as otherwise provided in section
1-4-1-1 of this chapter, any person convicted of a violation of any section or
provision of this code, where no other penalty is set forth, shall be punished
by a fine not less than one hundred dollars ($100.00) and not to exceed seven
hundred fifty dollars ($750.00) for any offense. The person upon whom any fine
or penalty is imposed, upon order of the court before which the conviction is
had, may be imprisoned until the fine, penalty and costs are paid. No
imprisonment, however, shall exceed six (6) months for any one offense. (Ord.
09-03-09)
1-4-1-1: IMPRISONMENT FINE SURCHARGE:
Notwithstanding anything to the contrary set forth in this code, in the event
that a person is arrested and imprisoned for a violation of any provision of
this code, and such person is subsequently convicted for said violation, the
fine assessed for the violation shall be increased by the amount of eighty
dollars ($80.00) per day for each day (or portion thereof) that the violator
was imprisoned in the village jail (the "imprisonment fine surcharge"). The
imprisonment fine surcharge shall be assessed by the court, the administrative
adjudication hearing officer, or such other tribunal of competent jurisdiction
before which the conviction is had. (Ord. 09-03-09)
1-4-2: CITATION PROCEDURE:
(Rep. by Ord. 08-16-41)
1-4-3: APPLICATION OF PROVISIONS:
The penalty provided in this chapter shall be applicable to every section of
this code the same as though it were a part of each and every separate section.
Any person convicted of a violation of any section of this code where any duty
is prescribed or obligation imposed, or where any action which is of a
continuing nature is forbidden or is declared to be unlawful, shall be deemed
guilty of a misdemeanor. A separate offense shall be deemed committed upon each
day such duty or obligation remains unperformed or such act continues, unless
otherwise specifically provided in this code.
In all cases where the same offense is made punishable or is created by
different clauses or sections of this code, the prosecuting officer may elect
under which to proceed; but not more than one recovery shall be had against the
same person for the same offense; provided, that the revocation of a license or
permit shall not be considered a recovery or penalty so as to bar any other
penalty being enforced.
Whenever the doing of any act or the omission to do any act constitutes a
breach of any section or provision of this code and there shall be no fine or
penalty specifically declared for such breach, the provisions of this chapter
shall apply. (1951 Code 19-1-3; 1985 Code)
1-4-4: LIABILITY OF OFFICERS:
No provision of this code designating the duties of any officer or employee
shall be so construed as to make such officer or employee liable for any fine
or penalty provided for a failure to perform such duty, unless the intention of
the board of trustees to impose such fine or penalty on such officer or
employee is specifically and clearly expressed in the section creating the
duty. (1951 Code 19-1-4; 1985 Code)
1-4-5: ALTERNATIVE PENALTIES; COMPLIANCE TICKETS:
(Rep. by Ord. 08-16-41)
1-4-6: RELEASE OF LIENS; FEES:
Whenever the village has placed a lien against a parcel of property as a result
of any provision of this code or state statute, said lien shall not be released
until the village has received reimbursement for the following costs, in
addition to payment of the lien amount:
   A.   The actual cost of preparing the lien document;
   B.   The actual cost of recording the lien; and
   C.   The actual cost of preparing the release of lien document. (Ord. 94-3-
11)
1-4-7: COMMUNITY SERVICE:
In lieu of, or in addition to, the general penalties set forth in section
1-4-1 of this chapter, or any other section of this code which provides for a
monetary penalty for violation thereof, any person convicted of a violation of
any provision or section of this code may be required to perform some
reasonable public service work/community service work. (Ord. 95-20-78)
1-4-8: REPEAT OFFENDERS:
   A.   Notwithstanding any other provision of this code which may require the
issuance of a notice of violation and a cure period prior to the issuance of a
court citation for a violation of this code, no such notice of violation or
cure period shall be required where a person or said person's property is in
violation of this code and said person has been issued a notice of violation
relative to the same violation within the previous twelve (12) month period. In
such case, a court citation can be immediately issued relative to the
identified code violation.
   B.   In the event that any person has been found guilty of a violation of
this code by a court of competent jurisdiction within the twelve (12) month
period preceding a second conviction for the same offense, unless a greater
minimum fine is provided for in the code provision that has been violated, the
minimum fine for said second offense shall be not less than one hundred dollars
($100.00). (Ord. 97-11-52)
CHAPTER 5
VILLAGE PRESIDENT
SECTION:
1-5-1: Election, Term Of Office, Qualifications
1-5-2: Salary
1-5-3: Function As Mayor
1-5-4: Powers And Duties
1-5-5: Ordinance Approval Or Veto
1-5-6: President Pro Tempore
1-5-7: Emergency Interim Village President
1-5-1: ELECTION, TERM OF OFFICE, QUALIFICATIONS:
There shall be elected at the regular village election a president of the
village, who shall hold his office for four (4) years and until his successor
is elected and qualified. He shall be a citizen of the United States, a
qualified elector, and shall have resided within the corporate limits of the
village for at least one year next preceding the election at which he is a
candidate. (1951 Code 1-2-1)
1-5-2: SALARY:
The president shall devote such time to the duties of his office as the
faithful discharge thereof may require and shall receive the following as
compensation:
   A.   As established in 2005, the president currently receives such salary as
reflected in the most recent pay and benefit ordinance.
   B.   Beginning June 1, 2025, for terms commencing after the consolidated
election in April of 2025, the use of pay and benefit ordinances to set
salaries will be discontinued, and, instead, the president shall receive a
salary of $4,200 per month, payable in accordance with the village’s regular
bi-weekly payroll schedule. Beginning June 1, 2026, and each June 1st
thereafter for as long as the individual remains in office, an incumbent
president shall receive an increase in the compensation then in effect equal to
the percent change in the Chicago Area Consumer Price Index for all Urban
Consumers (CPI-U) as calculated and published by the federal government for the
period beginning May of the previous year and ending April of the current year,
rounded to the next highest dollar amount as appropriate for purposes of
resulting in an even dollar amount for payroll purposes. Thereafter, newly
elected non-incumbent presidents’ compensation shall begin at a rate of $4,200
per month, payable in accordance with the village’s regular bi-weekly payroll
schedule, and after twelve months will be increased annually on June 1st of
each year, as indicated above based on the applicable CPI-U, for as long as the
individual remains in office.
   C.   Beginning June 1, 2025, for terms commencing after the April 2025
consolidated election, the village president shall not receive any additional
compensation for serving as Liquor Commissioner.
   D.   In addition, the village president is currently eligible, and will
remain eligible, to participate in the following village group employee benefit
plans, on the same basis and terms that are applicable to non-union employees:
group health insurance, health insurance buy-out, dental and vision insurance,
life insurance, and flexible benefit plan. In addition, the village president
is currently eligible, and will remain eligible, to participate in other
voluntary benefit plans, such as accident, critical illness, hospital, and
legal, with costs to be fully paid by the participants.
   E.   In accordance with Resolution No. 13-11-28, the village president is
not eligible to participate in the Illinois Municipal Retirement Fund (IMRF).
   F.   No other compensation shall be allowed the village president. (1951
Code 1-2-2, 1-2-3; Ords. 65-40-2, 68(8)-11-38, 82-1-4, 82-1-5, 82-11-42, 83-9-
18; 1985 Code; Ords. 93-9-27, 94-12-53, 05-15-50; 24-18-67)
1-5-3: FUNCTION AS MAYOR:
The president of the board of trustees shall perform the duties and exercise
the powers conferred upon the mayor of a city. The president may be referred to
as mayor. He may sign as mayor in his official capacity as president. Where the
word "president" is used throughout this code and in any village matter, it
shall also mean "mayor". If the word "mayor" is used, it shall also mean
"president". (1951 Code 1-2-3; Ord. 65-40-2)
1-5-4: POWERS AND DUTIES:
   A.   General: The president shall supervise the conduct of all appointed
officers and see that they faithfully and efficiently discharge the duties of
their respective offices; he shall inquire into all reasonable complaints made
against them and cause all their neglects or violations of duties to be
promptly corrected; and he shall, from time to time, give the board of trustees
such information relative to the affairs of the village as he may deem proper
and recommend to it consideration of any measure he may deem expedient, tending
to the well being, security and improvement of the village.
   B.   Pardons: The president may, for good and sufficient reasons, release
any person convicted of or any person imprisoned for violation of any village
ordinance. The president shall cause a proper record thereof to be made and
shall report such release with the cause therefor to the board of trustees at
its first session thereafter. The president shall have the power to direct the
prosecuting attorney to nonsuit any ordinance violation pending in the circuit
court.
   C.   Sign Documents; Revoke Licenses:
      1.   The president shall sign all commissions, licenses, permits and
warrants granted, issued or ordered by the board of trustees, or authorized by
the ordinances of the village, and may revoke any such licenses for the causes
herein provided.
      2.   The president shall sign, and is hereby authorized to sign in behalf
of the village, all instruments necessary to the prompt prosecution or defense
of any or all actions brought by or against said village including bonds for
injunction. (1951 Code 1-2-6, 1-2-8, 1-2-9, 1-2-11; 1985 Code)
1-5-5: ORDINANCE APPROVAL OR VETO:
   A.   Veto Powers: The president of the board of trustees may exercise the
same veto powers, and with like effect, as the mayor of a city, and the board
of trustees may pass ordinances over such veto in like manner as a city
council.
   B.   Procedure: The president shall carefully inspect all ordinances passed
by the board of trustees and fix his official signature to such as he may
approve and return them to the village clerk. Such ordinances as he shall not
approve shall be returned to the board of trustees, with his objections thereto
in writing, at the next regular meeting of the board of trustees occurring
after the passage thereof. Such veto may extend to any one or more items or
appropriations contained in any ordinance making appropriations, or to the
entire ordinance and in case the veto only extends to a part of such ordinance,
the residue thereof shall take effect and be in force; but in case the
president shall fail to return any ordinance with his objections thereto by the
time aforesaid, he shall be deemed to have approved such ordinance and the same
shall take effect accordingly. (1951 Code 1-2-3, 1-2-7; Ord. 65-40-2)
1-5-6: PRESIDENT PRO TEMPORE:
During a temporary absence of the president or his inability to act, the board
of trustees shall elect one of its members to act as a president pro tempore
who, during the president's absence or disability, shall possess the powers of
the president. (1951 Code 1-2-4)
1-5-7: EMERGENCY INTERIM VILLAGE PRESIDENT:
The village president shall designate in writing and file with the village
clerk and village manager at least three (3) and no more than seven (7)
emergency interim successor village presidents pursuant to the emergency
interim executive succession act 1  to act in the event of the unavailability
of the president during an emergency. As provided by the act, the following
terms shall have the meanings hereinafter ascribed to them:
ATTACK: An attack or series of attacks by an enemy of the United States
causing, or which may cause, substantial damage or injury to civilian property
or persons in the United States in any manner by sabotage or by the use of
bombs, missiles, shellfire, or atomic, radiological, chemical, bacteriological
or biological means or other weapons or processes.
DISASTER: A situation determined by the governor to be an occurrence or threat
of catastrophic and widespread injury or loss of life or property resulting
from any natural or manmade cause, including, but not limited to, fire, flood,
earthquake, epidemic and explosion.
EMERGENCY: An attack or a disaster.
EMERGENCY INTERIM SUCCESSOR: A person designated pursuant to this chapter in
the event the president is unavailable to exercise the powers and discharge the
duties of president until a successor is appointed or elected and qualified as
may be provided by the constitution, statutes, charters and ordinances or until
the lawful incumbent is able to resume the exercise of the powers and discharge
the duties of the office.
UNAVAILABLE: Means either that a vacancy in the office of president exists and
there is no deputy authorized to exercise all of the powers and discharge the
duties of the office or that the lawful incumbent of the office (including any
deputy exercising the powers and discharging the duties of an office because of
a vacancy) and his duly authorized deputy are absent or unable to exercise the
powers and discharge the duties of the office. (Ord. 09-13-28)
 
Notes
1 1. 5 ILCS 275/1 et seq.
CHAPTER 6
VILLAGE BOARD OF TRUSTEES
SECTION:
1-6-1: Legislative Body
1-6-2: Compensation
1-6-3: Meetings Of The Board
1-6-4: Quorum
1-6-5: Rules Of Order
1-6-6: Election Districts
1-6-1: LEGISLATIVE BODY:
The legislative body of the village shall consist of the president of the
village and six (6) trustees who shall possess the qualifications for office,
be elected by the legal voters of the municipality, take the oath of office and
perform the duties provided by the Illinois Compiled Statutes 1  and this code.
The six (6) trustees shall represent six (6) districts within the village such
that there shall be one trustee from each of six (6) districts as established
in section
1-6-6 of this chapter according to law. (1951 Code 1-1-1; 1985 Code)
 
Notes
1 1. 65 ILCS 5/1-5-3, 5/3-11-1 et seq., 5/5-2-15.
1-6-2: COMPENSATION:
   A.   As established in 1985, each trustee currently receives such
compensation as reflected in the most recent pay and benefit ordinance.
   B.   Beginning June 1, 2025, for trustees whose terms commence in May, 2025,
after the consolidated election in April of 2025, the use of pay and benefit
ordinances to set salaries will be discontinued, and, instead, such trustees
shall receive a salary of $1,500 per month, payable in accordance with the
village’s regular bi-weekly payroll schedule. Further, beginning June 1, 2026,
and each June 1st thereafter for as long as the individual remains in office,
such incumbent trustees shall receive an increase in the compensation then in
effect equal to the percent change in the Chicago Area Consumer Price Index for
all Urban Consumers (CPI-U) as calculated and published by the federal
government for the period beginning May of the previous year and ending April
of the current year, rounded to the next highest dollar amount as appropriate
for purposes of resulting in an even dollar amount for payroll purposes.
Thereafter, a newly elected non-incumbent trustee’s compensation shall begin at
a rate of $1,500 per month, payable in accordance with the village’s regular
bi-weekly payroll schedule, and after twelve months will be increased annually
on June 1st of each year, as indicated above based on the applicable CPI-U, for
as long as the individual remains in office.
   C.   Beginning June 1, 2027, for trustees whose terms commence in May, 2027,
after the consolidated election in April of 2027, the use of pay and benefit
ordinances to set salaries will be discontinued, and, instead, such trustees
shall receive a salary of $1,500 per month, payable in accordance with the
village’s regular bi-weekly payroll schedule. Further, beginning June 1, 2028,
and each June 1st thereafter for as long as the individual remains in office,
such incumbent trustees shall receive an increase in the compensation then in
effect equal to the percent change in the Chicago Area Consumer Price Index for
all Urban Consumers (CPI-U) as calculated and published by the federal
government for the period beginning May of the previous year and ending April
of the current year, rounded to the next highest dollar amount as appropriate
for purposes of resulting in an even dollar amount for payroll purposes.
Thereafter, a newly elected non-incumbent trustee’s compensation shall begin at
a rate of $1,500 per month, payable in accordance with the village’s regular
bi-weekly payroll schedule, and after twelve months will be increased annually
on June 1st of each year thereafter, as indicated above based on the applicable
CPI-U, for as long as the individual remains in office.
   D.   In addition, village trustees are currently eligible, and will remain
eligible, to participate in the following village group employee benefit plans,
on the same basis and terms that are applicable to non-union employees: group
health insurance, health insurance buy-out, dental and vision insurance, life
insurance, and flexible benefit plan. In addition, village trustees are
currently eligible, and will remain eligible, to participate in other voluntary
benefit plans, such as accident, critical illness, hospital, and legal, with
costs to be fully paid by the participants.
   E.   In accordance with Resolution Nos. 13-11-28 and 17-14-53, village
trustees are not eligible to participate in the Illinois Municipal Retirement
Fund (IMRF).
   F.   No other compensation shall be allowed to a village trustee. (1985
Code, Ord. 24-18-67)
1-6-3: MEETINGS OF THE BOARD:
   A.   Regular Meetings: Regular meetings of the president and board of
trustees shall be held on the second and fourth Tuesday of each month. The
regular meeting held on the second Tuesday of each month shall be at seven
thirty o'clock (7:30) P.M. at the village hall. The meeting on the fourth
Tuesday of each month shall be at nine o’clock (9:00) a.m. at the village hall.
In case such meeting time shall fall on a legal holiday, then such meeting
shall be held the following day after such legal holiday, or, by a two-thirds
(2/3) vote of the board, the meeting may be eliminated or rescheduled. Any
regular meeting which does not fall on a legal holiday may be eliminated or
rescheduled by a two-thirds (2/3) vote of the board. Any regular meeting which
does not fall on a legal holiday may be canceled or rescheduled by a two-thirds
(2/3) vote of the board.
   B.   Special Meetings: Special meetings may be held at any time on call of
the president or any two (2) or more trustees. Such call shall be in writing,
duly signed and shall be presented to the clerk who shall proceed immediately
to cause notice of the same to be served upon the president and trustees in
person or by registered letter, and in such notice shall be described in brief
the nature or object of the call.
   C.   Open To The Public: Meetings of every kind and nature shall be open to
the public, except where specifically exempted by statute. A meeting may be
open to the public and then closed to the public for the purpose of considering
matters specifically allowed to be considered at a closed meeting by statute 1
. Final action on all matters considered at a closed meeting shall be made at
an open meeting.
   D.   Public Notice: A schedule of all the regular meetings of the board of
trustees and of any board or committee shall be posted on all the buildings of
the village having an office. In addition thereto, there shall be posted on the
bulletin boards of all of said buildings at least twenty four (24) hours before
said meeting, a notice of any special meeting or any reconvened regular meeting
where the subject was not on the agenda at the scheduled regular meeting.
Notices shall be sent to news media when required by statute.
   E.   Public Announcement Policy:
      1.   Prior to regularly scheduled Oak Lawn village board meetings, Oak
Lawn families, Oak Lawn businesses, and nonprofit organizations will be given a
maximum three (3) minute time slot to present public announcements which will
be aired on the village's public access channel. Speakers will be required to
sign in and will be taken in that order. Any speaker that has had a time slot
within the last month will be moved to the end of the list to provide equal
access to all. Any props are limited to being of poster size. Filming will
begin promptly at seven o'clock (7:00) P.M. and end at seven twenty five
o'clock (7:25) P.M. for the regular meeting on the second Tuesday of the month,
filming will begin promptly at eight thirty o’clock (8:30) A.M. and end at nine
o’clock (9:00) A.M. for the regular meeting on the fourth Tuesday of the month.
Speakers will be taken as long as there is time allotted.
      2.   The village of Oak Lawn reserves the right to cancel any
announcement it deems inappropriate including, but not limited to, commercial
advertisements and political announcements in support of any one candidate,
cause, or viewpoint subject to a pending vote or ballot question or does not
meet the requirements of being a not for profit, an Oak Lawn family, or Oak
Lawn business. The village of Oak Lawn does not guarantee the number of
showings or the times of the showings. The village does not endorse any one
organization over another nor does it provide editing or video enhancements to
the output. (1951 Code 1-1-2, 1-1-3, 1-1-4; Ords. 69-6-17, 73-16-15, 73-29-56,
73-35-68, 73-40-78, 81-36-76; 1985 Code; Ords. 86-3-10, 92-17-64, 05-12-31, 12-
20-68, 19-28-91)
 
Notes
1 1. 5 ILCS 120/1 et seq.
1-6-4: QUORUM:
A majority of the board of trustees elected shall constitute a quorum to do
business, but a smaller number may adjourn from time to time, and may compel
the attendance of absentees under such penalties as may be prescribed by
ordinance. (1951 Code 1-1-4)
1-6-5: RULES OF ORDER:
The following shall be the rules of order for the conduct of village business
by the president and board of trustees:
   A.   Opening Of Meetings: The village president shall take the chair at the
time designated for the commencement of the meeting. The president shall
immediately call the members to order.
The clerk shall proceed to call the roll of members, marking the members
present and absent for inclusion in the minutes. If a quorum is present, the
clerk shall announce that fact, and the board shall proceed with the order of
business.
   B.   Procedure In The Absence Of The President: If the president is not
present, the Board of Trustees shall elect an acting Chairman who shall act in
the President's stead. At such a meeting, the Clerk shall preside at the
commencement of the meeting for the purpose of calling the roll of members and
entertaining motions on the question of an acting Chairman. The acting Chairman
shall serve only until the adjournment of that meeting.
   C.   Order Of Business: The order of business at a Village Board meeting
shall be:
      1.   Call to order, roll call, Pledge Of Allegiance.
      2.   Public participation. Each person who desires to speak shall write
his/her name on a sign in sheet to be provided by the village clerk and then
wait until they are recognized by the village president, or temporary
chairperson in the absence of the village president. When recognized, each
person who desires to speak will then state his/her name and any comment or
question he/she may have. Each person who desires to speak shall have a maximum
of three (3) minutes to address the village board. This portion of the meeting
shall generally not exceed thirty (30) minutes, unless a majority of the
village board agrees to extend the time allotted for public participation for
that specific meeting.
      3.   Approval of minutes of previous meeting(s).
      4.   Employee recognition and awards.
      5.   New business of trustees.
      6.   Consent agenda.
      7.   Village manager's report.
      8.   Village attorney's report.
      9.   Committee reports.
      10.   Village clerk's report.
      11.   Semimonthly disbursements.
      12.   Village president's report.
      13.   Adjournment.
   D.   Powers Of The President: The Village President, as Chairman, shall
preserve order and decorum. The President may speak upon and decide all
questions of order, subject to an appeal to the Village Board. The President
shall conduct all business before the Board with fairness and impartiality to a
speedy and proper result.
   E.   Questions And Motions: Every question shall be distinctly stated by the
President before it is open for debate. Every question shall be reduced to
writing by its maker if requested by the President, Clerk or any Board member.
   F.   Addressing The Board: Any Board member who wishes to speak shall
audibly address the Chair as "Mr. President" or "Madam President". The Board
member may not speak further until recognized and named.
Trustees shall treat each other with respect and will address and refer to each
other as "Trustee (name)". The Village President and Trustees shall treat the
other members as equals. No Board member shall make any comments about another
Board member which are not pertinent to the discussions of the issue at hand.
The Chairman is obliged to recognize any Board member who wishes to speak
before a question is put to a vote.
   G.   Order And Decorum:
      1.   The Chairman shall preserve order among the audience and act to
remove anyone from the premises who, after due notice, continues to speak to
the Board without being recognized or who disrupts the decorum of the meeting.
Should the Chairman fail to call such an audience member to order, a Trustee
may request that the Chairman call the person to order by calling for a point
of order. A Trustee who is called to order shall immediately stop speaking.
Upon a request for a point of order, the Chairman must rule as to whether the
person is out of order.
      2.   The Chairman shall enforce order seriously and without regard to
political allegiance. The Chairman shall prevent disturbances against the
Board.
      3.   If there is no appeal, the decision of the Chairman shall be
conclusive. However, if a Trustee appeals the decision of the Chairman, the
Board shall decide the matter without debate by a roll call. The question shall
be: "Shall the decision of the Chairman be upheld?".
   H.   Consideration Of Motions: When a motion has been made and seconded, it
shall be debated. Following debate, the question shall be called for a vote.
Should the Chairman fail to call the question, or refuse to call the question,
any Trustee can address the Chairman and state, "Call the question". If the
Chairman continues to refuse to call the question, the Trustee can call for a
point of order, which requires the Chairman to make a ruling, which ruling may
then be appealed.
   I.   Duty To Vote: Whenever any Trustee, or the Village President, has a
present interest in the matter before the Board, he or she shall not vote on
the matter and shall explain the reason for his or her abstention. The vote of
a member who passes, votes "present", or votes "abstain", shall be added to the
majority of those voting on the question, unless the vote is a tie.
   J.   Consideration Of Postponed Questions: When a question is postponed
indefinitely, it shall not be voted upon before the next regular Board meeting.
   K.   Standing Committees:
      1.   The Committee of the Whole shall consist of all Trustees. The
remaining standing committees shall consist of three (3) Trustees appointed
upon motion by one (1) Trustee and approved by a majority vote of the Village
Board. A standing committee may also consist of ex officio members as
identified in this chapter. The Chairperson of each standing committee shall be
appointed by the members of the committee.
      2.   The President shall be a nonvoting ex officio member of all standing
committees of which the President is not an appointed member. The President
shall be the Chairman of the Committee of the Whole.
      3.   The members of the standing committees shall be appointed annually
at the second regular meeting of the Board of Trustees in May of each year for
a term of one (1) year. If no action is taken to replace any committee member,
each serving committee member shall retain said committee member position for
an additional one (1) year term.
      4.   The standing committees of the Village Board shall be:
         a.   Finance and Technology;
         b.   Public Works;
         c.   Legislative, License, and Ethics;
         d.   Public Safety; and
         e.   Committee of the Whole.
   L.   Special Committees: Special committees may be appointed for the
consideration of any particular matter. When the Board accepts the final report
of a special committee, the committee shall stand discharged without vote
unless otherwise ordered.
   M.   Committee Reports: All committees to which any matter has been referred
shall report, in writing, if any Trustee so requests. Any committee member may
present a minority report to the Board.
   N.   Petitions And Communications:
      1.   Requirements: All petitions or communications shall be addressed to
the President and Board of Trustees, be in writing, and filed by the Clerk.
      2.   Presentation: Any Trustee presenting a communication, petition,
ordinance, resolution or similar matter shall briefly state its nature before
presenting it.
   O.   Preservation Of Papers By Clerk: The Clerk shall preserve all committee
reports, ordinances, resolutions, petitions and communications, and similar
materials to be voted on.
   P.   Rules Of Order: In all cases not covered by these rules, the provisions
in the latest published edition of "Robert's Rules of Order Revised" shall
govern in all cases to which they are applicable and in which they are not
inconsistent with the statutes or laws of the State of Illinois.
   Q.   Suspension Of Rules: The rules of procedure can be suspended by a two-
thirds (2/3) vote of the President and Trustees voting jointly. (Ords. 92-17-
64, 95-11-35, 96-10-50, 05-12-31, 05-13-40, 10-12-22, 13-09-29, 14-06-14, 17-
15-56, 21-14-71)
1-6-6: ELECTION DISTRICTS:
   A.   For purposes of electing the Trustees of the Village, the boundaries of
the Village’s six (6) districts shall be as follows:
      1.   The district map, together with the description of district
boundaries, identified as the "Official District Map for the Village of Oak
Lawn," is hereby adopted as the official village map for trustee purposes.
Districts of the Village shall be properly identified by the word "District"
followed by the Arabic numeral corresponding to the District number assigned by
the redistricting ordinance.
      2.   A copy of the map showing generally the area and boundaries of each
district together with a supplement describing the boundaries using census
blocks as determined by the United States Census Bureau of each District is on
file in the Village Clerk's office. In general, the centerline of the
identified right-of-way in a redistricting ordinance shall be the boundary line
unless otherwise noted.
      3.   Territory annexed into the Village following the adoption of a
redistricting ordinance shall, upon annexation and without further action by
the President and Board of Trustees be made a part of the adjacent or
surrounding district, provided that whenever two or more districts surround or
are contiguous with the annexed territory, the President and Board of Trustees,
shall provide as part of the ordinance annexing the territory, what District or
Districts the territory shall be made a part of.
   B.   All streets or other geographic features referenced in subsection A of
this section are those that are so shown on the maps published by the United
States bureau of the census for the 2000 census.
   C.   Any part of the village that has not been described as included in one
of the districts set forth in subsection A of this section shall be included
within the district that:
      1.   Is contiguous to the part; and
      2.   Contains the least population of all districts contiguous to the
part according to the 2000 census.
   D.   If any part of the village is described in subsection A of this section
as being in more than one district, said part shall be included within the
district that:
      1.   Is one of the districts in which that part is described in
subsection A of this section;
      2.   Is contiguous to that part; and
      3.   Contains the least population according to the 2000 census.
   E.   If any part of the village:
      1.   Is described in subsection A of this section as being in one
district; and
      2.   Is entirely surrounded by another district;
the part shall be incorporated into the district that surrounds the part.
   F.   If any part of the village:
      1.   Is described in subsection A of this section as being in one
district; and
      2.   Is not contiguous to another part of that district;
the part shall be included with the contiguous district that contains the least
population according to the 2000 census. (Ord. 2002-17-68, 22-16-53)
CHAPTER 7
COMMITTEES
SECTION:
1-7-1: Finance And Technology Committee
1-7-2: Traffic Safety Committee
1-7-3: Public Works Committee
1-7-4: Legislative, License, And Ethics Committee
1-7-5: Public Safety Committee
1-7-6: Committee Of The Whole
1-7-1: FINANCE AND TECHNOLOGY COMMITTEE:
   A.   Membership: A Finance and Technology Committee shall be appointed upon
motion by one (1) Trustee and approved by a majority of the Village Board. Said
Finance and Technology Committee shall consist of three (3) Trustees and the
committee shall appoint a Chairperson from among its members at the committee's
June meeting. The Village President, Village Treasurer, Village Manager, and IT
Director shall be ex officio members of the Finance and Technology Committee.
   B.   Meetings: The Finance and Technology Committee shall meet annually and
may hold special meetings as needed. Three (3) days' notice of the time and
place of regular and special meetings shall be given in writing by the
Secretary at the direction of the Chairman or upon the written request of not
less than two (2) members.
   C.   Powers And Duties:
      1.   The power of the Finance and Technology Committee shall be to review
policies and recommend changes to the Board of Trustees. The committee shall
have the power to require Village department heads and employees to appear
before it and answer questions and assist the committee in its deliberations.
In addition, the committee shall review and advise the Board of Trustees in
relation to fiscal and technology policies of the Village, including the
following:
         a.   Review and advise the Board of Trustees regarding short and long
term investment policy of funds under their control or direction.
         b.   Examine and interpret the financial reporting system for the
Board of Trustees and recommend possible changes in it.
         c.   Review and interpret for the Board of Trustees the annual audit
as prepared by certified public accountants retained by the Village.
         d.   Advise the Board of Trustees in the selection of certified public
accountants to be used by the Village, and frequency of their change.
         e.   On behalf of the Board of Trustees, review the reports required
to be filed with the Village pursuant to the terms of subsection
9-5-12B of this Code and make recommendations thereon to the Board.
         f.   Participate with and make recommendations to the Board of
Trustees in the three (3) year reviews of each licensee pursuant to subsection
9-5-14A of this Code.
         g.   From time to time, prepare and recommend to the Board of Trustees
plans for specific improvements to or amendments to title 9, chapter 5 of this
Code.
         h.   Study, review and advise the Village Board on utilizing
technology to make Village operations more efficient and resident friendly, and
to enhance communications with residents.
      2.   The Finance and Technology Committee, established pursuant to this
section shall exercise such powers and perform such functions in connection
therewith the BTN and title 9, chapter 5 of this Code as the Village Board
shall from time to time provide the following information including, but not
limited to:
         a.   The committee shall advise the Village Board with respect to all
aspects of the grantee's actions in connection with the BTN in the Village.
         b.   The committee shall review all aspects of the grantee's actions
in connection with the BTN.
         c.   The committee, if it deems it necessary and according to such
procedural rules as it may decide which afford the grantee and a subscriber a
fair hearing, may conduct hearings in review of grantee decisions and actions
with respect to section
9-5-10 of this Code. Decisions by the committee with respect to such hearings
shall be binding upon the grantee.
   D.   Relation With Other Agencies: The Finance and Technology Committee
shall not review matters under the control of the pension fund boards 1  or the
agencies having jurisdiction over Village related funds, but only those under
control or direction of the Board of Trustees. (Ord. 17-15-56)
 
Notes
1 1. See title 2, chapter 7 and 8 of this Code.
1-7-2: TRAFFIC SAFETY COMMITTEE 2 :
   A.   Appointment And Membership:
      1.   There is hereby created and established a seven (7) member Traffic
Safety Committee, whereby the President shall appoint one (1) member and each
Village Trustee shall appoint one (1) member to the Committee from their
district. All appointments, except to vacancies, shall begin on September 1 of
the calendar year. Members of the Traffic Safety Committee serving as of the
effective date hereof shall serve until the end of the term to which appointed
and until their successors are named and qualified. Effective September 1,
2019, a full term shall be four (4) years and terms shall be staggered so not
more than three (3) expire each year. Such staggered terms shall coincide with
District 1, 3, and 5 appointees to expire opposite years of District 2, 4, and
6 appointees. For purposes of this Chapter, such appointments for District 2,
4, and 6 beginning on September 1, 2019, shall have initial term limit to end
in calendar year 2021 so as to initiate such staggered terms. If a vacancy
exists following the failure and/or refusal by a Village Trustee to appoint a
member by October 1 of the calendar year, an appointment shall be made by a
recommendation from the President with approval of the majority of the Village
Board.
         a.   The President and Board of Trustees may compel attendance of any
absent member and may remove any such member from said Committee if that member
misses three (3) consecutive meetings. The removal shall be initiated by the
President and subject to the confirmation of such removal by the advice and
consent of the Village Board.
         b.   Each member of the Committee shall receive a sum of one hundred
dollars ($100.00) per meeting for every properly posted meeting attended, or
such prorated portion thereof as shall represent the time served in a given
calendar year, with a minimum annual renumeration of $500.00 per member if the
member attends 70% or more of all properly posted meetings. This remuneration
is intended to cover normal and necessary expenses of the member. Additional or
unusual expenses may be covered as budgeted upon receipt of proper evidence of
expenditure and approval by this Committee.
         c.   All appointments, except to vacancies, shall begin on September 1
the calendar year. Members of the Traffic Safety Committee shall serve as of
the effective date hereof and shall serve until the end of the term to which
appointed and until their successors are named and qualified.
      2.   A Chairman shall be selected by the committee from its membership.
      3.   Members of staff who shall sit with the committee and act as
advisors, but shall not have committee voting privileges, shall consist of at
least one (1) member from each of the following Village departments:
         Department of Community Development and Growth Management
         Police Department
         Public Works Department
   B.   Meetings:
      1.   Regular Meetings: Meetings shall be held at every month on the 1st
Thursday of said month, except that the Board may, by rule, provide for
changing the date for seasonal reasons in the event there is no pending
business.
      2.   Special Meetings: Special meetings may be called by the chairman or
by any three (3) members of the Board.
      3.   Open To The Public: All meetings of the Architectural Review &
Preservation Commission and its committees shall be open meetings as required
under the applicable statutes of the State of Illinois and codes of the
Village.
   C.   Duties And Responsibilities: The Traffic Safety Committee shall review
and advise the Board of Trustees in relation to the traffic policies of the
Village, including the following:
      1.   Review and advise the Board of Trustees regarding short term and
long term traffic policies;
      2.   Review and make recommendations to the Board of Trustees on all
citizens' petitions for traffic control changes, including, but not limited to,
requests for speed reductions, stop signs and traffic control devices;
      3.   Review and make recommendations to the Board of Trustees on all
traffic studies prepared by consultants hired by the Village.
   D.   Powers: The power of the Traffic Safety Committee shall be to review
traffic policies and recommend changes to the Board of Trustees.
   E.   Procedure For Traffic Changes: The following procedures shall be
followed for all requests for traffic changes:
      Step 1: All requests for traffic control devices must be in writing,
regardless of their origin. Requests must be submitted to the Department of
Community Development and Growth Management no later than ten (10) days prior
to the next scheduled meeting, to assure placement on the agenda.
      Step 2: The Department of Community Development and Growth Management
will assign a number and send a response to the petitioner indicating that his
request will be discussed at the next meeting and he will be invited to attend.
No more than three (3) persons involved with the petition will be permitted to
speak to the subject.
      Step 3: The Department of Community Development and Growth Management
will send a copy of the request to each of the members of the Traffic Safety
Committee.
      Step 4: Upon receipt of copy of request, the area representative will
make immediate personal contact with petitioner to discuss the nature of the
request.
      Step 5: The request will be submitted to the Traffic Safety Committee as
an agenda item. The Traffic Safety Committee, petitioner, and staff will
informally discuss the request made and study the information presented in
order to arrive at a consensus of the members regarding the request.
      Step 6: Pursuant to the discussions which take place in step 5 of this
subsection, staff will propose their recommendations regarding the item.
      Step 7: The item will again be placed on the agenda of the committee,
accompanied by staff recommendations.
      Step 8: The staff and Traffic Safety Committee will make a joint
recommendation as to the disposition of the request.
      Step 9: Staff will prepare any sign drawings which may be necessary to
assist in the posting of the signs as recommended.
      Step 10: The request will be submitted to the Village President and Board
for action, accompanied by the following documents:
         1. The final report of the staff;
          2. The joint recommendation of the Traffic Safety Committee and
staff;
         3. Any sign drawing which may be necessary.
      Step 11: The Board of Trustees will either approve or deny the ordinance
as presented.
      Step 12: If the request is denied by the Board of Trustees, the matter
will be referred back to the Traffic Safety Committee to reevaluate its
recommendations. If the conclusions of the Traffic Safety Committee remain as
originally suggested, the item will be placed on file for later routine follow-
up on an ongoing basis. If the committee recommendation is amended or revised
from that which it originally made, the matter will return to step 5 of this
subsection and continue on from there. If the request is approved by the Board
of Trustees, the Village Attorney will be authorized to prepare the required
ordinance for adoption.
      Step 13: The Village Attorney will submit a copy of the proposed
ordinance to the Department of Community Development and Growth Management to
assure conformity with the approved request.
      Step 14: Upon approval by the Department of Community Development and
Growth Management, the Village Attorney will present the proposed ordinance to
the Board of Trustees for approval and adoption.
      Step 15: Immediately after passage of the ordinance, the Department of
Community Development and Growth Management shall issue a written order to the
Public Works Department for the installation of signs, as required by the
ordinance.
      Step 16: No sooner than ten (10) days after adoption of the ordinance,
the Public Works Department will post signs as required by the ordinance, and
drawings (if necessary) as approved by the Board of Trustees.
      Step 17: The Police Department will begin enforcement of the ordinance
immediately following the installation of signs.
   F.   Emergency Traffic Changes: Where a requested traffic change involves
serious imminent safety issues that cannot be addressed adequately through the
procedure for traffic changes as outlined in subsection E of this section, the
requested change may be heard directly by the Village Board without going
through the procedure for traffic changes as outlined in subsection E of this
section. (Ord. 84-5-10, 1985 Code, Ords. 92-02-12, 92-11-45, 93-9-24, 01-09-31,
06-23-137, 19-16-67, 23-13-47)
 
Notes
2 2. See title 11, "Motor Vehicles And Traffic", of this Code.
1-7-3: PUBLIC WORKS COMMITTEE:
   A.   Appointment And Membership: A Public Works Committee shall be appointed
upon motion by one (1) Trustee and approved by a majority of the Village Board.
Said Public Works Committee shall consist of three (3) Trustees, and the
committee shall appoint a Chairperson from among its members at the committee's
June meeting. The Director of Public Works, Village President and Village
Engineer shall be ex officio members of the Public Works Committee.
   B.   Meetings: The Public Works Committee shall meet annually and may hold
special meetings as needed. Three (3) days' notice of the time and place of
regular and special meetings shall be given in writing by the Secretary at the
direction of the Chairman or upon the written request of not less than two (2)
members.
   C.   Powers And Duties:
      1.   The power of the Public Works Committee shall be to review policies
and recommend changes to the Board of Trustees. In addition, the committee
shall have the power to require Village department heads and employees to
appear before it and answer questions and assist the committee in its
deliberations.
      2.   The Public Works Committee shall review and advise the Board of
Trustees in relation to public works and waterworks policies of the Village,
including the following:
         a.   Investigate and make studies of the Public Works Department and
its policies and make recommendations to the Board of Trustees relative
thereto.
         b.   Investigate and make studies of the waterworks system and make
recommendations to the Board of Trustees relative thereto.
         c.   Review and advise the Board of Trustees regarding short term and
long term maintenance and repair needs of streets, curbs, sidewalks,
streetlights, sewers, water mains and appurtenances and public buildings under
the jurisdiction of the Department of Public Works of the Village.
         d.   Review and advise the Board of Trustees regarding short term and
long term maintenance and repair needs of water mains and appurtenances of the
complete waterworks system.
         e.   Review and advise the Board of Trustees regarding all contracts
for the sale of water by the Village to other communities and entities.
         f.   Periodically review and advise the Board of Trustees as to
personnel and equipment needs of the Public Works Department. (Ord. 17-15-56)
1-7-4: LEGISLATIVE, LICENSE, AND ETHICS COMMITTEE:
   A.   Appointment And Membership: The Legislative, License, and Ethics
Committee shall be appointed upon motion by one (1) Trustee and approved by a
majority of the Village Board. Said Legislative, License, and Ethics Committee
shall consist of three (3) Trustees, and the committee shall appoint a
Chairperson from among its members at its June meeting. The Village President
shall be an ex officio member of the Legislative, License, and Ethics
Committee.
   B.   Meetings: The Legislative, License, and Ethics Committee shall meet
annually and may hold special meetings as needed. Three (3) days notice of the
time and place of regular and special meetings shall be given in writing by the
Secretary at the direction of the Chairman or upon the written request of not
less than two (2) members.
   C.   Powers And Duties:
      1.   The power of the Legislative, License, and Ethics Committee shall be
to review policies and recommend changes to the Board of Trustees. In addition,
the committee shall have the power to require Village officers, department
heads, and employees to appear before it and answer questions and assist the
committee in its deliberations.
      2.   The Legislative, License, and Ethics Committee shall review and
advise the Board of Trustees in relation to legal policies, ordinances amending
this Code, licenses and license fees, and the Village of Oak Lawn ethics
ordinance including the following:
         a.   Review and advise the Board of Trustees on Municipal Code
violations and the prosecution and administrative adjudication thereof.
         b.   Perform detailed work and prepare the text of suggested
ordinances.
         c.   Periodically review this Code and recommend amendments,
additions, and repeals of Code provisions to the Board of Trustees.
         d.   Periodically review the procedure for issuance of business
licenses in the Village and make recommendations to the Board of Trustees
relative thereto.
         e.   Review the number and types of licenses issued in the Village and
make recommendations thereon to the Board of Trustees.
         f.   Review ethics ordinances adopted by other municipalities and
Ethics Code suggestions brought forward by the citizens of the Village and make
recommendations to the Board of Trustees relative thereto. (Ord. 17-15-56)
1-7-5: PUBLIC SAFETY COMMITTEE:
   A.   Appointment And Membership: The Public Safety Committee shall be
appointed upon motion by one (1) Trustee and approved by a majority of the
Village Board. Said Public Safety Committee shall consist of three (3)
Trustees, and the committee shall appoint a Chairperson from among its members
at its June meeting. The Village President shall be an ex officio member of the
Public Safety Committee.
   B.   Meetings: The Public Safety Committee shall meet annually and may hold
special meetings as needed. Three (3) days' notice of the time and place of
regular and special meetings shall be given in writing by the Secretary at the
direction of the Chairman or upon the written request of not less than two (2)
members.
   C.   Powers And Duties:
      1.   The power of the Public Safety Committee shall be to review policies
and recommend changes to the Board of Trustees. In addition, the committee
shall have the power to require Village department heads and employees to
appear before it and answer questions and assist the committee in its
deliberations.
      2.   The Public Safety Committee shall give advice to the Fire Chief and
Police Chief when asked.
      3.   The Public Safety Committee shall review and advise the Board of
Trustees in relation to public safety policies of the Village, including the
following:
         a.   Provide support to works of ongoing projects of the Police and
Fire Departments.
         b.   Provide guidance on police and fire activities in accordance with
the Village policy. (Ord. 17-15-56)
1-7-6: COMMITTEE OF THE WHOLE:
   A.   Membership: The Committee of the Whole shall consist of the Village
President and all Trustees currently holding office. The Village President
shall be Chairperson of the Committee of the Whole, and the Village Treasurer,
Manager and Director of Administrative Services shall be ex officio members of
the committee.
   B.   Meetings: Committee of the Whole meetings shall be held on the second
Tuesday of each month at six fifteen o'clock (6:15) P.M. at the Oak Lawn
Village Hall immediately preceding the regular Village Board meeting if
requested by the Village President or three (3) or more Trustees. When such
meeting time shall fall on a legal holiday, then such meeting shall be held on
the same day as the rescheduled, regular Village Board meeting. Committee of
the Whole meetings shall end at the hour of seven fifteen o'clock (7:15) P.M.,
when practicable.
   C.   Purpose: The purpose of the Committee of the Whole meetings shall be
primarily for discussing and vetting items which will be brought before the
Village Board for eventual consideration and approval. Routine committee and
commission referrals need not be placed on the Committee of the Whole agenda
unless said referrals require a presentation and/or upon which discussion is
anticipated. In no event shall the Committee of the Whole vote on ordinances or
resolutions.
   D.   Agenda: The agenda shall be prepared by the Village Clerk and shall be
in the following form:
      1.   Roll call
      2.   Pledge of Allegiance
      3.   Items for discussion on the regular meeting agenda
      4.   Items for discussion on the next or future meeting agendas
      5.   Adjournment. (Ord. 17-15-56)
CHAPTER 8
VILLAGE CLERK
SECTION:
1-8-1: Bond
1-8-2: Duties
1-8-3: Hold Office Of Collector
1-8-4: Deputy Village Clerk; Appointment And Powers
1-8-5: Compensation
1-8-1: BOND:
Before entering upon the duties of the office of village clerk, the clerk shall
execute a bond with security payable to the village in the penal sum of five
thousand dollars ($5,000.00), conditioned upon the faithful performance of the
duties of the office according to law and the ordinances of the village. (1951
Code 1-3, 2-4; Ords. 67-1-3, 76-2-5)
1-8-2: DUTIES:
   A.   General: The village clerk shall, in addition to the duties now imposed
upon such officer by statute, perform the following duties:
      1.   The clerk shall keep an office for the transaction of village
business.
      2.   The clerk shall issue notices of meetings to the president and
members of the village board, and when directed by that body, to the members of
the different committees of that body and to all persons whose attendance will
be required before that body or any such committee when so directed. The clerk
shall also issue and cause to be served notices of said meetings. The clerk
shall also cause the notices to be sent, posted and published as required by
subsection
1-6-3D of this title.
      3.   The clerk shall attest all the licenses granted under the ordinances
of the village.
      4.   The clerk shall, without delay, deliver to the officers of the
village and to the various committees, all bills, resolutions, communications
and other documents referring to such officers and committees.
      5.   The clerk shall, without delay, deliver to the president all
ordinances or resolutions under the clerk's charge which may require approval
or other action by the president, with all papers and information on which the
same were founded.
      6.   The clerk shall perform such other duties as may be required by the
president and board of trustees and the ordinances of the village.
   B.   Corporate Seal And Records: The clerk shall keep the corporate seal and
affix the same to all papers which require it. The clerk shall keep all records
and papers belonging to the village and attend all meetings of the president
and board of trustees, and keep a full record of its proceedings in the
journal. The clerk shall make proper copies of all papers duly filed in such
office and make transcripts from the journal and other records and files of
such office, certified by the clerk under the corporate seal.
   C.   Record, Publish Ordinances: All ordinances passed by the president and
board of trustees shall be recorded by the clerk in a book kept for that
purpose before the next regular meeting after they go into effect.
If the ordinance requires publication, the clerk shall cause the same to be
duly published. The board of trustees may authorize the publication of said
ordinance in a newspaper published in the village or to be published in a book
or pamphlet form. The clerk shall note at the foot of the record of each
ordinance, a memorandum of the date of its passage and approval or otherwise
becoming effective, and if published, the date of the publication of such
ordinances. The original shall be filed in the office of said clerk with a
certificate of publication or posting attached to the ordinance requiring
publication.
   D.   Notify Elected And Appointed Officers: It shall be the duty of the
village clerk, within five (5) days after the result of an election is declared
or an appointment made, to notify all persons elected or appointed to office.
Unless such persons shall respectively qualify in ten (10) days after such
notice, the office shall become and be vacant.
   E.   File Tax Levy Ordinances: It shall be the duty of the village clerk, on
or before the second Tuesday in September in each year, to file with the county
clerk of Cook County, Illinois, a certified copy of the levy ordinance in which
the total amount of appropriations for all corporate purposes of the village
legally made are levied and assessed for the current fiscal year.
   F.   Receive, Pass On Communications: All petitions, remonstrances, bids,
bills or other communications addressed to the village or board of trustees
shall be received by the clerk and read by the clerk at the board meeting next
succeeding their receipt, unless otherwise ordered by the village board.
   G.   Publication Of Legal Notices: The village clerk or a designated
representative shall be responsible for coordinating and publishing all legal
notices and designating the newspaper in which such notices are to be
published. All boards, commissions, committees and bureaus of the village shall
send all legal notices to the village clerk's office for publication,
designating the date or dates on which it is to be published. Upon request, the
village clerk shall supply any such board, commission, committee or bureau with
a publisher's certificate showing the date or dates on which any such notice
was published. (1951 Code 1-3, 2-4; Ords. 67-1-3, 76-2-5, 83-20-49)
1-8-3: HOLD OFFICE OF COLLECTOR:
Pursuant to 65 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/3-8-3, the village clerk shall hold
the office of village collector. No additional compensation shall be paid to
the village clerk for the fulfillment of such duties. (1985 Code, Ord. 24-18-
67)
1-8-4: DEPUTY VILLAGE CLERK; APPOINTMENT AND POWERS:
   A.   The village clerk may appoint one deputy village clerk, which deputy
village clerk shall have the power and duty to execute all documents required
by law or ordinance to be executed by the village clerk and to affix the seal
of the village thereto, whenever required.
   B.   The appointment of the deputy village clerk shall be made by the
village clerk and the deputy village clerk shall serve at the pleasure of and
perform such duties as directed by the village clerk.
   C.   In signing any document as described in subsection A of this section,
the deputy village clerk shall sign the name of the clerk followed with the
word "by" and the deputy village clerk's own name, and the words "deputy
village clerk".
   D.   Such duties shall be exercised at the direction of the village clerk,
or when the president and board of trustees have determined by resolution that
the village clerk is temporarily or permanently incapacitated and unable to
perform such functions.
   E.   The deputy clerk shall receive such compensation as determined by the
village clerk, in consultation with the Village Manager, and shall be eligible
to participate in all employee benefit plans, on the same terms as other non-
union village employees. (Ords. 93-11-40, 24-18-67)
1-8-5: COMPENSATION:
   A.   The Village Clerk currently receives such compensation as reflected in
the most recent pay and benefit ordinance.
   B.   Beginning June 1, 2025, for village clerks whose terms commence in May,
2025, after the consolidated election in April of 2025, the use of pay and
benefit ordinances to set salaries will be discontinued, and, instead, the
village clerk shall receive a salary of $5,000 per month, payable in accordance
with the village’s regular bi-weekly payroll schedule. Further, beginning June
1, 2026, and on each June 1st thereafter for as long as the individual remains
in office, incumbent village clerks shall receive an increase in the
compensation then in effect equal to the percent change in the Chicago Area
Consumer Price Index for all Urban Consumers (CPI-U) as calculated and
published by the federal government for the period beginning May of the
previous year and ending April of the current year, rounded to the next highest
dollar amount as appropriate for purposes of resulting in an even dollar amount
for payroll purposes. Thereafter, a newly elected non-incumbent village clerk’s
compensation shall begin at a rate of $5,000 per month, payable in accordance
with the village’s regular bi-weekly payroll schedule, and after twelve months
will be increased annually on June 1st of each year thereafter, as indicated
above based on the applicable CPI-U, for as long as the individual remains in
office.
   C.   In addition, as a part of compensation, the village clerk is currently
eligible, and will remain eligible, to participate in the following village
group employee benefit plans, on the same basis and terms that are applicable
to non-union employees: group health insurance, health insurance buy-out,
dental and vision insurance, life insurance, and flexible benefit plan. In
addition, the village clerk is currently eligible, and will remain eligible, to
participate in other voluntary benefit plans, such as accident, critical
illness, hospital, and legal, with costs to be fully paid by the participants.
   D.   The village clerk is currently eligible to participate in the Illinois
Municipal Retirement Fund (IMRF).
   E.   No other compensation shall be allowed to the village clerk. (Ord. 24-
18-67)
CHAPTER 9
VILLAGE OFFICERS AND EMPLOYEES 1
SECTION:
1-9-1: Administrative Organizational Plan
1-9-2: Election Of Officers
1-9-3: Oath Required
1-9-4: Bond Requirements
1-9-5: Failure To Give Bond
1-9-6: Payment Of Salaries
1-9-7: Duties
1-9-8: Records Open For Inspection, Reports
1-9-9: Office Hours
1-9-10: Benefits
 
Notes
1 1. A personnel policy manual is adopted as the policy of the village, a copy
  of which is on file with the village.
1-9-1: ADMINISTRATIVE ORGANIZATIONAL PLAN:
   A.   Short Title: This section shall be known as the ADMINISTRATIVE
REORGANIZATION ORDINANCE.
   B.   Administrative Officers Appointed: The village manager, as the
administrative head of the village, shall appoint people to five (5) positions
to be entitled directors, a fire chief and a police chief, who shall serve
until such time as they may resign or be replaced by the village manager, to be
given the following titles, which are hereby created:
      Director of administrative services
      Director of quality control
      Director of public works
      Fire chief
      Police chief
The village manager is authorized to appoint, suspend or discharge the
aforementioned three (3) directors, the police chief and the fire chief without
the consent of the board of trustees.
C.   Supervisory Duties; Assistants And Deputies Designated: The three (3)
directors shall each direct, coordinate and supervise two (2) or more specific
areas of service in the village, which positions shall be headed by persons
known as assistant directors or as otherwise entitled herein. The fire chief
shall supervise and direct one (1) deputy fire chief. The police chief shall
appoint, supervise, and direct one deputy police chief. All of these positions
shall be filled by an appointment by the village manager and shall serve until
such time as they resign or are replaced by the village manager, with the
exception of the deputy police chief, which shall be filled by an appointment
by the chief of police and shall serve until such time as he or she resigns or
is replaced by the chief of police.
      1.   The director of administrative services shall have under his direct
supervision the following:
         Assistant director for administration and personnel
         Assistant director for data processing
         Comptroller
      2.   The director of quality control shall have under his direct
supervision the following:
         Assistant director for code compliance
         Assistant director for quality control
      3.   The director of public works shall have under his direct supervision
the following:
         Assistant director for equipment maintenance
         Assistant director for streets and sewers
         Water Division Manager
         a.   The Water Division Manager shall have under his direct
supervision an Assistant Water Division Manager who shall be appointed by the
Village Manager.
   D.   When Office To Become Operational: All positions named in this section
shall become operational upon the appointment by the village manager of persons
to fill the named positions, subject to the guidelines of this section.
   E.   Effect Of Plan On Present Employees: It shall be the policy of the
village to retain persons presently in the employ of the village to fill
similar positions under the “administrative reorganization ordinance”. No
present employee shall be separated as a direct result of implementation of
this plan.
   F.   Reclassification, Wage Rate: When reclassification of a position shall
result in a reduction in grade, the employee so affected shall continue to be
paid at the same rate as the higher grade upon assumption of the new position,
but will receive no increases in salary until the pay set for the employee’s
new position classification reaches the level of this current salary.
Thereafter, said employee shall receive all pay increases due him pursuant to
the ordinances, rules and regulations of the village.
   G.   Implementation Of Plan: The positions created under this section shall
be implemented by enactment of an ordinance that will amend the position, pay
and benefits ordinance of the village by setting forth the new position classes
and grades and concurrently abolishing classes and grades being replaced.
   H.   Police And Fire Department Personnel: Until further implementation of
this section, the village manager shall have the authority to appoint, suspend
or discharge the chief of police, the fire chief, and the deputy fire chief
without the consent of the board of trustees. The chief of police, the fire
chief, and the deputy fire chief need not be appointed from the members of the
police or fire departments. The power of appointment or disciplinary action of
all other officers of the police and fire departments, except the police
department's code enforcement officer, shall rest in the board of fire and
police commissioners of the village 1 , heretofore created and now acting
pursuant to the provisions of the Illinois municipal code. The chief of police
shall have the power to appoint, suspend or discharge the code enforcement
officer for the police department. In the event the village manager discharges
the chief of police, fire chief, or the deputy fire chief prior to being
eligible to retire on pension, they shall be considered as on furlough from the
rank they held with the Village immediately prior to their appointment as chief
of police, fire chief, or deputy fire chief, if any, not including positions
previously appointed by the Village Manager. If the chief of police, fire
chief, or deputy fire chief resigns from or is discharged from their appointed
position prior to attaining eligibility to retire on pension, they shall revert
to and be established in such prior rank, if any, not including positions
previously appointed by the Village Manager, and thereafter be entitled to all
the benefits and emoluments of such prior rank, without regard as to whether a
vacancy then exists in such rank, as established under the fire and police
commission act. 2  
(Ords. 76-7-10, 81-12-19; 1985 Code; Ords. 89-13-30, 91-23-98, 92-02-10, 93-5-
13, 10-12-23, 19-15-51, 25-11-25)
 
Notes
1 1. See title 2, chapter 6 of this code.
2 2. 65 ILCS 5/10-2.1-1 et seq.
1-9-2: ELECTION OF OFFICERS:
   A.   Procedure For Election: Elections for municipal offices shall be held
in accordance with and at the time prescribed by statute and this code.
   B.   Village President, Village Clerk And Village Trustees:
      1.   The election for the officers of the village, to include the village
president, village clerk and three (3) village trustees shall be for a four (4)
year term and shall be held on the third Tuesday in April of every other odd
numbered year commencing April 15, 1969.
      2.   The election of three (3) village trustees for a four (4) year term
shall be held on the third Tuesday of April of the odd numbered year commencing
April 20, 1971.
      3.   Effective for any person elected to the office of village president,
village clerk, or village trustee at the April 9, 2015, consolidated election
and all those elected to such offices thereafter:
         a.   No person shall serve as village president for more than three
(3) consecutive four (4) year terms.
         b.   No person shall serve as village clerk for more than three (3)
consecutive four (4) year terms.
         c.   No person shall serve as village trustee for more than three (3)
consecutive four (4) year terms.
   C.   Library Trustees: The election of two (2) library trustees of the Oak
Lawn public library district for a six (6) year term shall be held on the third
Tuesday of April of every odd numbered year commencing April 15, 1969.
   D.   Vacancies In Office: In the event that any of the aforementioned
offices shall become vacant due to death, resignation, disqualification, or
other cause prior to ten (10) days before the last day for filing nominating
petitions for the next general election, then said office shall be filled for
the unexpired term at the next general election of the village.
   E.   Inauguration Date: Pursuant to 65 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/3.1-10-
15, the inauguration date for the elected officers of the village (village
president, 6 trustees and village clerk) is hereby established as the first
regular village board meeting in May following the election. (1951 Code 2-8-7;
Ords. 68(8), 04-19-94, 15-05-19)
1-9-3: OATH REQUIRED:
All officers of the village, whether elected or appointed, shall before
entering upon the duties of their respective offices, take and subscribe the
following oath or affirmation:
I do solemnly swear (or affirm, as the case may be), that I will support the
Constitution of the United States, and the Constitution of the State of
Illinois, and that I will faithfully discharge the duties of the office of
                      of the Village of Oak Lawn, according to the best of my
ability.
This oath, so subscribed, shall be filed in the office of the clerk, except
that the oaths of firemen and policemen shall be filed with the board of police
and fire commissioners. (1951 Code 2-1-3)
1-9-4: BOND REQUIREMENTS:
   A.   General: The officers of the village shall give bonds in the sums and
within the times prescribed by the board of trustees. Said bonds shall be as
provided by law. Should good cause exist in the judgment and discretion of the
board of trustees, any officer may be required, from time to time, to give
additional bonds in such sums and within such time as the board may by
resolution direct.
   B.   Conditions Of Bonds, Sureties 1 : The bond of each officer shall be
executed to the village with two (2) or more sureties conditioned for the
faithful performance of the duties of his office, and the payment to the proper
officer of all monies coming into his hands by virtue of his office. Each of
said sureties shall be freeholders of Cook County, and shall schedule real
estate therein, clear of all encumbrances and other legal obligations, in value
not less than the penal sum of said bond, which schedule shall be made
separately by said sureties and sworn to before some officer authorized to
administer oaths, and attached to said bond. Provided, that any officer of the
village may procure as surety upon any bond he may be required to give, any
responsible surety company or corporation; and provided, further, that no
member of the board of trustees shall sign any bond in any case wherein the
village is interested.
   C.   Approval, Filing Of Bonds: The bonds of all village officers shall be
subject to the approval of the president and board of trustees. After the
approval as aforesaid, the clerk shall endorse thereon the date thereof and
file the same in his office except the bond of the clerk, which shall be filed
with the treasurer.
   D.   Required Bond Amounts: Bonds shall be required for all village
officers, and certain specified employees, in the following amounts:
      1.   Village president: No bond required as per 65 Illinois Compiled
Statutes 5/5-3-8.
      2.   Trustees: No bond required as per 65 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/5-
3-8.
      3.   Village clerk: See section
1-8-1 of this title.
      4.   Village manager: Five thousand dollars ($5,000.00).
      5.   Village treasurer: See section
1-9C-2 of this chapter.
      6.   Village collector: See section
1-9E-2 of this chapter.
      7.   Village department heads: Five thousand dollars ($5,000.00) each.
      8.   Cashiers in the finance department: Five thousand dollars
($5,000.00) each.
      9.   All other village officers: One dollar ($1.00) each. (1951 Code
2-1-4,
2-1-5,
2-1-6; Ord. 94-12-53)
 
Notes
1 1. See section
  1-11-8 of this title.
1-9-5: FAILURE TO GIVE BOND:
If any person elected or appointed to any office of the village shall fail to
qualify by taking, subscribing and filing his official bond as herein provided,
within ten (10) days after he is officially notified of his election or
appointment, the office shall become vacant. (1951 Code
2-1-7)
1-9-6: PAYMENT OF SALARIES:
Unless otherwise specified in this code, the salary and pay of all officers and
employees shall be paid semimonthly to each person entitled thereto. (1951 Code
2-1-8)
1-9-7: DUTIES:
   A.   General: Each officer, besides the specific duties which may be
required of him by virtue of his office, shall also perform such other and
further duties, and be subject to such rules and regulations, as the manager or
president and board of trustees may, from time to time, require.
   B.   Deliver Effects To Successor: Any person being an officer or employee
of the village, whose term has expired or who has resigned or has been removed
from office, shall, within ten (10) days after notification and request,
deliver to his successor in office all property, books and effects of every
description in his possession belonging to the village or pertaining to his
office. (1951 Code
2-1-9, 2-1-10)
1-9-8: RECORDS OPEN FOR INSPECTION, REPORTS:
All books, papers, vouchers, warrants, entries, accounts and memoranda of each
and every officer and employee of the village may be examined at any time by
the manager or the president, and the president and board of trustees may, by
resolution, call upon any officer to make a report in writing of any subject
matter which said board may designate in such resolution and within such time
as said resolution may prescribe. (1951 Code 2-1-11)
1-9-9: OFFICE HOURS:
The offices of village officers shall be open for business at such hours as
shall be prescribed by resolution by the village board. (1951 Code 2-1-12)
1-9-10: BENEFITS:
   A.   Village Benefit Plans: Village employees and certain village officers
will be eligible to participate in employee benefit plans, as described in this
code and in the Oak Lawn Benefit Handbook, as amended from time to time.
   B.   Benefits Not Guaranteed And Subject To Change: The benefits provided in
this section are not guaranteed and are subject to change at any time in the
sole discretion of the Village in accordance with all applicable law.
   C.   Paid Leave; Minimum Wage:
      1.   Employees of the Village of Oak Lawn: Pursuant to Village of Oak
Lawn Ordinance No. 23-21-78 and Ordinance No. 24-01-04, such Ordinances govern
and supersede all provisions of the Illinois Paid Leave for All Workers Act and
Cook County Ordinance No. 24-0583 relative to the employment relationship
between the Village of Oak Lawn and its employees. No additional obligations
with regard to mandatory paid leave, including without limitation, any
obligations adopted under the Paid Leave for All Workers Act by the State of
Illinois and/or obligations adopted under the Cook County Ordinance No. 24-0583
by the County of Cook, shall apply to the Village in its capacity as an
employer, except those required by Federal or State of Illinois laws and
regulations preempting the Village’s home rule authority.
      2.   Other Employers within the Village of Oak Lawn: For the purposes of
this subsection, the term “employee” means an individual permitted and/or hired
to work by an employer regardless of the number of persons the employer
employs, or the number of hours worked, and the term “employer” means any
person, firm or corporation employing one (1) or more employees, or seeking to
employ one (1) or more employees, if the employer has its principal place of
business within the Village of Oak Lawn, or does business within the Village of
Oak Lawn.
   For the purposes of this subsection, the term “employer” does not mean:
         a.   The government of the United States or a corporation wholly owned
by the government of the United States;
         b.   An Indian tribe or a corporation wholly owned by an Indian tribe;
         c.   The government of the State or any agency or department thereof;
or
         d.   The government of any municipality.
   Employers located within the Village of Oak Lawn shall comply with all
Federal and State employment laws and guidelines with regard to paid leave,
including paid sick leave, and the payment of minimum wage. Employee
eligibility for paid leave, including paid sick leave, and payment of minimum
wage shall be pursuant to all Federal and State employment laws and guidelines.
   No additional obligations with regard to paid leave, including paid sick
leave, or payment of minimum wage, including, without limitation, any
additional obligations adopted by the County of Cook Board of Commissioners,
shall apply to employers located within the Village of Oak Lawn, including
units of local government, generally, and public school districts and park
districts, specifically, and the Village opts out of any such regulations or
requirements, except those required by Federal and State law or the Village of
Oak Lawn.
   Nothing in this subsection shall be deemed to affect the validity or change
the terms of any collective bargaining agreement in effect on January 1, 2024.
   Nothing in this subsection shall be deemed to prohibit or discourage any
employer from providing leave or benefits greater or more generous than what is
required under this subsection or state or Federal law. Employers shall remain
ultimately responsible for determining whether or not to provide paid leave,
and how much paid leave to provide to their employees, as long as the minimum
amount of paid leave required by this subsection is provided. The Village shall
not be responsible for any claims based on or relating to alleged non-
compliance with the Paid Leave for All Workers Act, any Cook County Ordinance,
or any regulations thereunder. (Ords. 11-22-54, 17-10-21, 24-02-07, 24-18-67)
ARTICLE A. VILLAGE MANAGER
SECTION:
1-9A-1: Village Manager; Appointment
1-9A-2: Term Of Office; Removal
1-9A-3: Qualifications (Rep. by Ord. 05-22-84)
1-9A-4: Powers And Duties
1-9A-5: Salary
1-9A-6: Acting Manager
1-9A-7: Emergency Interim Village Manager
1-9A-1: VILLAGE MANAGER; APPOINTMENT:
There shall be and is hereby established the office of village manager who
shall be appointed by the village president and board of trustees. The village
manager shall be appointed without regard to his political beliefs and need not
be a resident of the village, but shall reside at a residence within a ten (10)
mile radius of the village, as measured from the village hall, within one year
of his/her appointment. (Ord. 94-5-21)
1-9A-2: TERM OF OFFICE; REMOVAL:
The village manager shall be appointed for an indefinite term and may be
removed at any time by a majority vote of the president and board of trustees.
(Ords. 48, 420)
1-9A-3: QUALIFICATIONS:
(Rep. by Ord. 05-22-84)
1-9A-4: POWERS AND DUTIES:
   A.   The village manager shall be the administrative head of the government
of the village, and shall be responsible for the efficient administration of
all departments of the village.
   B.   The powers and duties of the village manager shall be:
      1.   To enforce the laws and ordinances within the village.
      2.   To appoint the following officers:
         a.   The chief of the police department;
         b.   The chief of the fire department;
         c.   The village attorney;
      and to appoint and remove or suspend all department heads. The village
manager is authorized to appoint, suspend or discharge the chief of the police
department and the chief of the fire department without consent of the board of
trustees.
      3.   To exercise control of all departments and divisions created by 65
Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/5-3-5(3), by any ordinances of the village, and by
this article and amendments thereto.
      4.   To attend all meetings of the board of trustees with the right to
take part in the discussion but with no right to vote.
      5.   To recommend to the board of trustees for adoption, such measures as
he may deem necessary or expedient.
      6.   To see that all terms and conditions imposed in favor of the village
or its inhabitants in any contract or public utility franchise are faithfully
kept and performed.
      7.   To make, in the ordinary course of business, all purchases of
materials, supplies, and services necessary for the village as provided for in
the annual appropriation ordinance; provided, that on purchases of more than
twenty thousand dollars ($20,000.00), he shall first procure the approval of
the village board before making such purchases and in all cases he shall comply
with the statutory provisions as to solicitation of bids.
      8.   To have and exercise the power, authority and duties of any director
of a department or division thereof during the time when the office of any such
director shall be vacant or no person shall have been appointed thereto.
      9.   To act as agent for the local liquor control commissioner or the
Liquor and Tobacco Licensing and Review Board in all matters pertaining to the
administration of the licensing and control of the sale of alcoholic liquors
within the village, and the administration of the statutes of the state, and
the ordinances of the village pertaining thereto1.
      10.   To issue all licenses and permits not otherwise provided for by
this code or the statutes of the state.
      11.   To appoint, remove or suspend all electrical and plumbing
inspectors of the village.
(Ords. 14-34-75, 19-16-63, 25-12-38)
1-9A-5: SALARY:
The manager shall receive the salary prescribed by the president and board of
trustees, payable semimonthly. (Ord. 545)
1-9A-6: ACTING MANAGER:
In case of the absence or disability of the manager, the president and board of
trustees may designate a qualified administrative officer of the village to
perform the duties of the manager during such absence or disability. (Ords. 48,
420)
1-9A-7: EMERGENCY INTERIM VILLAGE MANAGER:
The village manager shall designate in writing and file with the village clerk
and village president at least three (3) and no more than seven (7) emergency
interim successor village managers pursuant to the emergency interim executive
succession act 1  to act in the event of the unavailability of the village
manager during an emergency. As provided by the act, the following terms shall
have the meanings hereinafter ascribed to them:
ATTACK: An attack or series of attacks by an enemy of the United States
causing, or which may cause, substantial damage or injury to civilian property
or persons in the United States in any manner by sabotage or by the use of
bombs, missiles, shellfire, or atomic, radiological, chemical, bacteriological
or biological means or other weapons or processes.
DISASTER: A situation determined by the governor to be an occurrence or threat
of catastrophic and widespread injury or loss of life or property resulting
from any natural or manmade cause, including, but not limited to, fire, flood,
earthquake, epidemic and explosion.
EMERGENCY: An attack or a disaster.
EMERGENCY INTERIM SUCCESSOR: A person designated pursuant to this article in
the event the village manager is unavailable to exercise the powers and
discharge the duties of village manager until a successor is appointed or
elected and qualified as may be provided by the constitution, statutes,
charters and ordinances or until the lawful incumbent is able to resume the
exercise of the powers and discharge the duties of the office.
UNAVAILABLE: Means either that a vacancy in the office of village manager
exists and there is no deputy authorized to exercise all of the powers and
discharge the duties of the office or that the lawful incumbent of the office
(including any deputy exercising the powers and discharging the duties of an
office because of a vacancy) and his duly authorized deputy are absent or
unable to exercise the powers and discharge the duties of the office. (Ord. 09-
13-28)
 
Notes
1 1. 5 ILCS 275/1 et seq.
ARTICLE B. VILLAGE ATTORNEY
SECTION:
1-9B-1: Office Established
1-9B-2: Bond (Rep. by Ord. 94-12-53)
1-9B-3: Powers and Duties
1-9B-4: Compensation
1-9B-1: OFFICE ESTABLISHED:
There shall be and is hereby established the office of Village Attorney. (Ord.
71-19-45)
1-9B-2: BOND:
(Rep. by Ord. 94-12-53)
1-9B-3: POWERS AND DUTIES:
   A.   General: The Village Attorney shall conduct all the law business of the
Village and draw such ordinances, deeds, leases and other papers as may be
required of him by the President and Board of Trustees, or any committee
thereof or the Village Manager.
   B.   Legal Opinions: The Village Attorney shall give his opinion, in
writing, to the Board of Trustees or any committee thereof or the President or
the Village Manager, when requested, on legal questions arising under or
concerning any ordinance of the Village, and on questions and subjects in which
the Village shall be legally interested.
   C.   Surrender Effects of Office: Upon the expiration of his term of office,
the Village Attorney shall forthwith turn over to his successor in office all
dockets, deeds, leases or other papers in his hands belonging to the Village or
delivered to him by the Village or any of its officers and all papers in
actions prosecuted or defended by him then pending and undetermined. (1951 Code
2-2-2, 2-2-3, 2-2-4)
1-9B-4: COMPENSATION:
The Village Attorney shall be paid a monthly retainer for his general counsel
and advice in such an amount as may be agreed upon or directed by the President
and Board of Trustees. For litigation and special assessment or special
taxation he shall charge such reasonable fee as shall be agreed upon by the
President and Board of Trustees. For other services not covered in this
Section, he shall charge on a quantum meruit basis, in the absence of any
specific agreement with the Village Board. (1951 Code 2-2-5)
ARTICLE C. VILLAGE TREASURER
SECTION:
1-9C-1: Appointment
1-9C-2: Bond and Salary
1-9C-3: Duties
1-9C-4: Deputy Treasurer
1-9C-1: APPOINTMENT:
The Village Treasurer shall be appointed by the President, with the advice and
consent of the Board of Trustees. (Ord. 93-10-34)
1-9C-2: BOND AND SALARY:
   A.   Bond: The Village Treasurer, before entering upon the duties of his
office, shall execute to the Village a bond conditioned for the faithful
discharge of his duties with sureties to be approved by the President and Board
of Trustees in a sum not less than three (3) times the latest Federal census
population or any subsequent census figures used for motor fuel tax purposes by
the Village.
   B.   Salary: The compensation of the Village Treasurer shall be $4,800
annually, payable through the village’s regular payroll in twenty-six (26)
equal installments. The Treasurer is not eligible to participate in the
Illinois Municipal Retirement Fund (IMRF) or in any village benefit plan. (1951
Code 2-3-1, 2-3-5; Ords. 72-1-1, 24-18-67)
1-9C-3: DUTIES:
   A.   General: The Treasurer, through the office of the Department of
Administrative Services, shall receive all monies belonging to the Village and
shall render at the end of each and every month, and oftener if required, a
statement under oath to the Board of Trustees, showing the state of Treasury at
the date of such account and the balance of the money in the Treasury. Said
statement shall set forth all the monies received by him, from whom, and on
what account they shall have been received; also all monies paid out by him,
and on what account they shall have been paid, and shall perform such other
duties as are set forth in 65 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/3.1-35-40 to 5/3.1-
35-85.
   B.   Books Required, Open for Inspection: The Treasurer shall cause to be
kept books of account, in such manner as to accurately show all monies received
by him, from whom, and on what account they shall have been paid. Said books
shall be kept in such manner as to be readily understood and investigated;
which books and all papers and files of said office shall at all times be open
to the examination of the Manager and the President and Board of Trustees or
any member thereof.
   C.   Accounting: The Village Treasurer shall keep by double entry, a full
detailed account of all Village funds, general tax receipts and receipts from
other sources, also a bond account, sinking fund account and forfeit account in
the Village ledger. He shall keep a "special fund" ledger in which all special
funds shall be kept separately by numbers and names of the special funds,
showing the total amount of each fund and installment of same to be collected
and each account to show the total collections and disbursements on account of
same. He shall also keep an auxiliary account with the Village Clerk-Collector
and the County Collector, showing all moneys received and disbursed by the
Village Treasurer.
   D.   Annual Account: The Village Treasurer is specifically charged with the
duty to prepare, publish and file the annual account, in accordance with the
provisions of sections 3-10-5.1 to 3-10-5.4 of chapter 24 of the Illinois
Revised Statutes and with the preparation and filing of the annual audit as
provided for in article 8, division 8 of said chapter 24. (1951 Code 2-3-2, 2-
3-3, 2-3-4, 2-3-5; 1985 Code)
1-9C-4: DEPUTY TREASURER:
The Treasurer is hereby authorized to designate the Director of Administrative
Services to serve as Deputy Treasurer. Said Deputy Treasurer shall have all the
powers and authority granted by State statute or this Village Code to the
Treasurer. Said designation shall be made by way of letter, addressed to the
President and Board of Trustees, with copies to the Village Manager and the
Director of Administrative Services. (Ord. 91-10-45)
ARTICLE D. BUDGET OFFICER
SECTION:
1-9D-1: Office Established, Appointment, Compensation
1-9D-2: Powers and Duties
1-9D-1: OFFICE ESTABLISHED, APPOINTMENT, COMPENSATION:
There is hereby established the office of Budget Officer in the Village who
shall be appointed by the Village Manager and shall serve for an indefinite
term. The Budget Officer shall be compensated for his services as shall be
established by ordinance of the Village. (Ord. 68-(8)-8-29)
1-9D-2: POWERS AND DUTIES:
The Budget Officer shall have the following powers and duties:
   A.   Permit and encourage the use of efficient planning, budgeting,
auditing, reporting, accounting and other fiscal management procedures.
   B.   Compile an annual budget in accordance with section 8-2-9.3 of chapter
24 of the Illinois Revised Statutes, and any amendments thereto 1 .
   C.   Examine all books and records of all Municipal departments, commissions
and boards which relate to monies received by the Municipality, Municipal
departments, commissions and boards, and paid out by the Municipality,
Municipal departments, commissions and boards, debts and accounts receivable
and amounts owed by or to the Municipality, Municipal departments, commissions
and boards.
   D.   Obtain such additional information from the Municipality, Municipal
departments, commissions and boards as may be useful to the Budget Officer for
purposes of compiling a Municipal budget, such information to be furnished by
the Municipality, Municipal departments, commissions and boards in the form
required by the Budget Officer. Any department, commission or board which
refuses to make such information as is requested of it available to the Budget
Officer shall not be permitted to make expenditures under any subsequent budget
for the Municipality until such Municipal department, commission or board shall
comply in full with the request of the Budget Officer.
   E.   Establish and maintain such procedures as shall insure that no
expenditures are made by the Municipality, Municipal departments, commissions
or boards except as authorized by the budget. (Ord. 68-(8)-8-29)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  1-11-1 of this Municipal Code.
ARTICLE E. VILLAGE COLLECTOR
SECTION:
1-9E-1: Creation of Office, Appointment
1-9E-2: Bond Requirements
1-9E-3: Powers and Duties
1-9E-4: Salary
1-9E-1: CREATION OF OFFICE, APPOINTMENT:
There is hereby created the office of Village Collector which shall be an
appointed office. Pursuant to 65 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/3.1-30-15, the
Village Clerk shall hold the office of the Village Collector. (1985 Code)
1-9E-2: BOND REQUIREMENTS:
Before entering upon the duties of the office of Village Collector, the
Collector shall execute a bond with security payable to the Village in the
penal sum of five thousand dollars ($5,000.00), conditioned upon the faithful
performance of the duties of the office according to law and the ordinances of
the Village. Notwithstanding the foregoing, where the Village Clerk serves as
Village Collector, and the Clerk has been bonded pursuant to Section
1-8-1 of this Code, no additional bond shall be required under this Section
provided the Clerk's bond also references the Clerk's duties as Collector.
(1985 Code; Ord. 94-12-53)
1-9E-3: POWERS AND DUTIES:
The Village Collector shall have those duties and powers as set forth in the
Illinois Municipal Code 1 . (1985 Code)
 
Notes
1 1. S.H.A. 65 ILCS 5/3.1-35-120 et seq.
1-9E-4: SALARY:
The Village Collector shall serve without salary. (1985 Code)
ARTICLE F. VILLAGE PROSECUTOR
SECTION:
1-9F-1: Office Established, Appointment
1-9F-2: Bond (Rep. by Ord. 94-12-53)
1-9F-3: Powers and Duties
1-9F-4: Compensation
1-9F-1: OFFICE ESTABLISHED, APPOINTMENT:
There is hereby established the office of Village Prosecutor, which officer
shall be appointed by the Village Manager pursuant to the Village Manager's
power to appoint the Village Attorney. (1985 Code)
1-9F-2: BOND:
(Rep. by Ord. 94-12-53)
1-9F-3: POWERS AND DUTIES:
The Village Prosecutor shall prosecute those traffic and ordinance violations
within the Village for which complaints have been written on tickets, unless,
by law, the State's Attorney is the only one authorized to prosecute a
particular violation. (1985 Code)
1-9F-4: COMPENSATION:
The Village Prosecutor shall be paid monthly for prosecution work. He shall
charge such reasonable fee as shall be agreed upon by the President and Board
of Trustees. (1985 Code)
ARTICLE G. OTHER ATTORNEYS
SECTION:
1-9G-1: Selection of Attorneys
1-9G-2: Annual Evaluation
1-9G-1: SELECTION OF ATTORNEYS:
All attorneys used by the Village, including all attorneys used by the various
boards and commissions of the Village, other than the Village Attorney and
Village Prosecutor, shall be appointed by the President and Board of Trustees
and shall serve until such time as they may resign or be removed or replaced by
the President and Board of Trustees. (Ord. 87-13-53)
1-9G-2: ANNUAL EVALUATION:
All attorneys subject to appointment pursuant to Section
1-9G-1 above shall be evaluated annually by the President and Board of Trustees
at the first Village Board meeting in July of each year. (Ord. 92-11-46)
ARTICLE H. ASSISTANT VILLAGE MANAGER
SECTION:
1-9H-1: Office Created; Appointment; Term
1-9H-2: Office; Devotion To Duty
1-9H-3: Duties And Powers
1-9H-1: OFFICE CREATED; APPOINTMENT; TERM:
There is hereby created the office of the Assistant Village Manager. The
Assistant Village Manager shall be appointed by the Village Manager for a term
to be specified in a contract. The Assistant Village Manager shall be appointed
without regard to any political beliefs. (Ord. 20-15-44)
1-9H-2: OFFICE; DEVOTION TO DUTY:
The Assistant Village Manager shall maintain an office in the village hall and
shall spend his full time in the performance of his duties as may be required
by the Village Manager and the Board of Trustee. (Ord. 20-15-44)
1-9H-3: DUTIES AND POWERS:
The Assistant Village Manager shall be the chief assistant administrative
officer of the village. The Assistant Village Administrator may head one or
more departments as directed by the Village Manager and shall be responsible to
the Village Manager and the Board of Trustees for the proper administration of
all affairs of the village assigned to be performed by the Assistant Village
Manager. (Ord. 20-15-44)
CHAPTER 10
DEPARTMENTAL REORGANIZATION
SECTION:
1-10-1: Departmental Organization
1-10-1: DEPARTMENTAL ORGANIZATION:
Notwithstanding anything to the contrary set forth in this code, there are
hereby created and established the following departments and departmental
divisions in the village:
   A.   Department of public works:
      Facilities maintenance division
      Fleet maintenance division
      Forestry division
      Grounds maintenance division
      Sewer division
      Special events division
      Street division
      Water division
   B.   Department of community development and growth management:
      Building, planning and zoning division
      Engineering and transportation division
      Property maintenance, health and environmental services division
   C.   Department of community services:
      Family services division
      Senior services division
(Ord. 13-14-50)
CHAPTER 11
MUNICIPAL FINANCES
SECTION:
1-11-1: Annual Budget
1-11-2: Village Depositories
1-11-3: Investments
