modified at the liquor commissioner's discretion.
 
   E.   Appeals: All appeals from orders of the local liquor control
commissioner shall be limited to a review of the official record of proceedings
of such local liquor control commissioner only as provided for in 235 Illinois
Compiled Statutes 5/7-9 (dramshops). Within five (5) days after the notice of
an appeal has been filed with the state liquor control commission, a certified
official record of the proceedings for the local liquor control commissioner
shall be filed with the state of Illinois liquor control commission; provided,
that the appellant licensee pays for the cost of the transcript as provided by
235 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/7-9 (dramshops). (Ords. 16-07-18, 19-16-63)
3-3-19: PROHIBITED ENTERTAINMENT:
It shall be unlawful to permit the following conduct on licensed premises:
   A.   Performance of acts, or simulated acts of sexual intercourse,
masturbation, sodomy, bestiality, oral copulation, flagellation or other sexual
acts.
   B.   The actual or simulated touching, caressing or fondling of the breasts,
buttocks, pubic hair, anus or genitals.
   C.   The actual or simulated display of the breasts, buttocks, pubic hair,
anus, vulva or genitals.
   D.   Permitting any person to remain upon licensed premises who exposes to
public view any portion of his or her breasts, buttocks, pubic hair, genitals,
vulva or anus. (Ord. 90-9-39)
3-3-20: LIVE ENTERTAINMENT ON LICENSED PREMISES:
   Live entertainment shall only be allowed on the licensed premises where
permitted or licensed pursuant to the provisions of the Village Code.
(Ords. 96-7-27, 25-12-38)
3-3-21: REPORTS IN RELATIONS TO, AND ANNUAL REVIEW OF, LIQUOR LICENSEES:
   A.   Quarterly Reports: The local liquor control commissioner, or their
designee, shall file a quarterly report with the board of trustees relative to
liquor licensees within the village. Said reports shall be filed with the board
of trustees in January, April, July and October of each year, on or before the
tenth day of each month. Said reports shall, at a minimum, provide the
following information relative to all liquor licensees for the previous three
(3) month period:
      1.   Any change in the ownership interest in any liquor licensee.
      2.   Any police reports filed relative to a liquor establishment, and the
general circumstances of the incident resulting in each report.
      3.   Any citizen complaints filed against any liquor establishment and
the general nature of each complaint.
      4.   Any disciplinary action taken against a liquor licensee and any
reasons therefor.
      5.   Any other information that the local liquor control commissioner
determines should be brought to the board of trustee’s attention relative to a
liquor licensee.
   B.   Annual Review: The legislative, license, and ethics committee shall
conduct an annual review of all liquor establishments and liquor licensees at
said committee’s January meeting each year. The local liquor control
commissioner shall be present at said meeting. At said meeting, the committee
shall review, with the local liquor control commissioner, the quarterly reports
submitted during the preceding year by the local liquor control commissioner.
In addition, the committee and the local liquor control commissioner shall
review the village’s ordinances relative to liquor control to determine if any
changes are necessary, as well as discuss any problems relative to any
particular liquor establishment(s) or licensee(s). If, as a result of said
annual review, the local liquor control commissioner is requested to report on
any particular matter, said report shall be filed by the local liquor control
commissioner with the committee, at or before said committee’s March meeting.
(Ords. 90-11-50, 13-09-29, 19-16-63, 25-12-38)
3-3-22: VIDEO GAMING CAFES:
   A.   Definition: For purposes of this section a "video gaming cafe" shall be
defined as an establishment whose primary or major focus is to operate video
gaming terminals as defined under the Illinois Video Gaming Act 1  and the
service of alcohol and food is incidental to the operation of video gaming. The
following factors may be considered by the Local Liquor Commissioner when
determining if the establishment is a video gaming cafe:
      1.   The layout and design of the establishment;
      2.   The preparation and variety of food and beverages offered;
      3.   The absence of a full service kitchen;
      4.   The number of video gaming machines relative to the customer seating
capacity of the establishment;
      5.   The square footage of space devoted to video gaming relative to the
amount of space devoted to other activities;
      6.   The source of proposed or actual revenue derived from the
establishment;
      7.   The number of employees at the establishment and their proposed
function;
      8.   Any other factors determined as relevant by the Local Liquor Control
Commissioner.
   B.   Video Gaming Cafes Prohibited: A video gaming cafe shall not be
qualified to obtain any existing classification of Village of Oak Lawn liquor
licenses. No such license allowing video gaming cafes shall be created,
maintained or authorized by this chapter. (Ords. 16-18-73, 19-16-63)
 
Notes
1 1. 230 ILCS 40/5.
3-3-23: DELIVERY SERVICE INSTRUCTIONS:
Alcoholic liquor delivered to any address located within the Village of Oak
Lawn which does not hold a valid liquor license is subject to the following
restrictions:
   A.   Alcoholic liquor must be delivered by an individual of at least twenty
one (21) years of age or older.
   B.   Deliveries must not be between the hours of eleven o'clock (11:00) P.M.
and nine o'clock (9:00) A.M. on weekdays and eleven o'clock (11:00) P.M. and
eleven o'clock (11:00) A.M. on Sundays.
   C.   Payments may not be accepted nor orders placed at the delivery
location.
   D.   The express company, common carrier or contract carrier or agent of a
liquor license holder that carries or transports alcoholic liquor into or
within the Village shall not deliver or leave such deliveries without requiring
a signature of an individual twenty one (21) years of age or older.
   E.   Adequate evidence for proof of age should be produced to the delivery
agent in all instances of delivery.
   F.   A record shall be kept by the express company, common carrier or
contract carrier or agent of a liquor license holder who delivers alcoholic
liquor into or within the Village indicating the purchaser's name, address,
driver's license/state identification number, time, date and place of delivery
and the individual deliverer's identity.
   G.   Any violation of this section shall result in a fine of not less than
one thousand dollars ($1,000.00) per occurrence.
(Ord. 17-14-51)
3-3-24: BEVERAGE ALCOHOLIC SELLERS AND SERVERS EDUCATION TRAINING (BASSET)
REQUIRED:
   A.   BASSET Requirements: Every owner, manager, assistant manager,
bartender, agent and employee who dispenses, sells, delivers or serves
alcoholic liquor, as well as any employee or agent of a liquor license
establishment that provides security services, including, but not limited to,
bouncers, door staff, or floor personnel, shall provide evidence of successful
completion of a state-certified Beverage Alcoholic Sellers and Servers
Education Training (BASSET) program. Persons holding a temporary permit are
exempt from this requirement.
   B.   All persons required to successfully complete the Beverage Alcoholic
Sellers and Servers Education Training program as provided for in this Chapter
shall be BASSET certified or enrolled in a BASSET program within thirty (30)
days of employment and shall become certified no later than sixty (60) days
subsequent to such employment.
   C.   Evidence of BASSET certification or enrollment shall be kept in each
licensed premises and shall be presented upon the request of any law
enforcement officer.
   D.   All BASSET training shall be in conformance with 235 ILCS 5/6-27 and 6-
27.1, as amended, and all regulations promulgated thereunder, including 77 Ill.
Admin. Code 3500.
(Ord. 25-12-38)
CHAPTER 4
AMUSEMENTS
SECTION:
3-4-1: Applicability Of Provisions
3-4-2: Definitions
3-4-3: Amusement Tax Imposed
3-4-3.1: Push Tax
3-4-3.2: Tax Additional
3-4-3.3: Registration
3-4-3.4: Collection, Payment, And Accounting
3-4-3.5: Rules And Regulations; Authorized
3-4-3.6: Application Of Village Code
3-4-3.7: Violations; Penalties
3-4-3.8: Seizure For Unlawful Use
3-4-4: Inspections
3-4-5: Street Shows
3-4-6: Premises Requirements, Exit Signs
3-4-7: Conditions Of License
3-4-1: APPLICABILITY OF PROVISIONS:
The provisions of this chapter, except as otherwise provided, shall apply to
all amusements as hereinafter defined, whether specifically licensed or
regulated under other provisions of this code or other ordinances, or not.
(1951 Code 9-1-1; Ords. 77-15-19, 19-25-87)
3-4-2: DEFINITIONS:
   A.   The word "amusement" means:
      1.   Any theatrical, dramatic, musical or spectacular performance, motion
picture show, flower, poultry or animal show, animal act, circus, rodeo,
athletic contest, sport, game or similar exhibition for public entertainment,
including, without being limited to, boxing, wrestling, skating, dancing,
swimming, racing, or riding on animals or vehicles, baseball, basketball,
softball, football, tennis, golf, hockey, track and field games, bowling,
billiard and pool games.
      2.   Any entertainment offered for public participation, including,
without being limited to, dancing, carnival, amusement park rides and games,
bowling, billiard and pool games, or video gaming terminal.
   B.   “Person”: Any natural individual that participates in an amusement,
including a firm, organization, society, foundation, institution, partnership,
association, joint stock company, joint venture, limited liability company,
public or private corporation, receiver, executor, trustee or other
representative appointed by order of any court, or any other entity recognized
by law.
   C.   “Play”: Each individual push of the video gaming terminal which
initiates the simulation provided by the video gaming terminal. Play shall not
include the push of individual wager amounts, selection of types of games on
the video gaming terminal or entry of any information or printing of winning
receipts.
   D.   “Terminal Operator”: Any individual, partnership, corporation, or
limited liability company that is licensed under the Video Gaming Act, 230 ILCS
40/1 et seq., and that owns, services, and maintains video gaming terminals for
placement in licensed establishments, licensed truck stop establishments,
licensed large truck stop establishments, licensed fraternal establishments, or
licensed veterans establishments.
   E.   “Video Gaming Terminal”: Any electronic video game machine that, upon
insertion of cash, electronic cards or vouchers, or any combination thereof, is
available to play or simulate the play of a video game, including but not
limited to video poker, line up, and blackjack, as authorized by the Illinois
Gaming Board utilizing a video display and microprocessors in which the player
may receive free games or credits that can be redeemed for cash. The term does
not include a machine that directly dispenses coins, cash, or tokens or is for
amusement purposes only. (1951 Code 9-1-1; Ords. 77-15-19, 19-25-87)
3-4-3: AMUSEMENT TAX IMPOSED:
3-4-3.1: PUSH TAX:
Except as otherwise provided by this Chapter, an amusement tax is imposed upon
any person who participates in the play of a video gaming terminal that takes
place within the jurisdictional boundaries of the Village of Oak Lawn. The rate
of the tax shall be equal to one cent ($0.01) per play on a video gaming
terminal.
The terminal operator of a video gaming terminal may separately itemize and
charge each person who plays a video gaming terminal. (Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-3.2: TAX ADDITIONAL:
The tax imposed in this Chapter is in addition to all other taxes imposed by
the State of Illinois or any municipal corporation or political subdivision
thereof. (Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-3.3 REGISTRATION:
Every terminal operator of a video gaming terminal(s) located in the Village of
Oak Lawn shall apply for registration as a tax collector with the Village no
later than thirty (30) days after commencing such business or thirty (30) days
after the effective date of this ordinance imposing the push tax, whichever
occurs later. The application shall be submitted to the Village on the forms
provided by the Village and contain such information as reasonably required by
the Village to impose, collect, and audit all amounts related to the push tax.
(Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-3.4: COLLECTION, PAYMENT, AND ACCOUNTING:
It shall be the joint and several duty of every terminal operator of a video
gaming terminal(s) to secure from each person participating in the play of a
video gaming terminal the push tax imposed by this Chapter. For purposes of
this Chapter, it shall be presumed that the amount of the push tax imposed on
each person, unless the taxpayer or tax collector provides otherwise with
books, records, or other documentary evidence, has been collected from the
person by the terminal operator. Push tax payments accompanied by tax returns
prescribed by the Village shall be remitted to the Village on or before the
20th day of the month following the month in which payment for the push tax is
made.
Every terminal operator of a video gaming terminal who is required to collect
the push tax by this Chapter shall be considered a tax collector for the
Village. All push tax amounts collected shall be held by the terminal operator
as trustee for and on behalf of the Village. The failure of the operator to
collect the tax shall not excuse or release the person from the obligation to
pay the tax. The ultimate incidence of the push tax shall remain on the person
and shall never be shifted to the terminal operator.
Notwithstanding any other provision of this Chapter, in order to permit sound
fiscal planning and budgeting by the Village, no person shall be entitled to a
refund of, or credit for, the push tax imposed by this Chapter unless the
person files a claim for a refund or credit within one (1) year after the date
on which the push tax was paid or remitted to the Village.
The terminal operator of any video gaming terminal(s) shall be subject to
audit, inspection, and record keeping provisions of this Code. It shall be
unlawful for any terminal operator and/or person to prevent, hinder, or
interfere with the Village's officials, employees, and/or agents designated to
discharge their respective duties in the performance and enforcement of the
provisions of this Chapter. It is the duty of every terminal operator of a
video gaming terminal(s) to keep accurate and complete books and records to
which the Village's officials, employees, and/or agents will at all times have
full access. (Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-3.5: RULES AND REGULATIONS; AUTHORIZED:
The Village is authorized to adopt, promulgate, and enforce any additional
rules and regulations pertaining to the interpretation, collection,
administration, and enforcement of this Chapter. (Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-3.6: APPLICATION OF VILLAGE CODE:
Any citation under this Chapter may be in addition to any other citations
issued by the Village under any and all applicable sections of the Village
Code. (Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-3.7: VIOLATIONS; PENALTIES:
It shall be a violation of this Chapter for a terminal operator to fail to file
a report within the time prescribed in this Chapter.
A terminal operator who falsely reports or fails to report the amount of push
tax due required by this Chapter shall be in violation of Chapter and is
subject to suspension and/or revocation of their terminal operator license. All
payments not remitted when due shall be paid together with a penalty assessment
on the unpaid balance at a rate of 1.5% per month.
Suspension Or Revocation Of License: The Village President/Local Liquor
Commissioner or his or her designee shall have the power to suspend for not
more than thirty (30) days or revoke any video gaming license issued under the
provisions of this chapter for cause, or if he/she determines that a terminal
operator shall have violated any of the provisions of this Chapter, any of the
statutes of the State or any other valid ordinance or resolution enacted by the
President and Board of Trustees of the Village. However, no such license shall
be revoked or suspended except after the holding of a public hearing by the
Village President/Local Liquor Commissioner or his or her designee. Ten (10)
days' notice of the hearing shall be given to the terminal operator.
Alternatively, terminal operator shall have the opportunity to engage in a
prehearing conference and agree to negotiated penalties rather than proceed to
a hearing.
Fine Imposed: In addition, any terminal operator violating the provisions of
this Chapter shall be subject to a fine of five hundred dollars ($500.00) for
the first offense, and seven hundred fifty dollars ($750.00) for the second
offense, and one thousand dollars ($1,000.00) for a third offense and subject a
revocation of any license to operate a video gaming terminal for the third
offense. It shall be deemed a violation of this Chapter for any owner to
knowingly furnish false or inaccurate information to the Village. Each day a
violation continues shall constitute a separate violation. It shall be deemed a
violation of this Chapter for any person to knowingly furnish false or
inaccurate information to the Village. (Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-3.8: SEIZURE FOR UNLAWFUL USE: 
If the Village determines that the Village shall have a reasonable basis for
believing any owner is using a video gaming terminal in an illegal manner
contrary to the provisions of this Chapter or any other federal, state, or
municipal law and/or regulation, said video gaming terminal may be seized by
the Village, followed by an administrative hearing with notice to the owner
within seven (7) days of seizure to determine the appropriateness of the
seizure, and held until such time as the owner of such video gaming terminal
pays any delinquent push tax amount, reimburses the Village for actual cartage
costs incurred in the seizure and pays the Village twenty dollars ($20.00) for
each day the video gaming terminal has been in storage. (Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-4: INSPECTIONS:
It shall be the duty of the chief of police and the fire chief to see that
every exhibition, amusement, theatrical or other public show is inspected by a
member of the police and fire departments, and to ensure conformity with the
provisions concerning such amusements. (1951 Code 9-1-7; Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-5: STREET SHOWS:
No license shall be granted for any carnival, circus, exhibition show or other
amusement to be given on any public street or sidewalk or in such a place that
the only main accommodation for the public or audience will be in a public
place, except on order of the village board. (1951 Code 9-1-3; Ord. 19-25-87)
3-4-6: PREMISES REQUIREMENTS, EXIT SIGNS:
It shall be the duty of the owner or occupant in charge of any building or hall
used as an assembly hall with accommodations for one hundred (100) persons or
more in which theatricals, shows, amusements, lectures and other entertainments
are offered, operated or presented to provide and place a sign, on which the
word "Exit" shall appear in letters of at least six inches (6") high, over
every door or other opening from such hall to every means of egress therefrom,
and a light shall be provided with a red globe and placed at or over such sign,
which light shall be kept burning during the entire period the hall is open to
the public and until the audience has left the hall. (1951 Code 9-1-13; Ord.
19-25-87)
3-4-7: CONDITIONS OF LICENSE:
   A.   Order, Crowding:
      1.   The audience of any amusement, show or theatrical must be orderly
and quiet at all times, and it shall be unlawful for any person attending such
amusement, show or theatrical to create a disturbance in the audience.
      2.   It shall be unlawful to permit or gather such a crowd to witness any
amusement or show as to create a dangerous condition because of fire or other
risks.
   B.   Indecent Shows: It shall be unlawful to present, exhibit, conduct or
take part in any indecent show, theatrical, play, motion picture exhibition or
other form of public amusement or show.
   C.   Exhibition Of Criminals: It shall be unlawful to exhibit any criminal
or the body of any criminal or any person who shall have become notorious
because of the commission of a crime, in any theatrical, exhibit, carnival or
other public place.
   D.   Riots: It shall be unlawful to present any public amusement or show of
any kind which tends to or is calculated to cause or promote any riot or
disturbance.
   E.   Smoking 1 : It shall be unlawful to smoke or carry a lighted cigar,
cigarette or pipe on or beneath the stage or in a dressing room of any building
used as an assembly hall with seating accommodations for more than one hundred
(100) persons or in which theatricals, shows, amusements, lectures or other
entertainments are offered, operated, presented or exhibited. (1951 Code 9-1-6,
9-1-8, 9-1-9, 9-1-10, 9-1-11, 9-1-12; Ord. 19-25-87)
 
Notes
1 1. See also Title 8, Chapter 12 of this code.
ARTICLE A. AMUSEMENT DEVICES
SECTION:
3-4A-1: Mechanical Amusement Devices
3-4A-1-1: Permit Required
3-4A-1-2: Application For Permit, Issuance
3-4A-1-3: Deposit Required For Temporary Permit
3-4A-1-4: Liability Insurance
3-4A-1-5: Term Of Permit
3-4A-1-6: Construction Requirements And Safety Equipment
3-4A-1-7: Inspections
3-4A-1-8: Compliance With Provisions
3-4A-1-9: Penalties
3-4A-2: Automatic Amusement Devices
3-4A-2-1: Definition
3-4A-2-2: Licenses Required
3-4A-2-3: Applications For Licenses
3-4A-2-4: Restrictions On Licenses
3-4A-2-5: License Fees
3-4A-2-6: Investigation Of Applicant, Fee
3-4A-2-7: Payment Of Fees, Emblem Required
3-4A-2-8: Class A And B Licenses
3-4A-2-9: Gambling Devices
3-4A-2-10: Revocation Of License
3-4A-2-11: Penalties
3-4A-1: MECHANICAL AMUSEMENT DEVICES:
3-4A-1-1: PERMIT REQUIRED:
It shall be unlawful for any person to construct, erect or install any
mechanical amusement device, roller coaster, scenic railway, water chute,
parachute, rolling device or any other mechanical riding, sailing, sliding or
swinging device in either an existing or a new amusement park, or places or
sites, where such devices are installed, erected or constructed under carnival,
fair or similar auspices, without first obtaining a permit therefor. (Ords. 71-
20-56, 74-15-30, 74-19-39)
3-4A-1-2: APPLICATION FOR PERMIT, ISSUANCE:
Application for a permit shall be submitted to the director of office of
quality control of the village at least thirty (30) days prior to such
construction, erection, or installation of any mechanical amusement device. The
applicant shall be required to have the Illinois department of labor, carnival
and amusement ride inspection division, inspect all mechanical amusement
devices to be used by the applicant, and shall submit proof, that the Illinois
department of labor, carnival and amusement ride inspection division, has been
requested to make said inspection, within ten (10) days after making
application with the department of quality control. Upon receiving an
application for a permit, the department of quality control shall inform the
applicant of the necessity of immediately contacting the Illinois department of
labor, carnival and amusement ride inspection division, in regard to obtaining
the required inspections of the mechanical amusement devices. In the event that
the applicant does not submit proof of notification of the Illinois department
of labor, carnival and amusement ride inspection division, of the need for an
inspection, within ten (10) days of making application with the department of
quality control, the department of quality control shall give the applicant a
second and final notice, in writing, of the need for an inspection, and of the
potential for requiring the closing down of the mechanical amusement devices in
the event the proper inspections are not obtained. All costs associated with
said inspection shall be the responsibility of the applicant. A copy of the
inspection report shall be filed with the department of quality control.
Permits issued by the department of quality control shall be valid only as to
those mechanical amusement devices which have been inspected by, and have
passed the inspection of, the Illinois department of labor, carnival and
amusement ride inspection division. (Ords. 87-14-55, 87-19-77)
3-4A-1-3: DEPOSIT REQUIRED FOR TEMPORARY PERMIT:
At the time of application for a temporary permit, the applicant shall pay to
the Village as a deposit, an amount equal to one hundred dollars ($100.00) for
each day the aforesaid devices shall be operated. The deposit required shall be
in addition to any fees required under Section
3-1-1-7 of this Title. The following expenses incurred by the Village, as
ultimately determined by the Director of Office of Quality Control, shall be
deducted from the deposit and the balance returned to the applicant within
three (3) days following the removal of the devices:
   A.   Inspection fees, original and daily, as determined by the Director of
Office of Quality Control.
   B.   Special police services, including calls for disturbances created by
patrons as determined by the Chief of Police.
   C.   Cleanup and restoration of premises, as determined by the Public Works
Director. (Ords. 71-20-46, 74-15-30, 74-19-39, 81-12-19)
3-4A-1-4: LIABILITY INSURANCE:
Every permit application for a mechanical amusement riding device shall be
accompanied by a certificate of liability insurance, which will name the
Village and its officers, agents and employees as additional insured, in an
amount not less than one million dollars ($1,000,000.00) for any one person and
two million dollars ($2,000,000.00) for any one accident. The insurance company
is to be approved by the Department of Administrative Services of the Village
and shall be with a company licensed to do business in the State. (Ord. 71-20-
46; 1985 Code)
3-4A-1-5: TERM OF PERMIT:
Every permit issued for a ride or mechanical riding device where same is for a
temporary nature, shall be for a period not to exceed fourteen (14) days.
Permits for permanent installations shall be for a term of one year. (Ord. 71-
20-46)
3-4A-1-6: CONSTRUCTION REQUIREMENTS AND SAFETY EQUIPMENT:
All mechanical amusement riding devices shall be constructed in the manner
hereinafter set forth, and shall be so constructed and designed to withstand
shocks and to afford adequate protection for persons riding thereon. Protective
devices as handrails, handles, safety grips or straps, of suitable design shall
be provided.
   A.   Merry-go-round horses shall be equipped with a bridle and stirrups and
a strap on the horse rod to snap or buckle under the arms of the rider.
   B.   Except for portable-type Ferris wheels, same shall be constructed with
steel frames and tripods supported upon and anchored on concrete piers.
Portable-type Ferris wheels shall be constructed of steel and set on suitable
bases under the towers and the side tripods.
   C.   On all other mechanical amusement riding devices, automatic handlebars
shall be installed where vertical thrust is encountered and catwalks shall be
provided for emergency and maintenance purposes.
   D.   All rides shall be guyed, suitable to withstand wind pressure and
unbalanced load. Footings, blockings and outriggers shall be secured so as to
be stable under all operating conditions.
   E.   No amusement riding device shall be overcrowded or loaded in excess of
its rated safe carrying capacity or safe operating speed.
   F.   All internal combustion engines used in driving riding devices shall be
equipped with an overspeed governor.
   G.   All riding devices shall be fenced, enclosed, barricaded or otherwise
guarded for public protection.
   H.   Every roller coaster-type mechanical riding device shall be equipped
with a terminal brake; there also shall be provided an emergency brake that
will immediately stop the device, and same shall be located for each access.
The emergency brake shall be under the control at all times of the attendant at
the loading platform. In addition thereto there shall be provided a safety
device for each roller coaster-type ride to catch or hold the ride should the
propelling chain break, or any other break or accident that might occur while
the ride is in motion. Anti- rollback devices shall be installed on all
inclined tracks, slides or guides.
   I.   No person shall knowingly use or permit to be used an amusement riding
device which is not properly assembled or which is defective or unsafe in any
of its parts, components, controls or safety equipment. In no case shall a
safety device installed on any amusement riding device be made inoperative.
(Ord. 71-20-46)
3-4A-1-7: INSPECTIONS:
After the erection of the rides and mechanical riding devices, a physical
inspection shall be made of same by the Illinois Department of Labor, Carnival
and Amusement Ride Inspection Division, to ensure conformity with the
provisions of this Section and all other ordinances and statutes relating
hereto. Said inspection shall be made prior to the opening of said rides and
mechanical riding devices to the public.
Where such rides and mechanical riding devices are of a permanent installation,
said inspection shall be made annually prior to the opening of such devices to
the public. (Ord. 87-14-55)
3-4A-1-8: COMPLIANCE WITH PROVISIONS:
No device shall be operated unless compliance is had with the provisions of
this Code and the rules and regulations set forth herein. (Ords. 74-20-46, 74-
15-30, 74-19-39)
3-4A-1-9: PENALTIES:
Any person violating, resisting or opposing the enforcement of this Section
shall be fined not less than one hundred dollars ($100.00) nor more than seven
hundred fifty dollars ($750.00) for each offense. Each day such violation shall
continue shall be and constitute a separate offense. (Ords. 71-20-46, 99-5-11)
3-4A-2: AUTOMATIC AMUSEMENT DEVICES:
3-4A-2-1: DEFINITION:
The term "automatic amusement device" shall mean any machine which upon the
insertion of a coin, slug, token, plate or disc, may be operated by the public
generally for use as a game, entertainment or amusement, whether or not
registering a score. It shall include, but not be limited to, such devices as
jukeboxes, marble machines, pinball machines, movie and video booths or stands,
games played with any number of balls, spheres or electrically operated devices
upon a table or board having holes, pockets, cups or electrically activated
devices that are actuated by said balls, or spheres or electrical contacts,
electrical impulse and/or cathode tube games, and all games, operations or
transactions similar thereto under whatever name by which they may be
indicated. Said term shall not include a pool or billiard table licensed
pursuant to article B of this chapter. (Ords. 66-24-72, 77-15-18)
3-4A-2-2: LICENSES REQUIRED:
   A.   Automatic Amusement Device: Any person displaying for public patronage
or keeping for operation, any "automatic amusement device" as herein defined
shall be required to obtain a license from the director of administrative
services upon payment of a license fee 1 .
   B.   Operator's Business: No one shall engage in the "automatic amusement
device operator's business" which is herein defined to mean the conduct or
transaction of the business of distributing, placing, leasing or selling
automatic amusements with an agreement to maintain, service or supply, without
first obtaining a license therefor. (Ord. 77-15-18; 1985 Code)
 
Notes
  1. See section
1 3-1-1-7 of this title and section
  3-4A-2-5 of this article.
3-4A-2-3: APPLICATIONS FOR LICENSES:
Applications for such licenses shall be made to the director of administrative
services upon a form to be supplied by the village for that purpose and shall
contain the following information:
   A.   Name and address of the applicant, age, date and place of birth;
   B.   Name of manufacturer of the device(s) to be licensed;
   C.   Prior convictions, if any, of applicant;
   D.   Place where machine or device is to be displayed or operated, and the
business conducted at that place;
   E.   Description of machine or device to be covered by the license,
mechanical features and serial number;
   F.   A statement under oath that:
      1.   The machine or device is not a gambling device;
      2.   No more than ten (10) free games can be obtained by any one player;
      3.   The machine or device is not equipped with a "knock off" switch or
other similar mechanism which would cancel free games.
All applications for automatic amusement device licenses must also contain the
applicable information provided for in section
3-3-4 of this title. (Ords. 77-15-18, 82-9-33; 1985 Code)
3-4A-2-4: RESTRICTIONS ON LICENSES:
   A.   All applicants for such licenses shall be ineligible for a license
hereunder for the same applicable reasons as those set forth in section
3-3-5 of this title.
   B.   No license shall be issued to any applicant unless he shall be over
twenty one (21) years of age and a citizen of the United States. (Ords. 77-15-
18, 82-9-33)
3-4A-2-5: LICENSE FEES:
Every applicant, before being granted a license, shall pay the annual license
fee set forth in Section
3-1-1-7 of this Code for each license year, which fee shall not be prorated.
Ords. 77-15-18, 81-36-79, 84-6-13, 1985 Code, Ord. 03-20-81, 23-12-42)
3-4A-2-6: INVESTIGATION OF APPLICANT, FEE:
   A.   Investigation Of Applicant: The original application for a license
shall be referred to the chief of police for investigation and verification of
the facts stated therein and he shall ascertain if the applicant is a person of
good moral character, and either approve or disapprove the application. No
license shall be issued to any applicant unless approved by the chief of
police.
   B.   Fee: Before any action is taken upon any application as provided in
section
3-4A-2-3 of this article, applicant shall pay to the village the sum of two
hundred dollars ($200.00), no part of which shall be refundable, to cover the
village's costs in reviewing and investigating such applicant. Provided,
however, that in the event the application is approved by the board of trustees
and the applicant applies for the license and pays the initial license fee
within the time provided, said two hundred dollars ($200.00) shall be credited
toward the initial license fee.
   C.   Examination Of Records: In conformity with this section
3-4A-2, the department of administrative services, with reasonable prior
written notice, shall have the authority to examine all books and records of
automatic amusement device operators to ensure compliance with this section.
(Ords. 77-15-18, 81-36-79; 1985 Code)
3-4A-2-7: PAYMENT OF FEES, EMBLEM REQUIRED:
All fees shall be paid to the director of administrative services who shall
issue, as evidence of the payment thereof as to each device a self-voiding
adhesive emblem to be placed on each device. Such emblem shall bear the words
"Village of Oak Lawn" and the date it shall become void. It shall be unlawful
for any person to mutilate said emblem during the year for which it is issued.
Each such emblem shall be issued only for a particular machine and may not be
transferred or used in any way whatsoever as the required emblem of any other
machine whether or not such other machine is a new or replacement machine for
the original machine. Each and every machine must have such an emblem issued
for its particular use after the payment of the required fee. If an automatic
amusement machine is replaced with a similar machine, a new emblem will be
issued upon notification in writing to the director of administrative services.
(Ords. 81-36-79, 84-6-13; 1985 Code)
3-4A-2-8: CLASS A AND B LICENSES:
   A.   Class A License: A public place of amusement arcade as defined herein.
      1.   Number Of Licenses: The number of class A amusement arcade licenses
authorized to be issued shall be as amended from time to time by ordinance
adopted by the president and board of trustees and on record in the office of
the village clerk; provided, however, that any license in said class which is
revoked or not issued shall automatically reduce the number of authorized
licenses of the class by the total number of revoked or nonissued licenses in
said class. In addition, the business regulation officer shall notify, in
writing, the village clerk and the board of trustees of any revocation or
nonissuance of any authorized licenses within ten (10) days of such occurrence.
      2.   License Required: It is unlawful for any person to install, use or
permit the installation or use of ten (10) or more automatic amusement device
machines at any place, unless the person in control of such place has the
premises licensed as a "public place of amusement arcade". The term "arcade"
means a place of amusement that includes ten (10) or more automatic amusement
devices.
      3.   Application For License: The applicant for a license under the
provisions of this section must apply for the appropriate license and pay the
initial license fee for such license within fourteen (14) days of the adoption
of the ordinance creating or board approval authorizing the appropriate license
and directing its issuance to the applicant. In the event any applicant fails
to apply for a license and pay the initial license fee within said fourteen
(14) day period, such license shall be revoked and the number of authorized
licenses of the class A involved is automatically reduced by one.
      4.   License Fee: Ten (10) or more automatic amusement devices may be
placed in a single location only if the location has been licensed as a public
place of amusement arcade and the appropriate fee, as set forth in section
3-1-1-7 of this title, has been paid. While coin operated kiddie rides are
assessed the amusement device fee, they are not counted in determining the need
for the arcade license. These fees shall not be prorated.
      5.   Location Of Premises:
         a.   No such amusement arcade licensed under this code shall be
situated or located within one-half (1/2) statute mile in any direction of any
public or private school.
         b.   No class A license shall be issued unless the premises to be
licensed is located in a C-3 regional shopping center zoning classification
under the village's zoning title 1  and not more than one class A license shall
be issued for each such regional shopping center. Provided, however, that where
a class A license has been issued for a premises not located in a C-3 regional
shopping center in accordance with this code prior to the effective date
hereof, such class A license shall continue to be valid and remain in full
force and effect. In the event any such present class A license for a premises
located outside of a C-3 regional shopping center is ever revoked, not
reissued, or the original licensee shall cease operating the business for any
reason whatsoever, the number of class A licenses shall automatically be
reduced accordingly and no new class A license may be issued for any such
location.
      6.   Closing Hours: It shall be unlawful to keep open for business or to
admit the public to any such amusement arcade between the hours of twelve
o'clock (12:00) midnight and nine o'clock (9:00) A.M.
      7.   Location Of Devices, Restrictions: All amusement devices in arcades
shall be kept in a separate area segregated, separate and apart from the area
where food and drink are served. No service or consumption of alcoholic
beverages shall be permitted within the segregated area where such devices are
located.
   B.   Class B License: Each business location or place with nine (9) or less
amusement device machines on the premises.
      1.   Number Of Licenses: There shall be no limit on the number of class B
licenses issued.
      2.   Application For License: Applications for class B licenses shall be
made to the business regulation officer on forms to be supplied by said
officer. The business regulation officer shall forward all such applications to
the police chief for investigation. The police chief, within seven (7) working
days after receipt of any such application, shall forward his written
recommendation, together with a copy of the application, to the board of
trustees who shall approve or reject applications for class B licenses at its
next regular board meeting occurring at least seven (7) days after receipt of
the police chief's written recommendation. No class B licenses shall be issued
by the business regulation officer without prior approval of the board of
trustees.
      3.   Number Of Devices: Any premises may have up to, and including, nine
(9) automatic amusement devices; provided, that the annual amusement license
fee, as set forth in section
3-1-1-7 of this title, has been paid for each device and an emblem evidencing
payment of the license fee is affixed to the automatic amusement device at a
conspicuous location. (Ords. 77-15-18, 79-23-38, 80-5-15, 81-7-12, 81-9-17, 81-
14-23, 81-15-25, 81-21-39, 81-28-57, 81-31-59, 81-33-64, 81-35-69, 81-36-79,
81-36-80, 82-9-33, 82-11-43, 82-12-48, 82-16-54, 82-16-55, 82-19-66, 82-22-77,
82-24-92, 83-8-10, 83-16-40; 1985 Code; Ords. 96-3-10, 97-11-53, 01-06-21, 03-
20-81)
 
Notes
1 1. See title 4 of this code.
3-4A-2-9: GAMBLING DEVICES:
Nothing in this section shall in any way be construed to authorize, license or
permit any gambling devices whatsoever, including, but not limited to,
mechanical grab devices and bingo devices, or any mechanism that has been
judicially determined to be a gambling device, or in any way contrary to law.
Provided, however, that bingo games licensed by the state of Illinois and in
compliance with this code shall not be considered gambling.
If the chief of police shall have reason to believe any automatic amusement
device is used as a gambling device, such machine may be seized by the police
and impounded until a hearing is held as herein provided. A finding of guilty
shall result in the destruction of such machines by the police department. A
finding of innocent shall result in the return of said machines to the
licensee. (Ord. 77-15-18)
3-4A-2-10: REVOCATION OF LICENSE:
Every license issued under this code is subject to the right, which is hereby
expressly reserved, to revocation of same should the licensee directly or
indirectly permit the operation of any automatic amusement device contrary to
the provisions of this section, the ordinances of the village or the laws of
the state of Illinois. Said license may be revoked by the village manager after
written notice to the licensee, which notice shall specify the ordinance or law
violations with which the licensee is charged, if after the hearing the
licensee is found to be guilty of such violations. Ten (10) days' notice of the
hearing shall be given to the licensee. At such hearing the licensee and his
attorney may present and submit evidence of witnesses in his defense. (Ord. 77-
15-18)
3-4A-2-11: PENALTIES:
Any person violating any of the provisions of this section shall be fined not
less than one hundred dollars ($100.00) nor more than seven hundred fifty
dollars ($750.00) for each offense; and each day such violation shall continue
shall be regarded as a separate offense. In addition, any violation hereof may
result in having the premises business regulation certificate subject to
revocation proceedings. (Ords. 77-15-18, 99-5-11)
ARTICLE B. BILLIARD/POOL HALLS
SECTION:
3-4B-1: Definition
3-4B-2: License Required
3-4B-3: License Fees
3-4B-4: Application For License
3-4B-5: Number Of Licenses
3-4B-6: Conditions Of License
3-4B-7: Posting Of License
3-4B-8: Penalty
3-4B-9: Suspension/Revocation Of License
3-4B-1: DEFINITION:
For purposes of this code, a "billiard/pool hall" shall be defined as any
location at which more than two (2) billiard and/or pool tables are operated or
kept for use by the public at one business location. (Ord. 93-22-93)
3-4B-2: LICENSE REQUIRED:
It shall be unlawful for any person to operate a billiard/pool hall without
first obtaining the appropriate license(s) under this article. (Ord. 93-22-93)
3-4B-3: LICENSE FEES:
License fees in regard to billiard/pool halls shall be as set forth in section
3-1-1-7 of this title. (Ord. 03-20-81)
3-4B-4: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
   A.   Application for a billiard/pool hall license shall be made to the
village in writing and must be renewed annually providing the following
information:
      1.   The name, address and telephone number of the applicant;
      2.   The location and telephone number of billiard/pool hall;
      3.   A telephone number and the name of a person who may be contacted on
a twenty four (24) hour basis in case of emergency;
      4.   A statement of whether the business will be conducted by a manager
and, if so, the name, address and telephone number of the manager;
      5.   The number of billiard/pool tables to be located on the licensed
premises;
      6.   An overall security plan addressing all facilities and customer/
patron safety, which shall be submitted to the chief of police for
informational purposes, as well as review and comment; and
      7.   An overall parking plan, addressing those locations at which it is
anticipated that patrons will be parking, which shall be submitted to the
director of quality control for informational purposes, as well as review and
comment.
   B.   The village shall review the information in the application and shall
determine whether the premises wherein the billiard/pool hall is located fully
complies with all of the village's regulations including, but not limited to,
life safety, fire prevention, property maintenance, building and zoning
ordinances. (Ord. 93-22-93)
3-4B-5: NUMBER OF LICENSES:
The number of billiard/pool hall licenses authorized to be issued shall be as
amended from time to time by ordinance adopted by the president and board of
trustees and on record in the office of the village clerk. At any time a
billiard/pool hall ceases to do business in the village, the number of licenses
shall be reduced accordingly by operation of law until such time as a formal
amendment to this code is adopted. (Ords. 93-22-93, 96-3-10)
3-4B-6: CONDITIONS OF LICENSE:
Billiard/pool hall licenses shall be subject to the following conditions:
   A.   No billiard or pool table shall be used on any day except between the
hours of ten o'clock (10:00) A.M. and one o'clock (1:00) A.M.
   B.   No person under the age of sixteen (16) shall be allowed in a billiard/
pool hall, unless accompanied by a parent or guardian.
   C.   No licensee shall permit any gambling or riotous, disorderly or immoral
conduct in or about the licensed premises. (Ord. 93-22-93)
3-4B-7: POSTING OF LICENSE:
Every person licensed pursuant to this article shall post said license in a
conspicuous place in his/her/their/its business. (Ord. 93-22-93)
3-4B-8: PENALTY:
It shall be unlawful for any person to operate a billiard/pool hall, other than
in full compliance with this article and/or all other applicable village codes
or ordinances. Any person convicted of a violation of this article shall be
fined not less than fifty dollars ($50.00) nor more than seven hundred fifty
dollars ($750.00). (Ords. 93-22-93, 99-5-11)
3-4B-9: SUSPENSION/REVOCATION OF LICENSE:
   A.   In addition to any fine that may be assessed pursuant to section
3-4B-8 of this article, any license issued pursuant to this article may be
suspended, for a period not to exceed thirty (30) days, or revoked, upon a
finding by the village manager, or his/her designee, after a hearing, for any
one or more of the following reasons:
      1.   A violation of any federal, state or local law applicable to the
business or licensed premises; or
      2.   The conviction of the licensee, or any officer or director thereof,
of a felony.
   B.   The procedure for a hearing on suspension/revocation of a license shall
be as follows:
      1.   Notice of said hearing, in writing, shall be given to the licensee
by the village manager, or his/her designee, at least ten (10) days prior to
the time set for the hearing;
      2.   The notice shall set forth the specific grounds of the complaint as
well as the time and place of the hearing;
      3.   The Village Manager, or his/her designee, shall preside at the
hearing and the licensee shall be permitted counsel and the right to submit
evidence and cross examine witnesses;
      4.   The Village Manager, or his/her designee, shall render a written
decision after all evidence has been presented;
      5.   Said written decision may be appealed by the licensee to the
President and Board of Trustees provided a notice of appeal is filed with the
Village Clerk within ten (10) days after the issuance of said written decision.
(Ord. 93-22-93)
ARTICLE C. ROLLER SKATING RINKS
SECTION:
3-4C-1: Application for License
3-4C-2: Restrictions on License
3-4C-3: Building and Premises Requirements
3-4C-4: Conditions of License
3-4C-5: Revocation of License
3-4C-1: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
Each application for a license to establish, operate or maintain a roller
skating rink shall state thereon the name and address of the applicant, name
and address of the owners and beneficial owners and manager thereof, location
of the rink and the maximum number of persons to be accommodated. The
application shall be accompanied by a complete set of plans and specifications
as provided by the Building Code. Said application shall be examined and
approved by the Village Clerk, Director of Office of Quality Control,
Electrical Inspector, Plumbing Inspector, Chief of Police and Board of Trustees
before a license is issued 1 . (1951 Code 9-6-2; Ords. 53, 81-12-19)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Code for license fee.
3-4C-2: RESTRICTIONS ON LICENSE:
No license required by this Article shall be issued to:
   A.   A person who is not of good character and reputation in the community
in which he resides;
   B.   A person who is not a citizen of the United States;
   C.   A person who has been convicted of a felony under any Federal or State
law, if the Commissioner determines after investigation that such person has
not been sufficiently rehabilitated to warrant the public trust;
   D.   A person who has been convicted of being the keeper or is keeping a
house of ill fame;
   E.   A person who has been convicted of pandering or other crime or
misdemeanor opposed to decency and morality;
   F.   A person whose license issued under this Article has been revoked for
cause;
   G.   A person who, at the time of application for renewal of any license
issued hereunder, would not be eligible for such license upon a first
application;
   H.   A copartnership, unless all of the members of such copartnership shall
be qualified to obtain a license;
   I.   A corporation, if any officer, manager or director thereof, or any
stockholder or stockholders owning in the aggregate more than five percent (5%)
of the stock of such corporation, would not be eligible to receive a license
hereunder for any reason other than citizenship and residence within the
political subdivision;
   J.   A corporation unless it is incorporated in Illinois, or unless it is a
foreign corporation which is qualified under the Illinois Business Corporation
Act 1  to transact business in Illinois;
   K.   A person whose place of business is conducted by a manager or agent,
unless the manager or agent possesses the same qualifications required by the
licensee;
   L.   A person who has been convicted of a violation of any Federal or State
law concerning the manufacture, possession or sale of alcoholic liquor,
subsequent to the passage of this Section, or has forfeited his bond to appear
in court to answer charges for any such violation;
   M.   A person who does not beneficially own the premises for which a license
is sought, or does not have a lease thereon for the full period for which the
license is to be issued;
   N.   A person who is not a beneficial owner of the business to be operated
by the licensee;
   O.   A person who has been convicted of a gambling or drug related offense;
   P.   A person to whom a Federal gaming device stamp or a Federal wagering
stamp has been issued by the Federal government for the current tax period;
   Q.   A copartnership to which a Federal gaming device stamp or a Federal
wagering stamp has been issued by the Federal government for the current tax
period, or if any of the partners have been issued a Federal gaming device
stamp or Federal wagering stamp by the Federal government for the current tax
period;
   R.   A corporation, if any officer, manager or director thereof, or any
stockholder owning in the aggregate more than twenty percent (20%) of the stock
of such corporation, has been issued a Federal gaming device stamp or a Federal
wagering stamp for the current tax period; and
   S.   Any premises for which a Federal gaming device stamp or a Federal
wagering stamp has been issued by the Federal government for the current tax
period. (1951 Code
10-4-4; Ord. 78-7-12; 1985 Code)
 
Notes
1 1. S.H.A. 805 ILCS 5/1.01.
3-4C-3: BUILDING AND PREMISES REQUIREMENTS:
   A.   Building Regulations 1 :
      1.   No building shall be constructed or remodeled for use as a roller
skating rink unless the same is of fireproof construction, conforms to the
Building Code and contains a minimum of fifteen thousand (15,000) square feet.
      2.   A sufficient supply of drinking water shall be furnished from
approved bubblers.
      3.   Minimum sanitation facilities shall be provided as follows: four
hundred (400) to six hundred (600) capacity, seven (7) toilets and one
additional toilet for each two hundred (200) or fraction thereof of additional
capacity.
      4.   The minimum ventilation shall consist of a mechanical exhaust system
with a capacity equal to three (3) cubic feet of air per minute for each square
foot of floor area and the ventilating openings shall not be less than four
percent (4%) of the floor area.
   B.   Off-Street Parking: Off-street parking shall be provided for roller
skating rinks as set forth in subsection 4-13-4E of this Code. (1951 Code 9-6-
3, 9-6-7; Ord. 53; 1985 Code)
 
Notes
1 1. See Title 6 of this Code for building regulations.
3-4C-4: CONDITIONS OF LICENSE:
   A.   Closing Hours: It shall be unlawful to operate a roller skating rink
after the hour of eleven o'clock (11:00) P.M., except that the closing time on
Friday, Saturday and any day preceding a legal holiday shall be not later than
twelve o'clock (12:00) midnight.
   B.   Alcoholic Beverages, Gambling Devices: It shall be unlawful to sell,
give, store or permit the use of alcoholic beverages on the premises of any
roller skating rink and to install or operate any gambling device.
   C.   Order, Safety: The owners and manager shall conduct such rink in an
orderly manner and make adequate provision for the safety and protection of
patrons, employees and spectators, including regulations of the number and
speed of skaters.
   D.   Nuisances: It shall be unlawful for the owners or managers to permit or
suffer to permit the commission of a nuisance or breach of morality or safety
or health as defined under the existing laws of the Village and the State of
Illinois.
   E.   Outdoor Skating: It shall be unlawful for the owner or manager of a
roller skating rink to permit any outdoor roller skating on the premises of the
rink. (1951 Code 9-6-5; Ord. 53)
3-4C-5: REVOCATION OF LICENSE 1 :
The Board of Trustees may revoke any roller skating rink license for default in
the payment or performance of any obligation to the Municipality under the
provisions of this Article. (1951 Code 9-6-10; Ord. 53)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section 3-1-6 of this Code for hearing procedure.
ARTICLE D. BOWLING ALLEYS
SECTION:
3-4D-1: Application For License
3-4D-2: Closing Hours
3-4D-1: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
Applications for bowling alley licenses shall state thereon the intended
location of the place of business and the number of alleys to be used 1 . (1951
Code
9-3-1)
 
Notes
1 1. See section
  3-1-1-7 of this title for license fee.
3-4D-2: CLOSING HOURS:
Effective May 1, 2007, all business premises on which a bowling alley is
situated or operated shall be closed between the hours of twelve o'clock (12:
00) midnight and five o'clock (5:00) A.M. (Ord. 06-22-133)
ARTICLE E. MOTION PICTURES AND THEATRICALS
SECTION:
3-4E-1: Application For License, Fee
3-4E-2: Building Requirements
3-4E-3: Prohibited Acts And Conditions
3-4E-1: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE, FEE:
Applications for motion picture and theatrical licenses shall state, in
addition to the other information required, the place of the intended
performance and the seating capacity thereof. For motion pictures which are to
be presented in premises which are not covered by an annual license, the fee to
be paid shall be five dollars ($5.00) per day 1 ; provided, that no such motion
pictures shall be presented in or on any premises or building which does not
fully comply with the requirements for the construction or maintenance of
buildings for this purpose. (1951 Code 9-4-2, 9-4-4)
 
Notes
1 1. See section
  3-1-1-7 of this title for license fee.
3-4E-2: BUILDING REQUIREMENTS:
   A.   General: It shall be unlawful to present any public motion picture in
any building or structure which does not contain the number of exits required
by this code or the ordinances of the village or by statute for buildings or
places intended for motion picture performances, or in premises in which the
electric wiring does not fully comply with the ordinances. All places used for
the exhibition of theatricals must be kept adequately ventilated during the
performance and for so long a time as the audience may remain therein.
   B.   Obstructing Aisles, Exits: It shall be unlawful to obstruct or permit
the obstruction of any aisles, corridors or exits leading from any room or
enclosures in which a motion picture performance is being given or in which an
audience for such a performance is gathered. (1951 Code 9-4-8, 9-4-9)
3-4E-3: PROHIBITED ACTS AND CONDITIONS:
   A.   Types Of Pictures: It shall be unlawful to offer or present any motion
picture which has a tendency to cause a riot or public disturbance of the
peace, or any immoral, indecent or blasphemous picture or performance 1 .
   B.   Crowding: It shall be unlawful to permit any person, excepting ushers
or other theater employees, to remain standing in a hall or room in which a
motion picture is presented during the time of such performance; and it shall
be unlawful to admit to any such hall more persons than can be accommodated by
the seating arrangements for the premises.
   C.   Scenery: It shall be unlawful to use any scenery in any theater other
than noninflammable scenery or such as shall have been rendered noninflammable
by application of fire prevention coatings.
   D.   Smoking 2 : It shall be unlawful for any person to smoke in a theater.
(1951 Code 9-4-5, 9-4-6, 9-4-7; 1969 Code 11-115)
 
Notes
1 1. See section
  10-5-6-3 of this code.
2 2. See also title 8, chapter 12 of this code.
ARTICLE F. SHOOTING GALLERIES 1
SECTION:
3-4F-1: Placement Of Targets
3-4F-2: Conditions Of License
 
Notes
1 1. See section
  3-1-1-7 of this title.
3-4F-1: PLACEMENT OF TARGETS:
Targets shall be placed before a backstop of steel, sufficiently thick to
prevent any bullet from piercing it, and so arranged that there will be no
danger from ricocheting bullets or deflected pieces of bullets. (1951 Code
9-5-4)
3-4F-2: CONDITIONS OF LICENSE:
   A.   Order: All persons present in any shooting gallery shall conduct
themselves in an orderly manner, and shall not make or cause any loud or
unnecessary noise.
   B.   Gambling: It shall be unlawful to gamble or bet in any such place.
   C.   Sanitation And Code Compliance: Premises used for shooting galleries
shall be kept in a clean and sanitary condition and in compliance with all
provisions of this code applicable thereto. (1951 Code
9-5-2,
9-5-3; 1985 Code)
ARTICLE G. TRAMPOLINE AMUSEMENT GROUNDS 1
SECTION:
3-4G-1: License Required
3-4G-2: Application For License, Fee
3-4G-3: Building And Premises Requirements
3-4G-4: Sound Amplifiers
 
Notes
1 1. See title 6, chapter 17 of this code.
3-4G-1: LICENSE REQUIRED:
It shall be unlawful to establish, conduct or operate any trampoline amusement
ground, trampoline equipment or devices, which is open to the public, and for
admittance to which a fee is charged, without having first secured a license
therefor. (1951 Code 9-7-1; Ord. 377)
3-4G-2: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE, FEE:
Applications for licenses required by this article shall be made to the clerk
of the village, and shall comply with all of the general provisions of this
code or the ordinances relating to such applications 1 . The annual license fee
shall be as set forth in section
3-1-1-7 of this title. (1951 Code 9-7-1; Ord. 377; 1985 Code; Ord. 03-20-81)
 
Notes
1 2. See chapter 1 of this title.
3-4G-3: BUILDING AND PREMISES REQUIREMENTS:
It shall be unlawful to erect, establish, conduct or operate any trampoline
amusement ground, trampoline equipment or devices, and no license shall be
issued therefor, unless the following provisions are complied with:
   A.   Access To State Highways, Permit: Whenever a trampoline amusement
ground has automobile entrance and exits onto a state highway, access permit
for such shall first be obtained from the state highway department, and copy of
same shall be presented to the village clerk at the time of application for a
license.
   B.   Building Regulations And Requirements: Application for a license shall
be accompanied by a complete set of plans and specifications as provided by the
building code of the village. Said plans shall further provide outdoor
accessible drinking fountain, a minimum of two (2) separate sanitary toilet
facilities for male and female patrons, enclosure of trampoline area with
minimum height fencing of five feet (5'), and adequate lighting with proper
shading so as to not cast excessive illumination on adjacent premises. (1951
Code 9-7-3; Ord. 377)
3-4G-4: SOUND AMPLIFIERS:
In connection with the operation of a trampoline amusement ground, it shall be
unlawful to maintain or operate any loudspeaker or amplifier connected with any
radio, phonograph, microphone, or other device by which sounds are magnified
and made over the public street or public place so as to be disturbing to the
public and adjacent premises. (1951 Code 9-7-4; Ord. 377)
ARTICLE H. CARNIVALS
SECTION:
3-4H-1: License Required
3-4H-2: Application For License
3-4H-3: Scheduling, Community Events Calendar; Fees
3-4H-4: Penalty
3-4H-1: LICENSE REQUIRED:
It shall be unlawful to operate a carnival or amusement rides 1  in the village
without a license. (Ord. 70-9-13)
 
Notes
1 1. See also section
  3-4A-1 of this chapter.
3-4H-2: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
Application for license shall be made to the village forty five (45) days prior
to the proposed use of same. The applicant shall furnish the following
information for the issuance of said license:
   A.   Name and address of operator. If a corporation, state of incorporation
and names and addresses of all officers of same.
   B.   Certificates of insurance providing for liability insurance, with the
village as an additional party insured.
   C.   Site location in the village and duration of same.
   D.   Name and address of sponsoring organization, if any, and of officers of
same where applicable.
   E.   An overall parking plan, addressing those locations at which it is
anticipated that patrons will be parking, which shall be submitted to the
director of quality control and police chief for informational purposes, as
well as review and comment. In the event that said parking plan indicates the
use of private parking lots other than parking lots owned by the applicant,
said parking plan shall be accompanied by letters from the owner(s) of said
private parking lot(s) indicating the consent of said owner(s) relative to the
use of said private parking lot(s).
   F.   A site plan for the carnival which indicates the proposed locations of
all rides, game booths, refreshment booths, trucks and trailers used for
transporting the aforementioned rides and booths and mobile living quarters for
employees of the company used to provide said rides and booths. (Ords. 70-9-13,
93-24-112, 01-17-64)
3-4H-3: SCHEDULING, COMMUNITY EVENTS CALENDAR 1 ; FEES:
   A.   The fees required to be paid to the village by a carnival operator
shall be waived by the village if the sponsor is a resident nonprofit
organization. However, the deposit required under section
3-4A-1-3 of this chapter shall be paid. No more than two (2) carnivals may be
sponsored by one nonprofit organization in any calendar year.
   B.   No carnival shall be scheduled on the community events calendar, as
established in section
3-1-7-4 of this title, to commence less than ten (10) days after the scheduled
completion of the most recent one; provided, however, that the village board
may allow a carnival to commence less than ten (10) days after the scheduled
completion of, or at the same time as, another carnival; provided that a
finding is made as to each of the following conditions:
      1.   That the location of the second carnival will not be so close to the
first carnival that traffic congestion will result; and
      2.   That the scheduling of the second carnival, less than ten (10) days
after the scheduled completion of, or at the same time as, the first carnival
will not unduly burden the village's police department in carrying out its
duties throughout the village and, specifically, in maintaining order at both
carnivals; and
      3.   That strict adherence to the ten (10) day requirement would cause
particular hardship to the second carnival license applicant.
   C.   When a carnival is sponsored by a nonprofit organization, the operator
shall guarantee the sponsoring organization a minimum payment of one thousand
five hundred dollars ($1,500.00). (Ords. 76-10-21, 87-21-89)
 
Notes
1 1. See section 3-1-7-4 of this title.
3-4H-4: PENALTY:
Any person violating any provision of this article shall be fined not less than
one hundred dollars ($100.00) nor more than seven hundred fifty dollars
($750.00) for each offense, and each day during or on which a violation occurs
or continues shall constitute a separate offense hereunder. (Ord. 70-9-13; 1985
Code; Ord. 99-5-11)
ARTICLE I. TEEN NIGHTS
(Rep. by Ord. 23-12-42)
ARTICLE J. RAFFLES
SECTION:
3-4J-1: Authority
3-4J-2: Definitions
3-4J-3: Conditions of License
3-4J-4: License Fees, Issuance
3-4J-5: License Issue Restrictions
3-4J-6: Conduct of Raffles; Premises
3-4J-7: Raffles Manager, Bond
3-4J-8: Records
3-4J-9: Additional Restrictions
3-4J-10: Reciprocity
3-4J-11: Queen Of Hearts Raffles
3-4J-1: AUTHORITY:
Notwithstanding the provisions of Section
10-5-5  of this Code relating to gambling, raffles may be conducted in the
Village upon complying with the following regulations. (Ord. 87-11-44)
3-4J-2: DEFINITIONS:
For purposes of this Article, the following definitions apply:
BUSINESS: A voluntary organization composed of individuals and businesses who
have joined together to advance the commercial, financial, industrial and civic
interests of the community.
CHARITABLE: An organization or institution organized and operated to benefit an
indefinite number of the public. The service rendered to those eligible for
benefits must also confer some benefit on the public.
EDUCATIONAL: An organization or institution organized and operated to provide
systematic instruction in useful branches of learning by methods common to
schools and institutions of learning which compare favorably in their scope and
intensity with the course of study presented in tax- supported schools.
FRATERNAL: An organization of persons having a common interest, the primary
interest of which is to promote the welfare of its members and to provide
assistance to the general public in such a way as to lessen the burdens of
government by caring for those that otherwise would be cared for by the
government.
LABOR: An organization composed of workers organized with the objective of
betterment of the conditions of those engaged in such pursuit and the
development of a higher degree of efficiency in their respective occupations.
NET PROCEEDS: The gross receipts from the conduct of raffles, less reasonable
sums expended for prizes, local license fees and other reasonable operating
expenses incurred as a result of operating a raffle.
NONPROFIT: An organization or institution organized and conducted on a not-for-
profit basis with no personal profit incurred to anyone as a result of the
operation.
QUEEN OF HEARTS RAFFLE: A progressive raffle utilizing a standard playing card
deck of fifty-four (54) cards that continues until the “Queen of Hearts,” is
chosen. If the Queen of Hearts is not chosen, the net proceeds of the grand
prize pool carry to the next round, and new tickets are sold, until the winning
ticket reveals the Queen of Hearts.
RAFFLE: A form of lottery, as defined in Section
10-5-5-1  of this Code, conducted by an organization licensed under this
Article, in which:
   A.   The player pays or agrees to pay something of value for a chance,
represented and differentiated by a number or by a combination of numbers or
some other medium, one or more of which chances is to be designated the winning
chance, and
   B.   The winning chance is to be determined through a drawing or by some
other method based on an element of chance by an act or set of acts on the part
of persons conducting or connected with the raffle, except that the winning
chance shall not be determined by the outcome of a publicly exhibited sporting
contest.
   “Raffle” does not include any game designed to simulate: (1) gambling games
as defined in the Illinois Gambling Act, (2) any casino game approved for play
by the Illinois Gaming Board, (3) any games provided by a video gaming
terminal, as defined in the Video Gaming Act, or (4) a savings promotion raffle
authorized under Section 5g of the Illinois Banking Act, Section 7008 of the
Savings Bank Act, Section 42.7 of the Illinois Credit Union Act, Section 5136B
of the National Bank Act, or Section 4 of the Home Owners’ Loan Act.
   Bingo, as regulated by the State of Illinois pursuant to the Bingo License
and Tax Act (230 ILCS 25/1, et seq.), and the State Lottery, as regulated by
the State of Illinois pursuant to the Illinois Lottery Law (20 ILCS 1605/1, et
seq.), shall not be considered raffles subject to the provisions of this
Article.
RELIGIOUS: Any church, congregation, society or organization founded for the
purpose of religious workshop.
VETERANS: An organization or association comprised of members of which
substantially all are individuals who are veterans or spouses, widows or
widowers of veterans, the primary purpose of which is to promote the welfare of
its members and to provide assistance to the general public in such a way as to
confer a public benefit. (Ords. 87-11-44, 24-09-31)
3-4J-3: CONDITIONS OF LICENSE:
Raffles shall be conducted only by organizations licensed pursuant to this
Section, and such licenses are subject to the following restrictions:
   A.   The application for license must specify the area or areas within the
Village in which raffle chances will be sold or issued, the time period during
which raffle chances will be sold or issued, the time of determination of
winning chances, and the location or locations at which winning chances will be
determined.
   B.   The license application must contain a sworn statement attesting to the
not-for-profit character of the prospective license organization, signed by the
presiding officer and the secretary of their organization.
   C.   Each such license shall be valid only for the number of raffles
specified in the license; said raffles to be conducted during the specific
period of time specified in the license, not to exceed one year.
   D.   Licenses shall be issued to bona fide business, religious, charitable,
labor, fraternal, educational or veterans’ organizations that operate without
profit to their members and which have been in existence continuously for a
period of five (5) years immediately before making application for a license
and which have had during that entire five (5) year period a bona fide
membership engaged in carrying out their objects.
   E.   Licenses shall be issued to any nonprofit fund raising organization
that the Village determines is organized for the sole purpose of providing
financial assistance to an identified individual or group of individuals
suffering extreme financial hardship as the result of illness, disability,
accident or disaster. (Ords. 87-11-44, 87-24-101)
3-4J-4: LICENSE FEES, ISSUANCE:
   A.   There shall be no fee for the license issued under this Article.
   B.   When all the requirements of this Article have been met, the license
shall be issued by the Village Clerk. (Ord. 87-11-44)
3-4J-5: LICENSE ISSUE RESTRICTIONS:
No such license shall be issued to:
   A.   Any person who has been convicted of a felony.
   B.   Any person who is or has been a professional gambler or gambling
promoter.
   C.   Any person who is not of good moral character.
   D.   Any firm or corporation in which a person defined in subsections A, B
or C above has a proprietary, equitable or credit interest, or in which such a
person is active or employed.
   E.   Any organization in which a person in subsections A, B or C of this
Section is an officer, director of employee, whether compensated or not.
   F.   Any organization in which a person defined in subsections A, B or C
above is to participate in the management or operation of a raffle as defined
in this Article. (Ord. 87-11-44)
3-4J-6: CONDUCT OF RAFFLES; PREMISES:
   A.   The conducting of raffles is subject to the following restrictions:
      1.   The entire net proceeds of any raffle must be exclusively devoted to
the lawful purposes of the organization permitted to conduct that game.
      2.   No person except a bona fide member of the sponsoring organization
may participate in the management or operation of the raffle.
      3.   No person may receive any remuneration or profit for participating
in the management or operation of the raffle.
      4.   A licensee may rent a premises on which to determine the winning
chance or chances in a raffle only from an organization which is also licensed
under this Article.
      5.   Raffle chances may be sold or issued only within the area specified
on the license and winning chances may be determined only at those locations
specified on the licenses.
      6.   No person under the age of eighteen (18) years may participate in
the conducting of raffles of chances. A person under the age of eighteen (18)
years may be within the area where winning chances are being determined only
when accompanied by his parent or guardian.
   B.   If a lessor rents premises where a winning chance or chances on a
raffle are determined, the lessor shall not be criminally liable if the person
who uses the premises for the determining of winning chances does not hold a
license. (Ord. 87-11-44)
3-4J-7: RAFFLES MANAGER, BOND:
All operation of and the conduct of raffles shall be under the supervision of a
single raffles manager designated by the organization. The manager shall give a
fidelity bond in the sum of an amount determined by the Village in favor of the
organization conditioned upon his honesty in the performance of his duties.
Terms of the bond shall provide that notice shall be given in writing to the
licensing authority not less than thirty (30) days prior to its cancellation.
The Village may waive this bond requirement by including a waiver provision in
the license issued to an organization under this Article provided that a
license containing such waiver provision shall be granted only where a
unanimous vote of the members of the licensed organization has approved the
waiver of the raffles manager’s bond. (Ord. 87-11-44)
3-4J-8: RECORDS:
   A.   Each organization licensed to conduct raffles and chances shall keep
the records of its gross receipts, expenses and net proceeds for each single
gathering or occasion at which winning chances are determined. All deductions
from gross receipts for each single gathering or occasion shall be documented
with receipts or other records indicating the amount, a description of the
purchased item or service or other reason for the deductions, and the
recipient. The distribution of net proceeds shall be itemized as to payee,
purpose, amount and date of payment.
   B.   Gross receipts from the operation of raffles programs shall be
segregated from other revenues of the organization, including bingo gross
receipts, if bingo games are also conducted by the same nonprofit organization
pursuant to license therefor issued by the Department of Revenue of the State
of Illinois, and placed in a separate account. Each organization shall have
separate records of its raffles. The person who accounts for gross receipts,
expenses and net proceeds from the operation of raffles shall not be the same
person who accounts for other revenues of the organization.
   C.   Each organization licensed to conduct raffles shall report monthly to
its membership, and to the Village, its gross receipts, expenses and net
proceeds from raffles, and the distribution of net proceeds itemized as
required in this Section.
   D.   Records required by this Section shall be preserved for three (3) years
and organizations shall make available their records relating to operation of
raffles for public inspection at reasonable times and places. (Ord. 87-11-44)
3-4J-9: ADDITIONAL RESTRICTIONS:
   A.   The aggregate retail value of all prizes and merchandise awarded by a
licensee in a single raffle should not exceed two hundred thousand dollars
($200,000.00).
   B.   The maximum retail value of each prize awarded in a single raffle
should not exceed two hundred thousand dollars ($200,000.00).
   C.   The maximum price which may be charged for each raffle chance issued or
sold shall not exceed two hundred dollars ($200.00).
   D.   The maximum number of days during which chances may be issued or sold
shall not exceed one hundred eighty (180) days.
   E.   The above restrictions shall not apply to the Queen of Hearts Raffles.
(Ords. 87-11-44, 24-09-31)
3-4J-10: RECIPROCITY:
The Village Clerk may issue a raffle license to an organization upon payment of
the required license fee, provided that:
   A.   The organization has been issued a raffle license by a municipality
which is adjacent to the Village; and
   B.   Said adjacent municipality’s raffle ordinance is substantially similar
to the provisions of this Article and complies with the requirements of the
Illinois Raffles and Poker Runs Act, 230 ILCS 15/0.01, et seq.; and
   C.   The Village has a written mutual licensing agreement in regard to
raffles with said adjacent municipality.
If the foregoing conditions have been met, the organization need not make
application and need not comply with the provisions of Sections
3-4J-7  and subsection 3-4J-8C. (Ord. 87-11-44)
3-4J-11: QUEEN OF HEARTS RAFFLES:
To the extent there any conflicts with other provisions of this Article J, the
following requirements shall apply to and control Queen of Hearts Raffles:
   A.   In addition to the requirements of Section
3-4J-3 , an application for a Queen of Hearts Raffle license must also include:
      1.   A complete copy of the rules governing the Queen of Hearts Raffle,
which must indicate the percentage of net proceeds of the grand prize pool that
will be paid to the grand prize winner and the percentage of the sponsoring
organization’s share of receipts.
      2.   A plan to maintain the integrity and security of all funds received
for the Queen of Hearts Raffle, including a plan for security for the location
where the raffle will operate.
      3.   Manner in which payout(s) will be made.
      4.   Such proof and documents that the Village may reasonably request to
determine the organization’s ability to comply with this section and the
Illinois Raffles and Poker Runs Act, 230 ILCS 15/0.01, et. seq.
   B.   A Queen of Hearts Raffle shall not be subject to Section
3-4J-9  but shall be limited in the following manner:
      1.   A Queen of Hearts Raffle shall have a limit of two million dollars
($2,000,000.00) paid out to the grand prize winner.
      2.   Any secondary prize(s), if applicable based on the licensee’s rules,
should not exceed two hundred thousand dollars ($200,000.00).
      3.   The maximum price which may be charged for each Queen of Hearts
Raffle chance issued or sold shall not exceed two hundred dollars ($200.00).
      4.   No more tickets may be sold after such a time as sufficient money is
available to pay the $2,000,000.00 grand prize winner. In that event, the
drawing shall continue from that week’s Queen of Hearts Raffle until there is a
grand prize winner.
      5.   Purchasers of Queen of Hearts Raffle tickets must register their
names and addresses with the sponsoring organization.
      6.   The Village may, in its discretion, restrict purchase and
registration of tickets based on safety and health concerns.
   C.   A Queen of Hearts Raffle license shall be valid for a specified period,
not to exceed one (1) year. The Queen of Hearts Raffle must take place at the
location on the application, on the dates and times indicated on the
application. If a Queen of Hearts Raffle cannot be concluded within the
licensed period, the license holder must renew the license. If the license is
not renewed, the Queen of Hearts Raffle must conclude within the license
period. (Ord. 24-09-31)
ARTICLE K. TEENAGE CABARETS/JUICE BARS
SECTION:
3-4K-1: Definition
3-4K-2: License Required
3-4K-3: Application For License
3-4K-4: License Fee
3-4K-5: General Conditions Of License
3-4K-6: Special Conditions Of License
3-4K-7: Location Restriction
3-4K-8: Penalty; Revocation
3-4K-1: DEFINITION:
For purposes of this article, a "teenage cabaret/juice bar" is defined as a
private commercial, recreational and entertainment facility, designed or
intended to be used primarily by, but not limited to, patrons under the age of
twenty one (21) years old, where patrons enjoy dancing, live and recorded music
and entertainment or where videotape presentations are shown. (Ord. 89-21-67)
3-4K-2: LICENSE REQUIRED:
It shall be unlawful to operate a teenage cabaret/juice bar within the village
without first having obtained a license therefor as herein provided. (Ord. 89-
21-67)
3-4K-3: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
Application for such license shall be made to the village clerk and shall
include the following information:
   A.   Name and address of the owner/operator. If a corporation, the state of
incorporation, as well as the names and addresses of all officers of the
corporation;
   B.   The names and addresses and positions of all employees and nonemployee
crowd control and supervision personnel. Said information shall be updated as
new employees and nonemployee crowd control and supervision personnel are
hired;
   C.   The intended hours of operation; and
   D.   The maximum occupancy load of the building or premises to be used, as
determined by the Oak Lawn fire department in accordance with the provisions of
the latest edition of the BOCA basic national building code adopted by the
village.
   E.   Such other information as may be required by the village from time to
time. (Ord. 89-21-67)
3-4K-4: LICENSE FEE:
The fee for a teenage cabaret/juice bar license shall be as set forth in
section
3-1-1-7 of this title. (Ord. 03-20-81)
3-4K-5: GENERAL CONDITIONS OF LICENSE:
All teenage cabaret/juice bar licenses shall be subject to the following
conditions:
   A.   The building and premises used must be in full compliance with all
applicable provisions of this code.
   B.   The licensee shall be required to supply at least one qualified crowd
control and supervision person for every one hundred (100) persons, or portion
thereof, permitted to be within the building or premises licensed. Persons
qualified for crowd control shall include, but not be limited to, off duty
police officers and firefighters. Said qualified crowd control and supervision
personnel shall be responsible for crowd control both within the building used
by the licensee and the premises upon which said building is located. In the
event that the licensee uses village off duty police officers for crowd control
and supervision, the licensee shall include the police officers in the workers'
compensation insurance policy for their employees only while employed privately
in connection with the teenage cabaret/juice bar and the village shall be named
as an additional insured in the general liability insurance policy covering the
operation of said teenage cabaret/juice bar in an amount of not less than one
million dollars ($1,000,000.00) aggregate coverage.
   C.   All patrons shall enter at only one entrance and be properly identified
and a count of those entering be kept; that all persons and clothing be checked
to make sure that no dangerous implements are brought into said licensed
premises; that once a person has been admitted such person shall not leave and
reenter without again paying the admission charge and being identified and
checked.
   D.   No alcoholic beverages will be served or permitted on the licensed
premises.
   E.   The manager of said teenage cabaret/juice bar shall be an adult,
preferably with experience in the administration of such facilities; that all
employees and nonemployee crowd control and supervision personnel of said
teenage cabaret/juice bar shall be checked by the police department for
previous criminal records. Within three (3) days of the hiring of a new
employee or nonemployee crowd control and supervision person, the owner/
operator shall notify the village clerk in writing of the name of the new
employee or nonemployee crowd control and supervision person so that the police
department can perform the required criminal record check.
   F.   No license required by this article shall be issued to an establishment
which has an employee, or has a nonemployee crowd control and supervision
person, who is a convicted felon, or has been placed on supervision for, or
been convicted of, a violation of 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/11-6, 5/11-
9, 5/11-11, 5/11-14, 5/11-15, 5/11-15.1, 5/11-16, 5/11-17, 5/11-19, 5/11-19.1,
5/11-19.2, 5/11-20, 5/11-20.1, 5/11-21, 5/12-15 or 5/12-16.1.
   G.   The coat check area will be supervised and patrolled.
   H.   Building and fire code regulations shall at all times be enforced.
(Ord. 89-21-67)
3-4K-6: SPECIAL CONDITIONS OF LICENSE:
All teenage cabaret/juice bar licenses shall be subject to the following
special conditions during any period of time when individuals under the age of
eighteen (18) years old are permitted on the licensed premises.
   A.   The hours of operation shall be limited to:
      1.   Fridays: Eleven o'clock (11:00) A.M. to twelve o'clock (12:00)
midnight.
      2.   Saturdays: Eleven o'clock (11:00) A.M. to twelve o'clock (12:00)
midnight.
      3.   Evening preceding holidays: Eleven o'clock (11:00) A.M. to twelve
o'clock (12:00) midnight.
      4.   Sunday through Thursday: Three o'clock (3:00) P.M. to ten o'clock
(10:00) P.M.
   B.   Notwithstanding the aforesaid hours of operation, no provisions of law
establishing a curfew for persons of the ages affected are hereby modified or
repealed and that the licensee of said teenage cabaret/juice bar shall cause to
be audibly announced the curfew hours at least one-half (1/2) hour before said
curfew shall become effective.
   C.   The patrons of the teenage cabaret/juice bar shall be at least fourteen
(14) years of age, and under the age of twenty one (21) years old, and be the
holder of a valid high school identification card or other means of lawful
identification, unless entry is limited on a particular day or during
particular hours to only individuals under the age of fourteen (14) years old.
   D.   The licensee shall make responsible efforts not to permit persons under
seventeen (17) years of age to enter or remain upon the premises after ten
thirty o'clock (10:30) P.M. on Sunday through Thursday or after eleven thirty
o'clock (11:30) P.M. on Friday and Saturday.
   E.   The parents of patrons shall be admitted to observe and as part of the
operating schedule, open houses for parents shall be arranged.
   F.   No smoking or rough dancing shall be permitted, no matches, lighters or
cigarettes shall be allowed to be brought onto the premises. A sign shall be
conspicuously posted informing the patrons what items cannot be brought into
said teenage cabaret/juice bar.
The aforementioned special conditions shall be in addition to the general
conditions set forth in section
3-4K-5 of this article. Where the special conditions of this section are more
restrictive than the general conditions of section
3-4K-5 of this article, the special conditions shall prevail. (Ord. 89-21-67)
3-4K-7: LOCATION RESTRICTION:
Teenage cabarets/juice bars shall be located only in the C-3 regional shopping
district and the M-1 restricted manufacturing district, provided the operator
of said teenage cabaret/juice bar first applies for and receives a special use
permit to operate such an establishment in accordance with the provisions of
title 4, chapter 14 of this code, and teenage cabarets/juice bars shall not be
located within three hundred feet (300') of a park, a school or a church, as
measured from the closest lot line to the closest lot line of the respective
properties. (Ord. 01-14-48)
3-4K-8: PENALTY; REVOCATION:
   A.   Any person, firm or corporation violating the provisions of this
article shall be fined not less than twenty five dollars ($25.00) nor more than
seven hundred fifty dollars ($750.00).
   B.   In addition to the fine provisions of subsection A of this section, the
general license revocation provisions of section
3-1-6 of this title shall be applicable to teenage cabaret/juice bar licensees.
(Ords. 89-21-67, 99-5-11, 01-14-48)
CHAPTER 5
PEDDLERS, SOLICITORS AND ITINERANT MERCHANTS
SECTION:
3-5-1: Definitions
3-5-2: License Registration; Fee And Term
3-5-3: Application For License
3-5-4: Issuance Of License
3-5-5: Prohibited Acts
3-5-6: Security
3-5-7: Display Of License
3-5-8: Records Of Purchases
3-5-9: Report Of Rental Of Premises By Owner
3-5-10: Revocation Of License Or Registration
3-5-11: Exemptions
3-5-12:    Penalty
3-5-13:    Street Solicitation
3-5-1: DEFINITIONS:
CHARITABLE ORGANIZATION: Any benevolent, philanthropic, patriotic, or
eleemosynary person which solicits and collects funds for charitable purposes
including each local, county, or area division of such charitable organization.
CHARITABLE PURPOSE: Any charitable, benevolent, philanthropic, patriotic, or
eleemosynary purpose.
ITINERANT MERCHANT: Any individual, corporation, partnership, organization or
other entity, except those defined in section
3-8-1  of this title, engaged in the sale, delivery or purchase of goods, wares
or merchandise and who for the purpose of conducting such
business temporarily occupies any fixed place of business whether it is a
building, room, vehicle or structure of any kind or vacant lot or other place
and holds less than a year’s lease on any such space.
PEDDLERS: Any individual, corporation, partnership, organization or other
entity which offers goods, wares or merchandise for sale by seeking out
customers in door to door canvasses.
PERSON: Any individual, organization, group, association, partnership,
corporation, trust, or any combination of them.
SOLICITORS: Any individual, corporation, partnership, organization or other
entity which seeks financial or nonfinancial support for political, charitable
or other causes.
STREET SOLICITATION: The act of any person standing in or on any street,
roadway, highway, or intersection within the Village of Oak Lawn for the
purpose of requesting directly or indirectly, money, credit, property,
financial assistance, or other thing of value from the occupant of any vehicle.
(Ords. 93-14-57, 24-19-70)
3-5-2: LICENSE REGISTRATION; FEE AND TERM:
Peddlers, solicitors, and itinerant merchants shall register by applying to
obtain a license to engage in itinerant selling, peddling and soliciting within
the village. Notwithstanding the fact that a corporation, partnership,
organization or other entity may have several individual peddlers, solicitors,
or itinerant merchants in the village at one time, a separate application shall
be submitted for each individual, and each individual shall be required to
obtain a separate license. Each individual, corporation, partnership,
organization or other entity whom the individual is employed by or represents
shall be limited to holding any one or more licenses issued under this chapter
for a cumulative maximum of four (4) weeks per calendar year. The license fee
shall be $125 per week per license and shall be required for each individual
peddler or solicitor. Itinerant merchants, where applicable, shall be licensed
by the state under the Transient Merchant Act of 1987. License fees will be
waived for charitable organizations. (Ords. 93-14-57, 03-20-81, 23-12-42, 24-
19-70)
3-5-3: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
Application for license shall be made upon a form provided by the village
clerk. Each person engaged in peddling or soliciting shall apply for an
individual license. The applicant shall, under oath state in full the
following:
   A.   Applicant’s name, address, phone number, and birth date, together with
a copy of applicant’s driver’s license or state identification card.
   B.   Name of business or organization, whom the applicant is employed by or
represents, and address, phone number and state where business or organization
is incorporated (if applicable).
   C.   Nature of organization or business.
   D.   Name, address, and phone number of the organization’s or business’
representative, owner, manager or operator who will manage or conduct activity
which is subject of the license.
   E.   Type of license sought.
   F.   Location where sale will be held in village; or specific boundaries of
the area where peddling or soliciting will take place.
   G.   Dates during which sale, purchasing, peddling or soliciting will take
place and period of time of license requested, not to exceed a cumulative
maximum of four (4) weeks per calendar year, pursuant to section
3-5-2  of this chapter.
   H.   Description of goods to be sold or purchased, or nature of
solicitation.
   I.   Number of persons to be involved in peddling or solicitation, each of
whom must apply for an individual license, pursuant to section
3-5-2  of this chapter.
   J.   Dates on which previous licenses were issued by the village and
information regarding revocation of prior business licenses.
   K.   Information regarding any convictions of applicant and of owner,
manager or operator for violating any village business regulation, or any state
statute regarding the subject matter of the license.
   L.   Evidence that solicitors, where applicable, have complied with
provisions of the Solicitation for Charity Act, 225 ILCS 460.01 et seq.
   M.   Evidence that itinerant merchants and peddlers, where applicable, have
complied with the provisions of the Transient Merchant Act of 1987.
   N.   Evidence that the applicant has presented their application and
proposed plans for peddling or soliciting activities at a regular meeting of
the President of the Village and the Village Board of Trustees, confirming the
accuracy of all application information referenced in this section at said
meeting. The requirements of this section may be met by presenting plans for
peddling or soliciting activities during the public comment period of a regular
meeting, consistent with the rules governing public comments, or by scheduling
a time for presentation with the Village Clerk.
   O.   Any charitable organization seeking to solicit funds on streets in the
Village must also provide a certificate of insurance with an appropriate level
of insurance as required by village ordinance.
   P.   Any other information required by the village. (Ords. 93-14-57, 24-19-
70)
3-5-4: ISSUANCE OF LICENSE:
The clerk, upon receipt of a properly completed application, including evidence
that each applicant has presented their application and proposed plans for
peddling or solicitation activities to the President of the Village and the
Village Board of Trustees, pursuant to section
3-5-3  of this chapter, but in no event later than ten (10) working days after
the receipt of the application, shall either issue a license or notify the
applicant, in writing, that the application has been denied because:
   A.   The applicant has failed to comply with the requirements of this
chapter; or
   B.   The applicant, owner, manager or operator has, within the previous
year, been convicted of a violation of any of the provisions of this chapter,
its predecessor, or the ordinance of any other municipality, or state or
federal statute regulating selling, soliciting, or peddling; or a violation of
any law where the offense for which the applicant was convicted took place in
connection with the applicant’s alleged selling, soliciting or peddling. (Ords.
93-14-57, 24-19-70)
3-5-5: PROHIBITED ACTS:
   A.   Uninvited Peddling Or Soliciting:
      1.   No person shall engage in solicitation or peddling upon any
commercial premises, dwelling house, apartment or other residence after having
been asked by the owner, manager, agent or occupant thereof to leave such
commercial premises or residence.
      2.   It shall be unlawful for any person to engage in peddling or
solicitation upon any commercial premises or residence if such commercial
premises or residence is posted against solicitation or peddling by means of a
notice, prominently displayed upon which is printed the legend:
         NO SOLICITORS OR PEDDLERS
For purposes of this subsection A, a commercial premises, a dwelling house,
apartment or other place of residence shall be deemed to be posted against
solicitation or peddling if there is exhibited, on or near the main entrance to
the premises or on or near the main door to any residence located thereon, a
sign at least three inches by four inches (3" x 4") in size with letters at
least one-third inch (1/3") in height, which bears the above legend. Signs
complying with the foregoing requirements shall be made available at cost at
the office of the village clerk to persons requesting the same.
   B.   False Statements And Representations:
      1.   No person who engages in itinerant selling, purchasing, solicitation
or peddling shall use any plan, scheme or ruse or make any statement which
indicates or implies that his or her purpose is other than to make sales,
purchases, collect funds or seek nonfinancial support for the business or
organization named in the license issued.
      2.   No person who engages in itinerant selling or peddling shall
misrepresent the right of a buyer to rescind or cancel a sale under the
provisions of applicable law.
      3.   It shall be unlawful for any itinerant merchant, peddler or
solicitor to cheat, deceive or make fraudulent misrepresentations, or for any
itinerant merchant or peddler to barter, sell or peddle any goods, merchandise
or wares other than those specified in his or her application for a license.
   C.   Peddling Or Soliciting After Hours: It shall be unlawful for any person
to engage in peddling or solicitation at any time prior to nine o’clock (9:00)
A.M. or after seven o’clock (7:00) P.M., local time, of any weekday; at any
time prior to ten o’clock (10:00) A.M. or after six o’clock (6:00) P.M. on a
Saturday; or at any time on a Sunday or on a state or national holiday.
   D.   Obstructing Traffic: No itinerant merchant, solicitor or peddler shall
interfere with either vehicular or pedestrian traffic on any public right of
way.
   E.   Solicitation On Highways: Pursuant to 625 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/
11-1006(c), no person shall stand on a highway for the purpose of soliciting
contributions from the occupant of any vehicle except when expressly permitted
by village ordinance. The village shall determine by ordinance where and when
solicitations may take place based on the safety of the solicitors and the
safety of the motorists. The decision shall also take into account the orderly
flow of traffic and may not allow interference with the operation of official
traffic control devices. Solicitation on highways shall further comply with all
other applicable rules and regulations of this chapter. (Ords. 93-14-57, 08-04-
14, 24-19-70)
3-5-6: SECURITY:
Before any license, as provided by this chapter, except those licenses for
which fees are waived, shall be issued, an applicant shall file with the
village clerk security in the sum of one thousand dollars ($1,000.00), in the
form of a bond executed by the applicant, or owner, manager or operator, as
principal, and two (2) sureties upon whom service of process may be made in the
state to ensure that said applicant, owner, manager or operator shall comply
fully with all the ordinances of the village and the statutes of the state.
Security shall be terminated or returned within ten (10) days after the
expiration of the license if no charges of violations have been brought. If
charges are brought, security shall be held pending the outcome or settlement.
(Ords. 93-14-57, 24-19-70)
3-5-7: DISPLAY OF LICENSE:
Licenses under this chapter shall be posted conspicuously where business is to
be conducted. In regard to peddlers and solicitors, a large personal
identification license shall be displayed conspicuously on the outer garment of
the registered peddler or solicitor at all times when engaged in soliciting or
peddling within the village. Itinerant merchants, peddlers and solicitors shall
carry sufficient quantities of business cards, or other written materials, with
accurate contact information, including the name of the licensee and their
business, address, and phone number. Such business cards, or other written
materials with the licensee’s contact information, must be provided to
individuals upon request. (Ords. 93-14-57, 24-19-70)
3-5-8: RECORDS OF PURCHASES:
Every itinerant merchant who engages in purchasing goods shall keep a record of
those goods purchased including the amount paid for each item, the date of
purchase, the seller's name, address, driver's license number and a listing of
two (2) items of identification presented by the seller to verify the seller's
name and address. Such record shall be, at all reasonable times, open to the
inspection of the village's police department. (Ord. 93-14-57)
3-5-9: REPORT OF RENTAL OF PREMISES BY OWNER:
The owner, proprietor or manager of any hotel, motel, store or building of any
type shall report to the chief of police, within eight (8) hours after renting,
the name of any person who shall rent for less than a one year period a room or
other space for the purchase and/or sale of goods, wares or merchandise, giving
the location of the room or space so rented. (Ord. 93-14-57)
3-5-10: REVOCATION OF LICENSE OR REGISTRATION:
Any license or registration under this chapter may be revoked by the village
manager because of any violation of this chapter or of any other ordinance of
the village, or of any state or federal law, but not before the licensee or
registrant has been given at least three (3) days' notice and an opportunity to
be heard by the village manager on the issue of whether said license or
registration should be revoked. (Ord. 93-14-57)
3-5-11: EXEMPTIONS:
Notwithstanding anything to the contrary contained in this chapter, the
following shall not be subject to the registration and licensing/permitting
provisions contained in this Chapter:
   A.   Pursuant to the United States supreme court’s holding in Watchtower
Bible and Tract Society of New York, Inc. v. Village of Stratton, (2002 WL
1305851 - June 17, 2002), any person engaging in the door to door advocacy of a
particular position or cause, with or without the distribution of pamphlets,
handbills or literature relative to said position or cause, unless said person
is also engaging in the sale of goods or services, the taking of orders
relative to the sale of goods or services or the solicitation of funds while
engaging in the door to door advocacy of said position or cause; and
   B.   Minors engaged in fundraising activities to support: (1) a federally
chartered charitable organization (such as Girl Scouts or Boy Scouts of
America); (2) an activity organized and operated by a public or private school
or unit of local government; (3) an organization organized as a Not For Profit
entity and registered with the State of Illinois as a Charitable Organization;
or (4) a similar Not For Profit organization that has a chapter or club based
in the Village of Oak Lawn, as approved by the Village Manager.
   C.   Law enforcement personnel, firefighters, or other persons employed to
protect the public safety of the Village, who will be soliciting for charitable
purposes on behalf of a charitable organization, as defined in Section 2 of the
Charitable Games Act, who file an application with the Village in accordance
with Section 11-80-9 of the Illinois Municipal Code, 65 ILCS 5/11-80-9, in
which case the provisions of 65 ILCS 5/11-80-9 shall apply. (Ords. 02-18-71,
24-19-70)
3-5-12: PENALTY: 
Any person violating any provision of this chapter shall be fined not less than
two hundred fifty dollars ($250.00) nor more than seven hundred fifty dollars
($750.00) for each offense. Each day of violation, and each violating
individual at each violating location shall be considered a separate offense
under this chapter. (Ord. 24-19-70)
3-5-13: STREET SOLICITATION:
   A.   General Prohibition: Except as expressly permitted below, and subject
to the provisions of Section
3-5-5 of this Code, no person may stand in or on any street, roadway, highway,
or intersection within the Village of Oak Lawn for the purpose of soliciting
contributions from the occupant of any vehicle.
   B.   Exception: A permit may be granted as outlined below, to certain
charitable organizations to solicit contributions from the occupants of stopped
vehicles at certain signalized intersections within the Village of Oak Lawn.
   C.   Application: Applications for permits for street solicitation shall be
made at least thirty (30) days in advance of the earliest date requested, but
in no case prior to January 1st of the applicable year. Applications shall be
submitted to the village clerk, upon the form designated by the village clerk
therefor. Applications shall be accompanied by indications of compliance with
the eligibility requirements of Section
3-5-3 of this chapter and a certificate of insurance in the amount of not less
than $1,000,000.00 per claim and $2,000,000.00 per occurrence, naming the
Village of Oak Lawn as an additional insured party and a certificate holder,
and a signed agreement indemnifying and holding the Village of Oak Lawn
harmless for negligent acts of the organization’s solicitors resulting in
injuries to any person or property during the solicitation which is causally
related to an act of negligence of the soliciting agent. The current
certificate of insurance covering the actual date(s) of solicitation must be
provided to the village clerk at least thirty (30) days before the earliest
requested solicitation date. The village clerk may from time to time amend the
application and/or permit forms within the limitations of this chapter.
   D.   Eligibility: Charitable organizations eligible to apply for
solicitation permits shall be (1) registered with the Illinois Attorney General
as a charitable organization pursuant to the Solicitation for Charity Act, 225
ILCS 460/1 et seq.; (2) engaged in a statewide fund raising activity; and (3)
liable for any injuries to any person or property during the solicitation which
is causally related to an act of ordinary negligence of the soliciting agent.
   E.   Dates Permitted: No charitable organization may conduct more than one
(1) street solicitation fundraiser of up to two (2) consecutive days in length
per year. Permits may only be requested for dates between April 1 and November
15, inclusive.
   F.   Age; Safety Vest and Identification Required: Any person engaged in the
act of street solicitation shall be eighteen (18) years of age or more and
shall be wearing a high visibility yellow or orange safety vest and clear
identification of the organization undertaking the solicitation.
   G.   Safety; Adherence to Traffic Regulations:
      1.   Solicitors shall not approach a moving vehicle or one that does not
have a window down. Solicitors shall approach only after an occupant has given
some sign of assent to approach.
      2.   Solicitors shall only enter the roadway while the traffic signal for
that lane is red and shall not enter the roadway while traffic is moving.
      3.   No solicitor shall at any time enter or remain in the traveled
portion of the roadway or solicit in a manner which impedes the flow of
traffic.
      4.   No person shall solicit in the public right-of-way at any time or
place not designated in the application permit under which the person is
conducting the solicitation.
   H.   Locations; Hours: Solicitations shall only be conducted at approved
traffic control intersections, as designated in the approved permit, when
traffic has come to a full stop. Based on the safety of potential street
solicitors and the safety of motorists, and considering the safe and orderly
flow of traffic, solicitations may be permitted only during daylight hours, but
not earlier than seven o’clock (7:00) a.m. and not later than six o’clock (6:
00) p.m. Central Time, and only at signalized intersection within the corporate
limits of the Village of Oak Lawn.
   I.   Permits: The village clerk shall issue individual permits to street
solicitors affiliated with organizations meeting the application requirements
of this chapter, subject to the limitations above. However, no more than one
permit shall be issued by the Village for the same date for the same
intersection. If applicants affiliated with two (2) or more organizations apply
for permits for the same dates for the same intersection, the village clerk
shall resolve this conflict on a first come, first served basis, based upon the
date and time of application. All street solicitors shall carry, and show on
request, a copy of their permit for street solicitation. Solicitors may only
solicit on behalf of a permitted organization and such solicitation may only
occur during a permitted charitable solicitation event.
   J.   Violations; Adverse Weather: Any violations of the provisions of this
chapter or extraordinarily adverse weather conditions may cause the village to
suspend all soliciting activities.
   K.   Penalty: Any person or organization, charitable or other, violating the
prohibition of this chapter against street solicitation except as expressly
permitted shall be fined not less than two hundred fifty dollars ($250.00) nor
more seven hundred fifty dollars ($750.00) for each offense. Each day of
violation, and each violating solicitor at each violating location shall be
considered a separate offense under this chapter. Prior violations shall be
considered valid cause to deny subsequent permit applications. (Ord. 24-19-70)
CHAPTER 6
MERCHANDISING
ARTICLE A. COAL YARDS AND LUMBERYARDS
SECTION:
3-6A-1: Coal Yards
3-6A-1-1: Definition
3-6A-1-2: Application For License
3-6A-1-3: Storage Regulations
3-6A-2: Lumberyards
3-6A-1: COAL YARDS:
3-6A-1-1: DEFINITION:
For the purposes of this Chapter, the term "coal yard" shall mean any building,
enclosure, lot or other place where any anthracite, bituminous or lignite coal,
briquette coke, petroleum coke or any other manufactured or patented fuel not
sold by liquid or metered measure, is kept for sale, sold or offered for sale.
(1951 Code
11-2-1)
3-6A-1-2: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
The application for a license to operate or maintain a coal yard shall state
the location of such coal yard and the name of the person maintaining or
operating the same 1 . (1951 Code
11-2-3)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Title.
3-6A-1-3: STORAGE REGULATIONS:
   A.   Flammable Liquids: No coal, coke or other solid fuel kept in any coal
yard shall be stored for sale in the same building with fuel oil, gasoline, or
other flammable liquids unless separated therefrom by a fireproof wall.
   B.   Boilers And Furnaces: It shall be the duty of every person conducting
or operating a coal yard to store all soft coal away from the brickwork of
boilers and furnaces.
   C.   Prevention Of Spontaneous Combustion: Whenever coal in storage or
stockpiles in any coal yard shows indications of spontaneous ignition or gives
off gases, it shall be the duty of the licensee, agent or person in charge or
control of the premises to turn over and overhaul such coal pile and remove all
portions of coal showing indications of ignition or coking. The floor or ground
surface of all coal yards shall at all times be kept free from flammable waste
material and accumulations of combustible waste materials. (1951 Code
11-2-4)
3-6A-2: LUMBERYARDS:
The application for a license to keep, conduct or maintain a lumberyard or a
place for the sale, storing or keeping of lumber shall set forth the name of
the applicant and the place where it is desired to keep such lumberyard or the
place to be used for the storage or sale of lumber 1 . (1951 Code
11-9-2)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Title.
ARTICLE B. JUNK DEALERS 1
SECTION:
3-6B-1: Stolen Goods
3-6B-2: Vehicles To Be Marked
3-6B-3: Licenses
3-6B-4: Restrictions
3-6B-5: Definitions
3-6B-6: Penalty
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Title.
3-6B-1: STOLEN GOODS:
Every junk dealer who shall receive or be in possession of any goods, article
or things of value which may have been lost or stolen shall, upon demand,
produce such article or things to any member of the Police Department for
examination. (1951 Code
11-8-3)
3-6B-2: VEHICLES TO BE MARKED:
Every vehicle used by a junk dealer in the conduct of his business shall bear
thereon in legible characters, the name and address of the owner thereof. (1951
Code
11-8-4)
3-6B-3: LICENSES:
Every junk dealer shall obtain all licenses required of such junk dealer, and
pay such fees for said licenses, as are set forth in Section
3-1-1-7 of this Title. (Ord. 87-22-97)
3-6B-4: RESTRICTIONS:
   A.   It shall be unlawful for any junk dealer to rummage through any items
placed for collection by the Village's refuse collector so as to cause, permit
or suffer any of said items, including, but not limited to, garbage or
offensive substances, to be scattered about, or to remain at the location at
which they were placed for collection by the Village's refuse collector other
than as prescribed in the garbage, refuse and debris regulations contained in
Title 8, Chapter 5 of this Code.
   B.   It shall be unlawful for any junk dealer to collect, obtain, possess,
rummage through or pick up any recyclable item placed for collection as part of
the Village recycling program.
   C.   Under its home rule powers, the Village hereby restricts the hours
during which a junk dealer may collect, obtain or procure items set out for
collection by the Village's refuse collector to three o'clock (3:00) P.M. to
ten o'clock (10:00) P.M.
   D.   All junk dealer licenses issued within the Village shall contain a
statement in regard to the restrictions set forth in subsections A, B and C of
this Section, as well as the requirement set forth in Section
3-6B-2 of this Article. (Ord. 89-4-9)
3-6B-5: DEFINITIONS:
JUNK: When used in this Article, shall mean and include old iron, chain, brass,
copper, tin, lead or other base metals, old rope, old bags, rags, wastepaper,
paper clippings, scraps of woolens, clips, bagging, rubber, wood, plastic and
glass, empty bottles of different kinds and sizes when the number of each kind
or size is less than one gross, and all articles and things discarded or no
longer used as a manufactured article composed of, or consisting of, any one or
more of the materials or articles herein mentioned.
JUNK DEALER: When used in this Article, shall include every person, firm or
corporation that shall engage at a junk store in the business of buying,
selling, bartering or exchanging, or that shall collect, receive, store or hold
in possession for sale, barter or exchange any of the things defined herein as
"junk", whether dealing at wholesale or at retail, or as a junk peddler.
JUNK PEDDLER: When used in this Article, shall mean and include every person
who uses a "junk wagon" as defined herein and travels from place to place
within the Village for the purpose of purchasing, collecting, obtaining,
procuring or receiving junk, or makes a business of purchasing or collecting,
obtaining, procuring or receiving junk from anyone who desires to sell it or
give it away, and carries it away upon purchasing, collecting, obtaining,
procuring, or receiving it.
JUNK WAGON: When used in this Article, shall mean every truck, wagon,
automobile, pushcart or other vehicle used by a junk dealer or junk peddler in
the collection, disposition or transportation of junk from one place to
another. (Ord. 89-4-9)
3-6B-6: PENALTY:
Any person who violates the provisions of this Article shall be fined not less
than fifty dollars ($50.00), nor more than seven hundred fifty dollars
($750.00). (Ords. 87-22-97, 99-5-11)
ARTICLE C. FLORISTS
SECTION:
3-6C-1: Definition
3-6C-2: Application for License
3-6C-3: Sanitary Conditions
3-6C-4: Compliance With State Law
3-6C-1: DEFINITION:
The word "florist" as used in this Article shall be construed to mean any
building, room, stand, enclosure, premises, place, establishment or part of
establishment in the Village, operated, maintained or conducted for the sale,
or offering or keeping for sale at wholesale or retail, flowers, plants, trees
or shrubs of all varieties and description. (1951 Code
11-5-1)
3-6C-2: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
Application for a license to conduct the business of florist shall state the
name and address of the applicant, a description of the place where the
business is to be conducted and the name and home address of the proprietor,
owner and person designated to manage such place of business 1 . (1951 Code
11-5-3)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Municipal Code for license fee.
3-6C-3: SANITARY CONDITIONS:
Every establishment where the business of a florist is conducted, managed or
carried on shall be kept in a clean and sanitary condition. No decayed,
decaying, unwholesome, contaminated or diseased flowers, plants or other
things, shall be allowed to remain in or about said establishments, and said
establishments shall be kept clear of rubbish and inflammable matter. (1951
Code
11-5-4)
3-6C-4: COMPLIANCE WITH STATE LAW:
Florists' establishments shall comply with all State laws governing the sale,
possession, growing or disposition of noxious, diseased or infected plants,
trees or shrubs. (1951 Code
11-5-4)
ARTICLE D. DEPARTMENT STORES
SECTION:
3-6D-1: Definition
3-6D-2: Application for License
3-6D-1: DEFINITION:
A "department store" is defined as a store in which more than two (2) distinct
lines of merchandise are sold or offered for sale. (1951 Code
11-3-2)
3-6D-2: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
The application for a department store license shall state the name of the
applicant, the place where said business is to be carried on, the number of
persons engaged in the conduct of such business, and such other information as
shall be required by the Village Clerk 1 . (1951 Code
11-3-3)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Municipal Code for license fee.
ARTICLE E. CONSUMER COMMODITIES
SECTION:
3-6E-1: Definitions
3-6E-2: Price Marking and Display
3-6E-3: Exceptions to Provisions
3-6E-1: DEFINITIONS:
CONSUMER COMMODITY: Any article, product or commodity of any kind or class,
which is produced or distributed for sale at retail to the general public.
PERSON: Any individual, association, partnership or corporation.
SELLING PRICE: The price at which a consumer commodity is sold at retail on any
given date. In the case of commodities weighted by retailers, the selling price
shall only be computed by using the price per one pound as the basis for the
sale; provided, that the metric system may be employed where customary. (Ords.
78-18-44, 78-27-64)
3-6E-2: PRICE MARKING AND DISPLAY:
   A.   It shall be unlawful for any person to display for sale any consumer
commodity unless the selling price is accurately stated in a clear and
conspicuous manner. On any pre-packaged consumer commodity, except those sold
through vending machines, the selling price shall be marked directly on the
package.
   B.   On any consumer commodity which is not pre-packaged, the selling price
shall be stated on a sign contiguous to the point of display of the consumer
commodity so priced. (Ords. 78-18-44, 78-27-64, 78-29-79)
3-6E-3: EXCEPTIONS TO PROVISIONS:
The following items are exempt from the price marking requirements of Section
3-6E-2 above:
   A.   Individual packages of liquor, motor oils, candy, cigars, cigarettes,
razor blades, and small packages of commodities displayed at the check-out
stands, at aisle ends directly across from the check-out stand, or at service
islands, may have a selling price indicated by means of individual signs
adjacent to each commodity or by a general sign stating the selling price of a
group of similar commodities such as hosiery, underwear, shirts, bedsheets,
spreads, pillow cases, batteries, paint and one unit size packages of ice cream
novelties.
      Examples:
         "All gum $0.15 per package".
         "20W motor oil $0.75".
         "10W30 motor oil $0.95".
   B.   Individual cans of frozen fruit juices, envelopes of powdered drinks,
jars of baby foods and continuity or premium offerings may be price marked by
means of individual signs.
      Example:
         "All 71/2 ozs. baby food jars $0.22"
   C.   Multipack commodities may be marked with a total selling price for the
complete multipack unit. The price may be on carrying handle attaching the cans
or bottles. Individual cans or bottles within this multipack unit need not be
marked with a selling price.
      Example: "Soft drinks 6 for $1.00".
   D.   That portion of a display of sale commodities used strictly for display
purposes and not expected to be sold during normal customer response to
advertising may be exempt from selling price marking, provided that portion is
not directly available to customers.
      Example: A section of unpriced packages of canned corn covered with other
packages of canned corn which are marked with a selling price. The amount of a
commodity expected to be sold during a sale period shall be based upon the
individual businesses' previous sales of the identical or similar commodity at
a comparable price at the same location.
   E.   Unit pricing of individual packages of cold cuts in delicatessen
departments displayed on rods may be exempt from the unit pricing requirements
of Section
3-6E-2, provided they are displayed by grouping specific weight categories,
such as eight (8) ounces together, and a sign is clearly and conspicuously
displayed advising consumers "all commodities on rods in this section are
grouped by weight category".
   F.   As an alternative to the price marking requirements of Section
3-6E-2, a retailer may put in place an alternative price notification system,
provided said retailer, and the alternative price notification system, comply
with the following requirements:
      1.   All retailers using an alternative price notification system must
register with the Department of Quality Control. Such registration shall be in
a form as required by the Department of Quality Control and shall, as a
minimum, include the name of the retailer, the location of the store(s) in
which the alternative price notification system has been put in place, a brief
description of the type of system being used, and the name of an individual at
the store who can be contacted in regard to the alternative price notification
system.
      2.   The alternative price notification system must include shelf tags on
conventional grocery shelves which indicate item, size and retail price of each
consumer commodity in a clear and conspicuous manner at the location where each
such commodity is displayed for sale. Said shelf tags shall at all times
indicate the correct retail price, and shall be immediately changed upon any
change in the retail price, whether by reason of a sale on the corresponding
consumer commodity or otherwise.
      3.   The alternative price notification system must be set up so that
each sale is documented by a receipt given to each purchaser which shall
indicate each consumer commodity purchased by name, the selling price of each
such commodity, and the model number, size, weight measure or count where
appropriate.
      4.   The alternative price notification system must operate with a ratio
of no greater than one incorrect price charge for every fifty (50) consumer
items purchased, or ninety eight percent (98%), in accordance with industry
standards for error as to such systems. Once a pricing error is brought to the
attention of the retailer, his agents, or employees, said error shall be
immediately corrected.
      5.   All retailers using an alternative price notification system shall
provide for an automatic double check of such systems whereby a programmer
other than the original programmer verifies the accuracy of the computerized
register system each time that said system is reprogrammed. If an alternative
price notification system includes a computerized system partially controlled
off the store premises, said system must be automatically double checked by a
person on the store premises before the price is exposed to the consumer.
      6.   All retailers using an alternative price notification system shall
maintain a file as to complaints from consumers in regard to the system. Said
retailers shall file a report on a quarterly basis with the Department of
Quality Control. Said reports shall be due on the fifteenth of January, April,
July and October and cover any complaints in regard to the alternative price
notification system received by the retailer during the preceding three (3)
calendar months. Said reports shall indicate the name of the complainant, the
date of the complaint, the location of the store the complaint is in regard to,
the nature of the complaint, and how the complaint was resolved. If no
complaints have been received by a retailer during the three (3) calendar
months' period prior to a quarterly report due date, a letter to this effect
shall be filed with the Department of Quality Control in lieu of the required
report.
      7.   The savings realized by reason of using an alternative price
notification system shall be passed on to the consumer. (Ords. 78-26-70, 84-15-
46)
CHAPTER 7
SERVICES
ARTICLE A. AUCTIONEERS
SECTION:
3-7A-1: Application for License, Investigation
3-7A-2: Prohibited Act, Sales on Public Ways
3-7A-3: Exemptions from Provisions
3-7A-1: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE, INVESTIGATION:
Applications for auctioneers' licenses shall state the place of business, if
any, intended to be occupied; and the names of any employees, not to exceed two
(2), who are to be authorized to conduct auctions under the authority of the
license 1 . The Chief of Police shall investigate the character of every
applicant for an auctioneer's license and no such license shall issue to any
person who is not of good character. (1951 Code 11-1-2, 11-1-3)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Code for license fee.
3-7A-2: PROHIBITED ACT, SALES ON PUBLIC WAYS:
No person shall sell, or attempt to sell, or offer for sale at public auction,
any goods, chattels or personal or real property of any kind upon the public
streets or sidewalks of the Village. (1951 Code 11-1-4)
3-7A-3: EXEMPTIONS FROM PROVISIONS:
Nothing in this Article shall apply to any public auction or sale made or
conducted by a public officer by virtue of any authority contained in a
mortgage or trust deed. (1951 Code 11-1-5)
ARTICLE B. AUTOMOBILE SERVICES
SECTION:
3-7B-1: Automobile Filling Stations
3-7B-1-1: Definition
3-7B-1-2: Application for License
3-7B-1-3: License Nontransferable
3-7B-1-4: Fire Prevention Regulations
3-7B-1-5: Compliance with Other Provisions
3-7B-1-6: Inspections
3-7B-2: Self-Service and Service Stations
3-7B-2-1: Definitions
3-7B-2-2: Self-Service Station Licensing Provisions
3-7B-2-3: Self-Service Station Rules
3-7B-2-4: Prohibited Conduct at Service Stations
3-7B-3: Public Garages
3-7B-3-1: Definition
3-7B-3-2: Contents of License and Application
3-7B-1: AUTOMOBILE FILLING STATIONS:
3-7B-1-1: DEFINITION:
The term "automobile filling station" as used in this Section is hereby defined
as any building, structure, premises, enclosure or other place within the
Village where a container or containers, tank or tanks, either portable or
stationary, and containing either carbon, bisulphite, gasoline, naphtha,
benzole, hydrocarbon (gas drips) liquefied petroleum gas, acetone, kerosene,
turpentine, or other flammable liquids having a flash point below one hundred
sixty five degrees (165°) F., are kept or located for sale, or the distributing
of any such liquids from such container or tank; provided, however, that the
provisions of this Section shall not apply to any place where such flammable
liquids are kept or sold for medicinal purposes only. (1951 Code
11-4-1)
3-7B-1-2: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
An application for an automobile filling station license shall set forth the
name and address of the owner or operator of the filling station, the location
of the filling station, a description of each container or tank installed or to
be installed upon the premises, and the capacity of each measured in gallons,
and the type of construction of each building situated upon the premises 1 .
(1951 Code
11-4-3)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Code for license fee.
3-7B-1-3: LICENSE NONTRANSFERABLE:
No automobile filling station license granted under the provisions of this
Section shall be assigned or transferred to any person other than the licensee
named therein to do business or act under such license. (1951 Code
11-4-6)
3-7B-1-4: FIRE PREVENTION REGULATIONS:
   A.   Smoking: It shall be unlawful for any person to permit smoking in any
automobile filling station.
   B.   Use of Flammable Liquids for Cleaning: Gasoline, benzine, naphtha and
similar flammable liquids shall not be used for washing or cleaning purposes
within any automobile filling station nor in the open air within a distance of
fifty feet (50') of any repair pit or any opening from any basement space.
Provisions of the Basic Fire Prevention Code, 1984, shall apply 1 .
   C.   Waste Disposal: Waste oil or other flammable liquids shall be stored in
metal containers pending removal from the premises. No such waste shall be
permitted to drain into any sewer or storm water drainage system. Rags and
soiled waste shall be kept in metal containers. (1951 Code
11-4-7,
11-4-8,
11-4-9)
 
Notes
1 2. See subsection 7-3-1A of this Code.
3-7B-1-5: COMPLIANCE WITH OTHER PROVISIONS:
The construction and equipment of filling stations, including tanks, and the
requirements for safety clearances around same, shall be made and maintained in
accordance with the provisions of the Building Code and other applicable
regulations. (1951 Code 11-4-10)
3-7B-1-6: INSPECTIONS:
Automobile filling stations shall be inspected by the Fire Chief or his
representative at least once every year. (1951 Code 11-4-10)
3-7B-2: SELF-SERVICE AND SERVICE STATIONS:
3-7B-2-1: DEFINITIONS:
For the purpose of this Section, the following words and phrases shall have the
meanings respectively ascribed to them by these rules and regulations:
APPROVED SELF-SERVICE DISPENSING DEVICE: Any device listed by Underwriters
Laboratories and approved by the Village, but not including coin or card-
operated devices, used for the dispensing of such flammable and combustible
liquids into the fuel tanks of motor vehicles.
AUTHORIZED ATTENDANT: The owner, employee or agent who is authorized by the
licensee hereunder to dispense gasoline and other motor fuels. An authorized
attendant must be sixteen (16) years of age or older.
OWNER: Any person, firm or corporation holding title, lease, license or any
interest in a service station or self-service station.
SELF-SERVICE STATION: That portion of property of a service station where
flammable and combustible liquids used as motor fuels are stored and
subsequently dispensed from fixed approved dispensing equipment into the fuel
tanks of motor vehicles by persons other than an authorized service station
attendant.
SERVICE STATION: Any place of business where flammable and combustible liquids
(Class I, II or III liquids as set forth in Article 2, BOCA Basic Fire
Prevention Code, 1984) used as motor fuels are stored and subsequently
dispensed from fixed approved dispensing equipment into the fuel tanks of motor
vehicles. (Ord. 79-29-59; 1985 Code)
3-7B-2-2: SELF-SERVICE STATION LICENSING PROVISIONS:
   A.   License Required: No person shall engage in the business of a self-
service station without first obtaining a license therefor.
   B.   Application for License: An application for a self-service license
shall be made in conformity with the general requirements of Section
3-1-2 of this Code and shall be accompanied by complete, detailed plans, in
triplicate, for each service station sought to be used as a self-service
station. Such plans shall be drawn to scale, and shall include the following:
      1.   Lot lines and dimensions;
      2.   Building lines and dimensions;
      3.   Location and size of tanks and pump islands;
      4.   Location of control station;
      5.   Type, make, model and location of dispensing devices or equipment;
      6.   Fire extinguisher locations;
      7.   Clearances;
      8.   Such other relevant information as may be requested by the Fire
Department of the Village.
   C.   Issuance of License, Term and Display: No construction of a self-
service station or modification of an existing service station to conduct self-
service business shall be commenced until such application and plans are given
written approval by the Fire Department of the Village and no such self-service
station shall open for business until inspected and approved by the Fire
Department and until the Chief of the Fire Department issues a self-service
station license, which must be displayed prominently, at all times, at such
self-service station. Said license shall be issued for a period of twelve (12)
months. (Ord. 79-29-59)
3-7B-2-3: SELF-SERVICE STATION RULES:
The following rules shall be followed and enforced at all self-service
stations, and the violation of any one or more of said rules shall constitute
good and just cause for suspension or revocation of a self-service station
license pursuant to Section
3-1-6 of this Code:
   A.   Use of Unattended Devices: The installation or use of unattended self-
service dispensing devices for motor fuel of any kind is hereby prohibited.
   B.   Prohibited Use of Premises: No dwelling or sleeping facilities of any
kind for the owner, authorized attendant or any other person shall be permitted
at a self-service station.
   C.   Non-Public Locations: Dispensing of such flammable and combustible
liquids at locations not open to the public does not require an attendant or
supervisor. Such locations may be used by commercial, industrial, governmental
or manufacturing establishments for fueling vehicles used in connection with
their businesses. However, this shall not include private fraternal clubs or
associations.
   D.   Special Dispensing Devices: Special dispensing devices for motor fuels
are not to be installed or used without prior written approval of the Village
Fire Department.
   E.   Fire Extinguishers: One type ABC fire extinguisher with a capacity of
ten (10) pounds shall be provided for each pump island containing four (4) or
less dispensing pumps; on pump islands containing more than four (4) dispensing
pumps, an additional extinguisher shall be provided for each four (4)
additional dispensing pumps or fraction thereof. Such extinguishers shall be
located at points approved by the Fire Department, away from the dispensing
area but close enough to the dispensing area to be readily available (not to
exceed one hundred feet (100'). Such extinguishers shall be plainly marked,
properly mounted and protected from the weather, and shall be maintained in
operable condition and available at all times when the self- service station is
open to the public. All employees of a self-service station shall be thoroughly
instructed as to the location and proper use of such extinguishers; and shall,
upon request, demonstrate their ability in such use to the approval of
representatives of the Fire Department of the Village.
   F.   Signs:
      1.   Signs giving instructions for the operation of gasoline dispensing
equipment must be conspicuously posted at each self-service station.
      2.   Signs bearing the following words must conspicuously be posted on
each gasoline pump island where self-service is offered:
Self Service Island
WARNING:
(A) Stop Engine;
(B) No Smoking;
(C) It is unlawful and dangerous for any one
other than the authorized attendant to dispense
gasoline into approved containers;
(D) It is unlawful and dangerous to dispense
gasoline without an authorized attendant on duty.
      3.   The signs shall be made of all-weather rigid material and the
wording, lettering, size, design, material, mounting and location of said sign
shall be subject to the reasonable approval of the Village Fire Department.
   G.   Protection of Devices: All fuel-dispensing units shall be mounted or
protected against collision damage by suitable means in a manner reasonably
approved by the Village Fire Department.
   H.   Control Station:
      1.   Every self-service station shall maintain a control station in a
location approved by the Fire Department, equipped in such a manner that the
flow of liquid fuel from any or all fuel- dispensing units may be stopped from
such control station. A master electric switch shall be maintained at such
control station so that electric power and pump lighting to all pumps may be
disconnected at one time, and said switch shall be plainly marked and easily
accessible.
      2.   The control station shall be located at ground level not more than
one hundred feet (100") from the farthest pump and shall be so located that the
authorized attendant of such control station shall have a clear and
unobstructed view of all pumps.
      3.   The authorized attendant shall at all times be able to communicate
with persons in the dispensing area. The Fire Department of the Village may, if
necessary, require a communications system between the control station and each
pump island.
   I.   Authorized Attendants:
      1.   Approved self-service dispensing devices are permitted at service
stations available and open to the public; provided, that all dispensing of
such flammable and combustible liquids by a person other than the service
station authorized attendant is under the supervision and control of a
qualified authorized attendant, and the dispensing nozzle is a listed
automatic-closing type and must be approved by the Village Fire Department.
      2.   It shall be the responsibility of the authorized attendant to:
         a.   Dispense such flammable and combustible liquids into approved
portable containers;
         b.   Control sources of ignition; and
         c.   Immediately handle accidental spills and fire extinguishers, if
needed.
The authorized attendant on duty shall be mentally and physically capable of
performing the functions and assuming the responsibility prescribed in this
paragraph.
      3.   At all times when a self-service station is open for public use, the
number of authorized attendants on duty shall be sufficient to safely operate
the self-service station. Not less than one authorized attendant shall be on
duty. No motor fuel shall be dispensed at any time when such authorized
attendant is not at or near the control station or pump island. The authorized
attendant's primary duty shall be to supervise the dispensing of motor fuels,
motor oils and services normally related to such dispensing. Such authorized
attendant shall refuse service to any person to be unable to dispense fuel
safely, and shall shut off the dispensing unit if a patron fails to follow
instructions in compliance with the rules. All authorized attendants and other
employees of the service station shall be thoroughly instructed in the
operation and use of the communication system, the control station and fire
extinguishing equipment, the operation of the dispensing units and safety
regulations for the dispensing of motor fuels.
   J.   Sale Of Food And Grocery Items: When any self-service station also
sells food or grocery items, there shall be at least two (2) attendants on duty
at all times when gas is also being sold (1 to observe and control the gasoline
dispensing and the other to make sales of food and grocery items); except, that
if the fire department finds that the layout of the station is to allow an
attendant selling food or grocery items to clearly observe the dispensing of
gasoline and safely control the dispensing area, the fire department may
certify in writing that such self-service station is permitted to operate with
one attendant. (Ords. 79-29-59, 83-17-41)
3-7B-2-4: PROHIBITED CONDUCT AT SERVICE STATIONS:
Except at service stations which hold current and valid self- service station
licenses issued hereunder, no person other than an owner of a service station
or an authorized attendant, shall use or operate any motor fuel dispensing
equipment at any service station. No owner of a service station shall cause or
permit any person to violate the provisions of this section. (Ord. 79-29-59)
3-7B-3: PUBLIC GARAGES:
3-7B-3-1: DEFINITION:
The words "public garage" as used in this section shall be held to mean, and
are hereby defined as meaning any building or other place within the village,
where automobiles, trucks or any similar self-propelled vehicles are kept or
let for hire or reward to any person, whether such vehicles are so hired out or
let with or without an operator for same; or where such vehicles are kept ready
for use and where rent is paid to the keeper thereof for such keeping, and
where such vehicles are repaired or serviced. (1951 Code 12-5-1)
3-7B-3-2: CONTENTS OF LICENSE AND APPLICATION 1 :
   A.   The application for a public garage license shall set forth the name of
the applicant and if an individual or individuals, the place of his or their
residence, and if a corporation, the names of the officers and their places of
residence. Such application shall also contain the location of the place at
which it is intended to keep such garage and the number of vehicles to be kept
in such garage for the purpose of letting for hire or reward.
   B.   Each public garage license shall set forth the location of the garage
and the number of vehicles which the licensees keep or intend to keep at such
licensed garage. (1951 Code 12-5-3, 12-5-5)
 
Notes
1 1. See section 3-1-1-7 of this title for license fee.
ARTICLE C. DRY CLEANING AND DYEING ESTABLISHMENTS 1
SECTION:
3-7C-1: Definitions
3-7C-2: Sanitary Conditions
3-7C-3: Use Of Dangerous Substances
3-7C-4: Quality Of Work
3-7C-5: Inspections
3-7C-6: Violations
 
Notes
1 1. See section
  3-1-1-7 of this title for license fee.
3-7C-1: DEFINITIONS:
As used in this article:
CLEANING AND DYEING ESTABLISHMENT: Includes any place or vehicle where the
service of dry cleaning, wet cleaning as a process incidental to dry cleaning,
dyeing, spotting and/or finishing any fabric is rendered for hire, or is sold,
resold or offered for sale or resale.
CLEANING PLANT: Includes any cleaning and dyeing establishment equipped to
perform dry cleaning.
DRY CLEANING: The process of cleaning fabrics by immersion and agitation, or by
immersion only, in volatile solvents (including, but not by way of limitation,
solvents of petroleum distillate type, the coal tar distillate type, and the
chlorinated hydrocarbon type) and processes incidental thereto (including, but
without limitation, spotting cleaning, wet cleaning and finishing).
FABRIC: Any article of wearing apparel, household furnishings, rugs, textiles,
fur and leather.
FINISHING: The process of pressing and/or reshaping any fabric, which is
designed to restore as nearly as possible the shape, dimension and contour of
said fabric.
PROPRIETOR: Any person by or for whom a cleaning plant is operated.
RETAIL OUTLET: Includes any cleaning and dyeing establishment located within
the village where dry cleaning is sold, solicited or offered for sale, directly
to the retail consumer, and also includes any automobile, truck or other
vehicle operated within the village from which dry cleaning is sold, solicited
or offered for sale, directly to the retail consumer.
RETAILER: Any person or member of the cleaning and dyeing trade by or for whom
a retail outlet is operated.
SPOTTING: The process designed to remove spots or stains which remain in a
fabric after it has been subjected to the other process of dry cleaning. (1951
Code 12-7-1)
3-7C-2: SANITARY CONDITIONS:
   A.   Premises And Equipment: Every building or premises used or occupied by
a cleaning and dyeing establishment, cleaning plant or retail outlet located
within the village or used or occupied by a cleaning and dyeing plant not
located in the village but which has a retail outlet in the village, shall be
kept in a clean and sanitary condition as to its floors, sidewalls, ceilings,
woodwork and fixtures and as to the tools, machinery, equipment and storage
facilities used and kept in any such buildings or premises. At regular
intervals and at such other times as may be deemed necessary and as shall be
specified by the health and sanitation officer, the floors, sidewalls,
ceilings, woodwork and fixtures of any such building and premises and the
tools, machinery, equipment and storage facilities used or kept thereon shall
be washed or sprayed with a disinfectant in solution or other liquid form or
vapor approved by the Health and Sanitation Officer.
   B.   Employees: No person affected or afflicted with tuberculosis or any
other infectious or communicable disease shall be employed in any dry cleaning
establishment, cleaning plant or retail outlet located within the Village, or
in any cleaning and dyeing establishment or cleaning plant which is not located
in the Village but which has a retail outlet therein. The Health and Sanitation
Officer may require each employee of such cleaning and dyeing plant, cleaning
plant or retail outlet to be examined by a licensed and practicing physician
and may require any proprietor or retailer to file in the office of the Health
and Sanitation Officer, a certificate of health and freedom from tuberculosis
or other infectious or communicable disease for each person employed by him as
such proprietor or retailer, which certificate shall be signed by a licensed
and practicing physician. (1951 Code 12-7-3, 12-7-4; 1985 Code)
3-7C-3: USE OF DANGEROUS SUBSTANCES:
   A.   No chemical or other substance which is poisonous, injurious or
unwholesome to human beings and which is not essential to the service or
process of dry cleaning, dyeing, spotting or finishing shall be used in
rendering any dry cleaning service or performing any such process in any
cleaning and dyeing plant located within the Village or in any cleaning and
dyeing plant or cleaning plant which is not located within the Village but has
a retail outlet within the Village.
   B.   In case any such chemical or other substance which is poisonous,
injurious or unwholesome to human beings is used by any such cleaning and
dyeing establishment or cleaning plant in rendering any such service or
performing any such process essential thereto, then such chemical or other
substance shall be completely removed from the fabric dry cleaned, dyed or
spotted or finished or such chemical or other substance shall be completely and
entirely neutralized and rendered harmless and not noxious to human beings
before such fabric is permitted to leave such establishment or plant for
delivery to the person entitled to the possession thereof upon completion of
the process. (1951 Code 12-7-5)
3-7C-4: QUALITY OF WORK:
   A.   No finished or unfinished dry cleaning sold, solicited or offered for
sale within the Village shall show, upon the dry cleaned fabric being rerinsed
in water white solvent (one gallon to one pound of garment weight), sufficient
dirt or soil residue or other foreign deposits to darken dry solvent below
fifteen (15) Saybold Chorometer.
   B.   Finished dry cleaning sold, solicited or offered for sale within the
Village shall be free from solvent and chemical odors. (1951 Code 12-7-5)
3-7C-5: INSPECTIONS:
The Chief of the Fire Department or his designated representative shall have
the right to enter any building or premises situated within the Village and
used or occupied by any cleaning and dyeing establishment, cleaning plant or
retail outlet in order to examine same and to determine whether or not said
building and premises, or any material, articles or fabric dry cleaned, dyed,
spotted or finished as received, used, kept, maintained or stored on said
building or premises conform to the fire regulations within the Village. (1951
Code 12-7-6; 1985 Code)
3-7C-6: VIOLATIONS:
Any proprietor or retailer who shall represent any fabric as dry cleaned which
does not comply with the requirements of this Article, or who shall violate any
of the provisions of this Article shall be guilty of a misdemeanor. (1951 Code
12-7-12)
ARTICLE D. BARBER SHOPS
SECTION:
3-7D-1: Application for License
3-7D-2: Sanitary Conditions
3-7D-1: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
The application for a barber shop license shall state the name of the
applicant, the place where said barber shop is to be conducted and such other
information as may be pertinent 1 . (1951 Code 12-1-2)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Code for license fee.
3-7D-2: SANITARY CONDITIONS:
The premises used for a barber shop shall be kept in a clean and sanitary
condition and all persons engaged in the operation of such barber shop must
keep themselves clean both as to person and clothing. Utensils and tools must
be sterilized before being used on a patron. It shall be unlawful to keep as a
barber or other employee in any such shop, any person afflicted with a
contagious disease of any kind. The Health and Sanitation Officer shall make or
cause to be made such inspections as may be necessary to insure compliance with
the provisions of this Article. (1951 Code 12-1-4; 1985 Code)
ARTICLE E. UNDERTAKERS
SECTION:
3-7E-1: Application for License
3-7E-1: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
The application for an undertaker's or funeral director's license shall set
forth therein the name of the applicant and the place where such applicant's
business is to be conducted or carried on 1 . (1951 Code 12-3-2)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Code for license fee.
ARTICLE F. ADVERTISING BENCHES
SECTION:
3-7F-1: Permit And Application
3-7F-2: Permit Fee
3-7F-3: Conditions Of Permit
3-7F-4: Revocation Of Permit
3-7F-5: Advertising Benches On Private Property
3-7F-6: Penalty
3-7F-7: Permit Renewal, Five Year Option/Preference Period
3-7F-1: PERMIT AND APPLICATION:
   A.   Applications may be made to and on forms approved by the department of
quality control for rental permits allowing the installation of advertising
benches on public property along the streets and thoroughfares within the
village.
   B.   Each application for an advertising bench permit shall be accompanied
by the correct fee as defined in section
3-7F-2 of this article and said fee shall not be prorated.
   C.   Each year, applications for the one year advertising bench permit shall
be filed no sooner than April 1 preceding the permit year for which the
application applies. In the event that April 1 falls on a Saturday, Sunday or
holiday, the first business day after April 1 shall serve as the first day for
filing said applications.
   D.   The application for an advertising bench permit shall include:
      1.   The name, address and phone number of the business applying for the
license;
      2.   The type of ownership of the business, i.e. whether individual,
partnership, or corporation, etc.;
      3.   The names, complete residence addresses and residence telephone
numbers of all owners of the business; and if any such owner is a corporation,
of the principal officers and registered agent of such corporation;
      4.   A sketch showing the exact location of the proposed site for the
advertising bench in relation to property lines, building lines, street lines
and other facilities or improvements within fifty feet (50') of the proposed
location of said bench;
      5.   A statement as to the reason there is a need for the seating
services, provided by an advertising bench, at the proposed location for the
advertising bench;
      6.   Such other information as may be required by the village, from time
to time.
   E.   As the number of sites, at which advertising benches may be located,
may be limited by the provisions of this article, advertising bench permit
applications shall be processed in the order in which they are filed with the
department of quality control. In the event that two (2) or more applications
are received simultaneously, for the placement of an advertising bench at the
same location, the department of quality control shall break ties and determine
the order of filing as to each separate location, individually, by means of a
lottery or other fair and impartial method of random selection. All concerned
applicants shall be notified by mail of the date, time and place that the
department of quality control shall conduct said lottery or other fair and
impartial method of random selection.
   F.   The department of quality control shall either deny the application,
stating the reasons for such denial, or grant said permit subject to the
following terms:
      1.   The term "advertising bench" as used in this code shall mean any
seat located upon public property along any public street for the accommodation
of passersby or persons awaiting transportation and which contains thereon an
advertisement. The design of such bench shall be subject to approval by the
department of quality control. Detailed design plans shall be submitted with an
application for an advertising bench permit.
      2.   Advertising benches shall be placed at such locations applied for
and for which the applicant has shown, and the department of quality control
has determined, that an actual need for the seating services of said benches
exist. In addition, advertising benches shall only be placed at such locations
determined by the department of quality control not to cause an undue health or
safety hazard, interfere with the right of the public to the proper use of the
streets and thoroughfares, or cause a nuisance as proscribed by either 740
Illinois Compiled Statutes, or title 8, chapter 1 of this code. Provided
further, however, that no advertising bench shall be placed, installed, used or
maintained:
         a.   So as to reduce the clear, continuous combined sidewalk and/or
parkway width to less than five feet (5');
         b.   Within fifteen feet (15') of any fire hydrant or other emergency
facility;
         c.   Within twenty five feet (25') of any intersecting driveway, alley
or street;
         d.   Within ten feet (10') of any marked crosswalk;
         e.   At any location where the width of paved clear space in any
direction for the passageway of pedestrians is reduced to less than five feet
(5');
         f.   At any location where another advertising bench, for which a
valid permit has been obtained, is located, unless the applicant for the second
advertising bench permit can demonstrate to the department of quality control
an actual need for the placement of more than one bench at said location to
provide adequate seating facilities for the use of pedestrians. (Ord. 85-5-15)
3-7F-2: PERMIT FEE:
The permit fee shall be as set forth in section
3-1-1-7 of this title. (Ord. 03-20-81)
3-7F-3: CONDITIONS OF PERMIT:
The rental permit shall be granted upon the following conditions:
   A.   Within seven (7) days of the end of each permit year, the permittee
shall remove his advertising bench from the property of the village unless the
permittee has obtained another advertising bench rental permit for the
placement of the bench at its current location. In the event that the permittee
fails to remove said bench within the seven (7) day period, the village may
remove and store said bench and the permittee shall be responsible for the
village's costs in removing and storing said bench;
   B.   The permittee, upon the removal of an advertising bench, shall restore
the property of the village to the same condition as when the bench was
initially installed, ordinary wear and tear excepted;
   C.   The permittee shall maintain the bench in good working order and in a
safe and clean condition and keep the immediate area surrounding such bench
free from litter and debris;
   D.   The permittee shall save and hold the village harmless from any and all
liability for any reason whatsoever occasioned upon the bench and shall
furnish, at permittee's expense, such public liability insurance as will
protect permittee and the village from all claims for damage to property or
bodily injury, including death, which may arise from the operation under the
permit or in connection therewith and such policy shall name the village of Oak
Lawn as an additional insured, shall be in an amount not less than one hundred
thousand dollars ($100,000.00) combined single limit for any injury to persons
and/or damaged property, and shall provide that the insurance coverage shall
not be canceled or reduced by the insurance carrier without thirty (30) days'
prior written notice to the village. A certificate of such insurance shall be
provided to the village and maintained before and during the installation of
such benches;
   E.   The permittee shall, prior to placing any advertising bench on public
property, deposit with the village a removal and restoration bond issued by a
bonding company acceptable to the village in an amount sufficient to cover the
total cost of removing and storing each bench placed on public property
hereunder plus the cost to restore each site. Such bond is to provide that if
the permittee fails to remove and restore as required by subsections A and C of
this section, the bonding company shall pay the face amount of the bond to the
village for such removal, storing and restoration;
   F.   Rental permits shall be for a term of one year (May 1 through April 30)
and shall not be assignable;
   G.   Each bench shall contain the name of the owner of said bench along with
an emergency phone number for said owner; and
   H.   If at any time an advertising bench is found located on any public
sidewalk, right of way or public way within the village, without a valid
advertising bench rental permit for said location, the department of quality
control shall notify the owner thereof, in writing that said advertising bench
must be removed. In the event that said advertising bench is not removed within
seven (7) days of the date of the written notice to the owner thereof, the
village may remove and store said advertising bench and the owner thereof shall
be responsible for the village's costs in removing and storing said advertising
bench.
   I.   Such other terms and conditions deemed necessary and reasonable by the
department of quality control and/or the president and board of trustees.
(Ords. 85-5-15, 86-21-86)
3-7F-4: REVOCATION OF PERMIT:
   A.   Rental permits issued pursuant to this chapter may be revoked by the
village manager after notice and hearing for any of the following causes:
      1.   Fraud, misrepresentation or any false statement contained in the
application for such a permit;
      2.   Violation of any provision of ordinance regulating such rental
permit; or
      3.   Violation of the terms of the rental permit granted.
Notice of hearing for such a revocation shall be given in writing stating the
grounds of the complaint together with the time and place of hearing and shall
be mailed, postage prepaid, to the permittee at the address given in the rental
permit application at least five (5) days prior to the date set for hearing.
   B.   A person aggrieved by a decision of the village manager in revoking a
rental permit shall have the right to appeal to the president and board of
trustees. Such appeal shall be taken by filing a notice of appeal including a
statement of the grounds for the appeal with the village clerk within ten (10)
days after notice of the decision by the village manager has been given. The
president and board of trustees shall set the time and place for hearing such
appeal and notice of such time and place shall be given in the same manner as
specified hereinabove. The president and board of trustees shall have the power
to reverse, affirm, or modify the decision of the village manager and any such
decision made by the president and board of trustees shall be final.
   C.   In the event that no appeal is taken from the village manager's
decision to revoke a rental permit, the owner of the bench for which the permit
has been revoked shall have ten (10) days from the final date for filing an
appeal in which to remove said bench from the village. In the event that an
appeal is filed and the board of trustees upholds the decision of the village
manager to revoke a rental permit, the owner of the bench for which the permit
has been revoked shall have ten (10) days from the date of the board of
trustees' decision in which to remove said bench from the village. In the event
that the owner of the bench, in either of the above two (2) situations, fails
to remove the bench as required, in the ten (10) day period, the village may
remove and store said bench and the owner of said bench shall be responsible
for the village's cost in removing and storing said bench. (Ord. 85-5-15)
3-7F-5: ADVERTISING BENCHES ON PRIVATE PROPERTY:
Any advertising bench located on private property shall, for purposes of this
code, be considered an advertising sign subject to the rules, regulations,
permit requirements and fees applicable to signs and advertising structures as
set forth in title 6, chapter 8 of this code. (Ord. 86-21-86)
3-7F-6: PENALTY:
In addition to the revocation of permit provisions of section
3-7F-4 of this article, any person, firm or corporation violating the
provisions of this article shall be fined not to exceed seven hundred fifty
dollars ($750.00). (Ords. 86-21-86, 99-5-11)
3-7F-7: PERMIT RENEWAL, FIVE YEAR OPTION/PREFERENCE PERIOD:
   A.   Once a site has been initially determined appropriate for the location
of an advertising bench by the department of quality control, and an
advertising bench permit has been issued for said site, the holder of said
permit shall be entitled to renew said permit for each of the next four (4)
permit years (the useful life of an advertising bench having been determined to
be 5 years by the department of quality control), without said site being
subject to the lottery, as provided by subsection 3-7F-1E of this article, even
if another person, firm or corporation applies for said site, provided that
each year:
      1.   Said permit holder has complied with all the requirements set forth
in this article; and
      2.   Said permit holder has filed his, her or its application for said
site, as provided by section
3-7F-1 of this article, at the same time as, or before, all other applicants
for said site.
   B.   When an advertising bench permit for a particular site is issued to a
person, firm or corporation other than the person, firm or corporation who held
the permit for said site the previous permit year, said new permittee shall be
entitled to the five (5) year option/preference period for said site, provided
said new permittee complies with the requirements of subsection A of this
section. (Ord. 87-6-25)
ARTICLE G. HOME DAYCARE BUSINESSES
SECTION:
3-7G-1: Application For A Permit
3-7G-2: Conditions Of Operation
3-7G-1: APPLICATION FOR A PERMIT:
Every home daycare business operated in the village shall require an annual
home daycare permit. The application for such permit and for any renewal
thereof shall state the name of the applicant and the place where the business
will be conducted and shall include a copy of the applicant's current state
license issued by the Illinois department of children and family services
("DCFS") pursuant to 225 Illinois Compiled Statutes 10/1 et seq. The fee for
such permit, or renewal thereof, shall be seventy five dollars ($75.00). The
permit shall be valid only for the period May 1 through April 30. (Ord. 07-07-
26)
3-7G-2: CONDITIONS OF OPERATION:
   A.   The home daycare business shall be conducted in such a manner so that
it does not adversely affect the residential character of the residential
zoning district in which it is located or impinge upon the rights or privileges
of neighboring residential uses.
   B.   The home daycare business shall be operated by a member of the
immediate family residing within the dwelling unit used for the business.
   C.   There shall be no signs, advertising, display or activity that will
indicate from the exterior that the building is being used, in part, for any
purpose other than that of a dwelling.
   D.   Media advertising shall be limited to name and telephone number. The
address of the dwelling unit shall not be used in any advertising.
   E.   In lieu of a village business license, a valid license from DCFS shall
be on file with the village's business regulation officer at all times.
   F.   A state fire marshal's inspection shall be completed satisfactorily,
and a copy of the inspection shall be on file with the village business
regulation officer prior to issuance or renewal of a license.
   G.   All activities, including storage, shall be conducted completely within
the dwelling unit or accessory structures, and in no case shall there be
visible outside storage of goods, supplies or equipment with the exception of
playground equipment.
   H.   Should the licensee violate any of the provisions of this title, the
village may suspend or revoke the permit or may fine the permittee, all as
provided in chapter 1 of this title. (Ord. 07-07-26)
ARTICLE H. OUTDOOR FOOD AND BEVERAGE SERVICE
SECTION:
3-7H-1: Application For A Permit
3-7H-2: Permit For Liquor Licensee
3-7H-3: Approval By Police
3-7H-4: Conditions Of Operation
3-7H-1: APPLICATION FOR A PERMIT:
Every outdoor restaurant or cafe operated in the village shall require an
annual outdoor food and beverage service permit, which shall be in addition to
other business or liquor licenses required by the village. No additional fee
shall be charged for such permit. The application for such permit and for any
renewal thereof shall state the name of the applicant, the place where the
outdoor service will be conducted and the number of seating spaces to be
provided and shall include a plan illustrating the area where such outdoor
service will take place. The permit shall be issued in conjunction with a
certificate issued for a restaurant in accordance with the provisions of
chapter 1 of this title. (Ord. 07-20-56)
3-7H-2: PERMIT FOR LIQUOR LICENSEE:
When liquor is served on the premises of a restaurant for which an outdoor food
and beverage service permit is sought, the applicant for such permit shall also
apply for a class N liquor license. (Ord. 07-20-56)
3-7H-3: APPROVAL BY POLICE:
Prior to issuance of an outdoor food and beverage service permit, the
application shall be reviewed by the police chief or his designee, and approval
of such permit shall not be granted unless the police chief determines that all
necessary design features (e.g., fencing and location) have been provided so
that such service will not be an impediment to the health and welfare of
customers or passers by. (Ord. 07-20-56)
3-7H-4: CONDITIONS OF OPERATION:
   A.   The outdoor food and beverage service shall be conducted in such a
manner so that it does not adversely affect the character of the zoning
district in which it is located or impinge upon the rights or privileges of
neighboring uses.
   B.   The outdoor food and beverage service area shall be enclosed by a
fence, not less than two feet (2') high, and no portion of the service area
shall be permitted to encroach on a public right of way or sidewalk.
   C.   Exit doors from the business premises to the outdoor food and beverage
service area shall remain unblocked and unimpeded at all times.
   D.   The seating capacity in the outdoor food and beverage service area
shall not be included in the calculation of the off street parking requirement
for the business premises.
   E.   Should the permittee violate any of the provisions of this title, the
village may suspend or revoke the permit or may fine the permittee, all as
provided in chapter 1 of this title. (Ord. 07-20-56)
CHAPTER 8
PRECIOUS METALS DEALERS
SECTION:
3-8-1: Definitions
3-8-2: License Required
3-8-3: Application For License
3-8-4: License Fee
3-8-5: Bond
3-8-6: Display Of License
3-8-7: Records
3-8-8: Prohibited Acts
3-8-9: Report Of Rental By Owner
3-8-1: DEFINITIONS:
For the purpose of this Chapter, the following words as used herein shall be
construed to have the meanings herein ascribed:
ITINERANT PURCHASER OF PRECIOUS METALS: Any person, firm or corporation,
whether as owner, agent or employee, and whether a resident of the Village or
not, who engages in the business of purchasing precious metals as defined
herein in the Village, but who does not rent a store, loft, warehouse, office
space or show room by the year or holds less than a year's lease on any such
space.
LOCAL PRECIOUS METALS DEALER: Any person, firm or corporation who engages in
the business of purchasing precious metals as defined herein in the Village,
and who maintains a permanent place of business in the Village, renting space
therein by the year and holding at least a year's lease on such space.
PRECIOUS METALS: Includes, but is not limited to, gold, silver and platinum, in
such forms as coins, rings, bracelets, necklaces, jewelry of all types,
silverware, and any other item containing such precious metals with a retail
value in excess of twenty five dollars ($25.00). (Ord. 81-6-11)
3-8-2: LICENSE REQUIRED:
It shall be unlawful for any purchaser of "precious metals" as defined in
Section
3-8-1 of this Chapter to engage in such business within the Village without
first obtaining a license therefor in compliance with the provisions hereof.
(Ord. 81-6-11)
3-8-3: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
Applicants for license under this Chapter, whether a person, firm or
corporation, shall file a written sworn application signed by the applicant if
an individual, by all partners if a partnership, and by the president if a
corporation, with the Village Clerk, showing:
   A.   The name or names of the person or persons having the management or
supervision of applicant's business during the time that it is proposed that it
will be carried on in the Village; the local address or addresses of such
person or persons while engaged in such business; the permanent address or
addresses of such person or persons; the capacity in which such person or
persons will act (that is, whether as proprietor, agent or otherwise); the name
and address of the person for whose account the business will be carried on, if
any; and if a corporation, under the laws of what state the same is
incorporated;
   B.   The place or places in the Village where it is proposed to carry on
applicant's business, and the length of time during which it is proposed that
said business shall be conducted;
   C.   The place or places, other than the permanent place of business of the
applicant where applicant, within the six (6) months next preceding the date of
said application, conducted an itinerant business, stating the nature thereof
and giving the post office and street address of any building or office in
which such business was conducted;
   D.   A brief statement of the nature and character of the advertising done
or proposed to be done in order to attract customers, and, if required by the
Village Clerk, copies of all said advertising whether by handbills, circulars,
newspaper advertising, or otherwise, shall be attached to said application as
exhibits thereto. (Ord. 81-6-11)
3-8-4: LICENSE FEE:
The fee for such a license shall be as provided in Section
3-1-1-7 of this Municipal Code. Provided, however, that any local precious
metal dealer as defined herein who currently holds a valid regulatory
certificate (license) from the Village pursuant to Section
3-1-1 of this Code and who has paid the current regulatory fee is exempt from
payment of said license fee. (Ord. 81-6-11)
3-8-5: BOND:
Before any license, as provided by this Chapter, shall be issued for engaging
in an itinerant business as defined in Section
3-8-1 hereof, such applicant shall file with the Village Clerk a bond running
to the Village in the sum of one thousand dollars ($1,000.00), executed by the
applicant, as principal, and two (2) sureties upon whom service of process may
be made in the State of Illinois, conditioned that the said applicant shall
comply fully with all the ordinances of the Village and the statutes of the
State of Illinois. (Ord. 81-6-11)
3-8-6: DISPLAY OF LICENSE:
The license issued under this Chapter shall be post- ed conspicuously in the
place where the itinerant business is to be conducted. (Ord. 81-6-11)
3-8-7: RECORDS:
Every licensee hereunder shall keep a book in which shall be legibly written in
ink at the time of each purchase, an accurate account and description in the
English language of the goods, articles, items or things purchased, the amount
of money paid for each, the time of the purchasing the same, and the name
and address of the person selling said goods, article, item or thing, and the
driver's license and social security number of the seller. In the event the
seller does not have a current driver's license or social security number, two
(2) other items of identification verifying the name, address and identity of
the seller must be used as recorded. No entry made in such book shall be
erased, obliterated or defaced.
The said book, as well as every article or thing purchased, shall be at all
reasonable times open to the inspection of any member of the Village's Police
Department. (Ords. 81-6-11, 81-14-24)
3-8-8: PROHIBITED ACTS:
   A.   Resale or Change of Items: No coins shall be resold or changed in any
manner for a period of twenty four (24) hours after purchase by any licensee.
No other goods, articles, items or things containing precious metals shall be
resold or changed in any manner for a period of seventy two (72) hours after
purchase by any licensee.
   B.   Purchase From Minors: No licensee shall purchase any goods, articles,
items or things containing precious metals from any person under the age of
eighteen (18) years. (Ords. 81-6-11, 81-14-24)
3-8-9: REPORT OF RENTAL BY OWNER:
The owner, proprietor or manager of any hotel, motel, store or building of any
type shall report, within eight (8) hours after renting, to the Chief of
Police, the name of any person who shall rent a room or other space for the
purchase or sale of precious metals, giving the location of the room or space
so rented. (Ord. 81-6-11; 1985 Code)
CHAPTER 9
TAXICABS
SECTION:
3-9-1: Definition
3-9-2: Application for License
3-9-3: Restrictions on License
3-9-4: Bond or Insurance Required
3-9-5: Tag or Sticker, Display and Transfer
3-9-6: State License Required
3-9-7: Vehicle Requirements
3-9-8: Operation of Taxicabs
3-9-9: Receipt for Payment of Fare
3-9-10: Prohibited Acts
3-9-1: DEFINITION:
The term "taxicab" as used in this Chapter shall mean and include any vehicle
used to carry passengers for hire but not operating on a fixed route. (1951
Code 12-6-2)
3-9-2: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
Applications for taxicab licenses shall state the name of the applicant, the
intended place of business and the number of cabs to be operated. If the
applicant is a corporation, the names and addresses of the president and
secretary thereof shall be given 1 .
Whenever the number of taxicabs shall be increased during the license year, the
licensee shall notify the Village Clerk of such change. (1951 Code 12-6-1, 12-
6-4, 23-12-42)
 
Notes
  See Section
1 3-1-1-7 of this Municipal Code for license fee applicable to taxi offices or
  dispatch centers.
3-9-3: RESTRICTIONS ON LICENSE:
No such taxicab license shall be issued to or held by any person who is not a
person of good character or who has been convicted of a felony; nor shall such
license be issued to or held by any corporation if any officer thereof would be
ineligible for a license under the foregoing conditions. (1951 Code 12-6-3)
3-9-4: BOND OR INSURANCE REQUIRED:
No taxicab shall be operated unless it is covered by a bond or public liability
insurance policy as required by statute 1 . (1951 Code 12-6-7)
 
Notes
1 2. S.H.A. ch. 95 /2, §§8-101 et. seq.
3-9-5: TAG OR STICKER, DISPLAY AND TRANSFER:
The Village Clerk shall issue suitable tags or stickers for the number of cabs
covered by each license. Such tag or sticker shall be displayed in a prominent
place on each taxicab while it is in use and may be transferred to any taxicab
put into service to replace one withdrawn from service. (1951 Code 12-6-4)
3-9-6: STATE LICENSE REQUIRED:
No taxicab shall be operated unless it bears a State license duly issued. The
licensee shall notify the Village Clerk of the motor number and State license
number of each cab operated and of the corresponding Municipal tag or sticker
number. (1951 Code 12-6-4, 12-6-5)
3-9-7: VEHICLE REQUIREMENTS:
   A.   Equipment and Inspections: No cab shall be operated unless it is
equipped with proper brakes, lights, tires, horn, muffler, rear vision mirror
and windshield wipers in good condition. It shall be the duty of the Chief of
Police to inspect every taxicab as often as may be necessary to see to the
enforcement of the provisions of this subsection.
   B.   Marking of Vehicles: Each taxicab while operated shall have on each
side, in letters readable from a distance of twenty feet (20'), the name of the
licensee operating it. If more than one cab is operated by a licensee each cab
shall be designated by a different number also to appear on each side of such
cab.
   C.   Meter: No taxicab shall be operated unless it is equipped with a meter
in good condition to record the amount to be charged on each trip, which amount
shall be shown in figures visible to the passenger. (1951 Code 12-6-5, 12-6-11)
3-9-8: OPERATION OF TAXICABS:
   A.   Obedience to Traffic Regulations: It shall be the duty of every driver
of a taxicab to obey all traffic regulations established by statute, this Code
or ordinance 1 .
   B.   Passengers to be Accepted: It shall be the duty of the driver of any
taxicab to accept as a passenger any person who seeks to so use the taxicab,
provided such person is not intoxicated and conducts himself in an orderly
manner. No person shall be admitted to a taxicab occupied by a passenger
without the consent of the passenger.
   C.   Direct Route Required: The driver of a taxicab shall take his passenger
to his destination by the most direct available route from the place where the
passenger enters the cab. (1951 Code 12-6-8, 12-6-10)
 
Notes
1 1. See Title 11 of this Code.
3-9-9: RECEIPT FOR PAYMENT OF FARE:
Upon paying his fare each passenger shall be entitled, on request, to a receipt
showing the amount so paid and the name of the company or person operating the
cab, together with the number of the cab if such company or person operates
more than one taxicab in the Village. (1951 Code 12-6-11)
3-9-10: PROHIBITED ACTS:
   A.   Conduct of Drivers: It shall be unlawful for any driver of a taxicab
while on duty to drink any intoxicating liquor, or to use any profane or
obscene language, to shout or to call to prospective passengers, or to disturb
the peace in any way.
   B.   Permitting Unlawful Use of Cab: It shall be unlawful to knowingly
permit any taxicab to be used in the perpetration of an unlawful act.
   C.   Failure to Pay Fare: It shall be unlawful for a passenger to fail or
refuse to pay the lawful fare at the termination of a trip. (1951 Code 12-6-6,
12-6-10, 12-6-11)
CHAPTER 10
MOTELS AND HOTELS
SECTION:
3-10-1: Definitions
3-10-2: Application For License, Approval
3-10-3: Building And Premises Requirements
3-10-4: Sanitary Conditions
3-10-5: Prohibited Acts And Conditions
3-10-6: Inspections
3-10-1: DEFINITIONS:
As used in this chapter, the following words and terms shall have the meanings
indicated:
HOTEL: Includes every building or structure kept, used, maintained, advertised
and held out to the public to be a place where lodging, or lodging and food, or
apartments, suites, or other accommodations are offered for a consideration to
guests in which ten (10) or more rooms, apartments or suites, or other
accommodations are used for the lodging, or lodging and food, for such guests.
MOTEL: Any parking lot or garage for the accommodation of motor vehicles where
transient sleeping accommodations are afforded or provided on the same premises
for the public or for the occupants of such automobiles. Provided, that a hotel
which furnishes parking space or has a garage in connection with the hotel
shall not be construed as a motel.
TRANSIENT: The accommodations to any person or persons shall not be for more
than thirty (30) consecutive days. (1951 Code 12-10-1; Ords. 54, 76-36-76, 77-
1-1)
3-10-2: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE, APPROVAL:
The application for a motel or hotel license shall state thereon the name and
address of the applicant, name and address of the owner or manager thereof,
location of the motel or hotel, and the maximum number of persons and vehicles
to be accommodated. This application shall be accompanied by plans of the motel
or hotel showing fireproof construction and the proposed or existing locations
of all buildings, toilets, bath and washbasin facilities, slop sinks, water
faucets, sewer connections, driveways and other improvements.
The application for a motel or hotel license shall be examined and approved by
the Zoning and Planning Commission and the board of trustees of this village.
(1951 Code 12-10-2; Ord. 54; 1985 Code; Ords. 92-11-45, 93-9-24, 19-16-66)
3-10-3: BUILDING AND PREMISES REQUIREMENTS:
   A.   Area, Number Of Units: The minimum area for any one motel or hotel unit
shall be three hundred (300) square feet; and no motel or hotel shall be
constructed with less than fifteen (15) units.
   B.   Parking Areas: The motel or hotel areas and driveways shall be surfaced
with asphaltic concrete and service walks shall be constructed of concrete. A
minimum of four hundred (400) square feet of parking space shall be provided
for each unit.
   C.   Fireproof Construction Near Parking Areas: No parking space shall be
provided for motor vehicles within ten feet (10') of any building or structure
used for housing accommodations in a motel or hotel unless the wall facing such
parking space is constructed of fireproof materials and unless windows in such
wall, if any, are equipped with reinforced fire resistant glass.
   D.   Lighting: Any area or premises of a motel or hotel open to use by the
public or by all persons staying in or being accommodated in the motel or hotel
shall be kept adequately lighted at nighttime; provided, that such lights must
be so shaded or otherwise regulated so as to prevent them from shining upon any
adjacent premises. (1951 Code 12-10-2, 12-10-3, 12-10-8; Ord. 54; 1985 Code)
3-10-4: SANITARY CONDITIONS:
   A.   No premises shall be operated as a motel or hotel unless each unit is
equipped with a closet, lavatory and tub or shower. All such sanitary
facilities shall be properly connected to a sanitary sewer system of the
municipality. If the premises are located on a street served not by a municipal
water system, the water shall be tested for purity by the county or state
health department every thirty (30) days.
   B.   All motel or hotel premises must be kept clean and sanitary at all
times, and all waste material must be removed therefrom at least once every
twenty four (24) hours. (1951 Code 12-10-6, 12-10-7; Ord. 54; 1985 Code)
3-10-5: PROHIBITED ACTS AND CONDITIONS:
   A.   Illegal Acts: It shall be unlawful to permit any violation of any
ordinance or law on or in any motel or hotel.
   B.   Gambling, Immoral Acts: It shall be unlawful to use or permit the use
of any motel or hotel, or any portion thereof, for gambling, gaming devices or
any immoral purposes.
   C.   Trailers: It shall be unlawful to use or permit the use of any motel or
hotel for the accommodation of a house trailer.
   D.   Rates For Sleeping Accommodations: No hotel or motel licensed or
required to be licensed under this chapter shall: 1) rent any sleeping room by
the hour for any period of fewer than twelve (12) consecutive hours; or 2) rent
any sleeping room more than once within any consecutive twelve (12) hour period
measured from the commencement of one rental to the commencement of the next
rental; or 3) advertise an hourly rate or any other rate for a sleeping room
based on a rental period of fewer than twelve (12) consecutive hours. (1951
Code 12-10-7, 12-10-9; Ord. 54; 1985 Code; Ord. 08-13-35)
3-10-6: INSPECTIONS:
   A.   Premises: All motel or hotel premises shall be operated under the
inspection and approval of the health and sanitation officer.
   B.   Parking Areas: The chief of police shall inspect or cause to be
inspected each parking area to see to the compliance with the provisions of
this chapter. (1951 Code 12-10-7; Ord. 54; 1985 Code)
CHAPTER 11
MACHINE SHOPS
SECTION:
3-11-1: Definition
3-11-2: Application For License
3-11-3: Health And Safety Conditions
3-11-4: Nighttime Operations
3-11-1: DEFINITION:
For the purposes of this chapter, the term "machine shop" is hereby defined to
mean a workshop in which machines are made or parts thereof are repaired, or
where parts of machines or tools, implements, dies, gears, screws or other
articles are cut, filed, shaped, punched, stamped, planed, sawed or repaired by
means of a lathe, drill, punch, saw, planer or other machinery. (1951 Code 12-
4-2)
3-11-2: APPLICATION FOR LICENSE:
The application for a license to conduct or operate a machine shop in the
village shall state the location of said shop, the name of the owner or
operator thereof, a general description of the character of the machinery in
such shop and the nature of the business to be carried on or work to be done
therein and the number of persons expected to be employed in such shop. (1951
Code 12-4-3)
3-11-3: HEALTH AND SAFETY CONDITIONS:
   A.   General: No building, structure or part thereof used for or in
connection with any machine shop shall be so used as to endanger the health or
property of the employees or of the public.
   B.   Maintenance And Cleanliness: The owner, lessee, tenant, occupant or
manager of any machine shop shall cause all floors and other surface around or
beneath any machine or workbench to be kept in good order and repair and shall
have suitable and convenient sanitary receptacles for receiving waste and
cloths used in and about the machine shop for the purpose of cleaning machines
or parts thereof or which are intended for any other necessary use.
   C.   Loud Noise Near Residences: It shall be unlawful to operate within two
hundred feet (200') of any residence, a machine shop wherein pneumatic hammers
or other apparatus are used which cause loud or unusual noises. (1951 Code 12-
4-6, 12-4-7)
3-11-4: NIGHTTIME OPERATIONS:
No machine shop shall be operated in the nighttime between the hours of eight
o'clock (8:00) P.M. and six o'clock (6:00) A.M. in any block in which the
majority of the buildings on both sides of the street are used exclusively for
residential purposes or within one hundred feet (100') of such block. (1951
Code 12-4-5)
CHAPTER 12
ALARM SYSTEMS
SECTION:
3-12-1: Definitions
3-12-2: Permits Required; Exceptions
3-12-3: Alarm Users
3-12-4: Alarm Equipment Suppliers
3-12-5: Possession, Display and Surrender of Permits
3-12-6: License and Permit Fees
3-12-7: Monthly Fee, Reports and Records
3-12-8: Insurance Requirements
3-12-9: False Alarms
3-12-10: Service of Notices
3-12-11: Prohibitions
3-12-12: Automatic Protection Devices, Keying Operation
3-12-13: Operational Defects to be Remedied
3-12-14: Authority to Inspect Installations
3-12-15: Signaling Devices, Financial Institutions
3-12-16: Direct Connections to Police Department
3-12-17: Central Stations
3-12-18: Answering Services; Standards
3-12-19: Alarm Systems; Standards and Inspections
3-12-20: Inspection of Alarm Devices and Businesses
3-12-21: Statistical Data
3-12-22: Testing of Equipment
3-12-23: Notice of Disruption in Service
3-12-24: Liability of Village Limited
3-12-25: Administrative Rules
3-12-26: Penalties for Violations
3-12-1: DEFINITIONS:
For the purpose of this Chapter, the following terms have the definitions
ascribed to them:
ALARM EQUIPMENT SUPPLIER: Any person, firm or corporation that manufactures
alarm systems or sells alarm systems from its place of business and does not
sell, install, maintain, alter, repair, replace, service or respond to alarm
systems at the protected premise or premise to be protected.
ALARM SYSTEM: An assembly of equipment and devices (or a single device, such as
a solid state unit) arranged to signal the presence of a hazard requiring
urgent attention and to which police are expected to respond. In this Chapter,
the term "alarm system" shall include the terms, "automatic projection device",
"burglar alarm system" and "manual holdup alarm systems", as those terms are
hereinafter defined.
ALARM USER: Any person on whose premises an alarm system is maintained within
the Village of Oak Lawn except for alarm systems on motor vehicles. Also
included are those alarm systems which employ an audible signal emitting sounds
or a flashing light or beacon designed to alert or signal persons outside the
premises.
AUTOMATIC PROTECTION DEVICE: An electrically operated instrument composed of
sensory apparatus and related hardware which automatically sends over regular
telephone lines, by direct connection or otherwise a prerecorded voice alarm
upon receipt of a stimulus from the sensory apparatus that has detected a
physical force or condition inherently characteristic of a fire or unauthorized
intrusion. Also included are devices which are stimulated by direct action by
the alarm user or his employees.
BURGLAR ALARM SYSTEM: An alarm system signaling entry or attempted entry into
the area protected by the system.
CENTRAL STATION: An office to which remote alarm and supervisory signaling
devices are connected, where operators supervise the circuits and where guards
are maintained continuously to investigate signals.
CENTRAL STATION EQUIPMENT: The signal receiving, recording or retransmitting
equipment installed in the central station.
CENTRAL STATION SYSTEM: A system in which the operation of electrical
protection circuits and devices are signaled automatically to, recorded in,
maintained and supervised from a central station having trained operators and
guards in attendance at all times.
DIRECT LINE: A telephone line leading directly into the communications center
of the Fire Department or the Police Department that is for use only to report
emergency messages and signals on a person-to-person basis.
FALSE ALARM: The activation of an alarm system through mechanical failure,
malfunction, improper installation or the improper operation or negligence of
the owner or lessee of an alarm system or of his employees or agents. Such
terminology does not include, for example, alarms caused by hurricanes,
tornadoes, earthquakes or other violent conditions, nor by outside agencies or
external forces not under the control of owner or lessee.
FIRE CHIEF: The Chief of the Fire Department of the Village of Oak Lawn or his
authorized representative.
FIRE DEPARTMENT: The Fire Department of the Village of Oak Lawn, Illinois.
INDICATOR: The instrumentation on a monitor panel at the receiving terminal of
a signal line which produces both visual and audible alarm signals when
activated by a signaling device in the same electrical circuit at an
identifiable location of origin.
KEY (TO A TELEPHONE LINE): To use a telephone line for transmitting a message
either by direct connection or by a mechanism not so connected that utilizes
the microphone of a standard telephone to do so.
LOCAL ALARM: A signaling system which, when activated, causes an audible and/or
visual signaling device to be activated in or on the premises within which the
system is installed.
MANUAL HOLDUP ALARM SYSTEM: An alarm system in which the signal transmission is
initiated by the direct action of the person attacked, an observer of the
attack, or by the alarm user or his employees.
OPERATOR TRUNKLINE: A telephone line provided by the telephone company that
leads to an exchange where calls made to "operator" are received by the
company's operators, who respond appropriately to the various types of calls in
accordance with the general instructions of the company.
PERSON: Any individual, partnership, corporation, association or society, but
such term does not include the Village of Oak Lawn.
POLICE CHIEF: The Chief of the Police Department of the Village of Oak Lawn, or
his authorized representative.
POLICE DEPARTMENT: The Police Department of the Village of Oak Lawn, Illinois.
PRIMARY TRUNKLINE: A telephone line leading into the communications center of
the Fire Department or the Police Department that is for the purpose of
handling emergency calls on a person-to-person basis, and which line is
identified by a specific listing among the emergency numbers in the telephone
directory issued by the telephone company serving the Village of Oak Lawn.
PRIVATE ALARM CONTRACTOR: Any person who engages in a business that sells,
installs, maintains, alters, repairs, replaces, services, or responds to alarm
systems at a protected premises or premise to be protected on an emergency
basis and not as a full time security guard; but shall not include a person,
firm or corporation that only manufactures alarm systems or sells alarm systems
from its place of business and does not sell, install, maintain, alter, repair,
replace, service or respond to alarm systems at the protected premises or
premises to be protected, or an electrical contractor who repairs or services
alarm systems on an "emergency call in basis".
PRIVATE ALARM CONTRACTOR AGENCY: Any person, firm, association or corporation
which engages in the private alarm contractor business and which employs one or
more persons in conducting such business.
SIGNAL LINE: A line not connected to any standard telephone equipment which
leads into an indicator panel in the communications center of the police
department or such a panel in either a central station protective system or a
modified central station, and which is designed to transmit electrically an
alarm signal readily identifiable as to location or origin.
SIGNALING DEVICE: An electrically operated instrument composed of sensory
apparatus and related hardware that, upon detection of a physical force or
condition characteristic of an emergency, will excite a signal line in such a
way as to cause both visual and audible signals to be registered by indicators
on a monitor panel at the receiving terminal of that signal line in a central
location.
SPECIAL TRUNKLINE: A telephone line leading into the communications center of
the fire department or the police department that is for the primary purpose of
handling emergency messages which originate from automatic protection devices
and are transmitted directly or through an intermediary.
TELEPHONE COMPANY: The public utility that has a franchise from the village of
Oak Lawn authorizing it to furnish telephone service to subscribers in the
village.
VILLAGE: The village of Oak Lawn, Cook County, Illinois. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-2: PERMITS REQUIRED; EXCEPTIONS:
   A.   Alarm Business: It shall be unlawful for any person to own, manage,
conduct or carry on an alarm business within the village without complying with
the provisions of this chapter and without first obtaining a valid private
alarm contractor agency certificate from the state. A copy of said certificate
shall be filed with the chief of police.
   B.   Private Alarm Contractor: It shall be unlawful for any person to work
as a private alarm contractor within the village without complying with the
provisions of this chapter and without first obtaining a valid private alarm
contractor's license from the state. A copy of said license shall be filed with
the chief of police.
   C.   Alarm Equipment Supplier: It shall be unlawful for any person to own,
manage, conduct or carry on an alarm equipment supplier business within the
village without complying with the provisions of this chapter and without first
obtaining a valid alarm equipment supplier license.
   D.   Alarm User: It shall be unlawful for any person to own or possess an
alarm system without complying with the provisions of this chapter and without
obtaining a valid alarm user permit.
   E.   Exceptions: The provisions of this chapter shall not be applicable to:
1) audible alarms affixed to motor vehicles, 2) the village, its officers,
agents and employees, or 3) the fire alarms subject to the provisions of title
7, chapter 5 of this code. (Ords. 84-15-45, 09-10-24)
3-12-3: ALARM USERS:
   A.   Application For User Permit: Application for permits required under
this chapter shall be made to the chief of police on forms provided by the
police department. Each application for a permit shall be accompanied by the
correct fee, as defined in section
3-12-6 of this chapter.
      1.   Permit Required: Within ninety (90) days after the effective date of
this chapter, every alarm user shall obtain an alarm user permit for each alarm
system he operates within this village from the police chief. This subsection
does not require that private alarm contractor agencies or private alarm
contractors, that are licensed by the state, obtain a permit under this section
when they lease or provide services to alarm system users. If a private alarm
contractor or private alarm contractor agency, that is licensed by the state,
does, however, use an alarm system to protect its own premises, it shall obtain
a permit for such system as required in this section.
      2.   Application For Permit:
         a.   The alarm user applying for the permit required in subsection A1
of this section shall state on a permit application form to be prepared by the
police department and approved by the village attorney: his name, the address
of the residence or business or businesses in or upon which the alarm system
has been or will be installed, his telephone number, the type of alarm system
(local, direct connect, central station, etc.), the private alarm contractor or
private alarm contractor agency selling, installing, monitoring, inspecting,
responding to and/or maintaining the alarm system, including the address and
telephone number of said private alarm contractor agency or private alarm
contractor, and the name and telephone number of at least one other person (in
the case of a corporate alarm user applicant, at least 2 persons) who can be
reached at any time, day or night, and who is authorized to respond to an alarm
signal and who can open the premises in which the system is installed.
         b.   Any alarm user who installs a system ninety (90) days or more
after the effective date of this chapter must submit with the application a
certificate of a state licensed private alarm contractor or private alarm
contractor agency stating that in the opinion of such business the alarm system
complies with section
3-12-19 of this chapter. Such certificate shall satisfy the requirements of
subsection A4 of this section for the year in which the alarm permit is first
obtained.
      3.   Permit Fee: There shall be a twenty five dollar ($25.00) permit fee
yearly in relation to users of alarms, automatic protection devices and burglar
alarm systems other than solely "local alarms" as defined in section
3-12-1 of this chapter.
      4.   Inspections: Every alarm user licensed under this Section shall be
required to have its alarm system inspected at least once a year by a State
licensed private alarm contractor agency or private alarm contractor, and post
a certificate of such inspection on the premises where the alarm system is
maintained. The certificate shall state that, in the opinion of the private
alarm contractor agency or private alarm contractor:
         a.   The alarm system complies with the applicable provisions of
Section
3-12-19 of this Chapter if the system was installed ninety (90) days or more
after the effective date of this Chapter, or
         b.   The alarm system is safe and reliable if it was installed prior
to ninety (90) days after the effective date of this Chapter.
      5.   Temporary Waiving Of Requirements: An applicant for an alarm user
permit may file a request with the Police Chief, requesting that he temporarily
waive the requirements of subsection A2b and A4a of this Section in
circumstances in which the public's safety, health or welfare is involved.
      6.   The information contained in an alarm user permit application
required by this Section and other information received by the Police Chief
through correspondence or communications with an alarm user shall be securely
maintained and restricted to inspection only by the Police Chief or certain
officers or City employees specifically assigned the responsibility for
handling and processing alarm user permits, in the course of official duties.
If the Police Chief or any employee of the Village is found to have knowingly
or wilfully revealed the information contained in an alarm user permit
application or in correspondence or communications with an alarm user to any
other person for any purpose not related to this Chapter or official law
enforcement matters and without the express written consent of the alarm user
supplying such information, the Police Chief or the Village employee shall be
guilty of a misdemeanor as set forth in this Code.
      7.   Any alarm system user who operates an alarm system without first
obtaining a permit as required by this Section, or who, after having a permit
revoked, or suspended, and after exhausting his rights to hearing and appeal
fails to disconnect his alarm system, shall be in violation of this Chapter.
   B.   Issuance Of Permit: The Chief of Police shall issue a permit within
forty five (45) days of receipt of the completed application if, upon
investigation, there shall appear no reason to deny the application as provided
under subsection C herein.
   C.   Denial Of Application: An application for an alarm user permit shall be
denied if the applicant falsifies any information on the application.
   D.   Revocation Of Permit: An alarm user permit may be revoked by the Chief
of Police for any reason for which the granting of such permit might be
lawfully denied, or for a violation of any provision of this Chapter, or of any
rule or regulation made in implementation thereof by the Chief of Police. An
alarm user permit may be revoked when the alarm user or his agent:
      1.   Fails to respond to his activated alarm within a reasonable time
when requested to do so by the Police Department; or
      2.   His alarm system has signaled an excessive number of alarms.
   E.   Reporting Servicing Information: Each alarm user shall maintain a
current information card with the Police Department, containing his name,
address and telephone number as well as the name, address and telephone number
of a person or persons who can render service to the alarm at any hour of the
day or night. Each alarm user permittee shall immediately inform the Chief of
Police of any and all changes in the information on file with the Police
Department regarding such permit. (Ords. 84-15-45, 95-11-34)
3-12-4: ALARM EQUIPMENT SUPPLIERS:
   A.   Application For License: Each alarm equipment supplier, including those
selling protective devices in the Village for keying to a special trunkline or
an operator trunkline, shall apply to the Chief of Police for a license on a
form to be furnished him. Such application shall be filed within thirty (30)
days of the effective date hereof. Each application shall be signed by the
individual proprietor of such business or by a partner or authorized corporate
official, as appropriate for the type of business organization and shall
include the following information:
      1.   Name, address and telephone number of alarm equipment supplier and
type of business organization (individual, partnership or corporation). If a
partnership, names and addresses of partners; if a corporation, the names and
addresses of the principal officers and the state where incorporated.
      2.   A description of, and operational specifications for the automatic
protection devices offered to the public, together with a statement that the
equipment does or does not comply with standards of the Underwriters'
Laboratories and/or the National Fire Protection Association.
      3.   A list of criminal convictions, if any, (other than for minor
traffic offenses) of the applicant, or a list of criminal convictions, if any
(other than for minor traffic offenses) of any of the partners, directors and
officers, if the applicant is other than in individual.
      4.   A statement that the applicant is willing to comply with other
reasonable rules and regulations as may be issued by the Chief of Police.
      5.   A statement by the applicant, recognizing that any license granted
to him will relate to the automatic protection device or devices described in
accordance with subsection A2 of this Section, and that the applicant, before
offering to the public any other such device or devices developed in the
future, will file with the Chief of Police a supplemental description like the
one required in subsection A2 of this Section. Only upon approval of the Chief
of Police of the new device or devices shall an existing license be deemed
amended to include authority to sell the new device or devices.
Each application for a license shall be accompanied by the correct fee, as
defined in Section
3-12-6 of this Chapter.
Any alarm equipment supplier who sells an alarm system without first obtaining
a license as required by this Section, or who, after having a license revoked,
or suspended, and after exhausting his rights to hearing and appeal continues
to sell alarm systems, shall be in violation of this Chapter.
   B.   License Proration And Refund:
      1.   An alarm equipment supplier wishing to do business in the Village
shall pay standard licensing fees required of other merchants in the community.
      2.   If the license is issued after the beginning of the calendar year,
the license fee shall be prorated on the basis of remaining months of the
calendar year, including the month when the license is issued.
      3.   No refunds of license fees shall be made.
   C.   Action On Business License Applications: The Police Chief shall cause a
report on the applicant to be prepared, based on the information contained in
the application together with such other relevant information as may be
obtained pertaining to the applicant and his business.
On the basis of this report, the Police Chief shall, within sixty (60) days
after the receipt of an application for an alarm equipment supplier license,
either approve or deny the issuance of a license. Upon making a decision he
shall:
      1.   In the case of approval, notify the applicant, in writing, of the
approval, and inform him that upon the presentation of the acceptance to the
Village Clerk, or his designated representative, the applicant will be issued a
license to operate.
      2.   In the case of denial, notify the applicant, in writing, of the
denial and of the basis for the denial, which may be nonconformance with any
part of this Chapter. If the basis for the denial can be corrected, the notice
shall so state, and shall explain how these corrections may be made and set a
reasonable time limit for making such corrections. The notice of denial shall
inform the applicant that he may appeal the denial and set forth the procedure
for appeal. The procedure for appeal shall be as follows:
         a.   Within ten (10) days after receipt of the notice of denial, the
applicant shall file a notice of appeal with the Village Manager addressed to
the Board of Trustees and stating the basis of the appeal.
         b.   Within ten (10) days after receipt of the notice of appeal, the
Village Manager or his designated representative shall arrange for a conference
with the applicant and the Police Chief to attempt to reach an agreement
concerning the appeal.
         c.   If the conference fails to settle the matter in a way acceptable
to the applicant, arrangements shall be made to have the appeal heard before
the Board of Trustees. Such hearing shall be held within twenty (20) days after
the conference. The Village Manager shall cause the applicant to be given
written notice of the hearing at least seven (7) days in advance of the date of
the hearing. Notice of such hearing shall also be posted as part of the Board
of Trustees' agenda for that meeting. The applicant may appear before the Board
of Trustees, and the applicant or his designated representative may make an
oral presentation of his appeal, or he may make the appeal through a written
statement, or he may do both. The Board of Trustees shall rule on the appeal
within seven (7) days after it is heard and such ruling shall be final.
   D.   Revocation Of License:
      1.   In addition to the penalties proscribed by law for violation of any
provision of this Chapter, the Village may, after ten (10) days' prior written
notice, cancel or revoke the license of an alarm equipment supplier on any of
the following grounds:
         a.   Fraud, misrepresentation or false statement contained in any
application for such license.
         b.   Fraud, misrepresentation or false statement contained in the
conduct of the business authorized by such license.
         c.   Violation of any provision of this Chapter.
      2.   No alarm equipment supplier license shall be revoked until a hearing
is held by the Police Chief. Written notice of the time and place of the
hearing shall be served on the holder of the license at least ten (10) days
before the date set for the hearing. The notice shall set forth a summary of
the grounds advanced as the basis for the revocation of the license.
      3.   At the hearing before the Police Chief, the holder of the license,
or his authorized representative shall be given an opportunity to confront and
examine any adverse witness, and to present evidence on his own behalf. After
the hearing, the Police Chief shall either dismiss the complaint or shall
forward the complaint to the Village Manager with his recommendation that the
license be revoked. Within ten (10) days after receiving such recommendation,
the Village Manager shall approve or disapprove the recommendation. In either
event, the Village Manager shall cause the holder of the license to be given
notice in writing of his decision within ten (10) days after making it.
      4.   Any person whose license is revoked pursuant to subsection D3 of
this Section shall have the right, within ten (10) days after receiving notice
in writing of the revocation from the Village Manager, of filing a written
appeal with the Board of Trustees. Such appeal shall set forth in detail the
specific ground or grounds on which it is based. The Board of Trustees shall
hold a hearing on the appeal within thirty (30) days after its receipt by the
Village and shall cause the appellant to be given at least ten (10) days'
written notice of such hearing. At the hearing, the appellant or his authorized
representative shall have the right to present a written or oral argument, or
both, in support of his appeal. The determination of the Board of Trustees on
the appeal shall be final.
      5.   After notice of revocation has been given, an alarm business
licensee may continue to operate his business until all of his rights of appeal
under this Chapter have been exhausted. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-5: POSSESSION, DISPLAY AND SURRENDER OF PERMITS:
The private alarm contractor agency certificate issued by the State must be
conspicuously displayed at the principal place of business, and every private
alarm contractor license issued by the State must be carried by the private
alarm contractor while he is working, and shall be displayed upon request by
any police officer. An alarm equipment supplier license issued by the Village
must be conspicuously displayed at the principal place of business. Any alarm
user permit or alarm equipment supplier license shall be surrendered to the
Chief of Police upon suspension or revocation. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-6: LICENSE AND PERMIT FEES:
   A.   Alarm user permit fee, as per subsection
3-12-3A3 of this Chapter, yearly      $25.00
   B.   Alarm equipment supplier, yearly   $50.00
   C.   False Alarms 1 : For each false alarm in excess of three (3) in any
calendar year from any alarm system, the alarm user shall be charged a false
alarm service charge as follows:
 
1 – 3 alarms    No charge
4 – 6 alarms    $ 75.00 each
7 – 9 alarms    100.00 each
10 – 12 alarms  150.00 each
13 or more alarms 200.00 each
 
(Ords. 89-21-71, 95-11-34)
 
Notes
1 1. Service charges established by Ord. 95-11-34 are effective August 1, 1995.
3-12-7: MONTHLY FEE, REPORTS AND RECORDS 1 :
   A.   Monthly Fee: Every "private alarm contractor agency" or "private alarm
contractor", as defined in Section
3-12-1 of this Chapter, and security system company which maintains a direct
connection to the communication centers of the Village's Fire and/or Police
Departments by means of a "direct line", "signal line", or "special trunkline",
as defined in Section
3-12-1 of this Chapter, or by any means whatsoever for the benefit and use of
its customers, shall pay to the Village a monthly fee of ten dollars ($10.00)
per subscriber, user or lessee, to cover the Village's administrative and
monitoring costs relative to such connection(s). This ten dollar ($10.00)
monthly fee per subscriber, user or lessee shall be due and payable on the
tenth day of each and every month.
   B.   List Of Subscribers: On or before January 30 of each year, each private
alarm contractor agency, private alarm contractor, or security system company
required to make such monthly payments hereunder shall deliver to the Chief of
Police of the Village a statement sworn to by an officer or owner of any such
private alarm contractor agency or security system company, or by the
individual private alarm contractor, showing it or his total number of
subscribers, users or lessees for the preceding year.
   C.   Records Required: Each such private alarm contractor agency, private
alarm contractor or security system company shall keep and preserve, at all
times while conducting business in the Village, full, complete and true records
of all of its subscribers, users or lessees and shall permit the Chief of
Police or the Chief's representative to examine the records at any and all
reasonable times, and shall, upon the Chief's request, explain the method of
keeping the records.
   D.   Failure To Make Payment Or Report: If any such private alarm contractor
agency, private alarm contractor or security system company shall fail to
promptly make any of the required monthly payments to the Village or omit to
prepare and deliver promptly any yearly statement required hereunder, the
Village may, upon ten (10) days' written notice to both the alarm user and the
private alarm contractor agency, private alarm contractor or security system
company who has failed to make said monthly payments or prepare and deliver
said yearly statements, disconnect said direct connection to the communication
centers of the Village's Fire and/or Police Departments.
   E.   Exemption From Monthly Fees And Record Keeping:
      1.   Any person who is required by any Village code or ordinance or by
any State or Federal law or regulation to maintain an alarm or security system
with a connection to a central station owned and operated by the Village, is
exempt from payment of the ten dollar ($10.00) monthly fee and record keeping
requirements of this Section.
      2.   In addition, all units of local government, including school
districts, and all private individual homeowners who are required to pay a
yearly alarm user fee of twenty five dollars ($25.00) in accordance with
subsection 3-12-6A of this Chapter, are exempt from the ten dollar ($10.00)
monthly fee and record keeping requirements of this Section. (Ords. 84-15-45,
89-21-71, 96-05-21, 98-23-103)
 
Notes
1 1. Fees established by Ord. 98-23-103 are effective January 1, 1999.
3-12-8: INSURANCE REQUIREMENTS:
Each private alarm contractor agency, private alarm contractor or security
system company which maintains a direct connection to and/or equipment at the
communication centers of the Village's Fire and/or Police Department shall
immediately take out and keep in full force at all times while maintaining such
direct connection and/or equipment public liability insurance, including errors
and omissions for equipment malfunction, in the amount of one million dollars
($1,000,000.00) with an insurance company acceptable to the Village Manager.
All such policies of insurance shall name the Village as an additional insured.
Each such private alarm contractor agency, private alarm contractor or security
system company must furnish the Village with a copy of the insurance policy
required hereunder or with a certificate of insurance by the carrier of said
insurance showing that it is at all times kept in force.
Such insurance policy must contain a provision that it may not be cancelled
without at least fifteen (15) days' prior written notice to the Village. (Ord.
84-15-45)
3-12-9: FALSE ALARMS:
   A.   More than three (3) false alarms in a calendar year from any alarm
system for which an alarm user permit has been obtained may constitute grounds
for revocation of such permit, subject to the provisions of this Section.
   B.   After the Police Department has recorded three (3) false alarms within
a calendar year from any alarm user permit holder, it shall notify the permit
holder and the private alarm contractor or private alarm contractor agency
providing service or inspection to the permit holder in writing of such fact
and require that the permit holder submit a report to the Police Chief within
fifteen (15) days after receipt of such notice, describing efforts to discover
and eliminate the cause or causes of the false alarms. If, however, the alarm
user, by reason of absence from the Village or any other reasonable basis,
requests an extension of time to file the report, the Police Chief shall extend
the fifteen (15) day period. If the permit holder fails to submit such a report
within fifteen (15) days or within any such extended period, the Police Chief
may revoke the user's permit and under such circumstances the user shall not be
entitled to a hearing or appeal under this Section.
   C.   If the alarm user submits a report as required by subsection B of this
Section but the Police Chief determines that the report is unsatisfactory or
that the alarm user has failed to show by the report that he has taken or will
take reasonable steps to eliminate or reduce false alarms, then the Police
Chief may issue written notice of his intent to revoke or suspend the alarm
user's permit.
   D.   If after submission of a report required by subsection B of this
Section which is satisfactory to the Police Chief, the alarm system of the
permit holder incurs one or more false alarms during the same calendar year,
the Police Chief may issue written notice of his intention to revoke or suspend
the alarm user's permit.
   E.   Upon receipt of a notice of intent to revoke or suspend an alarm user's
permit pursuant to subsections C and D of this Section, the alarm permit holder
may, within ten (10) days of such receipt, submit a written request for a
hearing before the Police Chief, setting forth the reasons that his permit
should not be revoked or suspended.
   F.   Written notice of the time and place of the hearing shall be served on
the holder of the permit by the Police Chief at least ten (10) days prior to
the date set for the hearing.
   G.   At the hearing before the Police Chief, the holder of the permit, or
his authorized representative, shall have the right to confront and examine
witnesses and to present evidence on his own behalf. After the hearing, the
Police Chief may either issue an order of revocation, withdraw the notice of
revocation or suspend the permit until such time that he is satisfied that the
cause or causes of the false alarms have been eliminated.
   H.   Any person whose permit has been revoked or suspended pursuant to this
Section shall have the right, within ten (10) days after receiving notice of
revocation from the Police Chief, to file a written appeal by certified mail,
return receipt requested, or hand delivery with the Board of Trustees, and no
alarm user shall be required to discontinue use of his alarm system prior to
the expiration of such ten (10) day period. Such appeal shall set forth the
specific ground or grounds on which it is based. The Board of Trustees shall
hold a hearing on the appeal within thirty (30) days after its receipt by the
Village and shall cause the appellant to be given at least ten (10) days'
written notice of such hearing. At the hearing, the appellant or his designated
representative, shall have the right to present written or oral argument, or
both, in support of his appeal. The Board of Trustees shall issue its decision
within ten (10) days after the hearing.
   I.   If a permit holder files an appeal pursuant to subsection H of this
Section, he shall not be required to discontinue the alarm system until a final
decision is made on his appeal.
   J.   An alarm user whose permit has been revoked is not precluded under this
Section from applying for a new permit. The Police Chief, however, is not
required to issue a new permit unless he is satisfied that the alarm user's
system has been properly serviced and its deficiencies corrected. The Police
Chief may impose reasonable restrictions and conditions upon issuing a new
permit to an alarm user with respect to the particular system for which the
permit was revoked. (Ords. 84-15-45, 95-11-34)
3-12-10: SERVICE OF NOTICES:
When a person or the Police Chief, Village Manager, or Board of Trustees has
the right or is required to give notice pursuant to this Chapter, delivery of
said notice, unless specifically stated to be allowable by telephone, shall be
made by certified mail, return receipt requested, or, in lieu thereof, made by
hand by any person eighteen (18) years of age or older. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-11: PROHIBITIONS:
   A.   It shall be unlawful for anyone to activate any alarm system for the
purpose of summoning police, except in the event of what is reasonably believed
to be an unlawful act and/or an unauthorized entry on premises. Whenever an
alarm system has been designed and commonly understood to alert others of the
commission of a particular crime, it shall be unlawful for anyone to activate
such alarm system for the purpose of summoning police except in the event of
what is reasonably believed to be such particular crime.
   B.   It shall be unlawful to install or use an alarm system which, upon
activation, emits a sound similar to sirens in use on emergency vehicles or for
civil defense purposes.
   C.   No local (audible) alarm may be installed, maintained or used within
the limits of Oak Lawn unless it shall be controlled by an automatic timing
device which will shut off the audible device after a period not to exceed
fifteen (15) minutes. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-12: AUTOMATIC PROTECTION DEVICES, KEYING OPERATION:
   A.   Information as to Keying Operations: Each private alarm contractor
agency or private alarm contractor engaged on the effective date hereof in
selling or leasing, and/or installing automatic protection devices in the
Village for keying to a special trunkline or an operator trunkline, and each
alarm equipment supplier who sells automatic protection devices in the Village
for keying to a special trunkline or an operator trunkline, shall provide the
following information, in writing, to the Chief of Police:
      1.   Name, address and telephone number of private alarm contractor
agency, private alarm contractor, or alarm equipment supplier and type of
business organization (individual, partnership or corporation). If a
partnership, names and addresses of the partners; if a corporation, the names
and addresses of the principal officers and the state where incorporated.
      2.   A description of, and operational specifications for the automatic
protection device offered to the public, together with a statement that the
equipment does, or does not, comply with standards of the Underwriters'
Laboratories and/or the National Fire Protection Association.
      3.   If the device or devices operate at voltage exceeding thirty (30)
volts between conductors, then a statement that any installation in the Village
will be made by a registered electrician, and inspection and approval of the
Village Electrical Inspector.
      4.   A list of names and addresses of persons for whom installations in
the Village have been made by the applicant prior to the effective date hereof.
      5.   A list of criminal convictions, if any (other than for minor traffic
offenses) of the applicant, or a list of criminal convictions, if any (other
than for minor traffic offenses) of the partners, directors, and officers, if
the applicant is other than an individual.
      6.   A statement of the type and availability of service for automatic
protective devices that the applicant proposes to offer the public.
      7.   A statement that the applicant is willing to comply with other
reasonable rules and regulations about details of installation and operation of
such devices, as may be issued by the Chief of Police.
   B.   Restrictions on Keying:
      1.   No automatic protection device that is in stalled after the
effective date hereof by any person on premises of any kind in the Village
shall be keyed to a primary trunkline.
      2.   After the effective date hereof, any private alarm contractor agency
or private alarm contractor who installs automatic protective devices in the
Village for the purpose of sending prerecorded emergency messages directly to
the Police Department shall first obtain necessary instruction, including a
designated telephone number, from the Chief of Police with the type of message
in order to key such device to a special trunkline into that Department.
      3.   Within ninety (90) days after the effective date hereof, all
automatic protection devices in the Village that were keyed on that date to a
primary trunkline shall be disconnected therefrom. The owner or lessee of any
such device shall be responsible for the disconnecting of it.
      4.   An owner or lessee of an automatic protection device who has it
disconnected as required by subsection B3 of this Section, may authorize a
private alarm contractor agency or private alarm contractor to key the
automatic protection device to a special trunkline into the Police Department,
provided that the device meets operational requirements, as set forth in this
Chapter, and written approval is obtained from the Chief of Police.
      5.   After the effective date hereof, no one except a private alarm
contractor agency or private alarm contractor shall install any automatic
protective device in the Village and/or key the same to a special trunkline.
   C.   Current List of Installations Required:
      1.   Within thirty (30) days after the effective date hereof, every
private alarm contractor or private alarm contractor agency who has keyed any
automatic protection devices in the Village to a special or operator trunkline
shall furnish the Chief of Police with a current list of such installations,
showing the following:
         a.   Name, residence address and telephone number of owner or lessee.
         b.   Address of place where device is installed and telephone number
at that location.
         c.   Name and telephone number of any other person at a different
location who is authorized to respond to an emergency and open the place where
the device is installed.
Within ten (10) days after the end of each quarter following the last month
included in the initial list, every private alarm contractor or private alarm
contractor agency shall furnish the Chief of Police with a supplemental list of
any such additional installations that he has keyed to a special or operator
trunkline, along with any corrections or deletions.
      2.   A private alarm contractor or private alarm contractor agency who
enters business after the effective date hereof and keys an automatic
protection device in the Village to a special or operator trunkline shall, by
the tenth of the second month following the month in which the first
installation is made, furnish the Chief of Police with a list of installations,
as specified in subsection C1 above. Within ten (10) days after the end of each
quarter following the last month included in the initial list, he shall
likewise furnish the Chief of Police with a supplemental list of any such
additional installations, along with any corrections or deletions.
      3.   The information required in subsections C1 and C2 above shall be
restricted to use only by the Chief of Police and the departments concerned.
   D.   Application of Operational Requirements: The operational requirements
set forth herein apply to any automatic protection device that is keyed to a
special or operator trunkline, either through a direct connection to a line of
a telephone or without such a direct connection, but by a mechanism that
utilizes the microphone of a telephone for transmitting messages.
   E.   Operational Requirements: Automatic protective devices installed on
premises of any kind in the Village that are keyed to a special or operator
trunkline shall meet minimum operational requirements, as determined by the
Chief of Police as follows:
      1.   The content of the type of recorded message to be transmitted by
such device must be intelligible and in a format approved by the Chief of
Police concerned as appropriate for the nature of the alarm.
      2.   No recorded message shall be delivered to the Police Department more
than three (3) times as the result of a single stimulus of the sensory
mechanism.
      3.   The length of time for transmitting the recorded message must not
exceed fifteen (15) seconds.
      4.   The time gap between delivery of each recorded message must be in
the range from ten (10) to twelve (12) seconds.
      5.   Such device must be capable of transmitting an alarm message to two
(2) or more recipients, so that upon installation, any message will be sent not
only on a special or operator trunkline but also on the line of an authorized
person having a key to the premises who is available to respond to an
emergency.
      6.   The sensory mechanism used in connection with such devices must be
adjusted to suppress false indications of fire or intrusion, so that the
devices will not be actuated by impulses due to transient pressure changes in
water pipes, short flashes of light, wind noises such as a rattling or
vibrating of doors or windows, vehicular noise adjacent to the installation or
other forces unrelated to genuine alarms.
      7.   All components comprising such a device must be maintained by the
owner or lessee in good repair to assure reliability of operation.
   F.   Instructions on Operation:
      1.   Each alarm equipment supplier, private alarm contractor or private
alarm contractor agency that sells or leases to a person an automatic
protection device which is installed on such person's premises in the Village
after the effective date hereof, and which is keyed to a special or operator
trunkline, shall furnish that person with instructions as to the way the device
operates, along with a maintenance manual and a correct circuit diagram
pertaining to such device.
      2.   Each alarm equipment supplier, private alarm contractor or private
alarm contractor agency, as referred to in subsection F1 above, shall also
furnish the Chief of Police with a copy of the instructions as to the way the
device operates, along with a maintenance manual and a correct circuit diagram.
If the Chief of Police finds such to be incomplete, unclear, or otherwise
inadequate, he may require the alarm equipment supplier, private alarm
contractor or private alarm contractor agency to have the same revised to meet
the Chief of Police's approval and then promptly distributed to persons for
whom installations of such devices have already been made as well as to persons
for whom installations are henceforth made.
   G.   Repair Service:
      1.   Each private alarm contractor or private alarm contractor agency
that sells or leases to a person an automatic protection device which is
installed on such person's premises in the Village after the effective date
hereof, and which is keyed to a special or operator trunkline, must offer
service, directly or through an agent, on a twenty four (24) hour basis, seven
(7) days a week, to repair such device, so as to correct any malfunction of
same that may occur.
      2.   At the time of installation, each private alarm contractor or
private alarm contractor agency shall furnish to the person for whom an
automatic protection device has been installed, written information as to how
service can be obtained at any time, including the telephone number to call for
service; and such person shall be responsible for having the device repaired as
quickly as possible after he learns, either from his own sources or from
notification by the Chief of Police or any member of the Fire Department, that
the device is not working properly. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-13: OPERATIONAL DEFECTS TO BE REMEDIED:
When messages evidencing failure to comply with the operational requirements
set forth in subsection 3-12-12E are received by the Police Department, and the
Chief of Police concludes that the automatic protection device sending such
messages should be disconnected in order to relieve the Police Department of
the burden of responding to false alarms, he is authorized to demand that the
owner or lessee of such device, or his representative at the place where it is
installed, disconnect such device until it is made to comply with said
operation requirements. If disconnection of the defective automatic protection
device is not accomplished promptly by such demand and the Chief of Police
determines that the malfunctioning device is repeatedly sending false alarms in
succession without an intermittent valid alarm, he may then take whatever steps
are necessary to disconnect the defective automatic protective device. (Ord.
84-15-45)
3-12-14: AUTHORITY TO INSPECT INSTALLATIONS:
For the purpose of enforcing the provisions of this Chapter, the Chief of the
Fire Department and the Chief of the Police Department shall each have the
authority at reasonable times and upon oral notice, to enter upon any premises
within the Village to inspect the installation and operation of an automatic
protection device or signaling device, the purpose of which is to report an
emergency to the Police Department, either directly or through an intermediary.
(Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-15: SIGNALING DEVICES, FINANCIAL INSTITUTIONS:
Any financial institution that is required by regulations of Federal
supervisory agencies issued pursuant to the Bank Protection Act of 1968 to have
an appropriate device for notifying the Police Department that a burglary or
robbery has occurred or is in progress, may arrange to have the particular
signaling device installed so as to operate pursuant to approval by the Chief
of Police. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-16: DIRECT CONNECTIONS TO POLICE DEPARTMENT:
   A.   Upon the favorable recommendation of the Police Chief and the approval
of the Board of Trustees, alarms from business premises and financial
institutions may be terminated in the Police Department.
   B.   The Police Chief shall have the authority to request bids from private
alarm contractor agencies or private alarm contractors licensed by the State in
accordance with the normal practice in the community to furnish when possible,
at no cost to the Village, a standard annunciator panel. Such services shall be
set forth in the form of a written contract between the private alarm
contractor agency or private alarm contractor and each subscriber. This
subsection, however, relates solely to annunciator panels, connections to
annunciator panels by subscribers, and fees and charges related to the
installation and maintenance of such panels. Any subscriber or alarm user who
obtains authority from the Police Chief and Board of Trustees to terminate its
alarm system in the Police Department may contract with any State licensed
private alarm contractor or private alarm contractor agency of his choice for
the sale, installation, maintenance, and/or servicing of the alarm system to be
installed on its premises.
   C.   The alarm subscriber approved for a direct connection to the Police
Department or the private alarm contractor or private alarm contractor agency
contracting for servicing the subscriber's alarm system shall be responsible
for obtaining the leased telephone line between subscriber's premises and the
alarm receiving equipment at the Police Department and for furnishing the
appropriate interface equipment, if required, in order to provide an input
signal which is compatible with the receiving equipment used to operate the
standard annunciator panel.
   D.   The provisions of this Chapter contained in sections concerning alarm
user permits and false alarms shall apply to all subscribers or persons having
direct connect systems, except Municipal, County and State agencies exempted by
the Board of Trustees. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-17: CENTRAL STATIONS:
   A.   Standards: In addition to any other requirements imposed by this
Chapter, a private alarm contractor agency or private alarm contractor shall
not operate a central station unless the Police Chief finds that the central
station, if operated, will meet the following minimum requirements:
      1.   Compliance with the most recent "Central Station Burglar Alarm Units
Systems" (UL 611) issued by Underwriters' Laboratories or American National
Standards Institute (ANSI SE 2.2). Such standards are hereby adopted and made a
part of this Chapter, and a copy of such standards shall be kept on file at the
Police Department and be available for public inspection. The service provided
may correspond to any of the several grades of service listed in the standards,
but the owner, private alarm contractor agency, private alarm contractor or
other person in responsible control of the premises wherein such alarm system
is located must be present at such premises as soon as is reasonably possible
after being requested to do so by a representative of the Police Department. A
central station may also be a modified central station or telephone answering
service.
      2.   If a central station is currently registered with Underwriters'
Laboratories as a "UL listed central station" or if it is listed as such in
Underwriters' Laboratories' publication, "The Quarterly Supplement", such
registration or listing shall be sufficient evidence of compliance with this
Section.
   B.   Modified Central Stations; Standards: In addition to any other
requirements imposed by this Chapter, a private alarm contractor agency or
private alarm contractor shall not operate a modified central station unless
the Police Chief finds that the modified central station, if operated, will
meet the following minimum standards:
      1.   The premises from which the services are performed must meet any
applicable fire regulations.
      2.   The premises from which the services are performed must be secured
in a manner approved by the Police Chief to prevent entry by unauthorized
persons.
      3.   A large enough number of operators must be on duty at all times to
assure that all emergency messages received will be relayed immediately to the
Police Department over a special trunkline.
      4.   As soon as possible after notifying the Police Department, the
operator concerned shall notify the subscribers involved of such action and the
nature of the emergency message received.
      5.   The modified central station shall certify that all equipment
supervised by the modified central station shall be tested at least once a
year. Where a test result is unsatisfactory, the cause thereof shall be
corrected within twenty four (24) hours, unless circumstances exist that make
such corrections impossible within such time, in which event the cause shall be
corrected within a reasonable time. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-18: ANSWERING SERVICES; STANDARDS:
In addition to any other requirements imposed by this Chapter, a private alarm
contractor agency or private alarm contractor shall not operate an answering
service unless the Police Chief finds that the answering service, if operated,
will meet the following minimum standards:
   A.   The premises from which the services are performed must meet any
applicable fire regulations.
   B.   The premises from which the services are performed must be secured in a
manner approved by the Police Chief to prevent entry by unauthorized persons.
   C.   A large enough number of operators must be on duty at all times to
insure that all emergency messages received will be relayed immediately to the
Police Department over a special trunkline.
   D.   Emergency messages from automatic alarm devices shall be given priority
over all other messages received by the answering services except that fire
alarm signals may have equal priority.
   E.   All operators shall be trained to handle emergency messages.
   F.   As soon as possible after notifying the Police Department, the operator
concerned shall notify the subscriber involved of such action and the nature of
the emergency message received.
   G.   All subscribers of an answering service shall be required by the
service to cooperate in a test of its alarm devices at least once a year to
determine if the device is working properly. The answering service shall
certify that the test results shall be reported promptly, in writing, to the
subscriber and the Police Department. Until the device in question is again
working properly, the Police Department may require that its use be
discontinued. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-19: ALARM SYSTEMS; STANDARDS AND INSPECTIONS:
   A.   Within six (6) months after the effective date of this Chapter, all
alarm systems installed in commercial or public buildings shall utilize
equipment and methods of installation substantially equivalent to or exceeding
the most recent following minimum applicable UL or ANSI standards; UL 609 (ANSI
SE 2.1) "Local Burglar Alarm Units and Systems".
   B.   Within six (6) months after the effective date of this Chapter, all
alarm systems installed in residences shall utilize equipment which shall be
substantially equivalent to or exceed the most recent minimum applicable UL
Standard 1023 (ANSI SE 2.4) "Household Burglar Alarm Systems Units", and such
equipment shall be installed in a safe, reliable and workmanlike manner.
   C.   The equipment or hardware used and/or the manner of installation of the
alarm system may correspond to any of the several levels of protection or
grades of service listed in the applicable standards; provided, however, that
alarm system users shall not be required to provide protection for all of the
areas or openings which may be required to be protected by the applicable
standards.
   D.   The standards referred to in this Section are hereby adopted as part of
this Chapter subject to the modification and qualifications set forth herein
and a copy of such standards shall be kept on file at the Police Department and
be available for public inspection.
   E.   In those instances where UL and/or ANSI have not established standards
for categories of equipment or where new equipment is undergoing field testing,
the Police Chief may require that the alarm system be inspected at the expense
of the alarm user by a professional electrical engineer who shall certify
whether the alarm system appears to be safe and reliable.
   F.   Alarm systems which input into an automatic alarm device are subject to
the provisions of this Section.
   G.   Tape dialer alarm systems are specifically prohibited within the
Village. By January 1, 1985, all tape dialer alarm systems within the Village
shall be disconnected. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-20: INSPECTION OF ALARM DEVICES AND BUSINESSES:
   A.   For the purpose of enforcing the provisions of this Chapter, the Police
Chief shall have the authority, at reasonable times and upon reasonable oral
notice, to enter any premises in the Village in or upon which alarm systems or
private alarm contractor agencies or private alarm contractors subject to this
Chapter are located to inspect the installation and/or operation of such alarm
systems or private alarm contractor agencies or private alarm contractors on
official police business.
   B.   If such inspection reveals any violations of the provisions of this
Chapter, a written report detailing such violations shall be promptly sent to
the Village Manager and to the owner, lessee, or other person responsible for
the alarm system or private alarm contractor agency or private alarm contractor
in violation of this Chapter. Such report shall require the correction within
thirty (30) days after receipt of the notice of the violation discovered and
shall state that a failure to comply may result in the revocation of the alarm
user's permit in accordance with provisions of this Chapter relating to
revocation of licenses and permits. The alarm user shall be granted a
reasonable extension of time to correct such violation upon good cause shown.
   C.   It shall be a misdemeanor under this Code for the Police Chief, Village
Manager, or any Village employee to knowingly and wilfully reveal any business
information obtained during said inspection other than for official police
business or for the administration and enforcement of this Chapter. (Ord. 84-
15-45)
3-12-21: STATISTICAL DATA:
In addition to any other information that may be required to be supplied by the
provisions of this Chapter, the Police Chief may require a private alarm
contractor agency or private alarm contractor to furnish him with certain
statistical data which may be reasonably available relative to specified
periods of operation after the effective date of this Chapter. Any operational
data so furnished shall be for use only by the Village Manager, Police Chief
and Police Department and its use shall be subject to the sanctions of this
Chapter. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-22: TESTING OF EQUIPMENT:
No alarm system designed to transmit emergency messages directly to the Police
Department shall be tested or demonstrated without first obtaining permission
from the Police Chief. Permission is not required to test or demonstrate alarm
devices not transmitting emergency messages directly to the Police Department
unless the messages are to be relayed to the Police Department. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-23: NOTICE OF DISRUPTION IN SERVICE:
When a private alarm contractor agency's or private alarm contractor's service
to its subscribers is disrupted for any reason by the private alarm contractor
agency or the private alarm contractor, or the private alarm contractor agency
or private alarm contractor becomes aware of such disruption, it shall promptly
notify its subscriber by telephone that protection is no longer being provided.
If, however, the private alarm contractor agency or private alarm contractor
has written instructions from its subscriber not to make such notification by
telephone during certain hours, the private alarm contractor agency or private
alarm contractor may comply with such instructions. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-24: LIABILITY OF VILLAGE LIMITED:
The Village shall take every reasonable precaution to assure that alarm signals
and prerecorded alarm messages received by the Village are given appropriate
attention and are acted upon with dispatch. Nevertheless, the Village shall not
be liable for any defects in operation of automatic protection devices and
signal line systems, for any failure or neglect to respond appropriately upon
receipt of any alarm from such a source, nor for the failure or neglect of any
person with a license issued by the State or with a franchise, in connection
with the installation and operation of equipment, the transmission of alarm
signals and prerecorded alarm messages or the relaying of such signals and
messages. In the event that the Village finds it necessary to disconnect a
defective automatic protection device or signaling device, the Village shall
incur no liability by such action. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-25: ADMINISTRATIVE RULES:
The Police Chief shall promulgate such rules as may be necessary for the
implementation of this Chapter and for determination of grounds for clerical
suspension, or revocation or cancellation of any license or permit required by
this Chapter. The rules shall be approved by the Village Manager and Board of
Trustees and shall be open to inspection by the public. (Ord. 84-15-45)
3-12-26: PENALTIES FOR VIOLATIONS:
   A.   The failure of any person to:
      1.   Obtain an alarm system user permit as required in subsection
3-12-3A of this Chapter,
      2.   Obtain an alarm equipment supplier's license as required by
subsection
3-12-4A of this Chapter,
      3.   Make the corrections to alarm systems as required by Section
3-12-20 of this Chapter, or
      4.   Obey any order of the Police Chief or Board of Trustees of
suspension or revocation of an alarm user permit or alarm equipment supplier
license after such person has exhausted his rights to hearings or appeals,
constitutes an offense punishable by a fine of up to seven hundred fifty
dollars ($750.00). Each day that such violation continues after the expiration
of the period allowed for compliance under the provisions of this Chapter shall
constitute a misdemeanor.
   B.   Failure of any person to comply with the requirements of written notice
of a violation hereof within three (3) days of receipt of such notice shall
constitute an offense punishable by a fine not to exceed seven hundred fifty
dollars ($750.00). Such notice shall continue in force and effect until full
compliance with the requirements stated therein, and each and every failure to
comply with such notice within twenty four (24) hours after the three (3) days
allowed for compliance shall constitute a separate offense. (Ords. 84-15-45,
99-5-11)
CHAPTER 13
GARAGE AND RUMMAGE SALES
SECTION:
3-13-1: Definition
3-13-2: Permit Required, Fee
3-13-3: Number Of Permits And Duration Of Sale Restricted; Conditions Of Permit
3-13-1: DEFINITION:
The term "garage, rummage, or other like sale" shall mean the public sale by
the owner or occupant of a dwelling unit of items of personal property owned
and accumulated at the dwelling unit in question and sold in or around the
garage area but not within the dwelling unit itself. (Ord. 74-15-32)
3-13-2: PERMIT REQUIRED, FEE:
   The owner or occupants of a dwelling unit shall apply to the Community
Development Department for a permit at least twenty-four (48) hours prior to
beginning a garage, rummage, or other like sale. A permit fee in the amount set
forth in Section
3-1-1-7 of this Code shall be paid at the time of application for each sale.
(Ords. 91-19-82, 25-11-26, 25-12-37)
3-13-3: NUMBER OF PERMITS AND DURATION OF SALE RESTRICTED; CONDITIONS OF
PERMIT:
No more than two (2) permits shall be issued to a dwelling unit during one
calendar year for garage, rummage, or other like sales and no single garage,
rummage or other like sale shall be allowed to continue for more than three (3)
consecutive days.
   A.   Types Of Items To Be Sold: The items to be sold at such a sale shall be
items of personal property, including, but not limited to, household goods,
clothing and furnishings, which have been used in the dwelling unit located on
the premises where the sale is to be conducted. Sale items purchased for
intentional sale or resale may not be sold at such a sale.
   B.   Hours Of Sale: No garage, rummage or other like sale may commence
before the hour of nine o'clock (9:00) A.M. nor extend beyond the hour of six
o'clock (6:00) P.M.
   C.   Use Of Public Ways: No sidewalk or public parkway shall be obstructed
as a result of a garage, rummage or other like sale, nor shall any permanent or
temporary sign be placed in or on the parkway or other public right of way.
(Ords. 91-19-82, 93-18-73)
CHAPTER 14
AUTOMATIC VENDING MACHINES
SECTION:
3-14-1: Definition
3-14-2: Licenses Required
3-14-3: Applications For Licenses
3-14-4: License Fees
3-14-5: Investigation Of Applicant, Fee
3-14-6: Payment Of Fees, Emblem Required
3-14-7: Refund Notice
3-14-8: Revocation Of License
3-14-9: Penalties
3-14-1: DEFINITION:
The term "automatic vending machine" shall mean any machine which upon the
insertion of a coin, slug, token, plate or disc, dispenses a product,
including, but not limited to, candy, a beverage or snack food. (Ord. 96-9-45)
3-14-2: LICENSES REQUIRED:
   Operator's Business: No one shall engage in the automatic vending machine
"operator's business", which is herein defined to mean the conduct or
transaction of the business of distributing, placing, leasing or selling
automatic vending machines with an agreement to maintain, service or supply,
without first obtaining a license therefor and paying the license fee set forth
in section
3-1-1-7 of this Code.
(Ords. 96-9-45, 25-12-37)
3-14-3: APPLICATIONS FOR LICENSES:
Applications for such licenses shall be made to the director of administrative
services upon a form to be supplied by the village for that purpose and shall
contain the following information:
   A.   The applicant's name, sex, date of birth, social security number,
position and percentage of ownership of the business; and the name, sex, date
of birth, social security number, position and percentage of ownership in the
business of every sole owner, partner, corporate officer, director, manager,
and any person who owns five percent (5%) or more of the shares of the
applicant business entity or parent corporations of the applicant business
entity;
   B.   Name of manufacturer of the automatic vending machine to be licensed;
   C.   Prior convictions, if any, of applicant;
   D.   Place where the automatic vending machine is to be displayed or
operated, and the business conducted at that place;
   E.   A description of the automatic vending machine to be covered by the
license, mechanical features and serial number;
   F.   The description of the products available from the automatic vending
machine and the price therefor. (Ord. 96-9-45)
3-14-4: LICENSE FEES:
   No license may issue until the machine operator's business has paid the
appropriate license fees as set forth in section
3-1-1-7 of this title, with the license year being July 1 through the following
June 30.
(Ords. 96-9-45, 03-20-81, 25-12-37)
3-14-5: INVESTIGATION OF APPLICANT, FEE:
   A.   Investigation Of Applicant: The original application for a license(s)
shall be referred to the chief of police for investigation and verification of
the facts stated therein and he shall ascertain if the applicant is a person of
good moral character, and either approve or disapprove the application. No
license(s) shall be issued to any applicant unless approved by the chief of
police.
   B.   Fee: Before any action is taken upon any new application as provided in
section
3-14-3 of this chapter, the applicant shall pay to the village the sum of two
hundred dollars ($200.00), no part of which shall be refundable, to cover the
village's costs in reviewing and investigating such applicant. Provided,
however, that in the event the application is approved and the applicant
applies for the license(s) and pays the initial license fee(s) within the time
provided, said two hundred dollars ($200.00) shall be credited toward the
initial license fee(s).
   C.   Examination Of Records: As a condition of any license issued under this
chapter, the department of administrative services, with reasonable prior
written notice, shall have the authority to examine all books and records of
anyone holding an automatic vending machine operator's business license to
ensure compliance with this section.
   D.   Renewal: Although applications for the renewal of any licenses issued
pursuant to this chapter shall not require the payment of the investigation fee
set forth in subsection B of this section, said renewals shall be accompanied
by an update of the information required in section
3-14-3 of this chapter for new applications. (Ord. 96-9-45)
3-14-6: PAYMENT OF FEES, EMBLEM REQUIRED:
All fees shall be paid to the director of administrative services who shall
issue, as evidence of the payment thereof, to each automatic vending machine a
self-voiding adhesive emblem to be placed on each automatic vending machine.
Such emblem shall bear the words "Village of Oak Lawn", and the date it shall
become void. It shall be unlawful for any person to mutilate said emblem during
the year for which it is issued.
Each such emblem shall be issued only for a particular automatic vending
machine and may not be transferred or used in any way whatsoever as the
required emblem of any other automatic vending machine whether or not such
other automatic vending machine is a new or replacement automatic vending
machine for the original automatic vending machine. Each and every automatic
vending machine must have such an emblem issued for its particular use after
the payment of the required fee. (Ord. 96-9-45)
3-14-7: REFUND NOTICE:
The name, address and phone number of the person, firm or corporation who may
be contacted if there is a problem with the automatic vending machine shall be
conspicuously posted on each automatic vending machine licensed under this
chapter. It shall be the obligation of the licensee to make sure his/her/its
machines are in compliance with the provisions of this section. (Ord. 96-9-45)
3-14-8: REVOCATION OF LICENSE:
Every license issued under this section is subject to revocation in accordance
with section
3-1-6 of this title. (Ord. 96-9-45)
3-14-9: PENALTIES:
In addition to, and not in lieu of, the revocation of any license issued under
this section, any person violating any of the provisions of this section shall
be fined not less than one hundred dollars ($100.00) nor more than seven
hundred fifty dollars ($750.00) for each offense; and each day such violation
shall continue shall be regarded as a separate offense. (Ord. 96-9-45)
CHAPTER 15
REGULATION OF TOBACCO PRODUCTS
SECTION:
3-15-1: Definitions
3-15-2: License Required
3-15-3: License Application
3-15-4: License Fee
3-15-5: Number Of Tobacco Retailer Licenses
3-15-6: Responsibility For Acts Of Employees
3-15-7: Suspension; Revocation Of License; Fine; Costs
3-15-8: Use Of Premises After License Revocation
3-15-9: Prohibited Sales; Delivery; Signs
3-15-10: Prohibition Against Persons Under The Age Of Sixteen Selling
3-15-11: Purchase By Persons Under The Age Of Twenty-One Prohibited
3-15-12: Possession By Minors Prohibited
3-15-13: Certain Free Distributions Prohibited
3-15-14: Vending Machines - Locking Devices
3-15-15: Penalty
3-15-1: DEFINITIONS:
The following terms shall have the meanings ascribed to them below for the
purpose of this chapter:
ALTERNATIVE NICOTINE PRODUCT: A product or device not consisting of or
containing tobacco that provides for the ingestion into the body of nicotine,
whether by chewing, smoking, absorbing, dissolving, inhaling, snorting,
sniffing, or by any other means.
ELECTRONIC CIGARETTE OR E-CIGARETTE: (1) Any device that employs a battery or
other mechanism to heat a solution or substance to produce a vapor or aerosol
intended for inhalation; (2) any cartridge or container of a solution or
substance intended to be used with or in the device or to refill the device; or
(3) any solution or substance, whether or not it contains nicotine intended for
use in the device. The term "electronic cigarette" or "e-cigarette" includes
any such device, regardless of the details of the product appearance or
marketed name, generally manufactured to resemble a cigarette, cigar, pipe, or
other smoking device, including, but not limited to, vape pens, electronic
cigar, electronic cigarillos, electronic pipes, an electronic hookah, hookah
pen, personal vaporizer and any other alternative nicotine delivery systems
that may be battery operated devices designed to look like and be used in the
same manner as conventional tobacco products. The term "electronic cigarette"
or "e-cigarette" also includes the cartridges and component parts or
accessories of an electronic cigarette, individually or in any combination, as
well as nicotine or non-nicotine fluid and/or solids or other substance/
chemicals used to refill the cartridge.
ILLICIT THC PRODUCT: Any product, material, compound, isomer, acid, salt,
mixture, or preparation:
   (A)   Used or intended for human consumption;
   (B)   That is not made, manufactured, sold, given away, bartered, exchanged,
distributed, furnished, marketed, advertised, or otherwise held out for sale by
cannabis business establishments duly licensed under the Compassionate Use of
Medical Cannabis Program Act (410 ILCS 130/1, et seq.) or the Cannabis
Regulation and Tax Act (410 ILCS 705/1-1, et seq.); and
   (C)   That contains: (1) any amount of synthetic cannabinoids of any kind,
inclusive of acid forms, regardless of name, and including but not limited to
THC-O, delta-8 tetrahydrocannabinol, and delta-10 tetrahydrocannabinol, (2) a
total delta-9 tetrahydrocannabinol content concentration level in excess of
0.3% on a dry weight basis, or (3) a total delta-9 tetrahydrocannabinol content
in excess of 0.5 milligrams per labeled serving or individual unit or 2.5
milligrams per package regardless of the number of labeled servings or
individual units therein.
KRATOM: Any parts of the plant Mitragyna speciosa, whether growing or not, and
any compound, manufacture, salt, derivative, mixture, or preparation of that
plant, including but not limited to mitragynine and 7-hydroxymitragynine, as
defined in Section 5(a) of the Illinois Kratom Control Act, 720 ILCS 642/5(a),
as may be amended.
OWNER: The owner, lessor, or sublessor of any display of tobacco products,
electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products, or of any vending
machine which sells tobacco products, electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative
nicotine products. An owner can be one or more persons, a corporation, a
partnership, or any other legal entity, jointly or severally, in whom is vested
all or part of the legal title to the tobacco products, electronic cigarettes,
and/or alternative nicotine products display, or vending machine.
SYNTHETIC CANNABINOID: A cannabinoid-like compound that was produced using
chemical synthesis, chemical modification, or chemical conversion, including in
vitro biosynthesis or other bioconversion of such a method. Synthetic
cannabinoids include the conversion of cannabinoids from one chemical structure
to another, such as tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) that was produced by the
conversion of cannabidiol.
TETRAHYDROCANNABINOL OR THC: Any naturally occurring or synthetic
tetrahydrocannabinol, including its salts, isomers, and salts of isomers
whenever the existence of such salts, isomers, and salts of isomers is possible
within the specific chemical designation and any preparation, mixture, or
substance containing, or mixed or infused with, any detectable amount of
tetrahydrocannabinol or tetrahydrocannabolic acid, including, but not limited
to, delta-8-tetrahydrocannabinol, delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol, delta-10-
tetrahydrocannabinol, tetrahydrocannabolic acid, tetrahydrocannabipherol, or
hexahydrocannabinol, however derived. For the purposes of this definition,
"isomer" means the optical, position, and geometric isomers.
TOBACCO PRODUCTS: Any product containing or made from tobacco that is intended
for human consumption, whether smoked, heated, chewed, absorbed, dissolved,
inhaled, snorted, sniffed, or ingested by any other means, including, but not
limited to, cigarettes, cigars, little cigars, chewing tobacco, pipe tobacco,
snuff, snus, and any other smokeless tobacco product which contains tobacco
that is finely cut, ground, powdered, or leaf and intended to be placed in the
oral cavity. "Tobacco product" includes any component, part, or accessory of a
tobacco product, whether or not sold separately.
TOBACCO RETAILER: Any retail establishment in which the sale of tobacco
products, electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products occurs.
TOTAL DELTA-9 THC CONTENT: The value determined after the process of
decarboxylation, or the application of a conversion factor if the testing
methodology does not include decarboxylation, that expressed the potential
total delta-9 tetrahydrocannabinol content derived from the sum of the THC and
THCA content and reported on a dry weight basis, to be calculated either by
using a chromatograph technique using heat, such as gaschromatography, through
which THCA is converted from its acid form to its neutral form, or by using a
liquid chromatograph technique, which keeps the THCA intact, and using the
following conversion: [Total THC = (0.877 × THCA) + THC] which calculates the
potential total THC in a given sample.
VENDING MACHINE: Any mechanical, electric or electronic, self- service device
which, upon insertion of money, tokens or any other form of payment, dispenses
a tobacco product, electronic cigarette, and/or alternative nicotine products.
(Ords. 18-04-14, 21-08-41, 24-05-16, 25-12-36)
3-15-2: LICENSE REQUIRED:
   It shall be unlawful to sell, offer for sale, give away, deliver or keep
with the intention of selling, giving away or delivering tobacco products,
electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products within the Village
without first having obtained a tobacco retailer's license pursuant to this
chapter. Such license shall be in addition to any other licenses or permits
required by this Code.
(Ords. 16-02-08, 25-12-36)
3-15-3: LICENSE APPLICATION:
   Application for a license hereunder shall be made in writing to the Director
of Community Development or their designee, on a form to be supplied by the
Village, and shall be processed in accordance with the provisions of this Code.
   If the application is for a Class 2 Tobacco Retailer License as defined in
Section
3-15-15C of this Chapter, the applicant must attach with the application a map
showing that the business is not operating within one-hundred feet (one hundred
feet (100')) of any preexisting public or private preschool or elementary or
secondary school, day care center, playground, public library, or other
building used for educational or recreational programs for persons under the
age of eighteen (18) years.
   Effective as of July 1, 2025, as a condition to obtaining and maintaining a
Class 1 or 2 Tobacco Retailer License, licensees are required to install,
maintain, and operate a high-resolution video surveillance security system
within the licensed store premises, which shall record the activities within
all parts of the licensed store premises and shall function twenty-four (24)
hours a day and be linked to the Village's Axon Fusus security monitoring
system. Licensees shall have until December 31, 2025, to install such video
surveillance security system and link it with the Village's system. Licensees
shall post signage notifying patrons and employees that the store premises are
under video surveillance.
   Attached to each application, the applicant shall submit an affidavit
attesting that the applicant will conduct business in compliance with all
State, County, and local laws and ordinances.
(Ords. 18-04-14, 21-08-41, 25-12-36)
3-15-4: LICENSE FEE:
   A.   The annual license fees for Class 1 and Class 2 Tobacco Retailer
Licenses, effective for the licensing period beginning July 1, 2025, are set
forth in Section
3-1-1-7 of this Code.
   B.   The initial license fee for a tobacco retailer license shall be paid in
full within fourteen (14) days of the adoption of the ordinance creating the
new license and authorizing its issuance to the applicant. The initial license
shall be valid until the following June 30, unless suspended or revoked in
accordance with the provisions of this chapter.
   C.   A renewal tobacco retailer license shall be valid for a period of one
year beginning on July 1 of an applicable year and expiring on June 30 of the
following year, unless suspended or revoked in accordance with the provisions
of this chapter. The annual fee for a renewal license shall be paid in one lump
sum on or before September 30 of the applicable year.
   D.   A tobacco retailer's license is not transferrable or renewable.
   E.   A tobacco retailer's license shall be in addition to, and not in lieu
of, any automatic vending machine operator's license(s) which may be required
relative to the sale of tobacco products.
(Ords. 96-4-17, 98-9-46, 03-20-83, 21-01-07, 21-08-41, 24-05-16, 25-12-36)
3-15-5: NUMBER OF TOBACCO RETAILER LICENSES:
   A.   The number of Tobacco Retailer licenses authorized to be issued shall
be set and amended from time to time by the President and Board of Trustees and
shall be kept on record in the Office of the Village Clerk.
   B.   Class 1 Tobacco Retailer License:
      1.   Class 1 Tobacco Retailer Licenses shall be issued to grocery stores,
convenience stores, liquor stores, and gasoline stations. Class 1 Tobacco
Retailers sell products other than tobacco products, electronic cigarettes,
and/or alternative nicotine products as their primary business. Class 1 Tobacco
Retailer License authorizes the sale of cigarettes, cigars, and all other
tobacco types, as well as electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine
products. It excludes the sale of any product that contains THC, Illicit THC
Products, Kratom, or Synthetic Cannabinoids.
   C.   Class 2 Tobacco Retailer License:
      1.   Class 2 Tobacco Retailer Licenses shall be issued to any business
wishing to sell tobacco products, electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative
nicotine products, within the Village, excluding grocery stores, convenience
stores, liquor stores, and gasoline stations. Class 2 Tobacco Retailer License
authorizes the sale of cigarettes, cigars, and all other tobacco types, as well
as electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products. It excludes the
sale of any product that contains THC, Illicit THC Products, Kratom, or
Synthetic Cannabinoids. The authorized number of Class 2 Tobacco Retailer
Licenses is set to twelve (12).
   D.   Any Tobacco Retailer license which is revoked or not issued shall
automatically reduce the number of authorized licenses by the total number of
revoked or non-issued licenses. The applicant for a Tobacco Retailer license
under the provisions of this chapter must apply for the appropriate license and
pay the initial annual license fee for such license within fourteen (14) days
of authorizing its issuance to the applicant and adoption of the ordinance
increasing the number of Tobacco Retailer licenses. In the event any applicant
fails to pay the initial license fee within said fourteen (14) day period, such
license shall be revoked, and the number of authorized licenses involved is
automatically reduced by the number of revocations.
(Ords. 21-08-41, 24-05-16, 25-12-36)
3-15-6: RESPONSIBILITY FOR ACTS OF EMPLOYEES:
   Any person holding a tobacco retailer's license shall be responsible for the
acts of his/her/their/its employees, and any violation of this chapter or any
other provisions of this Code by any such employee that occurs in the course of
his/her employment shall also constitute a violation by the licensee.
(Ords. 18-04-14, 21-08-41, 25-12-36)
3-15-7: SUSPENSION; REVOCATION OF LICENSE; FINE; COSTS:
   A.   The Village President shall be charged with the administration of this
chapter. The Village President may suspend or revoke any license issued under
the provisions of this chapter if he determines that the licensee has violated
any of the provisions of this chapter. In lieu of, or in addition to, the
suspension or revocation of a license, the Village President may levy a fine on
the licensee as defined in section
3-15-14 of this chapter.
   B.   No such license shall be suspended or revoked and no licensee shall be
fined except after a hearing before the Village President, or his designee,
with at least seven (7) days’ prior written notice to the licensee, affording
the licensee an opportunity to appear and defend against the charges contained
in such notice. Notice of said hearing shall be given by certified mail, return
receipt requested, or by personal service.
   C.   The Village President shall within seven (7) days after such hearing,
if he determines after such hearing that the license should be revoked or
suspended, and/or that the licensee should be fined, state the reasons for such
determination in a written order along with the amount of the fine, the period
of suspension and/or that the license has been revoked. A copy of such order
shall be served upon the licensee, in the same manner as required for the
notice of the hearing, within seven (7) days of its issuance.
   D.   Any licensee determined by the Village President to have violated any
of the provisions of this chapter shall pay to the Village the costs of the
hearing before the Village President on such violation. Said costs shall
include, but not be limited to, court reporter fees, the costs of the
transcripts or the recording of the hearing, attorney fees, the cost of
preparing and mailing notices and orders, and all other miscellaneous expenses
incurred by the Village in regard to the hearing. The licensee shall pay said
costs to the Village within thirty (30) days of notification of the amount of
the costs by the Village President. Notification shall be sent in the same
manner as required for the notice of the hearing. Failure to pay said costs
within thirty (30) days of notification shall constitute a violation of this
chapter and may be cause for suspension or revocation of the license, and/or
the levy of a fine. (Ords. 18-04-14, 21-08-41)
3-15-8: USE OF PREMISES AFTER LICENSE REVOCATION:
   When any license issued under this chapter is revoked, no tobacco retailer
license shall be granted to said licensee for a period of six (6) months
thereafter for the premises described in such revoked license.
(Ords. 96-4-17, 04-27-21, 25-12-36)
3-15-9: PROHIBITED SALES; DELIVERY; SIGNS:
   A.   It shall be unlawful for any person to sell, offer for sale, give away
or deliver any tobacco products, electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative
nicotine products, to any person under twenty-one (21) years of age. Before
selling, offering for sale, giving, or furnishing a tobacco product, electronic
cigarette, or alternative nicotine product to another person, the person
selling, offering for sale, giving, or furnishing the tobacco product,
electronic cigarette, or alternative nicotine product shall verify that the
person is at least twenty one (21) years of age by examining from any person
that appears to be under thirty (30) years of age a government-issued
photographic identification that establishes the person to be twenty one (21)
years of age or older.
   B.   It shall be unlawful for any licensee to sell, offer for sale, give
away, possess, deliver or keep with the intention of selling, giving away or
delilvering any tobacco product, electronic cigarette or e-cigarette, and/or
alternative nicotine products, which is in violation of any State, County or
local law.
   C.   It shall be unlawful to sell cigarettes not in the manufacturer-sealed
packs. Single or loose cigarette sales are prohibited.
   D.   It shall be unlawful for any person to sell, offer for sale, give away
or deliver any tobacco products, electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative
nicotine products that contain THC, Illicit THC Products, Kratom, or Synthetic
Cannabinoids. This includes but is not limited to any product: (1) intended for
human consumption by any means, including but not limited to, oral ingestion,
inhalation, smoking, or topical absorption that (2) contains naturally
occurring or synthetic tetrahydrocannabinol, including its salts, isomers, and
salts of isomers whenever the existence of such salts, isomers, and salts of
isomers is possible within the specific chemical designation and any
preparation, mixture, or substance containing, or mixed or infused with, any
detectable amount of tetrahydrocannabinol or tetrahydrocannabolic acid,
including, but not limited to, delta-8-tetrahydrocannabinol, delta-9-
tetrahydrocannabinol, delta-10-tetrahydrocannabinol, tetrahydrocannabolic acid,
tetrahydrocannabipherol, or hexahydrocannabinol, however derived, or any other
substance determined to have similar intoxicating effects on the mind or body.
For the purposes of this definition, "isomer" means the optical, position, and
geometric isomers.
   E.   Signs informing the public of the age restrictions provided for herein
shall be posted at or near every display of tobacco products, electronic
cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products, and on or upon every vending
machine which offers tobacco products, electronic cigarettes, and/or
alternative nicotine products for sale by the owner or operator of such display
of tobacco products, electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine
products, or vending machine, or said owner's or operator's agent, employee, or
independent contractor. Each such sign shall be plainly visible and shall state
in letters at least one inch (1") in height as follows:
      THE SALE OF TOBACCO PRODUCTS, ELECTRONIC CIGARETTES, AND ALTERNATIVE
NICOTINE PRODUCTS TO PERSONS UNDER TWENTY-ONE YEARS OF AGE IS PROHIBITED BY LAW
   F.   Every act or omission regardless of its nature, constituting a
violation of any of the provisions of this section by any owner, operator, or
the agent or employee of such owner and/or operator shall be deemed and held to
be the act of the owner and/or operator; and such owner and/or operator shall
be punished in the same manner as if such act or omission had been done or
omitted by the owner and/or operator personally.
(Ords. 18-04-14, 21-08-41, 24-05-16, 25-12-36)
3-15-10: PROHIBITION AGAINST PERSONS UNDER THE AGE OF SIXTEEN SELLING:
It shall be unlawful for any person to engage, employ or permit a minor person
under sixteen (16) years of age to sell tobacco products electronic cigarettes,
and/or alternative nicotine products. (Ords. 16-02-08, 21-08-41, 24-05-16)
3-15-11: PURCHASE BY PERSONS UNDER THE AGE OF TWENTY-ONE PROHIBITED:
   It shall be unlawful for any minor person under twenty-one (21) years of age
to purchase any tobacco products, electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative
nicotine products, or to misrepresent his/her identity or age, or to use any
false or altered identification for the purpose of purchasing tobacco products,
electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products.
(Ords. 16-02-08, 21-08-41, 24-05-16, 25-12-36)
3-15-12: POSSESSION BY PERSONS UNDER TWENTY-ONE PROHIBITED:
   It shall be unlawful for any person under twenty-one (21) years of age to
possess any tobacco products, electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative
nicotine products.
(Ords. 16-02-08, 21-08-41, 24-05-16, 25-12-36)
3-15-13: CERTAIN FREE DISTRIBUTIONS PROHIBITED:
   It shall be unlawful for any person in the business of selling or otherwise
distributing, promoting or advertising tobacco products, electronic cigarettes,
and/or alternative nicotine products to give away or deliver tobacco products,
electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products free of charge to
any person on any right-of-way, sidewalk, park, playground or other property
owned by the Village, any school district, the Oak Lawn Park District or the
Oak Lawn Public Library.
(Ords. 16-02-08, 21-08-41, 25-12-36)
3-15-14: VENDING MACHINES - LOCKING DEVICES:
   A.   It shall be unlawful to sell, offer for sale, give away, deliver, or
keep with the intention of selling, giving away, or delivering any tobacco
products, electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products by use of
a vending machine, unless such vending machine is equipped with a manual,
electric or electronic locking device controlled by the owner or operator of
the establishment upon which the vending machine is located, or the agent or
employee of such owner or operator, so as to prevent its operation by any
person under twenty-one (21) years of age.
   B.   Any premises where access by any person under twenty-one (21) years of
age is prohibited by law, or premises where the public is generally not
permitted and where vending machines are strictly for the use of employees of
the business located on such premises, shall be exempt from the requirements of
subsection A of this section.
   C.   Vending machines used for the sale of tobacco products, electronic
cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products, and which are required to
have a locking device shall only be used to dispense tobacco products,
electronic cigarettes, and/or alternative nicotine products.
(Ords. 16-02-08, 21-08-41, 24-05-16, 25-12-36)
3-15-15: PENALTY:
   A.   Any person under twenty-one (21) years of age who is found to be in
violation of section
3-15-11 or
3-15-12 of this chapter shall be subject to the following penalties:
      1.   First violation within a twelve (12) month period: A fine of fifty
dollars ($50.00).
      2.   Second violation within a twelve (12) month period: A fine of not
less than fifty dollars ($50.00) nor more than one hundred dollars ($100.00).
      3.   Third violation within a twelve (12) month period: A fine of not
less than one hundred dollars ($100.00) nor more than five hundred dollars
($500.00).
      4.   Fourth or further violation within a twelve (12) month period: A
fine of not less than five hundred dollars ($500.00) nor more than seven
hundred fifty dollars ($750.00).
   B.   Any person who is found to be in violation of any section of this
chapter other than section
3-15-11 or
3-15-12 of this chapter shall be subject to the following penalties:
      1.   First violation within a twelve (12) month period: A fine of not
less than one thousand dollars ($1,000.00) nor more than two thousand dollars
($2,000.00). Said fine may be in addition to the suspension or revocation of
any tobacco retailer's license held by said person.
      2.   Second violation within a twelve (12) month period: A fine of not
less than two thousand dollars ($2,000.00) nor more than three thousand dollars
($3,000.00). Said fine may be in addition to the suspension or revocation of
any tobacco retailer's license held by said person.
      3.   Third violation within a twelve (12) month period: A fine of three
thousand dollars ($3,000.00) nor more than four thousand dollars ($4,000.00)
and the automatic revocation of any tobacco retailer's license held by said
person.
      4.   Fourth or further violation within a twelve (12) month period: A
fine of four thousand dollars ($4,000.00) nor more than five thousand dollars
($5,000.00) and a minimum of thirty (30) hours of community service. (Ords. 18-
18-70, 21-08-41, 24-05-16)
CHAPTER 16
SURCHARGE FOR EMERGENCY 911 TELEPHONE SYSTEM
SECTION:
3-16-1: Surcharge Imposed
3-16-2: Referendum Authorized
3-16-3: Definitions
3-16-4: Exempt Network Connections
3-16-5: Effective Date Of Surcharge
3-16-6: Remittance
3-16-7: Written Report Of Remittance Required
3-16-8: Erroneous Payment
3-16-1: SURCHARGE IMPOSED:
A surcharge is hereby imposed, subject to the provisions of Section
3-16-2 of this Chapter, upon all billed subscribers of network connections
provided by telecommunications carriers engaged in the business of transmitting
messages by means of electricity originating within the corporate limits of the
Village for funding of the maintenance and improvement of the Village's "911"
emergency telephone system. (Ord. 91-19-85)
3-16-2: REFERENDUM AUTHORIZED:
   A.   A referendum shall be placed by the County Clerk of Cook County on the
March 17, 1992, ballot for all legal voters residing in the Village to vote
upon the following question:
 
Shall the Village of Oak Lawn impose a surcharge of up to one and no/     Y 
100 dollars ($1.00) per month per network connection, which surcharge
expense will be added to the monthly bill you receive for telephone or    N 
telecommunications charges, for the purpose of main- taining and
improving a 9-1-1 Emergency Telephone System?
 
   B.   If a majority of the votes cast upon the question are in favor thereof,
a surcharge is hereby imposed at a rate of one dollar ($1.00) per month per in-
service network connection, as hereinafter defined. A network connection shall
not be deemed to be in service where a subscriber's account is deemed
uncollectible by the telecommunications carrier. (Ord. 91-19-85)
3-16-3: DEFINITIONS:
For purpose of this Chapter the following definitions shall apply:
NETWORK CONNECTIONS: The number of voice grade communications channels directly
between a subscriber and a telecommunications carrier's public switched network
without the intervention of any other telecommunications carriers' switched
network which would be required to carry the subscriber's interpremises
traffic.
TELECOMMUNICATIONS CARRIER: Any natural individual, firm, trust, estate,
partnership, association, joint stock company, joint adventure, corporation,
municipal corporation or political subdivision of this State, or a receiver,
trustee, conservator or other representative appointed by order of any court
engaged in the business of transmitting messages by means of electricity.
TRANSMITTING MESSAGES: Shall have the meaning ascribed to the term in section
8-11-2 of the Illinois Municipal Code 1 . (Ord. 91-19-85)
 
Notes
1 1. 65 ILCS 5/8-11-2.
3-16-4: EXEMPT NETWORK CONNECTIONS:
The Village Clerk shall provide any telecommunications carrier subject to the
surcharge with a certified list of those network connections assigned to the
Village to be exempt from imposition of the surcharge. The certified list may
be revised by the Village on sixty (60) days' prior written notice provided to
the telecommunications carriers. (Ord. 91-19-85)
3-16-5: EFFECTIVE DATE OF SURCHARGE:
The surcharge shall be imposed on the first day of the month following the
expiration of thirty (30) days from the date the Village Clerk certifies to any
of the telecommunications carriers who are subject to the surcharge that the
referendum referred to in Section
3-16-2 of this Chapter has passed. (Ord. 91-19-85)
3-16-6: REMITTANCE:
Every telecommunications carrier shall remit to the Village the amount of
surcharge collected for each calendar month within thirty (30) days following
expiration of any month to which the surcharge applies, net of any network or
other "911" or sophisticated "911" system charge then due the particular
telecommunications carrier as shown on an itemized bill. The telecommunications
carrier collecting the surcharge shall be entitled to deduct three percent (3%)
of the gross amount of the surcharge collected to reimburse the
telecommunications carrier for the expense of accounting and collecting the
surcharge. (Ord. 91-19-85)
3-16-7: WRITTEN REPORT OF REMITTANCE REQUIRED:
Simultaneously with the remittance subscribed in Section
3-16-6 of this Chapter, each telecommunications carrier shall make a written
report to the Village Treasurer and Director of Administrative Services for the
period for which the remittance applies stating as follows:
   A.   The name of the telecommunications carrier.
   B.   The telecommunications carrier's principal place of business.
   C.   The number of network connections to which the surcharge applies.
   D.   The amount of surcharge due.
   E.   Such other reasonable and related information as the corporate
authorities may from time to time require. (Ord. 91-19-85)
3-16-8: ERRONEOUS PAYMENT:
If it shall appear that an amount of surcharge has been paid which was not due
under the provisions of this Chapter, whether as the result of a mistake of
fact or an error of law, then such amount shall be credited against any
surcharge due, or to become due, under this Chapter from the telecommunications
carrier who made the erroneous payments; provided, that no amounts erroneously
paid more than three (3) years prior to the filing of a claim therefor shall be
so credited. (Ord. 91-19-85)
CHAPTER 17
MUNICIPAL TELECOMMUNICATIONS TAX
(Rep. by Ord. 03-20-85)
CHAPTER 18
LOCAL GOVERNMENT TAXPAYERS' BILL OF RIGHTS ACT
SECTION:
3-18-1: Definitions
3-18-2: Application Of Payments
3-18-3: Interest On Overdue Payments
3-18-4: Determination Of Tax Liability
3-18-5: Appeals
3-18-6: Credits And Refunds
3-18-7: Voluntary Disclosure
3-18-8: Liens
3-18-9: Penalties
3-18-1: DEFINITIONS:
As used in this chapter, the following terms shall have the following meanings:
CODE: The Oak Lawn village code.
TAX: Any pecuniary burden imposed by the village on individuals or entities to
support village government that is not a permit or other fee (excepting the
infrastructure maintenance fee), is not collected or administered by a
department or agency of the state of Illinois, and is not a tax on real
property under the Illinois property tax code 1 , including, but not limited
to, any of the following taxes that may now, or in the future be collected or
administered by the village:
   A.   Hotel and motel occupancy tax - See section
3-2-6 of this title;
   B.   Real estate transfer tax - See section
3-2-7 of this title;
   C.   Municipal telecommunications tax - See chapter 17 of this title;
   D.   Municipal electric utility tax - See chapter 21 of this title;
   E.   Municipal utility tax - See chapter 22 of this title; and
   F.   Municipal gas use tax - See chapter 23 of this title.
TAX ADMINISTRATOR: The village manager of the village or the village manager's
designee. (Ords. 00-21-72, 01-15-55, 03-20-89)
 
Notes
1 1. 35 ILCS 200/1-1 et seq.
3-18-2: APPLICATION OF PAYMENTS:
Notwithstanding any provision in this code to the contrary, any payment
designated by a taxpayer as payment of a particular tax liability shall be
applied to that taxpayer's liabilities pertaining to the particular tax as
follows: a) to the interest accrued on outstanding tax liabilities; b) to the
outstanding tax liability; c) to any penalties due on outstanding tax
liabilities; and d) to current tax liabilities. (Ord. 00-21-72)
3-18-3: INTEREST ON OVERDUE PAYMENTS:
Notwithstanding any provision in this code to the contrary, any tax not paid on
the date due shall accrue interest at the rate of one percent (1%) of the
amount due for each month or part of a month the tax remains unpaid. (Ords. 00-
21-72, 01-03-11)
3-18-4: DETERMINATION OF TAX LIABILITY:
To collect overdue taxes, a determination of tax liability notice shall be sent
to the taxpayer by registered or certified mail no more than four (4) years
after the end of the calendar year when the tax was due; however, if less than
seventy five percent (75%) of the tax due was paid, a determination of tax
liability notice may be sent no more than six (6) years after the end of the
calendar year when the tax was due. All determination of tax liability notices
shall include the following information:
   A.   The reason for the tax liability;
   B.   The amount of tax, interest and penalty due on the date of the
determination and information on how interest and penalties accrue;
   C.   The procedure for appeal; and
   D.   Information regarding the right of the village to audit the taxpayer's
books and records for a particular period and the village's obligation to
refund overpayments. (Ord. 00-21-72)
3-18-5: APPEALS:
Any taxpayer may, within forty five (45) days of the date of receipt of a
determination of tax liability notice or at any time before a current tax
becomes due, seek a hearing before the tax administrator to appeal the tax
liability. For reasonable cause shown in writing by the taxpayer and full
payment of the amount of tax protested plus accrued interest due, the tax
administrator shall extend the forty five (45) day period for appeal after a
determination has been issued for up to an additional forty five (45) days.
(Ord. 00-21-72)
3-18-6: CREDITS AND REFUNDS:
Notwithstanding any provision in this code to the contrary, taxpayers may, in
writing, claim a credit or refund for payment of taxes, interest or penalties
paid in error for up to four (4) years after the end of the calendar year in
which the payment was made. Claims must include dates, amount paid, and an
explanation of the error that occurred. The tax administrator shall determine
whether a credit or refund is justified and shall respond to the taxpayer in
writing within thirty (30) days of receipt of the claim. Tax amounts paid in
error shall be returned plus interest at the annual rate of five percent (5%).
(Ord. 00-21-72)
3-18-7: VOLUNTARY DISCLOSURE:
A taxpayer who has not received a written notice of an audit or determination
of tax liability may seek disclosure of tax liability by applying to the tax
administrator in writing for voluntary disclosure of the taxpayer's liability
pertaining to a particular tax and agreeing to pay, within ninety (90) days of
the date of the application, the tax due plus one percent (1%) interest per
month for all periods prior to filing the application, but not more than four
(4) years prior thereto. The taxpayer shall be liable for any underpaid taxes
and accrued interest during the four (4) year time period but, except for the
taxes and interest due under this section, the taxpayer shall not be liable for
any taxes imposed prior to the date of the application for voluntary
disclosure. (Ord. 00-21-72)
3-18-8: LIENS:
In the first month of each calendar year, the tax administrator shall cause all
liens against taxpayers to be reviewed. The village shall, in regard to liens
found to be improper, at its sole expense, release said liens, correct the
taxpayer's credit record and correct any public disclosure of said liens. (Ord.
00-21-72)
3-18-9: PENALTIES:
   A.   A penalty of five percent (5%) of the amount of tax due shall be
charged against any taxpayer whose tax payment is not made on or before the
date the tax is due.
   B.   A penalty of five percent (5%) of the amount of tax due shall be
charged against any taxpayer whose tax report or return is not filed on or
before the date said tax report or return is due.
   C.   A penalty of twenty five percent (25%) of the amount of tax due for the
period in relation to which a tax report or return is required to be filed
shall be charged against any taxpayer who fails to file, before the date that a
determination of tax liability notice is issued, a report or return for that
period.
   D.   The penalties set forth in subsections A and B of this section shall
not apply in the event the village imposes the penalty set forth in subsection
C of this section.
   E.   Any penalty may be abated by the tax administrator if reasonable cause
for nonpayment or failure to file is shown. (Ord. 00-21-72)
CHAPTER 19
SPECIAL SERVICE AREA NUMBER 2001-1 SALES TAX
SECTION:
3-19-1: Definitions
3-19-2: Tax Imposed
3-19-3: Exemptions
3-19-4: Exclusions
3-19-5: Passing On The Tax
3-19-6: Collection
3-19-7: Filing Returns And Remittance
3-19-8: Registration
3-19-9: Administration And Maintenance
3-19-10: Failure To Pay Tax
3-19-11: Resale Numbers
3-19-12: Severability
3-19-13: Penalty
3-19-1: DEFINITIONS:
   A.   For purposes of this chapter the following definitions shall apply
unless the context clearly indicates or requires a different meaning:
   PERSON: Any natural individual, firm, partnership, association, joint stock
company, joint venture, public or private corporation, limited liability
company, or a receiver, executor, trustee, guardian or other representative
appointed by order of any court.
   RETAILER: Means and includes every "person" engaged in the business of
making "sales at retail".
   SERVICEMAN: Any "person" who is engaged in the occupation of making "sales
of service".
   SPECIAL SERVICE AREA: The special service area established pursuant to
ordinance 01-21-76 adopted December 11, 2001, and legally described as the
following:
      That part of the Southeast 1/4 of the Southeast 1/4 of Section 3,
Township 37 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, bounded and
described as follows: Beginning at the point of intersection of the South line
of the North 468.00 feet of the Southeast 1/4 of the Southeast 1/4 of said
Section 3 with the West line of Crawford Avenue per document No. 19207514 being
a line 50.00 feet West of and parallel with the East line of the Southeast 1/
4 of said Section; thence South 00 degrees 14 minutes 10 seconds East along
said West line of Crawford Avenue 747.288 feet to the point of tangency with
the arc of a circle of 75.00 feet radius convex southeasterly whose chord bears
South 44 degrees 52 minutes 55 seconds West and whose tangents are the
aforesaid West line of Crawford Avenue and the North line of 95th Street per
easement document No. 10858729 being a line 50.00 feet North of and parallel
with the South line of the Southeast 1/4 of said Section; thence Southwesterly
118.12 feet along the aforesaid arc to the point of tangency with the aforesaid
North line of 95th Street; thence West along said North line 868.69 feet to the
West line of the East 994.00 feet of the Southeast 1/4 of the Southeast 1/4 of
said Section; thence North 00 degrees 14 minutes 10 seconds West along said
West line 400.00 feet to the North line of the South 450.00 feet of the
Southeast 1/4 of the Southeast 1/4 of said section; thence West along said
North line 92.075 feet to the East line of the West 241.00 feet of the
Southeast 1/4 of the Southeast 1/4 of said Section; thence North 00 degrees 12
minutes 27 seconds West along said East line 220.00 feet; thence North 44
degrees 37 minutes 06 seconds East 70.926 feet to the East line of the West
291.00 feet of the Southeast 1/4 of the Southeast 1/4 of said Section; thence
North 00 degrees 12 minutes 27 seconds West along said East line 150.00 feet to
the South line of the aforesaid North 468.00 feet of the Southeast 1/4 of the
Southeast 1/4 of said section; thence North 89 degrees 52 minutes 39 seconds
East along said South line 985.859 feet to the hereinabove designated point of
beginning, all in Cook County, Illinois;
      P.I.N.'s 24-04-400-018, -023, -024 and -025 (or such other permanent tax
index numbers that may be assigned to said legal description after a
resubdivision thereof);
      Commonly known as the Northwest corner of 95th Street and Pulaski Road.
   TAXPAYER: A "retailer" or "serviceman" who is required to pay either of the
taxes imposed by this chapter.
   VILLAGE: The village of Oak Lawn, Cook County, Illinois.
   B.   Any word or term used in this chapter, that is not specifically defined
in this section, including, but not limited to, those words and terms set forth
in this chapter in quotation marks (which are defined in either the Illinois
service occupation tax act and the Illinois retailers' occupation tax act),
shall be given the same meaning as given to those words or terms in the
Illinois service occupation tax act 1  and the Illinois retailers' occupation
tax act 2 . (Ord. 02-13-40)
 
Notes
1 1. 35 ILCS 115/1 et seq.
2 2. 35 ILCS 120/1 et seq.
3-19-2: TAX IMPOSED:
   A.   A tax is imposed upon all "persons" engaged in the business of making
sales of service (referred to as "servicemen") on all tangible personal
property "transferred" as an incident of a "sale of service", including
"computer software", and including photographs, negatives, and positives that
are the product of "photoprocessing", but not including products of
"photoprocessing" produced for use in motion pictures for public commercial
exhibition, within the "special service area". Unless otherwise provided in
this chapter, the tax imposed by this subsection is at the rate of one-half of
one percent (0.5%) of the "selling price" from the sales of tangible personal
property made in the course of business within the "special service area".
For the purpose of computing this tax, in no event shall the "selling price" be
less than the "cost price" to the "serviceman" of the tangible personal
property "transferred". The "selling price" of each item of tangible personal
property "transferred" as an incident of a "sale of service" may be shown as a
distinct and separate item on the "serviceman's" billing to the service
customer. If the "selling price" is not so shown, the "selling price" of the
tangible personal property is deemed to be fifty percent (50%) of the
"serviceman's" entire billing to the service customer. When, however, a
"serviceman" contracts to design, develop, and produce special order machinery
or equipment, the tax imposed by this subsection shall be based on the
"serviceman's cost price" of the tangible personal property "transferred"
incident to the completion of the contract.
At the election of any registered "serviceman" made for each fiscal year,
"sales of service" in which the aggregate annual "cost price" of tangible
personal property "transferred" as an incident to the "sales of service" is
less than thirty five percent (35%), or seventy five percent (75%) in the case
of "servicemen transferring" prescription drugs or "servicemen" engaged in
"graphic arts production", of the aggregate annual total "gross receipts" from
all "sales of service", the tax imposed by this subsection shall be based on
the "serviceman's cost price" of the tangible personal property "transferred"
incident to the sale of those services.
With respect to gasohol, as defined in the use tax act, 35 Illinois Compiled
Statutes 105/1, the tax imposed by this subsection shall apply to seventy
percent (70%) of the cost price of property transferred as an incident to the
sale of service on or after January 1, 1990, and before July 1, 2003, and to
one hundred percent (100%) of the cost price thereafter.
The tax shall be imposed at the rate of one-half of one percent (0.5%) on food
prepared for immediate consumption and transferred incident to a sale of
service subject to this chapter or the Illinois service occupation tax act, 35
Illinois Compiled Statutes 115/1, by an entity licensed under the hospital
licensing act, 210 Illinois Compiled Statutes 85/1, the nursing home care act,
210 Illinois Compiled Statutes 45/1-101, or the child care act of 1969, 225
Illinois Compiled Statutes 10/1.
For purposes of this chapter, prepaid telephone calling arrangements shall be
considered tangible personal property subject to the tax imposed under this
subsection regardless of the form in which those arrangements may be embodied,
transmitted, or fixed by any method now known or hereafter developed.
Evidence that tangible personal property was sold by any "supplier" for
delivery to a "person" residing or engaged in business in the "special service
area" shall be prima facie evidence that such tangible personal property was
sold for the purpose of resale as an incident to a "sale of service" taxable
under this subsection.
   B.   A tax is imposed upon "persons" engaged in the business of selling at
retail tangible personal property (referred to as "retailers"), including
"computer software", and including photographs, negatives, and positives that
are the product of "photoprocessing", but not including products of
"photoprocessing" produced for use in motion pictures for public commercial
exhibition, within the "special service area". Unless otherwise provided in
this chapter, the tax imposed by this subsection is at the rate of one-half of
one percent (0.5%) of "gross receipts" from the "sales at retail" of personal
property made in the course of business within the "special service area".
With respect to gasohol, as defined in the use tax act, 35 Illinois Compiled
Statutes 105/1, the tax imposed by this subsection shall apply to seventy
percent (70%) of the proceeds of sales made on or after January 1, 1990, and
before July 1, 2003, and to one hundred percent (100%) of the cost price
thereafter.
For purposes of this chapter, prepaid telephone calling arrangements shall be
considered tangible personal property subject to the tax imposed under this
subsection regardless of the form in which those arrangements may be embodied,
transmitted, or fixed by any method now known or hereafter developed.
   C.   Except as provided in section
3-19-3 of this chapter, the taxes imposed by this section shall be imposed on
the same basis as the taxes imposed by the Illinois service occupation tax act
1  and the Illinois retailers' occupation tax act 2 .
   D.   The taxes imposed by this section are not imposed on any business,
activity, act or privilege to the extent that such business, activity, act or
privilege may not, under the constitutions or statutes of the United States or
the state of Illinois, be made the subject of taxation by the "village".
   E.   The taxes imposed by this section are imposed in addition to all other
taxes imposed by the "village", the state of Illinois or any other municipal
corporation or political subdivision of the state of Illinois. (Ord. 02-13-40)
 
Notes
1 1. 35 ILCS 115/1 et seq.
2 2. 35 ILCS 120/1 et seq.
3-19-3: EXEMPTIONS:
   A.   Exempt from the tax imposed by subsection
3-19-2A of this chapter shall be those items of tangible personal property that
are exempt from taxation under the Illinois service occupation tax act 1 . To
the extent a specific item of tangible personal property described in the
Illinois service occupation tax act 2  is not defined in this chapter, such
specific item shall be given the same meaning under this chapter as given to
that specific item under the Illinois service occupation tax act 3 . In
addition, tangible personal property "transferred" incident to the completion
of a maintenance agreement is exempt from the tax imposed by subsection
3-19-2 A of this chapter.
   B.   Exempt from the tax imposed by subsection
3-19-2B of this chapter shall be the "gross receipts" from proceeds from the
sale of those items of tangible personal property that are exempt from taxation
under the Illinois retailers' occupation tax act 4 . To the extent a specific
item of tangible personal property described in the Illinois retailers'
occupation tax act 5  is not defined in this chapter, such specific item shall
be given the same meaning under this chapter as given to that specific item
under the Illinois retailers' occupation tax act 6 .
   C.   Exempt from the tax imposed by subsection
3-19-2A of this chapter are those items of tangible personal property that are
exempt from taxation under the home rule municipal service occupation tax act 7
. To the extent a specific item of tangible personal property described in
section
8-11-5 of the home rule municipal service occupation tax act 8  is not defined
in this chapter, such specific item shall be given the same meaning under this
chapter as given to that specific item under the home rule municipal service
occupation tax act 9 .
   D.   Exempt from the tax imposed by subsection
3-19-2B of this chapter are those items of tangible personal property that are
exempt from taxation under the home rule municipal retailers' occupation tax
act 1 . To the extent a specific item of tangible personal property described
in section
8-11-1 of the home rule municipal retailers' occupation tax act 2  is not
defined in this chapter, such specific item shall be given the same meaning
under this chapter as given to that specific item under the home rule municipal
retailers' occupation tax act 3 . (Ord. 02-13-40)
 
