(Ords. 79-2-5, 81-36-78, 92-11-45, 93-9-24, 19-16-66)
5-2-4: PLATS OF CONSOLIDATION:
   A.   A "plat of consolidation" is defined as a plat which combines one or
more existing lots of record, in whole or in part, into a single lot of record.
   B.   Notwithstanding any other provision of this Chapter which would require
the Zoning and Planning Commission and/or the Village Board to take action in
regard to a proposed plat of subdivision, a plat of consolidation may be
approved administratively by the Director of Community Development and Growth
Management, without need to bring said plat of consolidation before the Zoning
and Planning Commission or the Village Board, provided the lot created by said
plat of consolidation is in full compliance with Section
5-3-3-2 of this Title. (Ords. 98-19-86, 19-16-66)
CHAPTER 3
DESIGN STANDARDS
SECTION:
5-3-1: Conformance To Neighborhood Plan
5-3-2: Streets And Alleys
5-3-3: Lots And Blocks
5-3-3-1: Grading For Drainage
5-3-3-2: Lots
5-3-3-3: Blocks
5-3-4: Reserve Strips
5-3-1: CONFORMANCE TO NEIGHBORHOOD PLAN:
If a suggested plan for the neighborhood or area in which the tract to be
subdivided is located has been made by the Zoning and Planning Commission, the
street layout of the subdivision shall be in general conformance thereto.
(Ords. 79-2-5, 81-36-78, 92-11-45, 93-9-24, 19-16-66)
5-3-2: STREETS AND ALLEYS:
   A.   Street System: The street system shall relate functionally to the
existing streets in the area adjoining the proposed subdivision.
The street layout shall be devised for the most advantageous development of the
entire neighborhood area. Where necessary to the neighborhood pattern, existing
principal streets in adjoining subdivisions shall be continued and shall be at
least as wide as such existing streets and in alignment with them. The street
layout shall provide for the future projection of the principal streets into
unsubdivided lands adjoining.
   B.   Center Lines: The center lines of all streets wherever practicable,
shall be the continuation of the center lines of existing streets, or shall be
offset at least one hundred feet (100').
   C.   Major Streets: Wherever a subdivision includes a major street, as shown
on the Major Street Plan, such major street shall be platted in the location
and of the width indicated on the Major Street Plan.
   D.   Collector Streets: Collector streets shall be included when it is
apparent that sufficient traffic will flow from the minor streets within the
subdivision, and that such traffic will need to be channeled in control of the
number of access points to the major street.
   E.   Minor Streets: Minor streets shall be so laid out that their use by
through traffic will be discouraged.
   F.   Minor And Major Street Intersections: There shall be a minimum number
of intersections of minor streets with major streets.
   G.   Marginal Access Streets: Where a subdivision has frontage on a major
street, as shown on the Major Street Plan, or on a super highway or other high
volume trafficway, and where it is not feasible to have the lots back to such
high volume streets, provision shall be made for marginal access streets. These
marginal access streets shall be approximately parallel to and on each side of
the major street right of way and separated from such right of way by a strip
of land at least ten feet (10') wide. Separation strips may be provided with
screen planting, but such planting shall be so placed that it does not obstruct
vision at intersections.
   H.   Streets Adjacent to Railroads, Waterways or Major Streets: Where a
subdivision borders on or contains a railroad right of way, waterway or major
street, the Zoning and Planning Commission may require a street approximately
parallel to and on each side of such right of way or major street, at a
distance suitable for appropriate use of the intervening land. Such distance
shall also be determined with due regard for the requirements of approach
grades and future grade separations.
   I.   Alleys: Paved rear alleys not less than twenty feet (20') wide will be
required in all business and industrial districts, except where special
conditions make alleys impracticable; in such cases, adequate off-street
loading space, suitably surfaced, shall be provided. Dead-end alleys shall not
be allowed.
   J.   Half-Streets: Half-streets shall not be permitted.
   K.   Cul-De-Sacs: Cul-de-sacs shall not exceed four hundred feet (400') in
length as measured along the center line from the nearest right of way line of
the intersecting street to the center point of the cul-de-sac, and shall have a
turnaround not less than one hundred twenty feet (120') in diameter of right of
way, and pavement diameter of ninety feet (90') at the closed end.
   L.   Grades: Street grades shall conform in general to the terrain and shall
be not less than one-half of one percent (0.5%) nor more than five percent
(5%). Street grades shall be such as to provide natural surface drainage of
storm water regardless of the presence or absence of storm sewers. The intent
is to avoid depressions or inverts that will flood in flash storms for which
storm sewers are inadequate.
   M.   Right of Way Widths: The minimum right of way widths for streets,
alleys and crosswalkways shall be as follows:
 Feet
                                                                      Feet
Major streets including section line streets                            
(Except in cases where a greater or lesser width is shown on the major 100
street plan)
Half section line streets                                              80
Minor street                                                           66
Cul-de-sac (see also subsection K above)                               66
Marginal access street (without intersecting streets)                  40
Commercial alley                                                       20
Crosswalkway (when required)                                           10
 
   N.   Roadway Widths: The following roadway widths from outside to outside of
curbs are required:
 Feet
                                                     Feet
Major street                                          Variable
Collector street                                      40
Minor street                                          32
Cul-de-sac (see also subsection K above)              32
Marginal access street (without intersecting streets) 28
Alley (in business or industrial district)            20
Crosswalkway                                          8
Sidewalk                                              5
 
   O.   Minimum Curvatures: The minimum of curvature of streets on the center
line shall be:
 
                Feet
Major street     600
Collector street 300
Minor street     100
 
   P.   Tangent Between Reversed Curves: Between reversed curves on all
streets, there shall be a tangent at least one hundred feet (100') long.
   Q.   Angle of Intersections: Street intersections shall be at right angles
or nearly so. Where, because of topographic or other reasons, an intersection
cannot be at right angles, such intersections shall be so designed as to be in
accordance with good engineering practices.
   R.   Curb Radii: Curb radii shall not be less than twenty feet (20'). Where
an angle of intersection less than sixty degrees (60") is permitted, curb radii
shall be increased as necessary for safety.
   S.   Future Streets and Subdivisions: If a tract is subdivided into larger
parcels than ordinary building lots (see subsection
5-3-3-2A of this Chapter), such parcels shall be arranged to allow the opening
of future streets and logical future subdivisions. (Ords. 79-2-5, 81-36-78, 92-
11-45, 93-9-24, 19-16-66)
5-3-3: LOTS AND BLOCKS:
5-3-3-1: GRADING FOR DRAINAGE:
Lots and blocks shall be so graded as to eliminate depressions that would
accumulate storm water. Grades at building sites shall bear such relationships
to roadway and curb grades as to prevent flooding of basement windows or of
entryways, either to basements or to first-floor levels in the absence of
basements. Where deemed necessary, the Director of Office of Quality Control
may require the subdivider to submit working drawings showing contours to which
the property is to be graded in order to provide for the requirements of this
Section. In such cases, building permits shall not be issued unless the lot
plan indicates site and building grades consistent with the grading drawing.
(Ords. 79-2-5, 81-12-19)
5-3-3-2: LOTS:
   A.   Minimum Width and Depth: Recognizing the character of the various
neighborhoods within the Village and the need for flexibility in the
classification of lot sizes and areas, the minimum lot width and depth
requirement for lots to be subdivided under the requirements of this title
shall be divided into five (5) classes of lots. Applicability of the various
classes of lots shall depend solely upon the character of the existing
surrounding neighborhood, and shall comply with the following requirements:
      Class 1: Blocks in neighborhoods of predominantly twenty five (25) and
thirty foot (30') lots of record and zoned for single-family use, which were
recorded with the appropriate officials of the county of Cook prior to October
1, 1956, may be subdivided into buildable lots which are combinations of two
(2) or more lots or parts thereof. However, in certain situations, a
combination of three (3) existing lots of record to be resubdivided may be
allowed provided all of the lots as proposed contain at least forty five (45)
linear feet of frontage at the building line, and contain not less than five
thousand six hundred (5,600) square feet.
      Notwithstanding the foregoing, the minimum depth of the lots in this
situation shall be one and one-half (11/2) times the proposed frontage of the
lot or one hundred fifteen feet (115'), whichever is greater.
      Class 2: Blocks in neighborhoods where the lot depth of existing lots of
record exceeds one hundred seventy five feet (175') from the front right of way
line to the rear lot line (excluding double frontage lots) and zoned for
single-family uses, which were recorded with the appropriate officials of the
county of Cook prior to October 1, 1956. The minimal lot size in these areas
shall be fifty (50) linear feet at the front building line, and each lot shall
contain at least ten thousand (10,000) square feet. For purposes of this
paragraph, the requirements of subsection B of this section shall not apply.
      Class 3: See section
4-3-2 of this code.
      Class 4: All other lots zoned for single-family use. Lots for single-
family residential use shall be at least fifty five feet (55') wide at building
setback line for interior lots, and sixty feet (60') wide for corner lots, and
shall contain at least six thousand eight hundred seventy five (6,875) square
feet in area for interior lots, and seven thousand five hundred (7,500) square
feet in area for corner lots.
      Class 5: Lots for development or use other than single-family housing,
and lands zoned for regional shopping by the zoning title shall be exempted
from the minimum lot widths and depths, as outlined in this title. These
parcels shall be (re)subdivided in conformance with a plan approved by the
Zoning and Planning Commission and village board, and in compliance with all
other applicable provisions of this title.
   B.   Maximum Depth: The maximum depth of any lot, exclusive of unusable
land, shall be three and one-half (31/2) times the width of the lot, except for
class 2 lots above.
   C.   Lots To Abut On Streets: Every lot shall abut on a dedicated and
improved street for at least twenty five feet (25').
   D.   Setbacks And Side Yards: Minimum setback lines from property lines in
residential zones shall conform with the zoning regulations; except, that there
shall be a side yard along each lot line of a width not less than five feet
(5').
   E.   Property Lines At Corner: Property line at street intersection corners
shall be right angles, or nearly so.
   F.   Side Lines: Side lines of lots shall be approximately at right angles
or radial to the street line.
   G.   Shape: The shape of lots and their orientation shall be appropriate to
the location of the proposed subdivision, and to the type of development
contemplated.
   H.   Double Frontage Lots: Double frontage lots, other than corner lots,
will be permitted only under unusual circumstances.
   I.   Building Sites: Every lot must contain a suitable building site and be
able to conform to the requirements of the building and zoning regulations for
the erection and location of building structures. (Ords. 79-2-5, 81-36-78, 92-
11-45, 93-9-24, 05-15-52, 07-05-16, 19-16-66)
5-3-3-3: BLOCKS:
   A.   Length: Blocks shall not exceed one thousand two hundred feet (1,200')
in length nor be less than two hundred fifty feet (250') in length; blocks
longer than eight hundred feet (800') in length may be required to have
crosswalk ways.
   B.   Blocks For Industrial And Commercial Use: Blocks intended for
industrial or commercial use shall be designed specifically for such purpose
with adequate space set aside for off street parking and loading and meet
minimum requirements for parking. (Ord. 79-2-5)
5-3-4: RESERVE STRIPS:
There shall be no reservation of strips of land that prevent or limit access to
land dedicated or intended to be dedicated to public use.
Reserved strips controlling the access to public ways or which will prove
taxable for special improvements, will not be approved unless such strips are
necessary for the protection of the public welfare or of substantial property
rights, or both, and in no case except in which control and disposal of the
land comprising such strips is placed definitely within the jurisdiction of the
village under conditions approved by the Zoning and Planning Commission. (Ords.
79-2-5, 81-36-78, 92-11-45, 93-9-24, 19-16-66)
CHAPTER 4
REQUIRED IMPROVEMENTS
SECTION:
5-4-1: Improvements Required Generally
5-4-2: Roadway Improvements
5-4-3: Driveways
5-4-4: Easements
5-4-5: Stormwater Drainage
5-4-6: Landscaping, Fences
5-4-7: Natural Features
5-4-1: IMPROVEMENTS REQUIRED GENERALLY:
The Zoning and Planning Commission shall have the power to require one or more
of the following improvements depending upon the nature and scope of the
subdivision. The subdivider shall provide, at a minimum, those improvements
that are not existing at the time of submission of the final plat and the
subdivider shall be required to make provision for the installation of same in
accordance with section
5-4A-5 of this chapter. Particular attention shall be given to means for
disposition of stormwater and to the desirability of curb and gutter or other
provision to prevent breakdown of pavement edges. The design, layout and
carrying capacities of stormwater, sanitary sewer and water facilities shall
recognize the future requirements of adjacent undeveloped areas.
Crosswalks.
Curb and gutter.
Driveways and off street parking.
Pavement striping.
Rear yard drainage.
Sanitary sewage facilities.
Sidewalks.
Stormwater drainage facilities.
Street and alley paving.
Street signs.
Streetlights.
Survey monuments.
Topsoil where appropriate.
Traffic control signs.
Water mains and hydrants. (Ords. 79-2-5, 81-36-78, 92-11-45, 93-9-24, 19-16-66)
5-4-2: ROADWAY IMPROVEMENTS:
The subdivider shall, at his own expense, improve all streets in accordance
with the current specifications in effect as amended from time to time by the
village president and board of trustees; provided, however, that the president
and board of trustees may impose more stringent specifications for through
streets within or adjoining a subdivision as a condition precedent to accepting
any subdivision plat. (Ord. 79-2-5)
5-4-3: DRIVEWAYS:
Except where improved alleys are existing, every lot in a subdivision shall be
provided with not more than one paved driveway. Said driveway shall contain
sufficient paved area for the parking of at least two (2) full size
automobiles, unless the only parking space(s) located on said driveway is/are
in the front yard, in which case sufficient paved area for only one full size
automobile will be allowed. Said driveway shall comply with the following:
   A.   Minimum Length And Width: The minimum driveway length shall be from the
street access to a point forty feet (40') behind the minimum setback line, and
the minimum width of said paved driveway shall be nine feet (9'). When the only
parking space(s) located on a driveway is/are in the front yard, the minimum
driveway length, as provided by this subsection, shall not be applicable to the
driveway.
   B.   Maximum Width: In no case shall a driveway for access to a single-
family residential property exceed twenty feet (20') in width at the property
line.
   C.   Number Of Driveways: Not more than one driveway shall be allowed to
service any single-family home unless permission is first granted by the Zoning
and Planning Commission.
   D.   Circular Driveways: For purposes of this chapter a "circular driveway"
is defined as a continuous driveway that provides access to the street via more
than one curb cut on a single street frontage. Any new circular driveway must
be petitioned for before the Zoning and Planning Commission subject to the
provisions of sections
2-1-7-2 and
2-1-7-3 of this code.
   E.   Construction Materials And Standards: The driveway shall be constructed
with a minimum concrete thickness of five inches (5") and a granular bedding
base of two inches (2") minimum compacted thickness, or with a minimum
compacted crushed stone base course of six inches (6") in thickness and an
asphalt surfaced course of two inches (2") in thickness, and all such
construction shall be in accordance with sections
6-1G-1 through
6-1G-3 of this code.
   F.   Access To Streets: Except in the case of a corner lot, the driveway for
a single- family residence shall only access the street in front of the
property serviced by the driveway. In the case of a corner lot, the driveway
for a single-family residence may access either: 1) the street in front of the
property serviced by the driveway ("front load garage") or 2) the street which
abuts the property and intersects the street in front of the property ("side
load garage"). For purposes of this subsection, the "street in front of the
property" shall be defined as the street designated in the mailing address for
the property. In addition to the aforementioned street access, a driveway for a
single-family residence may also access an alley abutting the property. A
variation from the provisions of this subsection may be petitioned for before
the Zoning and Planning Commission subject to the provisions of sections
2-1-7-2 and
2-1-7-3 of this code. (Ords. 12-24-77, 19-16-66)
5-4-4: EASEMENTS:
   A.   All aboveground utilities shall be placed on rear or side property
lines. Where a subdivision is traversed by a watercourse, an appropriate
easement shall be provided for the watercourse in either an open cut or in an
underground conduit, depending upon the density of development.
   B.   Where water mains, sanitary sewers, storm sewers, gas mains, telephone
lines, power lines or other utilities are located on or adjacent to property
lines, adequate easements together with right of ingress or egress thereto and
therefrom shall be provided and platted.
   C.   Said easement shall provide that no structure or building, except a
division fence shall be placed on said easement.
   D.   Easements shall not be less than eight feet (8') in width. Where more
than one underground utility is to occupy the easement, the easement shall be
of such width as to extend five feet (5') at each side beyond the centerline of
the outermost utilities. Additional width shall be provided where necessary to
accommodate utility pole bracing. The Zoning and Planning Commission may
require evidence of approval by the public utility company of easement width.
   E.   The installation of all utilities underground is strongly encouraged.
(Ords. 79-2-5, 81-36-78, 92-11-45, 93-9-24, 19-16-66)
5-4-5: STORMWATER DRAINAGE:
The subdivider shall, subject to riparian rights, dedicate a right of way for
storm drainage purposes, conforming substantially with the lines of any natural
watercourse, channel, stream or creek that traverses the subdivision, or to
provide additional easements for both, to dispose of such stormwater. (Ord. 79-
2-5)
5-4-6: LANDSCAPING, FENCES:
Where a residential subdivision adjoins a railroad right of way, waterway, an
industrial area, a business area or other land use which could have a
depreciating effect on the residential use of the property, buffer strips shall
be provided as required by the zoning title and outlined in section
4-1-6 of this code. Only in unusual circumstances will a wire fence be allowed,
and then only after hardship has been demonstrated by the subdivider. (Ord. 79-
2-5)
5-4-7: NATURAL FEATURES:
Outstanding natural and cultural features, such as scenic spots and
watercourses, shall be preserved insofar as possible. (Ord. 79-2-5)
ARTICLE A. CONSTRUCTION REQUIREMENTS
SECTION:
5-4A-1: Conformance With Village Standards
5-4A-2: Construction Plans And Commencement, Procedure
5-4A-3: Licensed Contractors Required
5-4A-4: Inspections
5-4A-5: Guarantee Of Completion And Maintenance
5-4A-6: Fees
5-4A-7: Acceptance Of Improvements
5-4A-8: Responsibility For Maintenance And Repair
5-4A-9: Applicability Of Other Regulations
5-4A-10: Environmental Assessment As Condition Of Land Dedication Or Donation
5-4A-1: CONFORMANCE WITH VILLAGE STANDARDS:
All improvements shall be in accordance with village standards and requirements
as given in ordinances and regulations in effect at the time of submission of
the final plat. (Ord. 79-2-5)
5-4A-2: CONSTRUCTION PLANS AND COMMENCEMENT, PROCEDURE:
   A.   Preparation: The subdivider shall have working drawings and
specifications for improvements prepared by an Illinois registered professional
engineer retained by the subdivider. The engineer retained by the subdivider
shall be other than an engineer on the village payroll or one retained by the
village.
   B.   Submittal: Before considering the approval of the final plat for any
subdivision, the Zoning and Planning Commission will require that appropriate
working drawings and specifications be submitted for those improvements
required at the time of approval of the preliminary layout.
   C.   Review, Fees: All working drawings, specifications and cost estimates
shall be checked by the director of office of Community Development and Growth
Management or by a consulting firm duly retained by the village. The subdivider
shall reimburse the village for preparation and for checking of working
drawings, specifications and cost estimates in accordance with section
5-4A-6 of this article.
   D.   Conformance To Village Standards: Working drawings and specifications
shall be in accordance with village standards and requirements, as published by
the director of office of Community Development and Growth Management.
   E.   Approval: Approval of the village shall be secured before commencement
of construction of any improvement. Recording of the final plat shall
constitute permission to proceed with construction. If the subdivider elects,
as provided in this Article, to construct an improvement prior to approval of
the final plat, the Village Manager may extend permission to proceed upon
recommendation of the Zoning and Planning Commission together with
certification by the Director of Office of Community Development and Growth
Management that working drawings and specifications have been prepared and
approved, and that engineering fees have been paid and that an inspection bond
has been posted.
   F.   State, County Permission Required: The subdivider shall also secure any
permission required by laws of the State and County, and permission extended by
the Village shall not relieve the subdivider of responsibility to secure State
and County approval. If such permission is required, a copy of said written
permission is to be filed with the Director of Office of Community Development
and Growth Management. (Ords. 79-2-5, 81-12-19, 81-36-78, 92-11-45, 93-9-24,
19-16-66)
5-4A-3: LICENSED CONTRACTORS REQUIRED:
The developer shall file with the Director of Office of Quality Control a list
of all contractors and subcontractors who are to participate in construction of
public improvements. Such contractors and subcontractors shall be subject to
the licensing provisions of this Code and Village ordinances, and shall be
subject to disqualification by reason of faulty performance upon past
construction work done in the Village. (Ords. 79-2-5, 81-12-19)
5-4A-4: INSPECTIONS:
All public improvements, as defined herein, shall be subject to inspection by
Village inspectors during the course of construction and after construction is
completed. All materials and workmanship used in the construction of such
improvements shall be in compliance with all Village codes and ordinances. All
contractors shall make reasonable tests and provide proof of quality of
materials upon request of such inspectors.
   A.   Notice to Inspect: It shall be the responsibility of the subdivider or
his contractor to notify the Director of Office of Quality Control when work is
to be started and to request assignment of an inspector. The initial
notification that a particular improvement is to be started shall be in writing
and shall reach the Director of Office of Quality Control two (2) working days
in advance of the date that inspection is to begin. The notice shall be
accompanied by an inspection bond in accordance with Section
5-4A-5 of this Article.
   B.   Reports: The Village inspector shall prepare and submit to the Director
of Office of Quality Control each day, a report stating progress of work and
giving his findings as to quality and quantity of materials used. He shall, in
particular, call attention to any circumstances that entail departures from
working drawings and specifications such as unforeseen difficulties of
drainage, ground water, poor subsoil, unstable fill material or other
circumstances. Whenever, in his opinion, such departures are likely to cause
either a lower ultimate standard of performance or a higher construction cost
than could reasonably be anticipated, he shall immediately notify the Director
of Office of Quality Control. When an improvement has been completed, he shall
prepare a summary report and furnish a copy of said report to the developer and
his engineer.
   C.   Approval:
      1.   Approval by the inspector shall not be deemed acceptance of the
improvement by the Village. Acceptance shall be only by action of the Board of
Trustees, and acceptance shall be contingent upon a favorable inspection report
signed by the Director of Office of Quality Control.
      2.   Approval by the inspector shall in no way relieve the subdivider of
full responsibility for adherence by his contractors to specifications and
working drawings, nor for high standards of materials, methods and workmanship.
   D.   Fees: To defray the engineering and inspection expenses incurred by the
Village in preparation or checking of preliminary and final cost estimates,
working drawings, specifications, and in executing inspection responsibilities,
the subdivider shall be required to reimburse the Village as indicated in
Section
5-4A-6 of this Article. These are separate and distinct charges from the fee
paid at the time of submission of either the preliminary or final layouts.
(Ords. 79-2-5, 81-12-19)
5-4A-5: GUARANTEE OF COMPLETION AND MAINTENANCE:
The Village shall not record the final plat unless and until the subdivider has
guaranteed the completion of all required improvements within a reasonable
period and in compliance with working drawings and specifications, and has
guaranteed to maintain said improvements until they are accepted by the
Village. This guarantee shall be by one or a combination of one or more of
three (3) methods. Only one method shall be applied to one specific
improvement.
   A.   Performance Bond: The subdivider may post a performance bond prepared
in a form approved by the Village Attorney and underwritten by a surety company
listed as approved by the Village Attorney. The amount of the bond shall be not
less than one hundred twenty five percent (125%) of the final estimate of cost
of the improvement as estimated by the Director of Office of Quality Control
under Section
5-4A-6 of this Article. There shall be no reduction in the amount of that
portion of the bond applying to a particular improvement unless and until that
improvement is complete and accepted by the Village. The subgrade, base course
and surface course of a street, alley or parking area shall be considered as a
single improvement from the standpoint of acceptance.
   B.   Cash Deposit: The subdivider may deposit a cash bond with the Village
in accordance with a specific escrow agreement approved by the Village Attorney
and Village Manager and in an amount not less than one hundred twenty five
percent (125%) of the final estimate of cost of the improvements as estimated
by the Director of Office of Quality Control under Section
5-4A-6 of this Article. The deposit shall be held in a special account by the
Village and the agreement may provide for partial payouts as work progresses in
amounts recommended by the Director of Office of Quality Control as
corresponding to the original estimate of unit costs. Partial payouts shall be
contingent upon favorable inspection reports, and the sum of partial payouts
for a single improvement shall not exceed eighty five percent (85%) of the
original deposit for the improvement until and unless the improvement is
complete and accepted by the Village. Payment will be made by the Village only
upon submission of the proper waivers for all labor and materials. The
subgrade, base course and surface course of a street, alley or parking area
shall be considered a single improvement from the standpoint of acceptance.
   C.   Improvement Completion Prior to Final Plat Approval: Upon specific
permission of the Board of Trustees, as recommended by the Zoning and Planning
Commission, the subdivider may elect to complete one or more improvements prior
to approval of the final plat. Where the subdivider elects to guarantee
completion of an improvement by this method, approval of the final plat shall
be contingent upon certification by the Director of Office of Community
Development and Growth Management that the improvement has been completed in
accordance with drawings and specifications; that the inspection reports are
favorable; and that guarantee has been provided to cover cost of maintenance of
the improvement in accordance with subsection
5-4A-8A of this Article. (Ords. 79-2-5, 81-12-19, 81-36-78, 92-11-45, 93-9-24,
19-16-66)
5-4A-6:FEES:
The subdivider shall have working drawings, specifications and cost estimates
for improvements prepared by an engineer retained by himself; he shall submit
such drawings, specifications and estimates to the Director of Office of
Quality Control. The application shall be accompanied by two (2) sets of
construction prints, specifications and estimates. The Director of Office of
Quality Control shall inform the subdivider of the amount of the charge for
this service and the charge shall be two percent (2%) of the total estimated
cost of the improvement, as confirmed by the Director of Office of Quality
Control. Checking shall proceed after the subdivider has paid over this fee to
the Office of Quality Control. Additional fees shall be paid for rechecking of
drawings, specifications and estimates arising from revisions made by or
required of the subdivider. There shall be no refund of engineering fees if the
project is abandoned for any reason whatsoever.
   A.   Engineering Fees: The engineering fee is intended to include checking
of preliminary and final cost estimates, working drawings and specifications,
but it is not intended to include inspection or supervision of work.
   B.   Inspection Fees:
      1.   Before grading or construction of improvements is started, the
subdivider or his contractor shall post with the Office of Quality Control a
cash inspection bond in an amount agreed upon between the Director of Office of
Quality Control and the subdivider as being sufficient to provide for
inspection charges at the current per diem rates. From this bond shall be
deducted, each day, the inspection expense incurred by the Village at the rates
set forth below. Any excess of the bond over actual inspection charges will be
refunded to the depositor and cured by the subdivider by depositing a
sufficient sum of money to ensure continuity of inspection.
      2.   It shall be the responsibility of the subdivider to notify the
Director of Office of Quality Control in advance as to the dates that
construction work will commence and complete for purposes of determining the
number of days of inspection charge. In event of dispute only notification in
writing shall be considered as proof of notice.
      3.   Per diem inspection rates shall be equal to one hundred percent
(100%) of the engineer's quoted charge to the Village. If inspection is
performed by an inspector on the Village payroll, the charge shall be one
hundred twenty five percent (125%) of the regular salary paid to the inspector
computed to an hourly basis. There shall be not less than a four (4) hour
charge for time spent in any one day. For time spent outside of the regular
working hours of the inspector, the hourly charge shall be two hundred fifty
percent (250%) of the regular salary paid to the inspector computed to an
hourly basis. (Ords. 79-2-5, 81-12-19; 1985 Code)
5-4A-7: ACCEPTANCE OF IMPROVEMENTS:
Acceptance of an improvement by the Village shall be only by resolution of the
Board of Trustees upon recommendation of the Director of Office of Quality
Control. Recommendation shall be contingent upon:
   A.   Fulfillment of the requirements of Section
5-4A-5 of this Article.
   B.   An opinion by the Village Attorney that satisfactory and proper
conveyances and dedications have been made by the subdivider to the Village.
   C.   Improvements being complete and in good repair in accordance with
Village standards and requirements.
   D.   Inspection reports indicating compliance with working drawings and
specifications.
   E.   A single improvement shall not be accepted in part; it shall be
complete throughout the subdivision as indicated in the final plat and working
drawings. The subgrade, base course and surface course of a street, alley or
parking area shall be considered as a single improvement.
Acceptance of an improvement shall constitute release of the applicable portion
of the performance bond, or of the applicable portion of the balance of cash
deposit. (Ords. 79-2-5, 81-12-19)
5-4A-8: RESPONSIBILITY FOR MAINTENANCE AND REPAIR:
   A.   As further assurance of serviceable construction, and to provide for
repair of damage resulting from subsequent construction operations of the
subdivider or his contractors, the subdivider shall be responsible for all
maintenance of an improvement for a period of not less than fifteen (15) months
following completion and acceptance by the Village of construction of that
improvement; providing, in addition, that the subdivider shall be responsible
for all maintenance of roadways, curbs and gutters, sidewalks, parkway strips
and extension boxes and hydrants of water facilities until all construction
work has been completed upon other improvements, dwellings, buildings, fill and
landscaping adjacent to the improvement under consideration. The guarantee of
completion and maintenance shall recognize the possibility of maintenance costs
and the terms of the guarantee shall provide for reimbursement to the Village
for any maintenance expenses incurred by the Village in performing maintenance
work after failure of the subdivider to perform such work upon due notice. The
Village Attorney shall specify the form of such guarantee of completion and
maintenance upon request and recommendation of the Director of Office of
Quality Control.
   B.   The subdivider, his contractors and suppliers shall be responsible
[sic] that existing improvements and the property of the Village are not
damaged or rendered less useful or unsightly by the operations of the
subdivider, his contractors or suppliers. This provision is intended to include
damage or nuisance with respect to the land, improvements or landscaping of the
Village; damage to existing streets, sidewalks, curb and gutter, parkways by
passage thereover of equipment or trucks or by excavation for any purpose; the
spillage or tracking of earth, sand or rock onto existing streets, sidewalks,
curb and gutter or parkways; the washing by storm water of earth or sand onto
streets, sidewalks, curb and gutter or parkways or into catch basins or damage
to water mains, sanitary sewers, culverts or storm sewers. To reduce or
localize the possibility of damage to streets by heavy trucking, the Director
of Office of Quality Control shall instruct the subdivider as to the streets to
be used for access to the subdivision by equipment and trucks, and the
subdivider shall be responsible for enforcement of this instruction upon his
contractors and their suppliers. The subdivider shall make provisions to
prevent washing of earth or sand onto sidewalks, streets, curb and gutter and
into catch basins by storm water. Where deemed advisable, the Director of
Office of Quality Control shall have the power to require, either prior to
commencement of construction or after construction is in process, that the
subdivider post a surety bond to guarantee repair of damages or abatement of
nuisance. Where need for surety bond becomes apparent after construction is in
process, the Director of Office of Quality Control shall have the power to
order construction discontinued until said surety bond has been posted.
Expenses incurred by the Village in repairing damages, cleaning streets,
catchbasins and sewers shall be deducted from the surety. (Ords. 79-2-5, 81-12-
19)
5-4A-9: APPLICABILITY OF OTHER REGULATIONS:
None of the provisions of these regulations pertaining to permission, surety,
fees, acceptances and approval shall be construed as exempting subdividers,
general contractors, sidewalk contractors, cement contractors, sewer builders,
drain layers, electrical contractors, electricians or any other contractor or
craftsman from any other ordinance or regulation of the Village with respect to
licenses, fees, surety inspection or other control. (Ord. 79-2-5)
5-4A-10: ENVIRONMENTAL ASSESSMENT AS CONDITION OF LAND DEDICATION OR DONATION:
   A.   Not less than five (5) days prior to any dedication or donation of real
estate required under any Village ordinance or requirement, the grantor/
dedicator, at its sole cost and expense, shall have caused to be prepared and
submitted to the Village a written report of a site assessment and
environmental audit, in scope, form and substance, and prepared by an
independent, competent and qualified engineer, satisfactory to the Village (the
"environmental audit"), and dated not more than sixty (60) days prior to the
transfer date, showing the engineer made all appropriate inquiry into the
previous ownership and uses of the property consistent with good commercial and
customary practice in an effort to minimize liability, which takes into account
and satisfies the "innocent landowner" provision set forth at 43 U.S.C. 9601
(35), such that consistent with generally accepted engineering practice and
procedure, no evidence or indication came to light which would suggest there
was a release of substances on the property which could necessitate an
environmental response action, and which demonstrates that the property and the
facility complies with, and does not deviate from, all applicable environmental
statutes, laws, ordinances, rules and regulations, including any licenses,
permits or certificates required thereunder.
   B.   The environmental audit shall also demonstrate that the property and
the improvements located thereon, if any, do not contain:
      1.   Asbestos in any form;
      2.   Urea formaldehyde;
      3.   Transformers or other equipment that contain fluid containing
polychlorinated biphenyles;
      4.   Underground storage tanks; or
      5.   Any other chemical, material or substance, the exposure to which is
prohibited, limited or regulated by any Federal, State, County, regional or
local authority (the "authorities") or which poses a hazard to the health and
safety of the occupants of the property or the facility, or the occupants of
adjacent property.
   C.   The environmental audit shall also demonstrate that the property and
facility are not, and have not been, the subject of any past, existing or
threatened investigation, inquiry or proceeding concerning environmental
matters by the authorities, and that no notice or submission concerning
environmental matters has been given or should be given with regard to the
property and the facility to the authorities. The environmental audit shall
demonstrate that the property and facility are not subject to, or covered by,
the requirements of the Emergency Planning and Community Right-To- Know Act of
1986, 42 U.S.C. 11001, et seq., and that the property is not now being used and
has never been used for any activities involving directly or indirectly the
use, treatment, storage or disposal of any hazardous or toxic chemical,
material, substance or waste.
   D.   The grantor/dedicator of the property and/or facility acknowledges and
agrees that the Village shall not be obligated to take title to any land if, in
its sole and exclusive judgment, for any reason whatsoever (including, without
limitation, information revealed by the environmental audit) it determines that
the use or condition of the property, or any part thereof, poses a material
health, safety or environmental hazard. (Ord. 93-21-91)
TITLE 6
BUILDING REGULATIONS
CHAPTER 1
BUILDING CODE
SECTION:
6-1-1: Short Title
6-1-2: Definitions
6-1-3: Applicability to Existing Buildings
6-1-4: Compliance With Provisions
6-1-5: Building Officers
6-1-6: Architectural Design
6-1-7: Health and Safety Requirements
6-1-8: Violations
6-1-1: SHORT TITLE:
This Chapter shall be known and may be cited as The Building Code of Oak Lawn.
(1951 Code 21-17-2)
6-1-2: DEFINITIONS:
Words and terms used in this Chapter, not otherwise defined, shall have the
meanings defined in the zoning ordinance 1 . (1951 Code 21-1-3)
 
Notes
1 1. See Title 4 of this Municipal Code.
6-1-3: APPLICABILITY TO EXISTING BUILDINGS:
In construing the several sections of this Chapter, said section shall not be
construed as requiring alterations in the construction or equipment of
buildings or structures in existence at the time of the passage of this
Chapter, unless specific provision is made to the contrary, or unless the
provision is expressly made retroactive, or unless such building shall not have
sufficient or adequate means of egress of ingress. (1951 Code 21-5-26)
6-1-4: COMPLIANCE WITH PROVISIONS:
Every building or structure or part thereof, hereafter constructed, erected,
altered, enlarged, maintained or repaired within the Village shall be so
constructed, erected, altered, enlarged, maintained or repaired in accordance
with the provisions of this Chapter. (1951 Code 21-5-24)
6-1-5: BUILDING OFFICERS:
   A.   Building Officers Designated: The title "building officers" when used
in this Chapter shall mean and include the Director of Office of Quality
Control, the Chief of Police, the Health and Sanitation Officer and all
inspectors and assistants, as provided for by the Board of Trustees.
   B.   Right of Entry Powers: Building officers and their duly authorized
inspectors are hereby empowered, during reasonable hours, to lawfully enter
upon any premises or into any building or structure, or portion thereof,
whether completed or in process of construction, alteration or repair, when
necessary to do so in performance of any duty imposed upon them by this Chapter
or the provisions of the Zoning Ordinance of the Village, or any order or
resolution of the Board of Trustees. (1951 Code 21-1-1, 21-1-2; Ord. 81-12-19)
6-1-6: ARCHITECTURAL DESIGN:
Every building or structure hereinafter constructed, erected, altered or
enlarged, shall be so designed and constructed as to be suitable to its
environment from an architectural standpoint. (1951 Code 21-5-27)
6-1-7: HEALTH AND SAFETY REQUIREMENTS:
It shall be the duty of every owner or occupant of a building to keep in a safe
sanitary condition. (1951 Code 21-5-6)
6-1-8: VIOLATIONS:
   A.   Resisting Officers: Any person hindering or attempting to hinder,
resisting, or attempting to resist, any building officer in the performance of
duty, shall be guilty of a misdemeanor and shall be charged with resisting an
officer.
   B.   Generally: Any person who violates, disobeys, omits, neglects or
refuses to comply with, or who resists the enforcement of any of the provisions
of this Chapter, or who shall refuse to remedy a violation of any such
provision, shall be guilty of a misdemeanor and subject to a penalty as
provided in Section
1-4-1 of this Code. Each day upon which such violation continues shall
constitute a separate offense. (1951 Code 21-1-2, 21-17-1; 1985 Code)
ARTICLE A. BUILDING PERMITS AND FEES
SECTION:
6-1A-1: Permit Required
6-1A-2: Application For Permit, Accompanying Plans And Specifications
6-1A-3: Fees
6-1A-3-1: Building Permits
6-1A-3-2: Inspections
6-1A-3-3: Demolition Of Buildings
6-1A-3-4: Garages
6-1A-3-5: Street And Parkway Restoration Bond
6-1A-3-6: Use Of Water During Construction
6-1A-3-7: Exemptions From Payment Of Fees
6-1A-4: Bond Requirements
6-1A-5: Issuance Of Permit, Restrictions
6-1A-6: Construction Commencement, Requirements And Restrictions
6-1A-7: Temporary Permits
6-1A-8: Exemptions From Provisions
6-1A-9: Certificate Of Occupancy Fee
6-1A-10: Rear Lot Drainage Deposits
6-1A-11: Protection Of Sidewalks And Streets During Construction
6-1A-12: Contractor Licensing And Registration, Generally
6-1A-13: Contractor License And Registration Application
6-1A-14: Contractor Definitions
6-1A-15: License And Registration Fees
6-1A-16: Contractor License Fee Exemption
6-1A-17: Contractor Insurance Requirements
6-1A-18: Issuance Of Building Permits
6-1A-19: License Revocation And Reinstatement
6-1A-1: PERMIT REQUIRED:
Before proceeding with the erection, construction, alteration, addition,
repair, removal, demolition or the excavation for any building or structure in
the Village, a permit for such erection, construction, alteration, addition,
repair, removal, demolition or excavation shall first be obtained by the owner
or his agent from the Director of Office of Quality Control as provided herein.
(1951 Code 21-2-4; Ords. 79-34-72, 81-12-19, 20-08-21)
6-1A-2: APPLICATION FOR PERMIT, ACCOMPANYING PLANS AND SPECIFICATIONS:
   A.   Application for building permits shall be made by the owner or his
agent to the Director of Office of Quality Control on forms prepared and kept
on file in the office of said Director. Such application shall describe the
location of the proposed work by setting forth the correct legal description of
the land upon which the building, structure or part thereof is to be erected,
constructed, added, altered or repaired and the street name and number assigned
to the site, the real estate permanent index number, volume and item of the
Cook County Collector's tax warrant for the last year in which general real
estate taxes were assessed; the purpose for which the building or structure is
designed to be used; the length, width and height thereof; the number of rooms;
the total cost of the work proposed to be done under the permit sought; the
name and address of the owner or his agent or architect; and the names of the
licensed general contractors. Said application for a building permit shall also
be accompanied by a plat of survey, showing the proposed location of the
building to be erected on said lot, piece or parcel of land and an agreement to
be signed by the owner, his agent or architect, conditioned that he or his duly
authorized agent or architect, if granted the permit sought, will construct the
work in accordance with the description set forth in the application and
accompanying plans and specifications.
   B.   Applications for building permits (including garages) shall also be
accompanied by a plot plan in triplicate and a full set of plans and
specifications which shall be sufficiently complete so that the proposed
structure could be constructed therefrom by a competent contractor, builder or
engineer without further directions of any kind. Said plot plan and full set of
plans and specifications shall be reviewed by the Department of Quality Control
for compliances with the Village Code. When plans for specifications are
submitted for large, unique or complicated buildings or projects, the Director
of Office of Quality Control may send them to an independent, third-party
agency for plan review. The cost of such review shall be borne by the permit
applicant and is in addition to any such other fees charged by the Village. If
the plot plan and full set of plans and specifications are in compliance with
the Village Code, the three (3) copies of the plot plan shall be stamped
approved and initialed by the Director of Office of Quality Control. One copy
shall be returned to the applicant, one copy filed at the Village Hall, and one
copy retained by said Director.
   C.   All such plans and drawings shall be drawn to a scale of not less than
one-eighth of an inch to the foot (1/8" = 1') on paper or cloth in ink, or by
some process that will not fade or obliterate. All distances and dimensions
shall be accurately figured and drawings made explicit and complete, showing
the lot lines and the location of all plumbing fixtures within such building.
   D.   No permit shall be granted or plans approved unless such plans are made
and signed by such persons as are permitted under the laws of the State to make
plans for buildings as provided in "An Act to provide for the licensing of
architects and regulating the practice of architecture as a profession and to
repeal certain Acts therein named", approved June 24, 1919, and amendments
thereto, or in "An Act to revise the law in relation to the regulation of the
practice of structural engineering" approved June 24, 1919, and amendments
thereto 1 . The architect's seal and identification registration number shall
be attached to the original of said plans filed with the Director of Office of
Quality Control.
   E.   True copies of so much of said plans as may be required, in the opinion
of the Director of Office of Quality Control, to illustrate the features of
construction and equipment of the building referred to, shall be filed with the
Director of Office of Quality Control and shall remain on file in his office.
   F.   Complete plans and specifications of buildings for public or business
use for which permits are granted shall remain on file permanently in said
Director's office.
   G.   In all cases, the approved plan or plans, together with building
permits, must be kept on the premises while the work is in progress.
   H.   No deviation from the approved plans shall be allowed unless revised
plans are resubmitted for approval and approval in writing is granted by the
director of quality control.
   I.   All applications for permits issued under this Article A shall be
accompanied by a copy of each contractor's or subcontractor's current state and
Village licenses, registrations, and certifications, as applicable, as well as
proof of insurance in accordance with Section
6-1A-17. No permits shall be granted to a roofing contractor or subcontractor
without said contractor having first filed with the department of quality
control a copy (or copies, as applicable) of said contractor's current State of
Illinois and Village contractor licenses, registrations, and certifications and
the required proof of insurance.
   J.   Applications for building permits for new buildings or additions to
existing buildings shall be accompanied by a tree preservation plan. Said plan
shall consist of a survey of the parcel in question on which existing trees are
identified, with an indication as to whether said trees are to remain or be cut
down as a result of the proposed construction. Said plan shall be reviewed by
the village forester, and no permit shall be issued until the plan has been
approved by the village forester.
   K.   As a prerequisite to the issuance of any new or renewed business
license, each applicant shall submit to the business license department a
certificate of rodent abatement issued by a licensed exterminator. A copy of
the current rodent abatement certificate shall be kept on file with the
business license department at all times during the effectiveness of any
license.
      Applications for building permits for new commercial buildings, including
those for additions or demolition to existing buildings shall be accompanied by
a site-rodent abatement plan.
      1.   Prior to any demolition or excavation at a new commercial
construction site the owner of the site shall prepare a rodent control
management plan for the detection, inspection and treatment of rodents at the
site. At a minimum, the plan shall include provisions for abatement of the site
by a structural pest control company licensed by the State of Illinois by means
of above-ground bait boxes around the perimeter of the site until all
excavation is completed.
      2.   The owner shall maintain a written record of the plan and any pest
control measures performed by pest management professional at the site and
include any reports and receipts prepared by the structural pest control
company. The plan and records shall be maintained at the new construction site
until construction at the site is completed and open to inspection upon request
by authorized village personnel, including but not limited to employees of the
departments of streets and sanitation and buildings.
      3.   Commercial construction or demolition projects requiring excavation
must include rodent abatement on an as needed basis for all properties within a
three (3) city-block radius of the demolition site during the time of the
demolition project and up to but not more than ninety (90) days after the
demolition project is complete. Any abatement measures on properties included
within the three (3) city-block radius requires the individual property owner's
authorization;
         a.   Any person violating any of the provisions of this section shall
be fined not less than twenty five dollars ($25.00) nor more than seven hundred
fifty dollars ($750.00) for each offense. Each day that a violation continues
shall constitute a separate and distinct offense to which a separate fine shall
apply.
         b.   In addition to any other available penalties and remedies
provided, one or more violations of this section at the site may result in a
stop work order issued by the department that issued the citation, directing
that all activity cease for ten (10) days. Any further citation for violation
at the same new construction site within six months after the initial stop work
order may result in the issuance of another ten (10) day stop work order. The
department that issued the ten (10)-day stop work order under this section
shall lift that order only if sufficient evidence of compliance with this
section is provided to the department.
         c.   It shall be unlawful for any person to knowingly violate a stop
work order issued under this section, or to knowingly cause, permit, encourage,
assist, aid, abet or direct another person to violate a stop work order issued
under this section, or to knowingly in any manner be a party to a violation of
a stop work order issued under this section.
         Any person who violates this subsection upon conviction shall be
punished, as follows:
         (1)   Incarceration for a term not less than three days nor more than
six (6) months; or
         (2)   Community service of not less than 10 hours, nor more than one
hundred (100) hours; or
         (3)   A fine of seven hundred fifty dollars ($750.00).
      6.   It shall be unlawful for any person to knowingly destroy, deface,
remove, damage, impair, mar, cover or obstruct any stop work order issued under
this section that a village official has posted or affixed at a work site.
      7.   Any person who violates this subsection upon conviction shall be
punished, as follows:
         a.   Incarceration for a term not less than three days, nor more than
six (6) months; or
         b.   Community service of not less than ten (10) hours, nor more than
one hundred (100) hours; or
         c.   A fine of not less than twenty five dollars ($25.00), nor more
than seven hundred fifty dollars ($750.00).
The department of community development is authorized to enforce this section.
All references in Title 6 of the Village Code to the "department of quality
control" shall be interpreted to include the "department of community
development." (1951 Code 21-2-7; Ords. 51, 81-12-19, 86-6-15, 87-9-31, 87-9-32,
97-12-57, 20-08-21, 25-08-16)
 
Notes
1 1. 225 ILCS 305/1 et seq., and 225 ILCS 330/1 et seq., respectively.
6-1A-3: FEES:
6-1A-3-1: BUILDING PERMITS:
   A.   Building Construction: The fees to be charged for building permits for
erecting, altering, repairing or adding to buildings shall be twenty-five
dollars ($25.00) for any structure of which the estimated cost is not less than
one hundred dollars ($100.00) nor more than one thousand dollars ($1,000.00)
and twelve dollars fifty cents ($12.50) for each additional one thousand
dollars ($1,000.00) of estimated cost or part thereof.
   B.   Permit Renewal: Any building permit issued as herein provided, and
which has expired by the terms thereof as provided in subsection
6-1A-6B of this article, may be renewed by payment of an additional fee of
fifty percent (50%) of the original building permit and water/sewer fee.
      1.   If a permit expires by failure to begin construction operations
within six (6) months of the date of issuance of the permit, the permittee must
renew the permit within the succeeding six (6) months or all fees paid therefor
shall be forfeited to the village and the permit, along with zoning ordinance
variations if any, shall become null and void.
      2.   If a permit expires by failure to complete the building within one
year after the date of issuance of the permit, the permittee must renew the
permit within the succeeding three (3) months and before a final inspection is
made or the permits, along with the zoning ordinance variations if any, shall
become null and void. Furthermore, the permittee shall be required to restore
the property back to its original condition by removing any construction
thereon, and if the permittee does not do so, the village, through its
employees, or agents, shall remove same and recover its expenses by the filing
of a lien on the property.
   C.   Cancellation Of Permit: If a permit has been issued and all fees have
been paid and the permittee wishes to cancel the permit within a period of six
(6) months from the date of issuance, an amount in the sum of forty percent
(40%) of the building construction fee, or a minimum of ten twenty dollars
($120.00), whichever amount is greater, shall be retained by the village for
costs incurred by the village for plan review, processing, records and other
expenses.
   D.   Minor Improvement Permit Fees: Notwithstanding anything to the contrary
set forth in this article, a permit shall be required for minor improvements to
a structure or building with respect to roofing, siding and deck installation.
The fees for said permits shall be as follows:
 
Type Of Permit  Fee
Roofing
   Residentia$50.00
   Commercial$100.00
Siding          $50.00
Decking         $50.00
 
(1951 Code 21-2-12; Ords. 74-25-46, 76-16-30, 82-23-83; 1985 Code; Ords. 87-9-
32, 94-4-13, 05-23-86, 08-17-46, 08-19-55, 21-07-29)
6-1A-3-2: INSPECTIONS:
   A.   Generally: All work performed under any permit issued pursuant hereto
must be inspected and approved by the appropriate inspectors of the building
department. There shall be at least four (4) inspections on each single-family
residence building and as many as deemed necessary by the director of the
office of quality control on all other types of buildings. The fee for
inspections shall be as follows:
 
Single-family buildings:
New buildings            $ 50.00
Additions or alterations 50.00
Two-family buildings     100.00
Multiple-family          100.00
                         plus $30.00 per unit over 2 units
 
All other types of buildings based on gross square feet, as follows:
 
0 -      1,500     $ 75.00
1,501 -  2,500     100.00
2,501 -  5,000     125.00
5,001 -  7,500     150.00
7,501 -  10,000    175.00
10,001 - 50,000    200.00
   50,001 and ov250.00
 
The above fee schedule for buildings is based on normal construction practices
and use groups. If a building is in any way unique, then the inspection fees
shall be based on an estimated number of inspections necessary to adequately
ensure proper construction. A certificate of occupancy will only be issued
after all inspection fees have been paid to the village.
When the building being constructed is large, unique, complicated, or involves
extensive or unique structural problems, the director of the office of quality
control may bring in an independent, third party agency to perform the
inspections of the building in question. The cost for such independent, third
party agency inspections shall be borne by the permit applicant and is in
addition to any other fees charged by the village.
   B.   Reinspections: If a building fails to pass inspection and a
reinspection is necessary then a reinspection fee of forty dollars ($40.00)
shall be charged which shall be paid before the reinspection is made.
   C.   Stop Work Orders: If it is necessary to post and enforce a stop work
order, the stop work order will not be removed until a twenty five dollar
($25.00) fee is paid by the permittee. (Ords. 87-9-32, 94-4-13, 08-17-46, 08-
19-55, 21-07-29)
6-1A-3-3: DEMOLITION OF BUILDINGS:
The fees to be charged for demolishing entire existing buildings and related
structures shall be as follows:
 
Type Of Structure                          Fee     Cleanup Bond
Residential garage and accessory buildings $ 50.00 $ 100.00
All single-family homes                    150.00  500.00
All other structures                       250.00  1,000.00
 
Upon written recommendation of the building department and written
authorization by the village manager, fees and/or bonds may be modified or
waived in instances where it is determined that it is in the best interest of
the village to do so. (Ords. 87-9-32, 94-4-13, 05-23-86, 21-07-29)
6-1A-3-4: GARAGES:
The permit fee for garages of one hundred (100) square feet or more shall be
one hundred fifty dollars ($150.00). This fee shall be inclusive and include
all inspection and occupancy permit fees except optional electrical permits.
(Ords. 94-4-13, 05-23-86, 21-07-29)
6-1A-3-5: STREET AND PARKWAY RESTORATION BOND:
Before any building permits are issued by the village, a cash bond in the
amount of five hundred dollars ($500.00) shall be deposited with the village to
ensure the safety of the street and parkway areas during construction, and the
complete restoration and cleanup of the street and parkway. Said bond shall be
drawn upon by the village in the event that the depositor thereof fails to
restore or clean up the street and/or parkway after having been instructed to
do so, in writing, by the village. Upon completion of construction and all
street and parkway restoration and cleanup, the remainder of the bond shall be
returned to the depositor; provided, however, that nothing herein contained
shall preclude the village from maintaining an action against any person to
recover the damage done to any street or parkway area. (Ords. 87-9-32, 94-4-13)
6-1A-3-6: USE OF WATER DURING CONSTRUCTION:
In addition to the permit fees named in this section, there shall be paid the
fee for water used in connection with and during the construction of buildings
based on the following schedule:
   A.   The fee for single-family residential buildings shall be four hundred
seventy five dollars ($475.00) per building for water used in construction.
   B.   The fee for all other buildings shall be based on gross square foot
area of all floors of the building to be constructed, as follows:
   Square FeeFee    
   Square Feet  Fee    
0 - 1,500         $ 950.00
1,501 - 5,000     1,270.00
5,001 - 10,000    1,583.00
10,001 - 50,000   1,900.00
50,001 - 100,000  2,217.00
100,001 - 150,000 2,533.00
150,001 - 250,000 2,850.00
Over 250,000      3,167.00
 
(Ord. 08-19-55)
6-1A-3-7: EXEMPTIONS FROM PAYMENT OF FEES:
All building permit fees provided for in this chapter are waived as to all
governmental entities, churches, and eleemosynary institutions; provided,
however, that they shall be required to pay one hundred percent (100%) of the
plumbing and electrical inspection fees and one hundred percent (100%) of the
elevator review/inspection fees 1 . (Ord. 12-27-88)
 
Notes
1 1. See section
  6-2A-2 of this title for electrical inspection fees.
6-1A-4: BOND REQUIREMENTS:
   A.   Generally: The following bonds shall be deposited, in cash, with the
village before a building permit, electrical permit, or plumbing permit is
issued which requires said bonds. The bond inspection fee is for inspection of
the site and is not refundable. The cash bonds shall be refunded to the person
making the deposit within thirty (30) days after the operating departments have
inspected the site and have certified that all requirements of this chapter
have been complied with by the permittee. The cash bonds shall not be refunded
if work remains to be completed by the permittee to meet the requirements of
this chapter. If the permittee refuses or is unable to complete the work, the
village shall send written notice to the permittee, at the address listed on
the permit, that work shall be completed. If the work is not satisfactorily
completed within thirty (30) days after the notice, the village shall cause the
work to be completed, (including a 25 percent village administration fee) with
payment for said work to be deducted from the cash bonds and any remainder
refunded to the permittee. If the bonds are not sufficient to cover the
payment, the full amount of the bonds shall be forfeited and the permittee
shall be billed for the difference. Any bond remaining after four (4) years of
deposit shall be forfeited to the village to cover administrative costs.
   B.   Single-Family Residences; New Construction:
      1.   The general contractor shall deposit a five hundred dollar ($500.00)
cash bond for each single-family residence and shall pay a twenty five dollar
($25.00) inspection fee.
      2.   The drain layer and/or plumbing contractor shall deposit a five
hundred dollar ($500.00) cash bond for each single-family residence and shall
pay a twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee.
      3.   The electrical contractor shall deposit a five hundred dollar
($500.00) cash bond for each single-family residence and shall pay a twenty
five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee.
   C.   Single-Family Residences; Existing Residence:
      1.   Residential building which is to be owner occupied: The amount of
the bond shall be five hundred dollars ($500.00) where the amount of
construction work being permitted is five thousand dollars ($5,000.00) or more
and shall pay a twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee. For work less than
five thousand dollars ($5,000.00), the amount of the bond shall be two hundred
fifty dollars ($250.00) and shall pay a twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection
fee. An additional plumbing bond of five hundred dollars ($500.00) and
nonrefundable twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee and an additional
electrical bond of five hundred dollars ($500.00) along with a nonrefundable
twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee shall be required.
      2.   Vacant residential building which is to be non-owner occupied: The
amount of the bond shall be ten thousand dollars ($10,000.00) and a
nonrefundable twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee for any residential
building which is being rehabilitated where the building has been vacant just
prior to acquisition thereof and the building will be non-owner occupied after
rehabilitation. An additional plumbing bond of five hundred dollars ($500.00)
with a nonrefundable twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee and an
additional electrical bond of five hundred dollars ($500.00) with a
nonrefundable twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee shall be required.
This category covers buildings which are being substantially rehabilitated for
sale or for rent. A structure is "non-owner occupied" if the person or entity
which purchased the property and is making or contracting for the improvements
does not occupy the same as their principal residence for at least twelve (12)
months after the certificate of occupancy is issued. Such structure must be
entitled to a homeowner's exemption from the Cook County assessor for that
entire twelve (12) month period in order to be owner occupied.
   D.   Multiple-Family Buildings:
      1.   The general contractor shall deposit a cash bond for each building
in the amount shown in the following table.
      2.   The electrical contractor as well as the plumber and/or drain laying
contractor shall deposit an additional and separate cash bond for each building
in the amount shown in the following table.
      3.   In each case, a twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee shall be
paid for each bond required on each and every separate building.
 
Apartment Units      Amount Of
For Each Building    Cash Bond
2 units to 5 units   $ 750.00
6 units to 11 units  1,000.00
12 units to 23 units 2,000.00
24 units to 47 units 5,000.00
48 units and up      10,000.00
 
   E.   All Other Types Of Buildings:
      1.   The general contractor shall deposit a cash bond for each building
in the amount shown in the following table.
      2.   The electrical contractor, plumber and/or drain laying contractor
shall deposit an additional and separate cash bond for each building in the
amount shown in the following table.
      3.   In each case, a twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee shall be
paid for each bond required on each and every separate building.
 
Gross Square Foot Amount Of
Area Of Building  Cash Bond
0 - 4,999         $ 750.00
5,000 - 9,999     1,000.00
10,000 - 19,999   2,000.00
20,000 - 49,999   5,000.00
50,000 and over   10,000.00
 
   F.   Street Openings:
      1.   The plumber and drain layer contractor shall deposit a cash bond for
each street opening in the amount of four thousand dollars ($4,000.00). In each
case, a fifty dollar ($50.00) inspection fee shall be paid for each bond
required on each street opening.
   G.   Sidewalks:
      1.   A sidewalk installation bond shall be deposited with the village, in
cash, based upon ten dollars ($10.00) per linear foot. The sidewalk bond is in
addition to any other bond required in this chapter. A twenty five dollar
($25.00) inspection fee shall be paid for each bond required on each and every
separate site.
   H.   Occupation Of Streets During Construction:
      1.   Five thousand dollars ($5,000.00).
   I.   Permanent Bonds:
      1.   As there are many contractors who do work within the village
continuously through the years, a permanent bond may be deposited with the
village in lieu of separate bonds for each and every building, street opening
and sidewalk. The permanent bond will be refunded only after a written request
is received by the village. Said request shall list the address of the last
work done within the village. The bond will be refunded after one year has
passed from the issuance of the last permit which required a bond. It is
understood that a general contractor permanent bond will include the general
bond as well as the sidewalk bond for all buildings he may build within the
village. However, if the general contractor has a permanent bond of one
thousand dollars ($1,000.00) and wishes to construct multiple-family buildings,
he will be required to deposit additional money to bring his permanent bond up
to the required amount of the bond necessary for any given number of units
under multiple-family buildings as listed in the following table. If the
general contractor wishes to build a commercial building, he will be required
to deposit additional money to bring his permanent bond up to the required
amount of the bond necessary for any given gross square foot area as listed in
the following table. If the general contractor deposits a ten thousand dollar
($10,000.00) permanent cash bond with the village, he may build any type of
building, of any size, under the same permanent cash bond. If charges are made
against the permanent bond, no further permits shall be issued until the full
amount of the required permanent bond is restored. The following table shall
establish the type and amount of the permanent bonds required:
             Type Of Contractor
Type Of Bond General    Plumbing/Electrical/
             Contractor Drain Layer
                           Type Of Contractor
Type Of Bond               General    Plumbing/Electrical/
                           Contractor Drain Layer
Single-family residential  $ 1,000.00 $ 1,000.00
Multiple-family buildings:            
 2 units to 24 units      3,000.00   3,000.00
 24 units to 47 units     7,500.00   7,500.00
 48 units and up          10,000.00  10,000.00
All other buildings:                  
 0 - 20,000 sq. ft.       3,000.00   3,000.00
 20,000 sq. ft. and up    7,500.00   7,500.00
Street openings:                      
 3' - 8' water mains      7,500.00    
 
A twenty five dollar ($25.00) inspection fee shall be paid for each and every
permit used where a bond is required. (Ord. 13-17-65)
6-1A-5: ISSUANCE OF PERMIT, RESTRICTIONS:
   A.   Permits authorized to be issued and required to be obtained under this
chapter shall be promptly issued by the director of office of quality control
when the application, approval of plans, filing of frontage consents (if any
are required) and giving of notices are all completed as in this code provided.
But this shall not be construed to deny to the director ample time to
investigate any matters.
   B.   No permits herein provided to be issued for the erection, construction,
alteration, repair or excavation for any wall, structure, building or any
portion thereof, for which a fee is prescribed by this chapter shall be in
force until the fees prescribed therefor shall have been paid.
   C.   The director of office of quality control shall not issue any permit
authorizing the erection, construction, alteration, addition or repair of any
building or structure unless the plans submitted for his approval clearly show
that such building or structure with all its appurtenances, foundations and
attachments can be erected entirely within the limits of the lot or tract of
land upon which it is proposed to erect such building or structure, except as
provided by this chapter and the zoning ordinance of the village 1 , and no
permit to construct or alter any building or structure shall authorize the use
of any part of any public highway or other public ground for the construction
or maintenance of such building or structure except as provided by this chapter
2 , nor shall any permit be issued for the construction or maintenance of any
balcony or canopy extending over any public highway or other public ground
unless permits therefor have been obtained from the village board pursuant to
an ordinance specifically authorizing the same. (1951 Code 21-2-7, 21-2-8; Ord.
81-12-19)
 
Notes
1 1. See title 4 of this code.
2 2. See article B of this chapter.
6-1A-6: CONSTRUCTION COMMENCEMENT, REQUIREMENTS AND RESTRICTIONS:
   A.   Permit Card To Be Posted: As evidence that such permit has been issued,
the building permit card must be posted in the street side of the building when
excavation is started, and kept posted during the entire time of construction
and for ten (10) days after completion of the building.
   B.   Expiration Of Permit: If, after a permit has been granted, the
operations covered by said permit shall not be begun within six (6) months
after the date thereof, or if such operations are not completed within one year
from said date, then such permit shall expire by the terms thereof, and no
operations thereunder shall be begun or completed until a renewal of said
permit be obtained.
   C.   Deviations From Plans: It shall be unlawful to alter, or in any way
modify plans which have been approved by the director of office of quality
control. If during the progress of such work, it is desired to deviate, in any
manner affecting the construction or other essentials of the building, from the
terms of the application or drawing, notice of such intention to alter or
deviate shall be given to the director of office of quality control, and his
written assent shall first be obtained before the alteration or deviation may
be made; but alterations in buildings which do not involve any change in their
structural parts, or of their stairways, elevators, fire escapes or other
communications or ingress or egress, or in lighting or ventilation or in
plumbing or electric wiring, and that are not in violation of any of the
provisions of this chapter, or the provisions of the zoning ordinance, may be
made without the permission of the director of office of quality control. (1951
Code 21-2-4, 21-2-5, 21-2-9; Ord. 81-12-19)
6-1A-7: TEMPORARY PERMITS:
Where, in the judgment of the director of office of quality control, it is
justified, said director may authorize building operations to commence and
proceed for such limited time, not exceeding ten (10) days, as said director
may deem and fix as sufficient to allow the owner, his architect or agent to
complete the filing of detailed plans and other data required for the issuance
of the final permit. The application for a temporary permit shall furnish the
director of office of quality control with sufficient plans and data to enable
said director to determine that the work be commenced, and proposed
subsequently to be done, is of a lawful nature. Temporary permits shall be
revocable at the will of the director. (1951 Code 21-2-10; Ord. 81-12-19)
6-1A-8: EXEMPTIONS FROM PROVISIONS:
A permit will not be required for ordinary repair work incidental to the upkeep
of a building or structure; provided there is not contemplated nor involved,
any change in the classification or use or any increase in the fire hazard
thereof; nor the removal or installing of any external wall; nor the removal of
any supporting wall, column, post, truss, girder or beam; nor the installing,
closing or changing of any stairs, exits, scuttles, skylights or fire escapes;
nor changing the number, location or connections of the fixtures, traps, vents,
waste, soil or drain pipes of the plumbing system; nor changing the number,
location or connections of the fixtures of the electrical system.
Nothing in this section shall be interpreted or construed to allow any work
included in the permit exemptions hereof to be done in any manner contrary to
the requirements of this building code and other laws of the village. (1951
Code 21-2-6)
6-1A-9: CERTIFICATE OF OCCUPANCY FEE:
A fee of ten dollars ($10.00) shall be charged for each certificate of
occupancy as required by section
4-3-4 of this code. (Ord. 94-4-13)
6-1A-10: REAR LOT DRAINAGE DEPOSITS:
   A.   Findings: In regard to the issue of rear lot drainage, the president
and board of trustees find as follows:
      1.   That pursuant to this code, all property owners whose property is
determined by the director of quality control to have drainage problems must
have rear lot drainage installed at the time the property is subdivided and/or
built upon.
      2.   As an accommodation, the village allows subdividers and/or builders
to deposit money into the rear lot drainage fund, in lieu of constructing the
required rear lot drainage.
      3.   That even with the aforementioned deposits, the village is required
to subsidize the cost of installing rear lot drainage when said is installed.
   B.   Deposit Required: In lieu of installing rear lot drainage at the time
of the subdividing of the property or at the time building permits are
obtained, a subdivider and/or builder may make a nonrefundable deposit into the
rear lot drainage fund based on the following:
      1.   Twelve dollars ($12.00) per front foot of the lot(s) involved
relative to any R-1 or R-2 zoning district building permit.
      2.   Four dollars fifty cents ($4.50) per front foot of the lot(s)
involved relative to any subdivision application.
   C.   Use Of Funds: Funds deposited in the rear lot drainage fund shall be
used exclusively by the village for the construction of rear lot drainage at
those locations as determined by the village most in need of rear lot drainage.
(Ord. 94-4-13)
6-1A-11: PROTECTION OF SIDEWALKS AND STREETS DURING CONSTRUCTION:
During the construction of any building or structure on any property within the
village, if the portion of the property immediately abutting any street or
sidewalk, including the parkway area between the street and sidewalk, is not
landscaped with either a lawn or other landscaping materials, so as to prevent
erosion and the deposit of dirt and/or sediment upon such street or sidewalk,
the owner of the property in question, as well as any contractors working
thereon, shall be responsible for the proper erection and maintenance of a silt
fence at all locations where the property, including the parkway area between
the street and the sidewalk, abuts a street or sidewalk. Said silt fences shall
be maintained in proper working order until a lawn, or other landscaping
materials is/are installed to prevent the deposit of dirt and/or sediment upon
the street or sidewalk. (Ord. 96-19-88)
6-1A-12: CONTRACTOR LICENSING AND REGISTRATION, GENERALLY:
   A.   It shall be unlawful for a person to act or perform any work or
services as a contractor or subcontractor within the Village without first
having been licensed by or registered with the Village's Department of
Community Development, as herein provided. All contractor's licenses and
registrations under this Article A will be valid for one (1) year from the date
of issuance.
   B.   All contractors and subcontractors, as defined in Section
6-1A-14, performing work or services within the village shall comply with the
provisions of the Village Code and all ordinances for minimum building
standards regulated within the corporate limits of the village and shall comply
with all licensing and registration requirements of the State of Illinois and
the Village, including without limitation Sections
6-1A-12 through
6-1A-19 of this Article.
   C.   All general contractors and subcontractors licensed or registered under
Sections
6-1A-12 through
6-1A-19 of this Article A shall be responsible for any and all employees
working for them under the license or registration.
   D.   All general contractors shall hire only subcontractors who are licensed
through the State of Illinois when and if specified by the Illinois Compiled
Statutes (ILCS).
   E.   Licenses and registrations are issued for a twelve (12)-month period.
At the expiration of the period, the license or registration must be renewed to
continue performing work within the Village.
   F.   The contractor shall be solely responsible for the timely renewal of
their contractor's license or registration on an annual basis. It is expressly
understood that the Village is under no obligation to provide notice or
reminders to the contractor regarding the renewal of the license or
registration. The contractor is expected to be aware of and adhere to all
applicable renewal deadlines and procedures as set forth by the municipality.
(Ord. 25-08-16)
6-1A-13: CONTRACTOR LICENSE AND REGISTRATION APPLICATION:
   A.   Application required. All applications for a village contractor's
license or registration shall be made in writing or submitted electronically
upon forms furnished by the village.
   B.   Each application shall state:
      1.   The name, physical business address, business phone number, email
address, and type of legal business structure (Individual/Sole Proprietorship,
Partnership, or Corporation).
      2.   Whether the application is for a general contractor or subcontractor
license or registration. If it is a general contractor with tradesmen, a list
of trades and tradesmen performing each trade shall be provided. A
subcontractor will be required to list the trade or trades involved.
      3.   The application must be signed.
   C.   Electrical and Alarm Contractors are required to provide the name of
the Supervising Electrician, Type of License, city it was Issued, and License
number.
   D.   Plumbing and Lawn Sprinkler Contractors must provide and maintain a
current copy of their City of Chicago or State of Illinois Plumbing License
with the Village's Department of Community Development.
   E.   Roofing, Fire Alarm, and Fire Sprinkler contractors must provide and
maintain a current copy of their State License with the Village's Department of
Community Development.
   F.   Each application shall include a valid Certificate of Insurance, as
required in Section
6-1A-17 of this Article. (Ord. 25-08-16)
6-1A-14: CONTRACTOR DEFINITIONS:
For the purposes of contractor licensing and registration under this Article,
the following definitions shall apply, unless the context clearly indicates or
requires a different meaning.
CONTRACTOR: Any person, partnership, corporation or other business entity
engaged in the business of constructing, altering or repairing buildings or
other structures or lots and all trades and crafts associated with such work,
including but not restricted to, general building contractors, excavating
contractors, carpentry contractors, hearing, air conditioning, ventilating,
mechanical, electrical and plumbing contractors and "subcontractor" as defined
below.
SUBCONTRACTOR: A contractor who performs only the type of work that is
classified as a specific trade, such as, but not limited to, carpentry,
roofing, and electrical. (Ord. 25-08-16)
6-1A-15: LICENSE AND REGISTRATION FEES:
Except as provided in Section
6-1A-15, the annual fee for all contractors' licenses and registrations is one
hundred dollars ($100.00).
The license or registration is valid for one year from the date of issuance.
Yearly renewal is required if the Contractor continues to perform work within
the Village. (Ord. 25-08-16)
6-1A-16: CONTRACTOR LICENSE FEE EXEMPTION:
   A.   The following contractors must be licensed by the State of Illinois if
they are performing any work within the Village boundaries:
      1.   Roofing;
      2.   Fire Alarm;
      3.   Fire Sprinkler;
      4.   Plumbing;
      5.   Interior designer;
      6.   Lawn Sprinkler; and
      7.   Residential maintenance landscaper;
   Contractors listed in this section must still register with the Village's
Department of Community Development on an annual basis. Registration is valid
for one year from the date of contractor registration issuance. There is no
annual fee for registration.
   B.   A contractor who has obtained a valid business license from the
Village, and who has paid the applicable business license fee, shall be exempt
from paying a separate contractor license or registration fee, provided that
the business license covers all activities conducted by the contractor within
the scope of their professional work This exemption shall apply only if the
contractor's business license is current, active, and in good standing, and it
is specifically authorized by the Village's business licensing regulations. The
contractor shall remain subject to all other requirements of contractor
licensing and registration, including compliance with applicable laws,
regulations, and industry standards. In the event that the contractor's
business license is revoked, suspended, or otherwise invalidated, the
contractor shall be required to pay the appropriate contractor license fee, in
addition to any other applicable fees, before being permitted to perform work
requiring such a license or registration within the Village. (Ord. 25-08-16)
6-1A-17: CONTRACTOR INSURANCE REQUIREMENTS:
All contractors must provide and maintain with the Village's Department of
Community Development a valid certificate of insurance naming the Village as a
certificate holder and evidencing general liability coverage five hundred
thousand dollars ($500,000.00) per occurrence minimum) and the State of
Illinois Mandated Workers Compensation Coverage. (Ord. 25-08-16)
6-1A-18: ISSUANCE OF BUILDING PERMITS:
All contractors and subcontractors must be licensed or registered in accordance
with this Article prior to being issued any building permits by the Village.
Any change in the general contractor or subcontractors once a permit has been
issued shall be put in writing by the general contractor, or by the
subcontractor if no general contractor is present, within three business days
of such change, and delivered to the Department of Community Development. (Ord.
25-08-16)
6-1A-19: LICENSE REVOCATION AND REINSTATEMENT:
Any license or registration issued under the provisions of this Article A may
be revoked by the Community Development Director as deemed appropriate for
violation(s) of any of the provisions of Title 6 of the Village Code that is
not remedied within fourteen (14) days from the date of a notice of violation
(s), or within any extension of time as determined by the Community Development
Director in his/her discretion. Such revocation shall be in addition to fines
or penalties imposed and subject to the following:
   A.   No person whose license or registration has been revoked as herein
provided shall be eligible for a reinstated or new license for a period of up
to one year, to be determined by the Community Development Director based upon
the severity of the violation. A fifty dollar ($50.00) administrative fee shall
be paid to the village prior to any approved contractor license or registration
reinstatement.
   B.   All appeals from the action of the Community Development Director to
revoke a contractor's or subcontractor's license or registration shall be filed
within ten (10) days of such action with the Village Manager who shall conduct
a hearing on such revocation.
   C.   The Community Development Director when deemed appropriate may refuse
to issue or reinstate a license in the event that any of the provisions of this
Article A, which are conditions precedent to the issuance of the license, have
not been met. Contractors and subcontractors have the right to appeal the
denial of a license or registration by filing an appeal with the Village
Manager within ten (10) business days of the denial. The Village Manager shall
conduct a hearing on such appeal. (Ord. 25-08-16)
ARTICLE B. OCCUPATION OF STREETS
SECTION:
6-1B-1: Permit Required
6-1B-2: Application For Permit, Issuance
6-1B-3: Bond
6-1B-4: Term Of Permit
6-1B-5: Occupation Requirements And Restrictions
6-1B-1: PERMIT REQUIRED:
It shall be unlawful to deposit or store upon the street, parkway, sidewalk or
alley abutting upon or adjacent to the lot or tract, upon which any building or
structure is being erected, constructed, altered or repaired, or upon any
street, parkway, sidewalk or alley within the village, any building materials,
tools, apparatus or structure designed or intended to be used in the erection,
construction, alteration or repair of buildings, unless a street occupation
permit is first obtained from the director of office of quality control. Unless
such street occupation permit is obtained, all such building materials, tools,
apparatus or structures, when first delivered, shall be placed entirely within
the boundary lines of such lot or tract, and shall so remain until the
completion of such building or structure or until finally removed from such lot
or tract. (1951 Code 21-3-14; Ord. 81-12-19)
6-1B-2: APPLICATION FOR PERMIT, ISSUANCE:
All applications for street occupation permits shall be made to the director of
office of quality control and shall describe the location of the street,
parkway, sidewalk or alley proposed to be occupied, the use to be made of the
same, and the length of time of such intended occupation.
Street occupation permits shall be issued subject to the provisions of this
article as to bonds 1 , fees and all other requisites in connection with such
permit. (1951 Code 21-3-15; Ord. 81-12-19)
 
Notes
1 1. See section
  6-1A-3-5 of this chapter.
6-1B-3: BOND:
Before the director of office of quality control shall issue a street
obstruction permit, the applicant for such permit shall file with the village
clerk, on a form to be furnished by the village and approved by the village
attorney, a bond as provided in subsection 6-1A-4H of this chapter and
conditioned to indemnify, save and keep harmless the village from any and all
loss, costs, expense or liability of any kind whatsoever which the village may
suffer or be put to, or which may be recovered from it by reason of the
issuance of such permit, or by reason of any act or thing done, or neglected to
be done under or by virtue of the authority given in such permit. An annual
bond may be given to cover street obstruction permits. (1951 Code 21-3-19)
6-1B-4: TERM OF PERMIT:
The permission to occupy streets, parkways, sidewalks or alleys for the
purposes of building, is intended only for imperative use in connection with
the actual erection, repair, alteration or removal of buildings, and shall
terminate with the completion of such operation and at as much earlier time as
shall be feasible, at which time the person to whom the occupation permit has
been issued shall remove all materials and equipment and shall place the
street, parkway, sidewalk or alley in as neat and serviceable condition as
prior to the occupation thereof. (1951 Code 21-3-16)
6-1B-5: OCCUPATION REQUIREMENTS AND RESTRICTIONS:
   A.   Extent Of Occupation: The occupation of a street for the storage of
building materials shall never extend more than one- third (1/3) of the way
across the street pavement or alley, nor to a greater extent than is authorized
by the permit.
   B.   Location: It shall not be lawful to occupy with building materials, any
street, parkway, sidewalk or alley or any part thereof, other than that
immediately in front of or adjoining the lot or land upon which the building is
being erected; provided, that if the written consent of the owners, tenants or
occupants of properties adjoining the site of any proposed building is first
obtained and filed with the director of office of quality control, the
occupation of the street, parkway, sidewalk or alley may be extended in front
of the property so consenting, upon the same terms and conditions as those
fixed for the occupation of the street in front of the building site.
   C.   Mixing Concrete Prohibited: It shall be unlawful to mix mortar,
concrete or any other building material upon the surface of any sidewalk in the
village.
   D.   Sidewalk To Be Provided: During the progress of building operations, a
sidewalk not less than five feet (5') in width, or an equal width, of a clear,
hard roadway, made available and safe for a footway, and with adequate
guardrailings, shall at all times be kept open, clean and unobstructed for the
purpose and uses of a passageway in front of such a lot or lots.
   E.   Driveways: It is not intended hereby to prohibit the maintenance of a
driveway for the delivery of material across a sidewalk from the street to the
building site; but such driveway shall be under the supervision of the director
of office of quality control.
   F.   Barricades And Lights:
      1.   Any person to whom a building or street occupation permit is issued
shall erect and maintain such substantial and suitable fences, railings or
barricades, to guard all excavations, embankments or obstructions along the
street, parkway, sidewalk or alley abutting upon or adjacent to such lot or
tract as the director of office of quality control deems necessary for the
protection of the lives and limbs of the public; and shall place and maintain
proper and sufficient red lights to protect the public using such street or
sidewalk. Such lights shall be placed at each end of every such obstruction or
excavation and at intervals of not more than seventy five feet (75') along the
same.
      2.   Any person who maliciously or wantonly, and without legal cause,
removes, extinguishes, diminishes or disturbs such light or removes or disturbs
such barricades or lights, shall be deemed guilty of a misdemeanor.
   G.   Cleaning Streets: The permittee shall be, responsible for maintaining
the streets in a reasonably clean condition. However, as construction work does
produce mud and dirt on the streets and in storm sewer catch basins, there
shall be a fee paid for street cleaning before a permit is issued 1 . (1951
Code 21-3-17, 21-3-18; 1969 Code 6-52; Ord. 81-12-19)
 
Notes
1 1. See section
  6-1A-3-5 of this chapter.
ARTICLE C. ONE- AND TWO-FAMILY BUILDINGS
SECTION:
6-1C-1: Regulations Adopted
6-1C-2: Additions, Insertions, Deletions And Changes
6-1C-3: Screened Room Additions
6-1C-1: REGULATIONS ADOPTED:
The international residential code for one- and two-family dwellings, 2018
edition, published by the International Code Council, is hereby adopted by
reference and made a part of this article with the same force and effect as if
it were specifically and particularly set forth herein, and all regulations
contained therein, with the additions, insertions, deletions and changes
prescribed in section
6-1C-2 of this article, shall apply to all buildings for residential purposes.
One copy of said code shall be retained on file in the office of the clerk.
Where any provision of the international residential code for one- and two-
family dwellings, 2018 edition, conflict with any provisions of this village
code, the provisions of the village code shall prevail and shall be applied as
law of the village. (Ords. 14-04-10, 21-07-29)
6-1C-2: ADDITIONS, INSERTIONS, DELETIONS AND CHANGES:
The provisions of the international residential code for one- and two-family
dwellings, 2012 edition, are revised as follows:
   A.   Single-family buildings shall have the following minimum area, unless
otherwise provided in the zoning ordinance 1 :
1 floor, 2 bedrooms:       
 Without basement        1,008 sq. ft.
 With basement           950 sq. ft.
1 floor, 3 bedrooms:       
 Without basement        1,120 sq. ft.
 With basement           1,064 sq. ft.
11/2 story:                
 2nd floor not finished:  
 Without basement        1,008 sq. ft.
 With basement           950 sq. ft.
 2nd floor finished:      
 Without basement        900 sq. ft.
 With basement           850 sq. ft.
2 story:                   
 Without basement        672 sq. ft.
 With basement           622 sq. ft.
 
   B.   The following sections are revised as follows:
Part 1 - Administrative; Chapter 4 - Foundations
R-105.2 Work Exempt From Permit.
One-story detached accessory structures, provided the floor area does not
exceed 100 square feet.
Fences not over 2 feet high.
Sidewalks and Driveways.
R-309.5 Delete the entire section.
R-313 Delete the entire section.
R-402.1 Delete "wood foundations" from the paragraph.
R-403.1 Amend to read "All exterior walls shall be supported on solid concrete
footings or other approved structural systems which...".
R-403.1.1 The minimum spread footing size shall be 10" thick, 20" wide.
R-404.1 Delete the entire paragraph (Masonry foundation walls).
R-404.1.1 Delete the entire paragraph (Masonry foundation walls).
R-404.2 Delete the entire section through 404.2.6 - Wood Foundation Walls.
Part IV - Energy Conservation
Chapter 11 - Energy Efficiency Delete in its entirety.
Part IV - Plumbing
Chapters 25 through 33 Delete in their entirety.
(See Chicago plumbing code, 2003)
Part VIII - Electrical
Chapters 34 through 43 Delete in their entirety.
(See Chicago electrical code, 2013)
(Ords. 14-04-10, 14-27-62, 21-07-29)
 
Notes
1 1. See title 4 of this code.
6-1C-3: SCREENED ROOM ADDITIONS:
Notwithstanding the requirements of the regulations adopted pursuant to section
6-1C-1 of this article, a screened room addition to a single-family home may be
constructed without a forty two inch (42") deep concrete foundation provided
the following conditions are complied with:
   A.   The screened room addition shall be constructed with only framed
screens and no glass windows;
   B.   The nonframed screen portion of the screened room addition shall be of
a prefabricated manufactured construction with metallic (i.e., steel, aluminum)
roof rafters with metal leg supports, and with a covering of fiberglass,
canvas, aluminum or other durable material;
   C.   The screened room addition shall be constructed on a deck or concrete
slab, and the contractor constructing said screened room addition shall
certify, as part of the building permit application process, that the
contractor has examined the deck or concrete slab and has determined that it
will be structurally able to support the screened room addition;
   D.   The total area of the screened room addition shall not exceed two
hundred fifty (250) square feet;
   E.   The screened room addition shall not be located in the front yard;
   F.   No masonry construction or gypsum finishing shall be used in regard to
the screened room addition;
   G.   The screened room addition must comply with all applicable setback
regulations of this code;
   H.   The screened room addition must be constructed in full compliance with
all applicable wind and snow load regulations of this code;
   I.   No plumbing fixtures (i.e., sink, shower, tub or toilet) or piping may
be added to the screened room addition;
   J.   The screened room addition may not be artificially heated through the
use of electric baseboard heaters, natural gas forced air heating or other
artificial heating device; and
   K.   The screened room addition, once constructed, may not be further
enclosed.
Upon completion of the construction of a screened room addition that does not
have a forty two inch (42") deep concrete foundation, the village shall record,
at the property owner's expense, a notice, setting forth the foregoing
conditions applicable to the screened room addition, so that any subsequent
purchaser of the property on which the screened room addition has been
constructed will be put on notice in relation to the conditions applicable to
said screened room addition. (Ord. 05-12-37)
ARTICLE D. MULTI-FAMILY, COMMERCIAL, ASSEMBLY, MANUFACTURING AND PUBLIC
BUILDINGS
SECTION:
6-1D-1: Regulations Adopted
6-1D-2: Amendments To Code
6-1D-3: Facing Material (Rep. by Ord. 90-11-45)
6-1D-4: Emergency Lighting Systems
6-1D-5: Minimum Heating Requirements
6-1D-6: Limited Area Sprinkler Systems (Rep. by Ord. 97-20-78)
6-1D-7: Air Conditioning Requirements
6-1D-1: REGULATIONS ADOPTED:
The international building code, 2018 edition, published by the International
Code Council, is hereby adopted by reference and made a part of this article
with the same force and effect as if it were specifically and particularly set
forth herein, and all regulations contained therein, with the additions,
insertions, deletions and changes prescribed in section 6-1D-2 of this article
shall apply to building construction of multi- family, commercial, assembly,
manufacturing and public buildings in the village. One copy of said code shall
be retained on file in the office of the clerk. (Ords. 14-04-10, 21-07-29)
6-1D-2: AMENDMENTS TO CODE:
Where any provisions of the international building code, 2018 edition, conflict
with any provisions of this village code, the provisions of this village code
shall prevail and shall be applied as the law of the village. The following
sections and tables of said international building code, 2018 edition, are
amended as follows:
Sections 1001 through 1029 of the international building code are amended in
the same manner as sections 903 through 910 of the international fire code as
set forth below.
Section 3001.0 Tests And Inspections - Is amended by adding a subsection 3001.5
which shall read as follows:
3001.5 - Inspection And Testing Fees. The fees associated with the testing and
inspection of elevators shall be as follows:
Plan Review:                                                         
 New elevator construction                                         $100.00
 Elevator rehabilitation and upgrade                               $100.00
Testing And Inspection:                                              
 Initial test and inspection for new construction                  $100.00
 Regular semi-annual test and inspection for new construction      $75.00
 Full load governor safety test                                    $85.00
 Reinspection relative to regular semi-annual test and inspection  $60.00
 Reinspection other than in regard to regular semi-annual test and $85.00
  inspection
 
3002.4 Elevator Car To Accommodate Ambulance Stretcher - Is amended by deleting
the following language:
four or more stories above, or four or more stories below, grade plane.
Chapter 27 is deleted. Refer to village code section 6-2-3A.
Chapter 29 is deleted. Refer to village code section 6-3-2A.
(Ords. 14-04-10, 15-16-64, 21-07-29)
6-1D-3: FACING MATERIAL:
(Rep. by Ord. 90-11-45)
6-1D-4: EMERGENCY LIGHTING SYSTEMS:
In all retail stores, commercial buildings and office buildings where the floor
area of the entire building exceeds two thousand (2,000) square feet or larger
in floor area, and in all places of public assembly that accommodate fifty (50)
or more individuals, an emergency lighting system shall be installed. Said
emergency lighting system is to be energized by either an auxiliary electrical
generation system or by an electrical storage battery system whereby either are
activated automatically upon the failure of the normal source and supply of
electrical energy. Emergency illumination of sufficient lumens shall be
provided for all stairways, staircases, exit doors, internal fire hose
locations, exit aisles and corridors. (Ords. 533, 66-23-62, 78-14-29; 1985
Code)
6-1D-5: MINIMUM HEATING REQUIREMENTS:
Every family unit or rooming unit to which heat is furnished from a heating
plant used in common for the purpose of heating the various rooms of the
dwelling shall be supplied with heat from September 15 of each year to June 1
of the succeeding year so that the occupants of a family unit or rooming unit
may secure, without such undue restrictions, a minimum temperature of sixty
five degrees Fahrenheit (65°F) averaged throughout the family unit or rooming
unit. (Ord. 16-02-09)
6-1D-6: LIMITED AREA SPRINKLER SYSTEMS:
(Rep. by Ord. 97-20-78)
6-1D-7: AIR CONDITIONING REQUIREMENTS:
In every family unit or room unit in any multi-family building, where air
conditioning is provided in said family unit or rooming unit by way of either
central air conditioning or wall/window unit air conditioner(s), said central
air conditioning or wall/window unit air conditioner(s) shall be in good
working order so as to provide air conditioning from May 1 through September 30
of each year. In the case of a multi-family rental unit building, it shall be
the obligation of the owner, landlord and/or property manager of said building
to see that the family units or room units within said building comply with
this section. (Ord. 87-19-78)
ARTICLE E. PRIVATE GARAGES
SECTION:
6-1E-1: Compliance With Provisions
6-1E-2: Permit Required, Term
6-1E-3: Attached Garages
6-1E-4: Detached Garages
6-1E-5: Protection of Egress Facilities
6-1E-1: COMPLIANCE WITH PROVISIONS:
   A.   Generally: Every structure erected for or converted to the purpose of
private garages shall comply with the following special provisions in addition
to the general provisions of this Chapter.
   B.   Garages Near Residences: Private garages located within ten feet (10')
of any residence shall be constructed in accordance with Sections
6-1E-2 and
6-1E-3 of this Article as applicable thereto. (1951 Code 21-7-31, 21-7-33; Ord.
307)
6-1E-2: PERMIT REQUIRED, TERM:
The term shall be obtained prior to installation of the garage foundation or
slab and shall be valid for a period of two (2) years to complete the structure
1 . (1951 Code 21-7-33; Ord. 307)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  6-1A-3-4 of this Code, permit fees.
6-1E-3: ATTACHED GARAGES:
   A.   One or Two-Family Buildings: Private garages located beneath a one or
two-family dwelling, or attached thereto shall have walls, partitions, floors
and ceilings separating the garage space from the dwelling, constructed of not
less than one hour fire-resistance, with the sill of the door opening between
them raised not less than six inches (6") above the garage floor. The door
opening protectives shall be one hour fire doors complying with acceptable fire
resistive standards or one and three-quarters' inch (13/4") solid core wood
doors with a metal covering of twenty two (22) gauge or heavier applied to wood
passage doors between garage and house.
   B.   Strip Carports and Garages: Carports and/or garages that are arranged
so that they are in a continuous row (strip carports and/or garages), detached
and free-standing, shall be separated by masonry fire walls into bays of four
(4) cars or less to prevent car-to-car ignition by fire, along the entire row
of cars.
   C.   Motels and Multi-Family Buildings:
      1.   Private parking garages under or within motels or multi-family
buildings with a total number of car stalls of eight (8) cars or less, and in
which no gasoline or oil is stored or handled, shall have a one and one-half
(11/2) hour fire-rated ceiling, walls, columns, beams and structural elements.
The cars shall be arranged in isolated rows of four (4) cars or less, separated
by masonry fire wall separation, then the provisions of paragraph 2 shall be
followed.
      2.   Private parking garages under or within motels or multi-family
buildings with a total number of car stalls of sixteen (16) or less, and in
which no gasoline or oil is stored or handled, shall have a minimum one and
one- half (11/2) hour rated precast concrete ceiling. The walls, columns, beams
and all structural elements shall be of noncombustible construction and shall
be protected with a one and one-half (11/2) hour fire-rated assembly. A
positive exhaust system shall be designed and installed in compliance with
applicable Mechanical Code requirements 1 .
      3.   Private parking garages under or within motels or multi-family
buildings with a total number of car stalls of seventeen (17) or more, and in
which no gasoline or oil is stored or handled, shall have a precast concrete
ceiling. The ceiling, walls, columns, beams and all structural elements shall
be of noncombustible construction and shall be protected with a one and one-
half (11/2) hour fire-rated assembly. The exterior walls of the parking area
shall contain openings equal to twenty five percent (25%) of the square footage
of all perimeter walls of the parking area, arranged for cross ventilation to
dissipate heat and smoke generated from vehicle fires within the parking area.
Where the required wall openings are not provided, the parking area shall be
protected with a complete sprinkler system and a positive exhaust system shall
be installed in compliance with the Mechanical Code. (1951 Code 21-7-33; Ords.
307, 78-14-29)
 
Notes
1 1. See Chapter 4 of this Title.
6-1E-4: DETACHED GARAGES:
Private garages separated from frame residence buildings by a breezeway not
less than ten feet (10') in length may be of unprotected frame (type 4-B)
construction but the junction of garage and breezeway shall be firestopped and
constructed of not less than one hour fire-resistive material. (1951 Code 21-7-
33; Ord. 307)
6-1E-5: PROTECTION OF EGRESS FACILITIES:
Where living quarters are located above a private garage, required egress
facilities shall be protected from the garage area with three quarters' (3/4)
hour fire-resistive construction. (1951 Code 21-7-33; Ord. 307)
ARTICLE F. VEHICLE STORAGE AND REPAIR BUILDINGS; GAS STATIONS
SECTION:
6-1F-1: Compliance With Provisions
6-1F-2: Housing Vehicles Containing Fuel
6-1F-3: Electrical Regulations
6-1F-4: Disposal of Oil and Grease
6-1F-1: COMPLIANCE WITH PROVISIONS:
Every building erected or converted to the purpose of motor vehicle storage
and/or repair and gas station shall comply with the following special
provisions in addition to the general provisions of this Chapter. (1951 Code
21-9-53)
6-1F-2: HOUSING VEHICLES CONTAINING FUEL:
Vehicles containing volatile inflammable liquid shall not be housed above any
room containing a boiler, forge, emery wheel or flame soldering iron heater
unless such room has a ceiling constructed of concrete or other impervious
materials, or unless the floor of the room in which such vehicles are housed is
constructed of or surfaced with concrete or other impervious materials. (1951
Code 21-9-61)
6-1F-3: ELECTRICAL REGULATIONS:
   A.   All nonportable electric machinery or appliances not actually a part of
a vehicle shall be located not less than four feet (4') above the floor.
   B.   Lighting shall be by means of incandescent electric lamps only. Movable
lights shall be protected by vapor-tight globes enclosed in approved metal
cages and shall be fitted with approved keyless sockets and portable cords.
   C.   All electric switches and blocks shall be permanently located at least
four feet (4') above the floor in rooms where vehicles containing volatile
inflammable liquids are housed, and shall be enclosed in a fireproof cabinet.
(1951 Code 21-9-62)
6-1F-4: DISPOSAL OF OIL AND GREASE:
There shall be no direct connection between any garage waste basin, sink, floor
drain or waste pipe and any house drainage or sewer system. All such drains or
waste mains to the sewer system shall have intercepting grease and oil traps or
separators which will completely separate such substances from water and
sewerage and allow their safe and convenient removal. Such traps shall be
adequately ventilated. (1951 Code 21-9-63)
ARTICLE G. SIDEWALKS, DRIVEWAYS AND DRIVEWAY APRONS
SECTION:
6-1G-1: Sidewalks
6-1G-2: Driveways and Driveway Aprons
6-1G-3: Sidewalk, Driveway and Driveway Apron Construction Materials and
Standards
6-1G-1: SIDEWALKS:
   A.   General: Whenever a permit is issued by the Village for "development",
as defined in Section
6-14A-2 of the Village Code, including any reconstruction, rehabilitation, or
repair of an existing structure damaged in an amount that reduces the property
value by fifty percent (50%) or more, or any construction, reconstruction or
addition to an existing structure that increases the property value by fifty
percent (50%) or more, sidewalks shall be constructed as part of said
development by the developer, installer/contractor or property owner along all
streets adjacent to said property, except as otherwise provided in this
Article. If a street is planned for widening within five (5) years of the date
of final development approval, the developer, installer/contractor or property
owner shall, in lieu of constructing said sidewalk, place funds for sidewalk
construction in escrow with the Village in the amount of one hundred twenty
five percent (125%) of the estimated cost, as approved by the Director of
Quality Control.
   B.   Public Streets:
      1.   All collector and major or minor streets in residential districts
with average lot sizes of twenty thousand (20,000) square feet and under, or in
commercial and industrial districts located within walking distance of
pedestrian destinations or with frontages averaging less than two hundred feet
(200'): a minimum five foot (5') wide sidewalk with a five foot (5') wide
planting/green strip, is required on both sides of the street and around cul-
de-sacs. Wider sidewalks may be required in some commercial areas.
      2.   Streets in residential districts with average lot sizes exceeding
twenty thousand (20,000) square feet, or averaging a minimum of one hundred
fifty feet (150') of frontage: no sidewalks are required, except for sidewalks
along streets which serve as the primary collector streets serving at least
thirty (30) homes.
      3.   Any street within two (2) blocks of a school site, park or public
building that is determined by the Director of Quality Control to be a walking
route to those destinations: sidewalks are required on both sides of the
street.
      4.   New streets: sidewalks may be omitted on only one side if that side
cannot be developed and there are no existing or anticipated uses that would
generate pedestrian trips on that side.
      5.   Rights of way which are below standard widths: sidewalk installation
is subject to Village Engineer review.
   C.   Private Streets: The same sidewalk standards are required for private
streets as for public streets, unless other design guidelines are applied for
as part of a planned unit development.
   D.   Location: The location of sidewalks shall generally be one foot (1')
inside of the right-of-way boundary line. The location may be modified,
depending on factors that include, but are not limited to: relationship to
drainage ditches, easements, public utility rights of way and towers,
handicapped ramps, existing trees, landscaping, and topography. (Ord. 94-10-44)
6-1G-2: DRIVEWAYS AND DRIVEWAY APRONS:
   A.   Driveways Across Sidewalks and Parkways: No person, firm or corporation
shall construct or alter any driveway over, across or upon any public sidewalk
or parkway strip without first obtaining a permit from the Department of
Quality Control. Where ingress and egress is to be made from adjoining real
estate to a public street and where the Code requires off-street parking, such
off-street parking shall be made accessible to the public street and the
ingress and egress shall be made across the parkway strip and sidewalks by
means of a driveway constructed in accordance with this Article.
   B.   Permit Application: Application for a permit to construct a driveway
shall be on forms provided by the Department of Quality Control. The
application shall designate the location of the proposed driveway, the name and
address of the applicant, the name and address of the owner of the property to
be served by the proposed driveway, and the address of the applicant, if other
than the owner, and a plat of survey indicating the driveway location and
dimensions proposed. In those instances where a driveway is required to comply
with the requirements of the off-street parking and loading sections (Section
4-13-1 et seq.) of this Code, the permit application shall accompany the
application for the issuance of a building permit authorizing the new building
construction.
   C.   Permit Issuance and Fees: The Department of Quality Control shall issue
a permit to construct a driveway provided that the permit application is
complete, is in accordance with these regulations and the applicable permit fee
has been paid.
   D.   Permit Revocation: All permits for driveways issued pursuant to this
Article may be revoked at any time without the consent of the permittee by
order of the Director of the Department of Quality Control. A permit may be
revoked for failure of a permittee to comply with any of the provisions of this
Article or failure to comply with any permit-related provision of the Code. A
notice of noncompliance, setting forth the Code provision(s) violated, shall be
mailed by the Director via certified mail, or hand delivered, to the permittee
and the permittee shall have five (5) business days from the date of receipt of
said notice to cure any such Code violation. If the permittee fails to cure
said Code violation in the time period provided or if remedial action by the
permittee is insufficient to remove said Code violation, and the Director
revokes said permit, the permittee may appeal the revocation decision to the
Appeals and Review Board in accordance with the provisions set forth in Section
2-2-2 of this Code. Upon such revocation, all rights granted under the permit
shall be revoked, and if no appeal is taken, the sidewalk, parkway strips and/
or curbs shall be immediately restored to their former condition, at the
expense of the permittee and/or of the owner of the property served by the
driveway at the time of such revocation. If an appeal is taken, it shall be the
permittee's responsibility to barricade the construction site in accordance
with Section
9-1A-6 of this Code for the duration of the appeal.
   E.   Maximum Number: Single-family residences shall be permitted to have
only one driveway, unless an increase in the number of allowable driveways is
approved by the Board of Trustees upon recommendation of the Appeals and Review
Board. All other uses shall be allowed as approved by the Board of Trustees
upon recommendation of the Zoning and Planning Commission.
   F.   Minimum Length And Width: The minimum driveway length shall be from the
street access to a point forty feet (40') behind the minimum setback line, and
the minimum width of said driveway shall be nine feet (9') at the sidewalk line
and fifteen feet (15') at the curb. When the only parking space(s) located on a
driveway is/are in the front yard, the minimum driveway length, as provided by
this subsection, shall not be applicable to the driveway.
   G.   Maximum Width: The maximum width for driveways for single- family
residences shall be twenty feet (20') at the sidewalk line (or property line if
no sidewalk exists) and twenty six feet (26') at the curb line; provided,
however, that if the driveway is in conjunction with a three (3) car garage,
the maximum driveway width may be increased to thirty feet (30') at the
sidewalk line (or property line if no sidewalk exists) and thirty six feet
(36') at the curb line. No driveway shall encroach upon any portion of the
parkway strip adjacent to the adjoining properties. The maximum width for
driveways for all other uses shall be twenty five feet (25') at the sidewalk
line and shall be constructed with ten foot (10') radius returns unless
otherwise required or determined by the Director of Quality Control.
   H.   Grades And Curbs: Driveways shall conform to the existing sidewalk
grade. Where it is necessary to break the existing curb for the driveway
opening, the curb and gutter shall be completely removed and a new section
constructed. Each such driveway shall be constructed and maintained so as to
permit free and unobstructed passage on, over or across the sidewalk and in
such a manner as not to interfere with the proper drainage of streets. The
slope of any driveway and the approaches thereto shall not exceed one inch (1")
per foot in any direction. Each such driveway shall be so constructed and
maintained that its surface at the point of crossing any sidewalk pavement
shall be flush with the adjoining sections of such sidewalk.
   I.   Lots Abutting Both A Minor Street And A Collector Or Major Street: In
subdivisions where lots abut both a minor or marginal access street and a
collector or major street, no driveways shall be installed so as to exit onto
the collector or major street.
   J.   New Driveway Aprons Abutting Existing Sidewalks Or Curbs: New driveway
aprons abutting existing sidewalks or curbs shall be constructed to meet the
grade of such sidewalks or curbs. If necessary, the sidewalk shall be relocated
to accommodate the maximum driveway apron slope. The location and grade for
driveway aprons, when no adjacent sidewalks or curb and gutter exists, shall
match the adjacent parkway. The maximum slope of the driveway apron shall be
eight and thirty three hundredths percent (8.33%), and the minimum slope shall
be two percent (2%).
   K.   Concrete:
      1.   All single-family and two-family residential driveways and driveway
aprons, if constructed of concrete, shall have a minimum concrete thickness of
five inches (5") and a granular base of two inches (2") minimum compacted
thickness or an equivalent pavement section with a minimum structural number of
2.5. Expansion joints shall be used as specified in this Article.
      2.   Concrete driveways and driveway aprons for multi-family, commercial,
office and industrial property uses shall have a minimum concrete thickness of
eight inches (8"), plus a three inch (3") minimum compacted granular bedding
base. Expansion joints shall be used as specified in this Article.
   L.   Asphalt:
      1.   Single-family and two-family residential asphalt driveways and
driveway aprons shall be constructed in two (2) courses and shall not be less
than eight inches (8") in thickness after compaction. The crushed stone base
course shall be six inches (6") in thickness and compacted until all voids are
filled with finely crushed stone or screening. The asphalt surfaced course
shall be two inches (2") in thickness after compaction and shall be constructed
of bituminous plant-mix Type B-4 or B-5 conforming to the Standard
Specifications for Road and Bridge Construction as prepared by Illinois
Department of Transportation, Division of Highways, most recent edition.
      2.   All other asphalt driveways and driveway aprons shall be constructed
in two (2) courses and shall not be less than eleven inches (11") in thickness
after compaction. The crushed stone base course shall be eight inches (8") in
thickness and compacted until all voids are filled with finely crushed stone or
screening. The asphalt surfaced course shall be three inches (3") in thickness
after compaction and shall be constructed of bituminous plant-mix Type B-4 or
B-5 conforming to the Standard Specifications for Road and Bridge Construction
as prepared by the Illinois Department of Transportation, Division of Highways,
most recent edition. (Ords. 94-10-44, 96-1-3, 19-16-65, 19-16-66)
6-1G-3: SIDEWALK, DRIVEWAY AND DRIVEWAY APRON CONSTRUCTION MATERIALS AND
STANDARDS:
All materials shall comply with the following standards of quality:
   A.   General Requirements:
      1.   Portland cement:
         ASTM C150 Type I, normal.
         ASTM C150 Type II, moderate sulfate resistance.
      2.   Fine aggregate:
         ASTM C33, clean sand graded between #200 and #4 sieve limits.
      3.   Coarse aggregate:
         ASTM C33, uncoated crushed stone or washed gravel. Slag shall not be
permitted.
      4.   Water-cement ratio:
         Maximum 0.45.
      5.   Water:
         ASTM C94.
      6.   Water-reducing admixture:
         ASTM C494 Type A (normal) or Type D (retarder).
      7.   Air entraining agent:
         ASTM C260
      8.   Premolded filler strips:
         ASTM D994
      9.   Curing compound:
         ASTM C309, Type 2 (white, pigmented)
      10.   Reinforcement:
         ASTM A615, Grade 40.
   B.   Concrete Quality:
      1.   Ready-mixed concrete that complies with ASTM C94 shall be used for
sidewalks, driveways, and driveway aprons. The concrete shall be supplied by a
ready-mixed source which is approved by the Illinois Department of
Transportation.
      2.   Concrete mix shall be designed using the following data:
         a.   Minimum compressive strength of: three thousand five hundred
(3,500) psi at twenty eight (28) days, prior to first seasonal frost; four
thousand (4,000) psi at twenty eight (28) days after the first seasonal frost.
         b.   Minimum of six (6) sacks of cement per cubic yard.
         c.   Maximum size aggregate of one inch (1").
         d.   Air entrainment of six percent (6%) ± one and one-half percent
(11/2%).
         e.   Maximum slump of four inches (4").
         f.   Concrete shall contain a water-reducing admixture with no
reduction in cement content.
   C.   Subgrade Preparation: Where a fill condition exists, all topsoil and
unsuitable material shall be removed. Subgrade shall be rolled or tamped before
granular fill is placed. Fill shall be placed in six inch (6") layers to the
proper subgrade elevation and properly compacted to ninety five percent (95%)
ASTM standard density. Side slopes of fill material shall not exceed one foot
(1') vertical to three feet (3') horizontal. Where a cut condition exists, all
topsoil and organic or unsuitable material shall be removed. Subgrade shall be
removed to the proper elevation to allow sufficient width to accommodate the
forms.
   D.   Granular Base: A granular base of a minimum thickness, as specified in
this Article or as otherwise directed by the Village Engineer, shall be placed
on the prepared subgrade. The base shall extend the full width of the sidewalk,
driveway or driveway apron. The granular base shall consist of CA6 aggregate
conforming to the Standard Specifications for Road and Bridge Construction as
prepared by the Illinois Department of Transportation, Division of Highways,
most recent edition.
   E.   Forms: Side forms shall be of lumber of not less than two inches (2")
nominal thickness or steel of equal rigidity. The minimum depth of the side
forms shall be equal to the required sidewalk, driveway or driveway apron
thickness. No two inch by four inch (2" x 4") lumber forms shall be permitted.
Forms shall be held securely in place by stakes or braces with the top edge
true to line and grade. The forms for the sidewalk shall be set so that the
slab will have a fall of one inch (1") vertical to four feet (4') horizontal
from the edge nearest the property line toward the edge farthest from the
property line, except as may be otherwise directed by the Office of Quality
Control.
   F.   Expansion Joints: Expansion joints of the thickness specified below
shall consist of a premolded filler strip with the top strip placed one-fourth
inch (1/4") below the surface of the sidewalk, driveway or driveway apron.
      1.   One-Half Inch Thick Expansion Joints: Expansion joints one-half inch
(1/2") thick shall be placed between sidewalks and all structures which extend
through sidewalks, such as light standards, traffic light standards, and
traffic poles. one-half inch (1/2") thick expansion joints shall also be
provided at the junction of the driveway apron and the sidewalk and at the
junction of the driveway apron and the curb.
      2.   Three-Fourths Inch Thick Expansion Joints: Expansion joints three-
fourths inch (3/4") thick shall be placed at maximum intervals of fifty feet
(50') in sidewalks and where specified by the Department of Quality Control.
Where sidewalks are constructed adjacent to pavement or curbs that have
expansion joints, the expansion joints in the sidewalk shall be placed opposite
the existing expansion joints as nearly as practicable. Expansion joints shall
also be placed where the sidewalk abuts existing sidewalks, between the
driveway pavement and sidewalk, and between sidewalk and curbs where the
sidewalk abuts a curb.
   G.   Placing and Finishing Concrete:
      1.   The Department of Quality Control shall be notified when the
subgrade has been finished and is ready for inspection. A minimum of three (3)
hours notice shall be given prior to placing concrete. No concrete shall be
placed until the subgrade has been inspected and approved by the Department of
Quality Control.
      2.   Materials should not be placed on ice or frozen subgrade.
      3.   The subgrade shall be moistened just before the concrete is placed.
The concrete shall be placed in successive batches for the entire width of the
slab, struck off from one-half inch (1/2") to three-fourths inch (3/4") higher
than the finished slab, tamped until all voids are removed and free mortar
appears on the surface, thoroughly spaded along the edges, struck off to the
true grade, and to eliminate high and low spots and to embed large aggregate
particles, a bull float or darby should be used immediately after strikeoff but
before bleedwater accumulates on the surface. No finishing operation shall be
performed on the surface of a concrete slab while bleedwater is present. After
the bleedwater evaporates, the concrete shall be finished to a true and even
surface with floats. Lastly, the surface shall be given final finish by
brushing with a broom. The broom shall be drawn across the sidewalk, driveway
or driveway apron at right-angles to the edges of the slab, with adjacent
strokes slightly overlapping, producing a uniform, slightly roughened surface
with parallel marks.
   H.   Control Joints:
      1.   Sidewalks: Control joints shall be constructed at right angles to
the center line of the sidewalk and shall extend one-fourth (1/4) the depth of
the sidewalk. They shall not be less than one-eighth inch (1/8") nor more than
one-fourth inch (1/4") in width, and shall be edged with an edging tool having
a one-fourth inch (1/4") radius. All slabs shall be five feet (5') long on any
one side, unless otherwise ordered by the Director of Quality Control.
      2.   Driveways: Control joints shall be constructed in conformance with
the American Concrete Institute (ACI) Standards and Practices, as amended,
unless otherwise ordered by the Director of Quality Control.
      3.   Driveway Aprons: Control joints shall be constructed so as to divide
the driveway apron into sections which are approximately square with no side
longer than fifteen feet (15'). The control joints shall be not less than one-
eighth inch (1/8") nor more than one-fourth inch (1/4") in width, and shall be
edged with an edging tool having a one-fourth inch (1/4") radius. The minimum
depth shall be one and one-fourth inches (11/4").
   I.   Protection from Low Temperatures: If concrete is placed after the first
seasonal frost, concrete shall be protected from freezing in accordance with
the "Recommended Practice for Cold-Weather Concreting" (ACI 306). Air-entrained
concrete shall not be allowed to freeze and thaw in a saturated condition prior
to developing the required design compressive strength (ACI 308). The developer
or installer/contractor shall be responsible for all concrete damaged by low
temperatures, and any damaged concrete shall be removed and replaced by the
developer or installer/contractor at the developer or installer/contractor's
expense.
   J.   Curing:
      1.   After finishing the concrete, in accordance with subsection G above,
all sidewalks, driveways and driveway aprons shall be cured, in addition to the
curing requirements set forth in subsection J2 below, by one of the following
methods:
         a.   Burlap shall be placed on the finished surface, saturated with
water and covered with an impermeable covering.
         b.   Polyethylene sheeting shall be placed as soon as the concrete has
sufficiently hardened to prevent marring of the surface. The surface of the
concrete shall be wet immediately before the sheeting is placed. The edges of
the sheeting shall be weighted securely with a continuous windrow of earth or
any other means satisfactory to the Department of Quality Control to provide an
airtight cover.
         c.   Liquid-membrane-forming/curing compound shall be applied per ASTM
C309 immediately after broom finish.
      2.   A minimum thirty (30) day drying period after seven (7) days of
moist curing, if concrete is placed in the fall and will be exposed to freeze-
thaw cycles and deicers when saturated, is required. When temperatures fall to
near forty degrees (40°) Fahrenheit, a fourteen (14) day curing period may be
necessary when using Type I, normal cement. The required curing period can be
reduced to seven (7) days by using a Type III high-early-strength cement or a
higher cement content in the concrete.
Any of the three (3) curing methods set forth in subsection J1 above must
remain in place for not less than six (6) days.
   K.   Backfill: After the concrete has been cured, forms shall be removed and
the space near the edges of the sidewalk, driveway or driveway apron shall be
backfilled to the required elevation with material approved by the Department
of Quality Control. The material shall then be compacted until firm and the
surface evenly graded.
   L.   Disposal of Surplus Material: Surplus or waste material resulting from
the sidewalk, driveway or driveway apron construction shall be disposed of by
the developer, installer/contractor or property owner.
   M.   Control of Materials: The developer or installer/contractor shall, when
requested by the Village and at the developer or installer/contractor's
expense, have a commercial testing laboratory prepare and test samples of
delivered concrete. One set of tests shall be taken for the first twenty five
(25) cubic yards, or fraction thereof, and one set of tests shall be taken for
each additional fifty (50) cubic yards. A set of tests shall consist of four
(4) standard cylinders (2 shall be broken at 7 days and 2 shall be broken at 28
days), one slump test and one air content test. The laboratory shall perform
tests in accordance with recognized ASTM standards and shall submit written
reports of such tests to the Village Engineer for review.
   N.   Enforcement and Remedies: All construction in accordance with this
Article shall be subject to the terms and remedies contained in Section
6-1-8 of the Code. (Ord. 94-10-44)
ARTICLE H. ELEVATOR STANDARDS
SECTION:
6-1H-1: Codes Adopted
6-1H-2: Amendment
6-1H-3: Upgrades
6-1H-4: Conflict
6-1H-1: CODES ADOPTED:
The following are hereby adopted:
   A.   American Society Of Mechanical Engineers (ASME):
      1.   Safety code for elevators and escalators (ASME A17.1-2005), A17.1
(a)-2005, and A17.1(s)-2005;
      2.   Guide for inspection of elevators, escalators and moving walks (ASME
A17.2-2004);
      3.   Safety code for existing elevators and escalators (ASME A17.3-2005);
      4.   Safety standard for platform lifts and stairway chairlifts (ASME
A18.1-2005);
      5.   Standard for the qualification of elevator inspectors (ASME QEI-1-
2004).
   B.   American National Standards:
      1.   Safety requirements for personal hoists and employee elevators (ANSI
A10.4-2004).
   C.   American Society Of Civil Engineers (ASCE):
      1.   Automated people mover standards (ASCE 21-2000). (Ord. 07-15-46)
6-1H-2: AMENDMENT:
Should the village amend any of the standards set forth in section
6-1H-1 of this article, the village shall submit to the office of the state
fire marshal a copy of the amendment and its reason for the change. (Ord. 07-
15-46)
6-1H-3: UPGRADES:
Upgrades required by ASME A17.3-2005, safety code for existing elevators shall
be completed no later than January 1, 2009, except that upgrades to the
hydraulic cylinder system and firefighter control system shall be completed by
January 1, 2011. (Ord. 07-15-46)
6-1H-4: CONFLICT:
Where any provisions of this article conflict with any provisions of this code,
the provisions of this article shall prevail and shall be applied as the law of
the village. (Ord. 07-15-46)
CHAPTER 2
ELECTRICAL REGULATIONS
SECTION:
6-2-1: Definitions
6-2-2: Administration
6-2-3: Rules And Regulations Adopted
6-2-4: Amendments To Regulations
6-2-5: Permit Requirements
6-2-6: Inspections, Certificate Of Approval
6-2-7: Construction Regulations
6-2-7-1: New Single-Family Residences
6-2-7-2: Single-Family Residence Service Changes And Repairs
6-2-7-3: Service Entrance Cable
6-2-7-4: Garages
6-2-7-5: Central Air Conditioning Units
6-2-7-6: Exit And Emergency Lighting
6-2-7-7: Smoke And Carbon Monoxide Detectors
6-2-7-8: Appliance Circuits In Apartments
6-2-7-9: Cables, Conductors And Conduits
6-2-7-10: Ground Fault Circuit Interrupters
6-2-7-11: Exterior Outlets And Fixtures
6-2-7-12: Installation Of Electrical Conduit For Remote Water Meter Cable
Wiring In New Construction
6-2-7-13: Electrical Rooms/Closets
6-2-7-14: Carbon Monoxide Detectors
6-2-8: Penalty
6-2-1: DEFINITIONS:
When used in this chapter, the following words and terms shall have the
meanings ascribed to them:
CIRCUIT: Any set of branch lighting conductors which have been extended from a
distribution center and which may be utilized for the transmission of
electrical energy.
ELECTRICAL EQUIPMENT: Conductors and other equipment installed for the
utilization of electricity for light, heat or power. It does not include radio
apparatus or equipment for wireless reception of sounds and signals; nor
ordinary household appliances such as toasters, vacuum cleaners or washing
machines; and it does not include apparatus, conductors or other equipment
which are under the jurisdiction of the Illinois commerce commission, for use
in their operation as public utilities. (1951 Code 21-10-1; Ord. 76-16-30)
6-2-2: ADMINISTRATION:
   A.   Electrical Inspection Department: There is hereby established for the
village, an electrical inspection department for the purpose of regulating the
installation, alteration and use of all electrical equipment within the
village.
   B.   Electrical Inspectors: Effective as of August 1, 2023:
      1.   Compensation: The remuneration of the electrical inspector
supervisor and the electrical inspector assistant for all electrical inspection
work performed shall be as follows:
         a.   Electrical inspector supervisor: Fourteen thousand dollars
($14,000.00) per year.
         b.   Electrical inspector assistant: Seven thousand dollars
($7,000.00) per year.
      The compensation amounts listed above includes remuneration for any
travel, mileage or miscellaneous expenses incurred while conducting official
Village business.
      The remuneration of any additional part time electrical inspector(s),
designated by the Director of Community Development, shall be one-half (½) of
all electrical inspection fees collected under the applicable ordinances of the
village relative to inspections actually performed by any part time electrical
inspector.
      The Electrical Inspector Supervisor and Electrical Inspector Assistant
are considered part-time employees of the Village and as such are not eligible
for benefits including insurance and retirement plans. In addition, the
policies and procedures outlined in the Village of Oak Lawn Employee Handbook
are applicable to these positions, unless otherwise stated.
   C.   Salary Review: The salary figure for electrical inspector supervisor
and electrical inspector assistant, as set forth in subsection B of this
section, shall be reviewed on a semi-annual basis by the Village Manager, to
determine whether it should be increased, decreased or remain as is. Said
review shall consider the length of employment with the Village and will
consist of a comparison between the salaries paid and the electrical permit and
inspection fees received by the village during the previous six (6) months.
(1951 Code 21-10-11, 21-20-17, 21-10-18; Ords. 6-10-52, 10-14-52, 248, 96-9-42,
10-01-02, 23-03-15, 25-03-03)
6-2-3: RULES AND REGULATIONS ADOPTED:
The following rules are hereby adopted as the safe and practical standard for
the installation, alteration, repair and use of electrical equipment in the
village:
   A.   Chicago Electrical Code: The rules and regulations of the city of
Chicago electrical code 2018 Edition of the Chicago Electrical Code, based on
the 2017 National Electrical Code, are hereby adopted, save and except such
portions as are hereafter deleted, modified or amended, and with the addition
of those sections hereafter set forth as additions thereto, the same are
incorporated as if fully set forth herein; one copy having been and now being
on file with the clerk, with the exceptions set forth in section
6-2-4 of this chapter.
   Where any provision of 2018 Edition of the Chicago Electrical Code conflict
with any provisions of this village code, the provisions of the village code
shall prevail and shall be applied as law of the village.
   B.   Village Regulations: The rules and regulations regarding the
installation, alteration, repair and use of electrical equipment as last
adopted by the village, with the exception that entrance wiring shall be in
accordance with local utility company rules. (1951 Code 21-10-2, 21-10-16;
Ords. 66-23-62, 71-20-50, 76-16-30, 84-26-76; 1985 Code; Ords. 94-3-8, 97-20-
78, 01-09-32, 06-10-54, 14-04-10, 21-07-29)
6-2-4: AMENDMENTS TO REGULATIONS:
The rules and regulations of the 2018 Edition of the Chicago Electrical Code,
based on the 2017 National Electrical Code, (the “Chicago Electrical Code”) as
adopted in subsection
6-2-3A of this chapter is amended by the following changes and/or additions
thereto:
   Chapter 27 Electrical Regulations, Article 100. Definitions, Part A.
General.
   Recessed Incandescent Fixture. A lamp housing assembly located within a
ceiling, wall or floor space.
   Section 13-12-290. Qualifications.
   For the purposes of registering as an electrical contractor and/or
qualifying as a supervising electrician in the village of Oak Lawn, compliance
with the testing requirements set forth in chapter 13-12, article II, part C,
section 13-12-290 of the municipal code of Chicago shall be done by way of
taking and passing the tests offered by the city of Chicago in regard to
registering as an electrical contractor and/or qualifying as a supervising
electrician. As such, the tests given by the city of Chicago in regard to
registering as an electrical contractor and/or qualifying as a supervising
electrician are hereby adopted as the tests of the village of Oak Lawn and such
tests shall be given on such dates as determined by the city of Chicago
department of inspectional services.
   It shall be unlawful for any person to engage in the business of electrical
contractor, as herein defined, without being registered as an electrical
contractor in the manner hereinafter set forth; provided, however, that if such
person is already registered for the current year in any one of the following
municipalities:
   City of Chicago
   City of Waukegan
   Village of Orland Park
   Village of Libertyville
   Village of Lansing
   City of Elgin
   City of Naperville
   Village of Schaumburg
   City of Ottawa
   City of Woodstock
   Village of Buffalo Grove
   Such electrical contractor shall not be required to be registered or to pay
a registration fee in the village.
   Notwithstanding the foregoing, pursuant to the village's home rule powers as
provided by article VII, section 6 of the 1970 Illinois constitution, such
electrical contractor shall be required to file a copy of his/her registration
certificate with the village's quality control department and pay an
administrative fee of twenty-five and no/100 dollars ($25.00) relative to said
filing. The purpose of said administrative filing is to allow the village's
quality control department to create and maintain a list of properly registered
licensed electricians so as to facilitate the efficient processing of
electrical permit requests.
   Chapter 27, Article 220, Section 18-27-220.3(c)(1). Dwelling Units.
   Loads for structural additions to an existing dwelling unit or to a
previously unwired portion of an existing dwelling unit, either of which
exceeds two hundred (200) square feet in area, shall be computed in accordance
with paragraph (b) of this section. Loads for new circuits or extended circuits
in previously wired dwelling units shall be computed in accordance with either
(a) or (b) of section 18-27-220.3.
(Ords. 01-09-32, 01-11-38, 06-10-54, 14-04-10, 21-07-29)
6-2-5: PERMIT REQUIREMENTS:
   A.   Permits Required; Work Without Permits:
      1.   No electrical equipment shall be installed or altered except upon a
permit first issued by the electrical inspector authorizing the installation,
alteration, use or repair of electrical equipment.
      2.   Where an electrical installation has been started prior to the
issuance of a permit for such work, the permit fee for such work shall be twice
the amount of the normal permit fee prescribed in this chapter but no permit
fee shall exceed the normal permit fee for such work plus two hundred fifty
dollars ($250.00).
      3.   Where a registered electrical contractor is found doing electrical
work without a permit on three (3) separate occasions in one calendar year, a
hearing shall be held by the director of the office of quality control, at
which time the permit privileges of said electrical contractor may be suspended
for a period of time not to exceed one year.
   B.   Application For Permit:
      1.   No permit for the installation, alteration, repair or use of
electrical equipment shall be issued except pursuant to an application therefor
made out on a printed form, to be furnished by the electrical inspector, or his
duly authorized deputy, and upon payment of a fee in advance as hereinafter
provided.
      2.   The application for each permit shall contain the name of the owner,
or user of electrical equipment to be installed, altered, repaired or used, and
the location of the premises where such installation or alteration is to be
made by a street and number and a detailed description of the work to be done.
(Ord. 84-26-76)
6-2-6: INSPECTIONS, CERTIFICATE OF APPROVAL:
   A.   Certificate Required: It shall be unlawful to use or turn on power into
or induce any electric current to flow through any wires or equipment hereafter
installed for which a certificate of approval has not been issued.
   B.   Inspection, Issuance Of Certificate: The electrical inspector shall
issue a certificate of approval after each inspection in which the wiring or
apparatus is found to be in compliance with the terms of this chapter and with
the rules adopted herein.
   C.   Reinspections, Dangerous Conditions: The electrical inspector shall
make, periodically, a thorough reinspection of the installation of all electric
wiring, electric devices and electric material now installed in buildings
within the village, and when the installation of any such wiring, devices or
material is found to be in a dangerous or unsafe condition, the person using or
operating the same shall be notified and shall make the necessary repairs or
changes required to place such wiring, devices and materials in a safe
condition, and have such work completed within fifteen (15) days or any longer
period specified by the electrical inspector in said notice. The electrical
inspector is hereby empowered to disconnect or order the discontinuance of
electrical service to such wiring devices or material so found to be defective
until the installation of such wiring, devices and material has been made safe
as directed by said inspector.
   D.   Extra Inspections: Where extra inspections are made because of
inaccurate or incorrect information, failure to make necessary repairs, or
faulty construction. (1951 Code 21-10-9, 21-10-10; Ord. 01-09-32)
6-2-7: CONSTRUCTION REGULATIONS:
6-2-7-1: NEW SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENCES:
The following regulations shall apply to electrical services for new single-
family residences:
   A.   For single-family residences having a total floor area of one thousand
five hundred (1,500) square feet or more, as determined by the office of
quality control:
      1.   The service entrance conduit shall be two inch (2") trade size,
aluminum, IMC or galvanized iron conduit. No thin wall conduit shall be used.
The minimum service size shall be two hundred (200) amps.
      2.   The service entrance conductors shall have a current carrying
capacity of two hundred (200) amps and shall be equal to or better than AWG
size 3/0, THWN minimum size.
      3.   The service entrance switch shall contain:
         a.   A two hundred (200) ampere main switch or breaker which will
disconnect all distribution in the building. No split buss panels shall be
used.
         b.   A minimum thirty (30) circuit panel and a maximum forty two (42)
circuit panel.
   B.   For single-family residences having a total floor area less than one
thousand five hundred (1,500) square feet, as determined by the office of
quality control:
      1.   The service entrance conduit shall be one and one-fourth inch (11/
4") trade size, aluminum, IMC or galvanized iron conduit. No thin wall conduit
shall be used. The minimum service size shall be one hundred (100) amps.
      2.   The service entrance conductors shall have a current carrying
capacity of one hundred (100) amps and shall be minimum AWG size 2, THWN.
      3.   The service entrance switch shall contain a one hundred (100) ampere
main switch or breaker, which will disconnect all distribution in the building.
No split buss panels shall be used. A minimum twenty (20) circuit panel and a
maximum twenty four (24) circuit panel shall be provided.
   C.   As a minimum, circuit loads shall be connected as follows:
      1.   Two (2) appliance circuits, #12 wire to the kitchen area. All
countertop receptacles shall be GFCI protected. These can be used in the dining
area for appliances. No split plugs to be used. Soffit light and fan shall not
be fed from appliance circuits.
      2.   Gas range and hood fan shall be on a separate fifteen (15) ampere
circuit.
      3.   One 15- or 20-ampere circuit shall be provided for microwave oven.
      4.   One 15- or 20-ampere circuit shall be provided for dishwasher.
      5.   One 20-ampere circuit shall be provided for refrigerator.
      6.   One 15-ampere circuit shall be provided for garbage disposal unit.
      7.   One 20-ampere circuit shall be installed in all bathrooms for GFCI
receptacles only.
      8.   One circuit shall be provided each for sump pump and ejector pump.
      9.   One circuit for heating system.
      10.   One circuit for laundry plug, twenty (20) ampere, #12 wire (not
GFCI protected).
      11.   Living room and family room shall be on a separate fifteen (15)
ampere circuit, GFCI protected below switch.
      12.   Three (3) circuits for general lighting load.
      13.   Two (2) spare circuits for future use.
Where required and approved, additional circuits in accordance with the
requirements set forth in the adopted electrical code rules and regulations may
be permitted.
   D.   Ground straps shall not be located in any crawl space less than forty
eight inches (48") in depth.
   E.   The two hundred twenty (220) volt appliance circuit shall be double
pole; no tie bar to be used.
   F.   Panel circuit breakers shall conform to Chicago Electrical Code section
240.85.
   G.   A #8 copper clad grounding electrode shall be installed for all new
service installations to meter socket, and to grounding bushings.
   H.   All service grounding cable shall be located at line side of water
meter, with an appropriately sized bonding jumper connection across the meter
space.
   I.   Aluminum wire shall not be used anywhere in building. (Ords. 94-3-8,
94-12-54, 01-09-32, 21-07-29)
6-2-7-2: SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENCE SERVICE CHANGES AND REPAIRS:
Service changes and repairs for single-family residences shall be in accordance
with the requirements set forth in subsections 6-2-7-1A, B, D, E, F, G, H and I
of this chapter. (Ord. 01-09-32)
6-2-7-3: SERVICE ENTRANCE CABLE:
   A.   Service entrance cable shall not be approved for a new service or
service change installation. Permission may be granted on service change
installations where conditions warrant.
   B.   Post lights and all permanent outside lighting shall be wired with
three (3) conductor direct burial cable or in conduit, with one wire grounded
to fixture and to the nearest metallic ground in system. (Ords. 66-23-62, 76-
16-30)
6-2-7-4: GARAGES:
Garages shall have at least two (2) ceiling lights on a general lighting
circuit, one ceiling receptacle on a fifteen (15) ampere circuit, and three (3)
GFCI receptacles, one installed on each of three (3) different walls. All wall
receptacles shall be on the same twenty (20) ampere circuit and separate from
all other lighting and receptacles. All receptacles must be installed at least
four feet (4') above finished floor. All outside doors shall be illuminated.
All electrical feed to the garage shall be underground, in accordance with the
minimum cover requirements found in table 18-27-300.5. Only rigid, IMC heavy
wall or rigid nonmetallic (PVC, schedule 40, with ground wire) conduit shall be
used to feed the garage. One end of the conduit or ground wire shall be
grounded to the conduit system in the garage and other end to the nearest
metallic ground within the house. Special permission will be given to overhead
aerial wires. If the condition warrants this, if in writing, and the inspection
department, upon investigation, is aware of existing conditions. (Ord. 01-09-
32)
6-2-7-5: CENTRAL AIR CONDITIONING UNITS 1 :
   A.   For installation of central air conditioning units in single-family
dwellings, hereafter all equipment that is required, including the power unit,
shall only be permitted to be installed within a rear or side yard as defined
in section
4-5A-3 of this code. In no event shall a central air conditioning unit be
installed in a manner that encroaches more than three feet (3') into the
applicable yard, with a minimum side yard width of six feet (6'). All
installations shall be made by a licensed and registered electrical contractor
after the proper permits are taken out. All units shall be wired from the house
to the unit with sealtite flexible conduit to eliminate any vibrations from the
unit to the house. (Ords. 12-24-76, 21-07-29)
 
Notes
1 1. See also section 6-4-4 of this title.
6-2-7-6: EXIT AND EMERGENCY LIGHTING:
   A.   There shall be installed in all apartment buildings of three (3) floors
or more, containing five (5) apartment units or more, a complete exit and
emergency system, exit signs to be fed from an emergency panel that is fed from
the line side of the main switch. Emergency and exit sign illumination shall be
obtained from battery powered emergency lighting units located so as to provide
emergency illumination to all points of egress. Exit signs may be constructed
of plastic pursuant to Chicago Electrical Code section 700.66(c)(3).
   B.   In all retail stores, commercial buildings, office buildings,
restaurants, lounges or other places of public use or assembly, where an
occupancy is eight hundred (800) square feet or larger, a battery powered exit
sign system shall be installed which shall operate on the battery during a
power failure.
   C.   In all retail stores, commercial buildings and office buildings, of two
thousand (2,000) square feet or larger, and in all restaurants, lounges or
other places of public use or assembly, of eight hundred (800) square feet or
larger, an emergency lighting system shall be installed. Said emergency
lighting system is to be energized by either an auxiliary electrical generating
system, or by a wet electrical storage battery system, whereby either are
activated automatically upon the failure of the normal source and supply of
electrical energy. Emergency illumination of sufficient lumens shall be
provided for all stairways, staircases, exit doors, internal fire hose
locations, exit aisles and corridors. Also, emergency lighting with battery
backup shall be provided in all washrooms having two (2) or more water closet
fixtures.
   D.   For purposes of this section, the phrase "other places of public use or
assembly" shall mean something other than a restaurant or lounge used by fifty
(50) or more persons for religious, recreational, educational, political,
social, or amusement purposes, and not specified in this section, including,
but not limited to: gymnasiums, club rooms, conference rooms, courtrooms, and
banquet halls. (Ords. 76-16-30, 78-24-60, 84-6-12, 94-3-8, 01-09-32, 21-07-29)
6-2-7-7: SMOKE AND CARBON MONOXIDE DETECTORS:
   A.   Detectors: Detectors shall comply with the international fire code with
the changes outlined in section
7-3-1 of this code.
   B.   Dead Batteries: Smoke alarms shall be maintained in an operative
condition at all times. If during an inspection, the fire code official or a
duly authorized representative finds a solely battery operated smoke alarm
inoperable due to a dead battery, the building owner shall immediately replace
the solely battery operated smoke alarm with a listed electrically operated
smoke alarm. The smoke alarm shall receive primary power from the building
wiring where such wiring is served from a commercial source and shall be
equipped with a battery backup. (Ords. 14-04-10, 14-27-62)
6-2-7-8: APPLIANCE CIRCUITS IN APARTMENTS:
In all apartments of six hundred fifty (650) square feet or over, there shall
be two (2) appliance circuits in the kitchen area. See single-family
requirements for location of receptacles. (Ord. 76-16-30)
6-2-7-9: CABLES, CONDUCTORS AND CONDUITS:
   A.   Signs On Stores: On new and remodeling jobs, a three- fourths inch (3/
4") conduit shall be installed to the front of stores for signs from the main
distribution panel.
   B.   Nonmetallic Conduit:
      1.   All nonmetallic conduit and fittings including fiber, asbestos,
soapstone, cement, rigid PVC and high density polyethylene, ABS or any other
chemical compound material will be used for outside use only.
      2.   All services using the above materials will be stubbed within five
feet (5') of the building and continued in rigid conduit. Inside the building,
below or above grade, the service will be installed in rigid conduit or
electrical metallic tubing to ensure a continuous electrical metallic ground.
      3.   No nonmetallic sheathed cable shall be used in the village.
   C.   Aluminum Conductors: No aluminum conductors shall be used in the
village.
   D.   Armored Cable: Armored cable may be used only by special permission of
the chief electrical inspector. (Ords. 66-23-62, 76-16-30, 78-24-60)
6-2-7-10: GROUND FAULT CIRCUIT INTERRUPTERS:
   A.   Swimming Pools: Ground fault circuit interrupters shall be used on all
outlets in all swimming pools, ornamental pools or other similar bodies of
water both inside and outside of the building.
   B.   Bathrooms: All bathrooms and powder rooms shall have ground fault
circuit interrupter receptacles on a twenty (20) ampere circuit for all
outlets. Exhaust fans and lighting shall be connected to the load side of GFCI
receptacles on a fifteen (15) ampere circuit to the nearest GFCI receptacle in
the bedrooms. Spas, whirlpool tubs, hot tubs and similar bathing fixtures shall
have their power supply connected to a GFCI circuit breaker on a separate
circuit within the distribution panel.
   C.   Rooftop HVAC Equipment: All rooftop air conditioning units and heating
units shall be on a separate GFCI protected receptacle. (Ords. 94-3-8, 01-09-
32)
6-2-7-11: EXTERIOR OUTLETS AND FIXTURES:
   A.   Exterior Outlets To Be Weatherproofed: All exterior outlets shall be
installed in weatherproof boxes, one-fourth inch to one-half inch (1/4" - 1/2")
from wall.
   B.   Exterior Receptacles: All exterior receptacles for outlets shall be
GFCI protected.
   C.   Box For Exterior Bracket Lights: All exterior bracket lights shall be
installed in an Underwriters' Laboratory approved metal box. (Ords. 66-23-62,
76-16-30, 94-3-8, 01-09-32)
6-2-7-12: INSTALLATION OF ELECTRICAL CONDUIT FOR REMOTE WATER METER CABLE
WIRING IN NEW CONSTRUCTION:
   A.   In all new construction, a one-half inch (1/2") diameter EMT galvanized
steel thin wall conduit shall be installed between the point of location of the
water service meter and a location on the outside wall surface, a minimum of
twelve inches (12"), but no more than eighteen inches (18") above the gas
meter. Conduit bends shall be no more than six inches (6") in radius and have
no more than one back to back bend. All conduit piping is to be flush with
walls both inside and outside of the building. Said conduit run shall be
provided with three (3) conductor bell wire, end to end, including twelve
inches (12") of wire slack at each end, for the purpose of connecting the
necessary conductor wire(s) by the village water department.
   B.   It shall be the responsibility of the village licensed electrical
contractor to install this conduit, and such installation shall be agreed to by
said contractor as a part of the application for an electrical permit. (Ords.
87-24-104, 94-3-8, 01-09-32)
6-2-7-13: ELECTRICAL ROOMS/CLOSETS:
All electrical rooms/closets shall be used for electrical equipment only and
proper venting shall be provided. No plumbing or HVAC ducts shall be installed
within or through said room, except for automatic fire suppression system (fire
sprinkler) piping and sprinkler heads as may be approved by the village fire
department. (Ord. 01-09-32)
6-2-7-14: CARBON MONOXIDE DETECTORS:
Carbon monoxide alarms shall be required and installed in all residential
occupancies, new and existing, in accordance with this section:
   A.   Newly Constructed Single-Family And Single-Family Attached Dwellings:
Carbon monoxide detectors will be required to be installed in or adjacent to
(within 15 feet of) all sleeping areas. They shall be both AC and DC (battery)
powered, and detectors remote to one another shall be interconnected.
   B.   Existing Single-Family And Single-Family Attached Dwellings: Carbon
monoxide detectors shall be required to be installed in or adjacent to (within
15 feet of) all sleeping areas. They shall be either AC or DC (battery)
powered.
   C.   Newly Constructed Apartment Buildings: Carbon monoxide detectors shall
be required to be installed within each dwelling unit in or adjacent to (within
15 feet of) all sleeping areas. They shall be both AC and DC (battery) powered,
and detectors remote to one another shall be interconnected.
   D.   Existing Apartment Buildings: Carbon monoxide detectors shall be
installed within each dwelling unit in or adjacent to (within 15 feet of) all
sleeping areas. They shall be either AC or DC (battery) powered.
   E.   New And Existing Hotels/Motels: One carbon monoxide detector shall be
installed on each floor where occupants will be sleeping in a common area as
approved by the building official. An approved carbon monoxide detection
system, designed to warn occupants, or sound a warning in a constantly attended
location, may be installed in lieu of individual floor detectors. Floor
detectors and/or systems shall be installed in lieu of individual floor
detectors. Floor detectors and/or systems shall be both AC and DC (battery)
powered. Individual sleeping rooms with fuel burning appliances located within
the room shall each have a carbon monoxide detector installed within each room,
where not protected by an otherwise approved detection system.
   F.   New And Existing Healthcare/Institutional Occupancies: One carbon
monoxide detector shall be installed on each floor where occupants will be
sleeping, in a common area as approved by the building official. An approved
carbon monoxide detection system, designed to warn occupants, or sound a
warning in a constantly attended location, may be installed in lieu of
individual floor detectors. Floor detectors and/or systems shall be both AC and
DC (battery) powered. Individual sleeping rooms, with fuel burning appliances
located within the room, shall each have a carbon monoxide detector installed
within each room, where not protected by an otherwise approved detection
system. (Ord. 07-04-15)
6-2-8: PENALTY:
Any person violating any provision of this chapter shall be fined not less than
ten dollars ($10.00) nor more than seven hundred fifty dollars ($750.00) for
each offense, and a separate offense shall be deemed committed on each day
during or on which a violation occurs or continues. (Ords. 84-26-76, 99-5-11)
ARTICLE A. ELECTRICAL FEES
SECTION:
6-2A-1: Fees Generally
6-2A-2: Inspection Fees
6-2A-3: Reinspections
6-2A-4: Illuminated Signs
6-2A-5: Garages And Swimming Pools
6-2A-6: Examination Of Plans
6-2A-7: Searchlights
6-2A-1: FEES GENERALLY:
   A.   The inspection fee for each nominal 20 ampere or less, branch circuit,
including fixtures, sockets or receptacles, shall be ten dollars ($10.00) per
circuit for the first fifty (50) circuits and five dollars ($5.00) for each
nominal 20 ampere or less, branch circuit thereafter. The inspection fee for 21
to 50 ampere branch circuits shall be twenty dollars ($20.00) per circuit.
   B.   The inspection fee for a 21 ampere to 50 ampere fixed lighting circuit
shall be twenty dollars ($20.00) per circuit.
   C.   The inspection fee for 3-wire circuits and 3-phase, 4-wire circuits
shall be:
      1.   3-wire circuit, 20 ampere or less: double the cost per circuit as
set forth in subsection A of this section.
      2.   3-phase, 4-wire circuit, 20 ampere or less: triple the cost per
circuit as set forth in subsection A of this section. (Ords. 84-26-76, 94-3-9)
6-2A-2: INSPECTION FEES:
   A.   Motors And Current Consuming Devices: The inspection fees for the
inspection of each electric motor or current consuming device, other than
lighting fixtures, shall be as follows:
 
One motor or current consuming device             $20.00
Each additional motor or current consuming device 7.00
 
   B.   Temporary Installations, Underground And Overhead Wires And Apparatus:
Inspections of temporary installations, underground or overhead wires and
apparatus, and all other inspections not specifically provided for herein shall
be charged at the rate of fifty dollars ($50.00) per hour or fraction thereof,
per inspector.
   C.   Temporary Installations Such As Displays, Exhibitions, Carnivals And
Similar Temporary Installations:
      1.   Inspection Fees: The fees for inspection of the electrical work in
connection with displays, exhibitions, carnivals and similar temporary
installations shall be fifty dollars ($50.00) per hour or fraction thereof per
inspector.
      2.   Overtime: The fees for inspection of the electrical work in
connection with displays, exhibitions, carnivals and similar temporary
installations as described in subsection C1 of this section, which, by their
nature must be inspected during other than normal working hours, shall be
double the fees described in subsection C1 of this section. (Note: Other than
normal working hours shall include all Saturdays, Sundays, posted holidays and
all weekdays between the hours of 12:00 midnight and 8:00 A.M. and 4:30 P.M.
and 12:00 midnight.)
   D.   Communication And Alarm Systems: The fees for the inspection of
electrical communication systems and burglar alarms shall be as follows
      Low voltage burglar alarms, fifty dollars ($50.00) per hour or fraction
thereof per inspector.
      Aural communication system, fifty dollars ($50.00) per hour or fraction
thereof per inspector.
      Visual communication systems, fifty dollars ($50.00) per hour or fraction
thereof per inspector.
      Antenna systems, fifty dollars ($50.00) per hour or fraction thereof per
inspector.
   E.   Services:
      1.   The inspection fees for electrical services shall be as follows:
 
0 to 100 amperes     $40.00
101 to 200 amperes   50.00
201 to 400 amperes   65.00
401 to 600 amperes   75.00
601 to 1,000 amperes 100.00
 
      2.   Fees for services in excess of 1,000 amperes shall be computed on
the basis of the rating of the service disconnects installed, prorated
according to the schedule above and shall include feeders, risers and all
wiring and equipment up to the branch circuit distribution panels or motor
power panels or control centers.
      3.   Fees for the inspection of vaults, except for utility vaults, shall
be fifty dollars ($50.00) for each vault. (Ords. 84-26-76, 94-3-9, 01-09-32)
6-2A-3: REINSPECTIONS:
   A.   Inspection fees for the reinspection of any existing, previously
approved electrical installation shall be fifty dollars ($50.00) per hour or
fraction thereof, per inspector and shall be payable by the electrical
contractor who subsequently obtains a permit to make the required corrections
to that electrical installation.
   B.   Where extra inspections are made because of inaccurate or incorrect
information, failure to make necessary repairs, or faulty construction, a
charge of fifty dollars ($50.00) per hour or fraction thereof shall be made for
each such inspection. (Ords. 84-26-76, 93-4-9)
6-2A-4: ILLUMINATED SIGNS:
   A.   Permit Fees: The original permit fee shall cover the first annual
inspection fee. Subsequent annual inspections shall be subject to the annual
inspection fee stated in subsection D of this section. Permit fees shall be
computed as follows:
      1.   Electric or illuminated signs projecting over the public way -
twenty two dollars ($22.00) per sign, plus seventy cents ($0.70) per square
foot of area of each face. The area of an irregular shaped sign shall be
computed by using the area of the outer perimeter design of the sign.
      2.   Electric or illuminated signs on private property, illuminated signs
flat against the building, illuminated painted wall signs and illuminated
signboards - twenty two dollars ($22.00) per sign.
      3.   Electric or illuminated roof signs - thirty two dollars ($32.00) per
sign, plus for each sign over five hundred (500) square feet, thirty five cents
($0.35) per square foot for each square foot over five hundred (500). The fee
shall be computed on the actual area of the display surface.
      4.   Electric or illuminated ground signs, any part of which projects
over the public way, fees shall be the same as computed for projecting signs as
specified in subsection A of this section.
Electrical or illuminated ground signs, entirely over private property - twenty
two dollars ($22.00) per sign.
      5.   Permits issued for the reerection or alteration of any electric
sign, illumination of signboards or illumination of flat or wall signs - twenty
two dollars ($22.00) per sign.
      6.   Permits issued for electric or illuminated signs to be erected for a
period not to exceed sixty (60) days - one-half (1/2) of the sign permit fee.
No fee shall be less than eleven dollars ($11.00).
      7.   The fee for cancellation of any sign permit shall be eleven dollars
($11.00) and shall be deducted before the remaining amount is refunded.
   B.   Sign Plan Examination Fees: The fees for the examination of sign plans
shall be twenty five dollars ($25.00) per one-half (1/2) hour or fraction
thereof.
   C.   Annual Inspection Required: The commissioner of inspectional services
shall make an annual inspection of all electric or illuminated signs projecting
over the public way, electric or illuminated signs on private property,
illuminated signs flat against a building (flat signs), illuminated painted
wall signs, illuminated signboards, electric or illuminated roof signs and
electric or illuminated ground signs.
   D.   Annual Inspection Fees: The annual inspection fee to be charged for the
signs in subsection C of this section shall be as follows:
      1.   Electric or illuminated signs projecting over the public way -
twenty two dollars ($22.00) per sign plus seventy cents ($0.70) per square foot
of area of each face. The area of irregular shaped signs shall be computed
using the area of the outer perimeter design of the signs.
      2.   Electric or illuminated signs on private property, illuminated signs
flat against a building (flat signs), illuminated painted wall signs and
illuminated signboards - twenty two dollars ($22.00) per sign.
      3.   Electric or illuminated roof signs - thirty two dollars ($32.00) per
sign, plus for each sign over five hundred (500) square feet thirty five cents
($0.35) per square foot for each square foot over five hundred (500). The fee
shall be computed on the actual area of display surface.
      4.   Electric or illuminated ground signs, any part of which projects
over the public way, the fees shall be the same as computed for projecting
signs in subsection A of this section.
      5.   Electric or illuminated ground signs entirely over private property
- twenty two dollars ($22.00) per sign. (Ords. 84-26-76, 94-3-9, 01-09-32)
6-2A-5: GARAGES AND SWIMMING POOLS:
The following electrical permit fees shall be paid for garages and swimming
pools:
 
Garages        $40.00
Swimming pools 40.00
 
(Ords. 84-26-76, 94-3-9, 01-09-32)
6-2A-6: EXAMINATION OF PLANS:
The fees for examination of plans shall be twenty five dollars ($25.00) per
one-half (1/2) hour or fraction thereof per plan examiner.
NOTE:
      1.   For new buildings, service including new or remodeled loads in
existing building:
         1/2 hour for each 100 kVA of service up to 1,000 kVA.
         1 hour for each 500 kVA of service over 1,000 kVA.
      2.   For emergency lighting and fire alarm:
         1 hour per drawing unless the drawing exceeds 1 floor.
         1/2 hour for each additional floor, providing it is the same tenant.
      3.   For equipment, unit batteries, etc.:
         2 hours minimum.
      4.   For plenum fixtures:
         2 hours for the first fixture.
         1 hour for each additional fixture submitted at the same time.
      5.   For switchboards and motor control centers:
         1 hour per switchboard or motor control center.
         1/2 hour per each identical switchboard over 2.
      6.   Plans submitted without fee:
         1/2 hour additional handling and billing charge.
(Ords. 84-26-76, 94-3-9)
6-2A-7: SEARCHLIGHTS:
The fee for searchlights shall be three dollars ($3.00) per light for three (3)
nights, with a minimum fee of ten dollars ($10.00). (Ords. 66-23-62, 76-16-30,
94-3-9)
CHAPTER 3
PLUMBING REGULATIONS
SECTION:
6-3-1: Intent
6-3-2: Illinois Plumbing Code
6-3-3: Permit Required, Issuance
6-3-4: Fees
6-3-5: Water Saving Devices
6-3-6: Miscellaneous Provisions
6-3-7: Administration
6-3-8: Sanitary Sewer Laterals And Taps
6-3-1: INTENT:
The intent of this chapter is to be liberally construed and the content to be
used in conjunction with the existing regulations. (1951 Code 21-11-4; Ord.
279)
6-3-2: ILLINOIS PLUMBING CODE:
   A.   Code Adopted: A certain document, at least one (1) copy of which is on
file in the Office of the Village Clerk of the Village of Oak Lawn, Cook
County, Illinois, being marked and designated as the Illinois Plumbing Code (77
Ill. Admin. Code 890), as promulgated by the Illinois Department of Public
Health, and any subsequent amendments adopted by the department thereto, is
hereby adopted as the Plumbing Code of the Village of Oak Lawn, Cook County,
Illinois for the control of buildings and structures as herein provided; and
each and all of the regulations, provisions, penalties, conditions and terms of
said Plumbing Code are hereby referred to, adopted and made part of, as if
fully set out in this section. (Ord. 17-11-27)
6-3-3: PERMIT REQUIRED, ISSUANCE 1 :
The Director of the Office of Quality Control shall issue permits, which are
required in all cases for all new installations, additions and/or alterations
to existing plumbing installations, upon application, where the proposed
installation, addition or alteration as described in such application is shown
to be in conformity with this Code. (Ord. 96-9-43)
 
Notes
1 1. See section 6-1A-2 of this title.
6-3-4: FEES:
   A.   Permits: The applicant shall pay to the Village a fee as follows:
Single-family dwelling:
 Minimum fee                                                          $ 50.00
  New building: Basic fixture count of 1 3-fixture bathroom, 1 kitchen
 sink, 1 set of laundry tubs, 1 hot water heater, 1 sump pump or a    100.00
  lesser count thereof
 Each additional fixture over the basic fixture count, including      15.00
  washing machines, garbage disposals and dishwashers
 Additions and/or alterations, each fixture                           15.00
Multiple-family buildings:
 Minimum fee                                                          200.00
  New building: For each and every apartment unit, basic fixture count
 of 1 3-fixture bathroom, 1 kitchen sink, 1 hot water heater, 1       90.00
  dishwasher or a lesser count thereof
 Each additional fixture over the basic fixture count, including      15.00
  washing machines, garbage disposals and dishwashers
 Additions and/or alterations, each fixture                           15.00
 Each fixture within or part of the building, exclusive of the        15.00
  apartment units
All other types of buildings:
 Minimum fee                                                          200.00
 Each fixture within or part of the building, exclusive of the        15.00
  apartment units
 Building additions: Minimum fee with a basic fixture count of 3 or   100.00
  less fixtures
 Each additional fixture over 3                                       15.00
Air conditioning equipment: Fees shall be paid at the time the application for
a permit is applied for, based on $6.00 per $1,000.00 cost of installation.
 
   B.   Reinspections: If a building fails to pass inspection and a
reinspection is necessary, then a reinspection fee of fifteen dollars ($15.00)
shall be charged. The reinspection fee shall be paid before a reinspection is
made. (1951 Code 21-11-3; Ords. 279, 69-20-59, 76-16-30, 81-13-22, 86-5-13, 08-
17-46, 08-19-55)
6-3-5: WATER SAVING DEVICES:
It is herewith required that the following water saving plumbing fixtures shall
be henceforth installed:
   A.   Flow Rates For New Construction And Replacement Fixtures: In all new
construction and in all repair and/or replacement of fixtures or trim, only
fixtures and trim not exceeding the following flow rates and/or water usage
shall be installed. These ratings are based on a pressure at the fixture of
forty (40) to fifty (50) psi:
 
                               Gallons
                                Per Flush
Water closets, tank type        3.5
Water closets, flushometer type 3.0
Urinals, tank type              3.0
Urinals, flushometer type       3.0
Showerheads                     3.0
Lavatory, sink faucets          3.0
 
   B.   Lavatories For Public Use: In addition to the requirement of subsection
A of this section, in all new construction and replacement of fixtures, faucets
of lavatories located in restrooms intended for public use shall be of metering
or self- closing type.
   C.   Car Wash Installations: In all new construction and replacement of
fixtures, car wash installations shall be equipped with a water recycling
system.
   D.   New And Replacement Plumbing Fixtures And Irrigation Controllers:
Pursuant to 17 Ill. Admin. Code 3730.307(c)(4), and subject to the Illinois
Plumbing Code (77 Ill. Admin. Code 890) and the Lawn Irrigation Contractor and
Lawn Sprinkler System Registration Code (77 Ill. Admin. Code 892), all new and
replacement plumbing fixtures and irrigation controllers installed after the
effective date of this subsection shall bear the WaterSense label (as
designated by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency WaterSense Program),
when such labeled fixtures are available. (Ords. 77-20-34, 77-37-66, 17-11-27)
6-3-6: MISCELLANEOUS PROVISIONS:
   A.   Meters: It shall be required that all new water services be
individually metered.
   B.   Sanitary Sewer Flushing: The flushing of sanitary sewers with potable
water will be allowed only if the flushing is done with a high velocity type
sewer jet. Strict accounting of the water used for sanitary sewer flushing must
be made.
   C.   Misuse Of Plumber's Name: No plumber shall allow his name to be used by
any other person or party directly or indirectly for the purpose of obtaining
permits or doing any work under his license. (1951 Code 5-4-6; Ords. 77-30-24,
77-37-66)
6-3-7: ADMINISTRATION:
   A.   Plumbing Inspection Department: There is hereby established for the
Village, a Plumbing Inspection Department for the purpose of regulating the
installation, alteration and use of all plumbing equipment within the Village.
   B.   Plumbing Inspectors: Effective January 1, 2022:
      1.   Compensation: The remuneration of the Plumbing Inspector Supervisor
and the Plumbing Inspector Assistant for all plumbing inspection work performed
shall be as follows:
         a.   Plumbing Inspector Supervisor: Ten thousand dollars ($10,000.00)
per year.
         b.   Plumbing Inspector Assistant: Seven thousand dollars ($7,000.00)
per year.
   The compensation amounts listed above includes remuneration for any travel,
mileage or miscellaneous expenses incurred while conducting official Village
business.
   The remuneration of any additional part time plumbing inspector(s),
designated by the Director of Community Development, shall be one-half (½) of
all plumbing inspection fees collected under the applicable ordinances of the
Village relative to inspections actually performed by any part time plumbing
inspector.
   The Plumbing Inspector Supervisor and Plumbing Inspector Assistant are
considered part-time employees of the Village and as such are not eligible for
benefits including insurance and retirement plans. In addition, the policies
and procedures outlined in the Village of Oak Lawn Employee Handbook are
applicable to these positions, unless otherwise stated.
    C.   Salary Review: The salary figure for Plumbing Inspector Supervisor and
Plumbing Inspector Assistant, as set forth in subsection B of this section,
shall be reviewed on a semi-annual basis by the Village Manager, to determine
whether it should be increased, decreased or remain as is. Said review shall
consider the length of employment with the Village and will consist of a
comparison between the salaries paid and the plumbing permit and inspection
fees received by the Village during the previous six (6) months. (Ords. 96-9-
43, 10-01-02, 23-03-16, 25-03-03)
6-3-8: SANITARY SEWER LATERALS AND TAPS:
   A.   New Sanitary Sewer Laterals And Taps: All new construction of single-
family homes, multi-family buildings, commercial zoned structures,
manufacturing and institutional structures, and "additions" to greater than
fifty percent (50%) of the floor area of an existing structure, shall be
required to provide a new sanitary sewer service lateral and sanitary sewer
tap. Sanitary sewer laterals and sanitary sewer taps shall be constructed
according to the methods and materials defined in section
6-3-2 of this chapter.
   B.   Identification Of New Sanitary Sewer Laterals And Taps On Building
Plans: No building permit shall be issued for a site until the owner of the
site or his authorized agent has submitted building plans to the Building
Division identifying the new sanitary sewer service laterals and taps.
   C.   Existing Sewer Laterals And Taps: All existing sanitary sewer service
laterals and sanitary sewer taps shall be capped and sealed at the main wye
location to prevent the old sewer service lateral from leaking water into the
Village sewer line.
   D.   Exemptions: Parties wishing exemption from this requirement shall
provide written documentation and video to the Village Building Division and
Public Works Sewer Division. The written documentation and video must clearly
show to the satisfaction of Public Works, Engineering and Building Division
staff that the existing lateral and tap meet current Building and Plumbing
Codes and do not leak.
   E.   Enforcement: Enforcement of this Code shall be primarily conducted by
the Department of Public Works or their designated representative.
   F.   Standard Detail For Typical Riser For Sanitary Service Lateral:
Construction of sanitary service laterals shall conform to the methods and
materials shown in the standard detail "Typical Riser for Sanitary Service
Lateral (SAN-5)". A printed copy of this standard detail is available upon
request from the Building Division.
   G.   Standard Detail For Sanitary Sewer Installation: Construction of
sanitary sewers shall conform to the methods and materials shown in the
standard detail "Sanitary Sewer Installation (SAN-6)". A printed copy of this
standard detail is available upon request from the Building Division. (Ord. 12-
01-01)
CHAPTER 4
MECHANICAL SYSTEMS
SECTION:
6-4-1: International Mechanical Code; International Fuel And Gas Code
6-4-2: Inspections
6-4-3: Safety Of Existing Installations
6-4-4: Air Conditioning Units
6-4-4-1: Definitions
6-4-4-2: Regulations
6-4-1: INTERNATIONAL MECHANICAL CODE; INTERNATIONAL FUEL AND GAS CODE:
   A.   Codes Adopted: The International Mechanical Code, 2018 edition, and the
International Fuel and Gas Code, 2018 edition, are adopted by reference by the
village for regulation of mechanical systems in the village. One copy of said
codes have been and are now filed in the office of the clerk, and the same are
herein incorporated as fully as if set out at length herein, and the provisions
thereof shall be controlling.
   B.   Applicability Of Codes: Where any provisions of the International
Mechanical Code, 2018 edition, or the International Fuel and Gas Code, 2018
edition, conflict with any provisions of this code, the provisions of this code
shall prevail and shall be applied as the law of the village.
   C.   Amendments To Code:
   Section 3001.2: Delete in its entirety.
   Table 3001.2: Delete "Automotive Lift Reference ALI ALCTV"
   Section 3002.4: Replace with the following language: "Elevator car to
accommodate ambulance stretcher. Where elevators are provided in buildings, not
fewer than one elevator, shall be provided for fire department emergency access
to all floors regardless of rise.
   Such elevator car shall be of such size and arrangement to accommodate a
minimum 24 inch by 84 inch ambulance stretcher in the horizontal open position
and shall be identified by the international symbol for emergency medical
services (Star of Life).
   The symbol shall be not less than 3 x 3 inches high and wide (76 mm x 76 mm)
and shall be placed inside on both sides of the main lobby hoistway door
frame."
   Section [F] 3003.3: Add the following language: “. . . for new and
modernized (existing) elevators only.”
   Section 3004.4: Amend the last sentence to read as follows: "Field tests
shall be conducted upon the completion of an installation or following a jump
or a major alteration of personnel hoist."
   Add the following sentence: "All miscellaneous hoisting an elevating
equipment shall be subjected to tests and inspections as required by the AIIJ
to ensure safe operation."
   Section 3005.1: Add the following language: "This means is not to be used as
a passageway though the space to other areas of the building."
   Section 3005.2: Delete "Exception #2" in its entirety.
(Ords. 14-04-10, 21-07-29)
6-4-2: INSPECTIONS:
The director of the office of quality control, or his authorized
representative, is responsible for the inspection of mechanical refrigeration
installations. Advisory consultants on inspection functions may be engaged by
the director with the approval of the village manager. (1951 Code 21-17A-2;
Ords. 270, 81-12-19; 1985 Code)
6-4-3: SAFETY OF EXISTING INSTALLATIONS:
Existing refrigeration systems in operation previous to June 1, 1958, shall be
maintained in a safe condition. (1951 Code 21-17A-3; Ord. 270)
6-4-4: AIR CONDITIONING UNITS 1 :
 
Notes
1 1. See also section 6-2-7-5 of this title.
6-4-4-1: DEFINITIONS:
Unless otherwise expressly stated, whenever used in this section, the following
words shall have the meanings given to them herein:
AIR CONDITIONING: The cooling or dehumidification, or both, of space for human
occupancy, food preservation and industrial processing.
AIR CONDITIONING SYSTEM, AIR CONDITIONING INSTALLATION: One or more air
conditioning units, the water for which passes through a common meter, whether
or not the units are owned by the property owner, the tenant or a combination
of both. It is intended that separate submetering for any one property owner or
tenant occupying different space in the same building could create separate
systems. It is not intended that submetering of individual units serving the
same space or adjacent space under one owner or tenant would create separate
systems.
INSTALLED CAPACITY: The total number of tons of refrigeration installed on one
air conditioning system.
TON OF REFRIGERATION: The heat required to melt ice at the rate of one ton in
twenty four (24) hours. One compressor horsepower shall be considered
equivalent to one ton of refrigeration.
WATER CONSERVING DEVICES: A cooling tower, spray pond, evaporation condenser or
other equipment by which water is cooled and recirculated, thereby limiting the
use of water from the mains to that amount lost through evaporation.
WATER REGULATING DEVICE: A regulating valve or other device, the purpose of
which is to limit the maximum use of water to a predetermined rate. (Ord. 81-
13-22)
6-4-4-2: REGULATIONS:
It shall be unlawful for any person to have installed or to operate air
conditioning equipment without first conforming to the following regulations:
   A.   Permit And Notice Required: All water customers who desire to install
air conditioning and refrigeration equipment after the effective date of this
code are required to make written application therefor, and to obtain approval
and a permit therefor in advance from the quality control department, and are
required to give notice of the completion of the installation. All customers
who have installed air conditioning equipment prior to the effective date of
this code shall, in lieu of a permit, notify the chief building inspector of
such existing installation. Failure to give notice shall be considered a
violation of this code.
   B.   Water Conservation Devices Required: All air conditioning units shall
be equipped with water conservation devices so that water from the village
mains shall be used for make up and flushing purposes only. Any air
conditioning system is prohibited from using water from the mains in excess of
two-tenths (0.2) gallon per minute per ton. Each air conditioning system shall
be metered through the domestic water meter which is acceptable to the village.
   C.   Inspections, Right Of Entry Powers: The chief building inspector and
persons designated by him shall, at all reasonable hours, have free access to
premises supplied with water for air conditioning purposes, to examine the
equipment and to ascertain if there is more water being used than allowed by
this chapter.
   D.   Appeals: Whenever the chief building inspector shall reject or refuse
to approve any plan or specification for, or the mode or manner of construction
proposed to be used in the installation of any air conditioning equipment, or
when it is claimed that the provisions of this chapter do not apply or when it
is claimed that the true intent and meaning of this chapter have been
misconstrued or improperly interpreted, the owner or any person aggrieved may
appeal to the Appeals and Review Board pursuant to the provisions of the zoning
ordinance of the village. No public advertising shall be required as a
condition precedent to such appeal.
   E.   Compliance With Provisions: All air conditioning units installed prior
to the effective date of this code and contrary thereto must be converted,
modified or adjusted to comply with the same within an eighteen (18) month
period of time. (Ord. 81-13-22; 1985 Code; Ord. 19-16-65)
CHAPTER 5
PROPERTY MAINTENANCE REGULATIONS
ARTICLE A. GENERAL PROVISIONS
SECTION:
6-5A-1: Purpose
6-5A-2: International Property Maintenance Code Adopted
6-5A-3: Additions, Insertions, Deletions And Changes
6-5A-4: Clothing Drop Boxes
6-5A-1: PURPOSE:
The purposes of this chapter are to establish the minimum regulations governing
the conditions and maintenance of all property, buildings and structures; to
provide the standards for supplied utilities and facilities and other physical
things and conditions essential to ensure that structures are safe, sanitary
and fit for occupation and use; to provide for the condemnation of buildings
and structures unfit for human occupancy and use and the demolition of such
structures. (Ord. 07-27-89)
6-5A-2: INTERNATIONAL PROPERTY MAINTENANCE CODE ADOPTED:
The international property maintenance code, 2018 edition, is adopted by
reference by the village for the regulation of property maintenance in the
village. One copy of said code has been and is now filed in the office of the
clerk, and the same is herein incorporated as fully as if set out at length
herein, and the provisions thereof shall be controlling.
Where any provision of the International Property Maintenance Code, 2018
edition, conflict with any provisions of this village code, the provisions of
the village code shall prevail and shall be applied as law of the village.
(Ords. 14-04-10, 21-07-29)
6-5A-3: ADDITIONS, INSERTIONS, DELETIONS AND CHANGES:
Section 301.3.1: Add the following language, "Vacant dwellings must be
registered with Cook County department of building and zoning,
vbo.cookcountyil.gov/01022, within 30 days of vacancy".
Section 108.4: Add the placarding term, "uninhabitable."
(Ord. 14-04-10)
6-5A-4: CLOTHING DROP BOXES:
The placement or maintaining of clothing drop boxes on any commercial property
in the village shall be prohibited, provided, however, clothing drop boxes may
be maintained on property owned by public or private schools, churches,
hospitals and charitable organizations. (Ord. 08-04-16)
ARTICLE B. NON-OWNER OCCUPIED RESIDENTIAL DWELLING UNITS
PERMIT PROGRAM
SECTION:
6-5B-1: Permits For Non-Owner Occupied Residential Dwelling Units
6-5B-2: Applicability
6-5B-3: Inspections/Fees
6-5B-4: Preinspection Of Residential Dwelling Units
6-5B-5: Compliance Report/Notice Of Violations
6-5B-6: Application For And Content Of Non-Owner Occupied Residential Dwelling
Unit Occupancy Permit
6-5B-7: Responsibilities Of Owner, Agent And/Or Manager
6-5B-8: Fossil Fueled Equipment
6-5B-9: Responsibility Of Tenants
6-5B-10: Criminal Activity On The Premises
6-5B-11: Revocation Of Non-Owner Occupied Residential Dwelling Unit Occupancy
Permit Or Code Compliance Report Due To Physical Defects In The Dwelling Unit
6-5B-12: Revocation Of Non-Owner Occupied Residential Dwelling Unit Occupancy
Permit Or Code Compliance Report Due To Maintenance Of A Nuisance
6-5B-13: Hearing Procedures For Revocation, Suspension Or Nonrenewal Of A Non-
Owner Occupied Residential Dwelling Unit Occupancy Permit Or Code Compliance
Report
6-5B-14: Expenses Related To Nuisance Abatement
6-5B-15: Penalty
6-5B-1: PERMITS FOR NON-OWNER OCCUPIED RESIDENTIAL DWELLING UNITS:
   It is unlawful for any owner, manager, or agent thereof to knowingly permit
the occupation of any non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit, to include
multi- family dwellings, apartment units, townhouses, condominiums, duplexes,
or single-family rental units, or additions thereto, including monthly rentals
or occupation of same, for any purpose until a Code compliance report as
required in this article has been issued by the Department of Community
Development, Division of Code Compliance, confirming conformity with this
chapter (hereinafter referred to as a “Code compliance report”) and a non-owner
occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit has been completed, signed
by the prospective tenant(s), and approved by the Division of Code Compliance.
Thereafter, all properties shall be inspected annually and an updated
certificate of occupancy issued. No occupancy permit shall be issued to any
owner, manager, or agent thereof, if such an owner, manager, or agent is
indebted to the Village and until all violations of this article and all other
applicable codes and ordinances of the Village have been brought into
compliance. The possession of a Code compliance report in and of itself does
not authorize occupancy of said property. The occupancy permit issued to the
tenant shall state that the premises and occupancy thereof comply with all the
provisions of this article at all times.
(Ords. 19-06-11, 25-12-34)
6-5B-2: APPLICABILITY:
This article shall be applicable to all residential properties in the Village
that are rented or leased, which shall be defined as any residential dwelling
unit, rooming unit or such other residential building or structure wherein the
owner or owner's agent allows an individual or individuals to occupy space
while charging a daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly or annual fee for the
privilege of occupying said space. All areas of the residential dwelling unit
to which the lessee or renter has the right of use or access shall be subject
to inspection for compliance with the applicable codes and ordinances of the
Village. This section shall not apply to any occupancy in existence at the time
of the adoption of this article until the change of occupancy occurs. Further,
this section shall not apply to any land contract, contract for sale or any
other property transaction wherein the new occupant has legally committed to
acquire legal title to the property and has occupied the premises within sixty
(60) days of the date of the contract. (Ord. 07-27-89)
6-5B-3: INSPECTIONS/FEES:
   A.   Inspections:
      1.   Prior to the time that the department is scheduled to conduct an
inspection, the department shall deliver to the owner and occupants of the
structure written notice that includes the following:
         a.   The date and time of the inspection.
         b.   A statement that the owner or tenant has the right to withhold
his or her consent to the inspection and require the Village to attempt to
obtain a warrant to conduct the inspection.
      2.   If the owner or occupant does not consent to the attempted
inspection, the Director of Inspectional Services ("Director") or his or her
designee shall seek in the Circuit Court of Cook County a warrant in accordance
with the appropriate laws of the State of Illinois to allow an inspection. The
application for the warrant shall specify the basis upon which the warrant is
being sought and shall include a statement that the inspection will be limited
to a determination whether there are violations of the Village Building and
Zoning Ordinances or any applicable Housing, Fire or Property Maintenance Codes
or regulations. The court may consider any of the following factors along with
such other matters as it deems pertinent in its decision as to whether a
warrant shall issue:
         a.   Eyewitness account of violation;
         b.   Citizen complaints;
         c.   Tenant complaints;
         d.   Plain view violations;
         e.   Violations apparent from Village records;
         f.   Property deterioration;
         g.   Age of property;
         h.   Nature of alleged violation;
         i.   Similar properties in the area;
         j.   Documented violations on similar properties in the area;
         k.   Passage of time since last inspection;
         l.   Previous violations on the property.
   B.   Annual Inspection Fees:
      1.   No fee for the first inspection.
      2.   First reinspection: One hundred dollars ($100.00).
      3.   Second reinspection: Two hundred dollars ($200.00).
      4.   Third and subsequent reinspections: Three hundred dollars ($300.00).
(Ord. 15-06-22)
6-5B-4: PREINSPECTION OF RESIDENTIAL DWELLING UNITS:
   The owner, agent or manager of a residential unit subject to the terms of
this article may request that the unit be inspected for code compliance even
though there is no prospective new tenant for the unit.
(Ords. 07-27-89, 25-12-34)
6-5B-5: COMPLIANCE REPORT/NOTICE OF VIOLATIONS:
   A.   Report And Certificate Issued: If the annual inspection establishes
that the non- owner occupied residential dwelling unit complies with all the
provisions of the property maintenance code as well as all other health,
zoning, and building ordinances of the village, then the department of
inspectional services shall issue a code compliance report and certificate of
occupancy for said dwelling or structure. The certificate shall indicate the
date of the inspection; and that such dwelling or structure complies with the
requirements of the property maintenance code as well as other health and
building ordinances that apply to the dwelling. One copy of the certificate
shall be delivered to or mailed to the owner of the dwelling unit. A record of
all certificates shall be kept on file by the department, and copies shall be
furnished upon request to any person having a proprietary interest or tenancy
interest in the dwelling affected. Every certificate of occupancy shall be
issued for a period of one year after its date of issuance, unless sooner
revoked.
   B.   Notice Of Violations: Whenever the building official determines that
any non- owner occupied residential dwelling unit or the premises surrounding
fails to meet the requirements set forth in this chapter or in applicable rules
and regulations issued pursuant thereto, the building official in accordance
with the property maintenance code and the other village health, property
maintenance and building codes shall issue a notice setting forth the alleged
failures and advising the responsible party that such failures must be
corrected. This notice shall:
      1.   Be in writing.
      2.   Set forth the alleged violations of this chapter or of applicable
rules and regulations issued pursuant thereto.
      3.   Describe the non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit where the
violations are alleged to exist or to have been committed. Such written notice
shall specify an appropriate or acceptable method of correction.
      4.   Specify a specific date for the correction of any violation alleged.
      5.   Be served upon the responsible party of the non-owner occupied
residential dwelling unit by the building official, or mailed to the
responsible party. If one or more persons to whom such notice is addressed
cannot be found after diligent effort to do so, service may be made upon such
persons by posting the notice in or about the non-owner occupied residential
dwelling unit described in the notice.
      6.   At the end of the period of time allowed for the correction of any
violation alleged, which was stated in the inspection notice, the building
official shall reinspect the non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit or
property described in the notice.
      7.   If, upon reinspection, the violations are determined by the building
official not to have been corrected, the building official, in turn, shall
issue a code compliance ticket to initiate legal proceedings to be handled in
the village of Oak Lawn’s housing court for the immediate correction of the
alleged violations or shall order the non- owner occupied residential unit
vacated within thirty (30) days, or both.
(Ords. 15-06-22, 25-12-34)
6-5B-6: APPLICATION FOR AND CONTENT OF NON-OWNER OCCUPIED RESIDENTIAL DWELLING
UNIT OCCUPANCY PERMIT:
   The non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit
application must be completed and signed by the tenant before the date of
occupancy. Non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit
applications may be completed at the department of community development,
division of code compliance or completed in the presence of the landlord or the
landlord's agent before the time of occupancy. A non-owner occupied residential
dwelling unit occupancy permit application completed in the presence of the
landlord or the landlord's agent must contain the signature of the landlord or
agent following a certification that the application form was completed in his
presence and contains true and accurate information regarding the tenants who
will be occupying the non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit. All non-
owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit applications must be
submitted to the division of code compliance no later than seven (7) days after
completion. The non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit
shall state the names and the number of people who will occupy the residential
unit. It shall be unlawful for any person to knowingly make any false statement
in his/her application for a non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit
occupancy permit as to the names, relationships, or number of occupants of the
dwelling unit. All persons who occupy the premises of a residential unit,
including persons who are added to the household following the initial
occupancy of the dwelling unit, with the exception of minor children born to
the family, must be listed on the non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit
occupancy permit.
(Ords. 15-06-22, 25-12-34)
6-5B-7: RESPONSIBILITIES OF OWNER, AGENT AND/OR MANAGER:
   A.   Definitions: For the purposes of this article, the following terms
shall have the meanings hereinafter ascribed to them:
   DWELLING: Any enclosed space which is wholly or partly used or intended to
be used, rented, leased, let or hired out to be occupied, or that is occupied
for living purposes.
   DWELLING UNIT: A single unit providing complete, independent living
facilities for one or more persons, including permanent provisions for living,
sleeping, eating, cooking and sanitation.
   IMMEDIATE FAMILY: Immediate family means kinship members including:
      (1)   Mother: A female parent;
      (2)   Father: A male parent;
      (3)   Son: A male child of the parent(s);
      (4)   Daughter: A female child of the parent(s);
      (5)   Brother: A male child of the same parent(s);
      (6)   Sister: A female child of the same parent(s);
      (7)   Grandfather: A parent's father;
      (8)   Grandmother: A parent's mother;
      (9)   Grandson: A child's son;
      (10)   Granddaughter: A child's daughter.
   MANAGING AGENT: Any person or firm, acting for another, with authority to
rent or otherwise authorize occupancy, manage and make expenditures.
   NON-OWNER OCCUPIED RESIDENTIAL DWELLING UNIT: Any multiple dwelling or
dwelling unit which is not owner occupied, and which is either rented, leased,
occupied or made available for leasing or rental or occupancy to others by the
property owner or his/her agent. The occupancy of a single room or the sharing
of a dwelling unit between the property owner of the dwelling unit and others
shall not constitute a non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit. The term
includes the occupancy of property pursuant to articles of agreement for
purchase or a contract for deed until the deed is delivered by the owner.
   B.   Permit Required: It shall be unlawful for any person to rent, lease or
otherwise allow a dwelling or dwelling unit under their ownership to be
occupied by others as a non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit unless a
non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit permit is in effect, as provided
by this article. Permits shall be issued by the Community Development Director
or their designee for a period of one year from July 1 to June 30. Any permit
may be transferred to another person, subject to completion and approval of a
new permit application by the new permitee. In the event an owner can submit a
sworn affidavit that an immediate family member is occupying the dwelling, the
owner shall not be required to register the property after submittal and
Village review and approval of said affidavit. The owner shall instead submit
the name and contact phone number for the occupant for the Village records.
   C.   Application: No non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit permit
shall be issued except upon a receipt of a completed Village of Oak Lawn
landlord permit application filed with the Community Development Director or
his designee. Except as hereinafter provided, such applications shall be
submitted to the Community Development Director or his designee no later than
July 1 preceding the permit year being applied for with the appropriate fees.
The applications shall set forth, but not be limited to, the following
information:
      1.   Full name and address of the legal owner; if owned by a trust, a
trust disclosure is required.
      2.   The permit application shall designate three (3) contacts, including
the property owner, their names, addresses and phone numbers for twenty four
(24) hour contact in case of an emergency.
      3.   The permit application shall list all the non-owner occupied
residential dwelling units owned or managed by the applicant in the Village of
Oak Lawn.
      4.   Proof of ongoing rodent abatement services and quarterly rodent
activity reports are required to register all non-owner occupied residential
dwelling unit properties.
   D.   Reports: The permitee shall report to the Code Enforcement Department
any changes in the information provided on the application or a conveyance of
the property, within seven (7) days of said change or conveyance.
   E.   Issuance Of Permit; Payment Of Permitting Fees; Fee: No permit shall be
issued until the applicable permit fees as set forth in Section
3-1-1-7 of this Code have been paid.
   Renewal shall be required annually.
   F.   Outstanding Monies Due And Owing The Village: Before a permit may be
issued to a permitee hereunder, the permittee shall pay all outstanding fees,
fines or penalties due and owing the Village.
(Ords. 15-06-22, 15-23-82, 19-05-09, 21-20-91, 25-12-34)
6-5B-8: FOSSIL FUELED EQUIPMENT:
   At the discretion of the Code enforcement official, whenever a compliance
inspection is required by the provisions of this article, an owner, agent or
manager of non-owner occupied residential dwelling units, who is responsible
for renting or leasing or occupation of the dwelling units, shall have all
fossil fueled equipment inspected by a qualified service person registered with
the village in order to ensure safe operation of all appliances and
appurtenances attached thereto, provided that, such inspection of fossil fueled
equipment shall not be required when the equipment has been inspected and
approved less than twelve (12) months prior to such compliance inspection of a
unit. The owner, agent or manager of the dwelling shall submit documentation to
the code enforcement department certifying the fossil fueled equipment is in
compliance, and a non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit permit shall not
be issued until such documentation is submitted. If any areas have been found
to be deficient, a qualified service person registered with the village shall
repair the deficiency immediately or take such other action as is necessary to
make the condition safe. When this is not possible, the device’s operation
shall be terminated immediately. Where any action is taken under this section,
the community development department shall be notified within one hour of such
occurrence.
(Ords. 07-27-89, 25-12-34)
6-5B-9: RESPONSIBILITY OF TENANTS:
   Each tenant or lessee or occupant shall provide the division of code
compliance with the information required on the non-owner occupied residential
dwelling unit occupancy permit. The tenant shall complete a new non-owner
occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit if there is any change as
to the names or number of tenants living in the residential unit, with the
exception of minor children born to the family. Any falsification of the
information on the non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy
permit shall constitute a violation of this article. Any person responsible for
the falsification of information or failure to update information shall be
subject to the penalties of this article. Further, no tenant shall damage or
cause to be damaged any unit or building leased, nor shall any damage be caused
to the general premises of any building used by the tenants. Each tenant and
the family of each tenant shall maintain his/their non-owner occupied
residential dwelling unit free of any litter, and tenants shall not litter any
of the premises or the buildings provided for use by tenants.
(Ords. 07-27-89, 25-12-34)
6-5B-10: CRIMINAL ACTIVITY ON THE PREMISES:
   In the event that the residential dwelling unit is used by the tenant or
lessee or occupant for activities which would constitute a felony under the
laws of the state of Illinois or the laws of the United States, including, but
not limited to: a) the wrongful using, keeping or selling of controlled
substances or b) use of the premises for purposes of lewdness, assignation or
prostitution, the lease or occupancy shall, at the option of the lessor, become
void and the lessor or owner shall have the like remedy to recover possession
thereof as against a tenant holding over after the expiration of his term. From
and after  January 1, 2008, all leases shall contain the following addendum in
addition to all other terms of the lease:
   A.   Landlord and tenant agree as follows:
      1.   The tenant, any member of the tenant’s household, any guest or any
other person associated with the tenant on or near the leased premises:
         a.   Shall not engage in criminal activity, including drug related
criminal activity, on or near the leased premises. “Drug related criminal
activity” means the illegal manufacture, sale, distribution, use or possession
of any illegal or controlled substance as defined in 21 USC 802;
         b.   Shall not engage in any act intended to facilitate criminal
activity;
         c.   Shall not permit the dwelling unit to be used for or to
facilitate any criminal activity; or
         d.   Shall not maintain a “nuisance” on the premises as defined in
title 8, chapter 1 of this code.
      2.   Any activity prohibited by this agreement shall constitute a
substantial violation of the lease, material noncompliance with the lease, and
grounds for termination of tenancy and eviction.
(Ords. 07-27-89, 25-12-34)
6-5B-11: REVOCATION OF NON-OWNER OCCUPIED RESIDENTIAL DWELLING UNIT OCCUPANCY
PERMIT OR CODE COMPLIANCE REPORT DUE TO PHYSICAL DEFECTS IN THE DWELLING UNIT:
   A non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit or code
compliance report may be immediately revoked at any time during the occupancy
of the non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit for which the code
compliance report and/or non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy
permit has been issued, should the code enforcement officer determine,
following a reasonable investigation, that the unit no longer complies with the
provisions of this article due to physical defects in the non-owner occupied
residential dwelling unit. Prior to revoking a non-owner occupied residential
dwelling unit occupancy permit and/or code compliance report, hereunder, the
code enforcement officer shall provide the owner, agent or manager and the
tenant with a written report stating the defects noted during the investigation
and providing a reasonable time for the correction of said defects, except when
the conditions of the unit pose an immediate threat to the life and safety of
the occupants, in which case, upon application by the code enforcement officer,
the village manager may issue an emergency notice of revocation to the owner,
agent or manager and the tenant immediately and shall then cause the unit to be
vacated without delay. Procedures for revocation of a non-owner occupied
residential dwelling unit occupancy permit or code compliance report shall be
in accordance with section
6-5B-13 of this article, except in the case of an emergency notice of
revocation.
(Ords. 07-27-89, 25-12-34)
6-5B-12: REVOCATION OF NON-OWNER OCCUPIED RESIDENTIAL DWELLING UNIT OCCUPANCY
PERMIT OR CODE COMPLIANCE REPORT DUE TO MAINTENANCE OF A NUISANCE:
   A non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit and/or code
compliance report may be suspended, not renewed or revoked at any time during
the occupancy of the dwelling unit for which the code compliance report has
been issued, should the code enforcement officer determine, following a
reasonable investigation, that the unit no longer complies with the provisions
of this article due to maintenance of a nuisance on the premises.
   A.   Cause for revocation, suspension or refusal to renew a tenant’s non-
owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit due to maintenance of
nuisance shall include, but is not limited to, the following:
      1.   A tenant’s knowingly permitting illegal drug activity to occur on or
within the premises subject to inspection under this article. The term “illegal
drug related activity”, as used herein, shall mean any conduct which would
violate any provision of the Illinois controlled substances act, as amended,
the Illinois cannabis control act, as amended, the Illinois drug paraphernalia
control act, as amended, or any other local, state, or federal law prohibiting
the manufacture, distribution, delivery, use, or possession of a controlled
substance.
      2.   A tenant’s knowingly permitting prostitution related activity to
occur on or within the premises subject to inspection under this article. The
term “prostitution related activity”, as used herein, shall mean any conduct
which violates any provision of the prostitution related portions of the
Illinois criminal code, as amended, or any other local, state, or federal law
prohibiting prostitution related activity.
      3.   Any activity by a tenant, or by a guest of the tenant with the
tenant’s sufferance or permission, which would constitute a nuisance. The term
“nuisance”, as used herein shall mean any conduct of individuals or condition
of property that injures or endangers the health, safety, peace, and welfare of
the surrounding community or that obstructs the reasonable use of the property.
The term “nuisance” shall include that conduct or condition of property that
has been defined by Illinois or local law to constitute a nuisance. In
addition, a non- owner occupied residential dwelling unit shall be treated as a
nuisance if either:
         a.   The non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit has been the
subject of three (3) or more complaints for police service within a twelve (12)
month period due to the misconduct either of the tenant(s) or of the guest(s)
of the tenant(s) with the tenant’s sufferance or permission. The twelve (12)
month period referred to in this subsection shall be a continuously rolling
period that shall be measured not on a calendar year basis but on the basis of
the twelve (12) months immediately preceding the complaint, call for service,
or violation; or
         b.   The village has declared the non-owner occupied residential
dwelling unit or the lot on which dwelling is located a public nuisance due to
the condition of the property or the conduct of the tenants or guests occupying
the property.
      Any policeman, building inspector or code enforcement officer, upon
receipt of a tenant conduct complaint, shall issue a notice directed to the
owner of record of the property on which the nuisance occurs, as shown in the
records of the recorder of deeds, and to the tenant or occupant of the property
or to both, which notice shall describe the nature of the complaint and the
penalties which may be imposed if the nuisance is not abated.
   B.   Cause for revocation, suspension or refusal to renew a code compliance
letter due to maintenance of a nuisance shall include, but is not limited to,
the following:
      1.   An owner, manager or agent’s knowingly permitting illegal drug
activity to occur on or within the premises subject to inspection under this
article. The term “illegal drug related activity”, as used herein, shall mean
any conduct which would violate any provision of the Illinois controlled
substances act, as amended, the Illinois cannabis control act, as amended, the
Illinois drug paraphernalia control act, as amended, or any other local, state,
or federal law prohibiting the manufacture, distribution, delivery, use, or
possession of a controlled substance.
      2.   An owner, manager or agent’s knowingly permitting prostitution
related activity to occur on or within the premises subject to inspection under
this article.
      The term “prostitution related activity”, as used herein, shall mean any
conduct which violates any provision of the prostitution related portions of
the Illinois criminal code, as amended, or any other local, state, or federal
law prohibiting prostitution related activity.
      3.   Any activity by a tenant or guest of the tenant with the owner,
manager or agent’s sufferance or permission which would constitute a nuisance
as set forth in subsection A3 of this section.
(Ords. 07-27-89, 25-12-34)
6-5B-13: HEARING PROCEDURES FOR REVOCATION, SUSPENSION OR NONRENEWAL OF A NON-
OWNER OCCUPIED RESIDENTIAL DWELLING UNIT OCCUPANCY PERMIT OR CODE COMPLIANCE
REPORT:
   A.   Upon the determination by the supervisor of the division of code
compliance or the supervisor of the community policing unit or their designees
that there is reasonable cause to believe that a non-owner occupied residential
dwelling unit occupancy permit and/or code compliance report should be revoked,
suspended or not renewed because of a violation of this article, notice of a
hearing, together with a written statement of charges, shall be sent to the
owner, manager or agent and the tenant(s). The hearing officer designated under
title 11, chapter 12 of this code shall act as the hearing officer. Such a
hearing shall be held not less than five (5) calendar days after notice of
time, place and subject of the hearing has been received by the owner, manager
or agent and tenant, at their last known address or business address. The
village’s representative shall present evidence in support of the suspension,
revocation or nonrenewal, and the owner, manager, agent and/or tenant shall be
permitted to rebut such evidence and present any other evidence that is
relevant and material. Based on the evidence presented at the hearing, the
hearing officer shall issue a written decision. The hearing officer’s decision
shall be final and binding unless appealed as hereinafter provided. The
suspension or revocation of any non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit
occupancy permit and/or code compliance report shall not release or discharge
the owner, manager, agent or tenant, as appropriate, from paying any fees due
under this article, nor shall such owner, manager, agent or tenant be released
from prosecution for violating this article.
   B.   If the hearing officer determines that a violation has occurred, the
hearing officer may fine the violator and may also revoke, suspend or refuse to
renew the non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy permit and/or
code compliance report or may allow a time certain for abatement of the
nuisance (the “cure period”). If a cure period is allowed by the hearing
officer, immediately upon termination of the cure period, the policeman, code
enforcement officer, or inspector of the building department who served the
notice, or any other policeman or inspector who shall be assigned by the police
(the “village code official”), shall investigate to determine whether or not
the nuisance complained of has been abated.
   C.   In the event the person upon whom notice has been served pursuant to
this article has failed within the prescribed time to demonstrate that the
nuisance has been abated, in addition to the payment of any fines or costs
ordered by the hearing officer as a penalty, the village code official may
request that the village attorney seek a restraining order and a preliminary
injunction, as well as a permanent injunction, before a court with proper
jurisdiction over such an action in order to remedy and abate the violation and
to enjoin further violations of this article.
   D.   The hearing officer’s decision may further authorize the village to
recover any expenses incurred by the village in abating a public nuisance under
the provisions of this article or as authorized by any other applicable law or
ordinance.
   E.   Any person whose non-owner occupied residential dwelling unit occupancy
permit and/or code compliance report has been suspended or revoked or not
renewed by the hearing officer after a hearing under this section may appeal
the hearing officer’s decision to the board of trustees by filing a written
notice of appeal with the village board within three (3) business days of the
person’s receipt of the hearing officer’s decision. The board of trustees, or
its designated hearing committee, shall review the hearing officer’s decision
and may affirm or reverse the decision or remand it to the hearing officer for
further action or review.
(Ords. 07-27-89, 25-12-34)
6-5B-14: EXPENSES RELATED TO NUISANCE ABATEMENT:
In addition to any penalty, fine or other remedy established by law or
ordinance, all expenses incurred by the village in abating a nuisance at a
building or structure that has been declared a public nuisance by the village
shall be chargeable to and collected from the person who created, continued or
permitted the nuisance to exist. These expenses may include, but are not
limited to, the cost of personnel, equipment and materials, including
firefighting personnel and equipment, used to abate the nuisance. It is not a
defense to the payment of these expenses that the village may have otherwise
experienced such costs in the ordinary course of business. (Ord. 07-27-89)
6-5B-15: PENALTY:
   A.   The penalty for a violation of section
6-5B-7 of this article shall be as follows:
      1.   One hundred dollars ($100.00) per unit if the license is not
obtained by the due date.
      2.   The penalty provided in subsection B of this section, if the license
is not obtained within the sixty (60) day period after the due date.
   B.   The penalty for a violation of any section of this article, except as
otherwise provided herein with respect to section
6-5B-7 of this article violations, shall be not less than one hundred dollars
($100.00) per unit nor more than fifty thousand dollars ($50,000.00), and a
separate offense shall be deemed committed on each day during or on which a
violation occurs or continues.
(Ords. 15-06-22, 25-12-34)
ARTICLE C. ABATEMENT OF PUBLIC NUISANCES
SECTION:
6-5C-1: Definitions
6-5C-2: Public Nuisances Prohibited
6-5C-3: Preabatement Procedure
6-5C-4: Judicial Abatement Procedure
6-5C-5: Judicial Abatement Remedy
6-5C-6: Penalty
6-5C-1: DEFINITIONS:
As used in this article, the following words and phrases shall have the
meanings ascribed to them herein:
GUEST OR INVITEE: Any person physically present on the property with the
permission or sufferance of the owner(s) or occupant(s) of the property.
NUISANCE OFFENSE:
   A.   Any offense defined and prohibited by article 9 (homicide) of the
criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/9-1 et seq.
   B.   Any offense defined and prohibited by article 10 (kidnapping and
related offenses) of the criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes
5/10-1 et seq.
   C.   Any offense defined and prohibited by section 11-14 (prostitution),
section 11-15 (soliciting for a prostitute), section 11-16 (pandering), section
11-17 (keeping a place of prostitution), section 11-20 (obscenity), section 11-
20.1 (child pornography), or section 11-21 (harmful material to minors) of the
criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/11-14, 5/11-15, 5/11-
16, 5/11-17, 5/11-20, 5/11-20.1 and 5/11-21.
   D.   Any offense defined and prohibited by article 12 (bodily harm) of the
criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/12-1 et seq.
   E.   Any offense defined and prohibited by article 16 (theft and related
offenses) of the criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/16-
1 et seq.
   F.   Any offense defined and prohibited by section 20-2 (possession of
explosives or incendiary devices) of the criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois
Compiled Statutes 5/20-2 et seq.
   G.   Any offense defined and prohibited by article 24 (deadly weapons) of
the criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/16-1 et seq.
   H.   Any offense defined and prohibited by article 25 (mob action) of the
criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/25-1 et seq.
   I.   Any offense defined and prohibited by section 26-1 (disorderly conduct)
of the criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/26-1 et seq.
   J.   Any offense defined and prohibited by article 28 (gambling) of the
criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/28-1 et seq.
   K.   Any offense defined and prohibited by article 31 (interference with
public officers) of the criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes
5/31-1 et seq.
   L.   Any offense defined and prohibited by section 6-16 (prohibited sales
and possession) or section 6-20 (purchase or acceptance of gift of liquor by
persons under age 21) of the liquor control act of 1934, 235 Illinois Compiled
Statutes 5/6-16 and 5/6-20.
   M.   Any offense defined and prohibited by the Illinois controlled
substances act, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 570/100 et seq.
   N.   Any offense defined and prohibited by the cannabis control act, 720
Illinois Compiled Statutes 550/1 et seq.
   O.   Any inchoate offense defined and prohibited by article 8 (inchoate
offenses) of the criminal code of 1961, 720 Illinois Compiled Statutes 5/8-1 et
seq., which is relative to the commission of any of the aforesaid public
offenses.
   P.   Any conduct of persons or condition of property that injures or
endangers the health, safety, peace and welfare of the surrounding community or
that obstructs the reasonable use of the property, including conduct or any
condition of property that has been defined by Illinois or local law to
constitute a nuisance.
OCCUPANT: Any person in actual or constructive possession of any real property
within the corporate limits of the village, or any of the buildings,
structures, fixtures of improvements located thereon.
ONE YEAR PERIOD: A three hundred sixty five (365) day period commencing on the
date of commission of a nuisance offense.
OWNER: Any person having any legal or equitable ownership interest whatsoever
in any real property within the corporate limits of the village, together with
the buildings, structures, fixtures or improvements located thereon, or who, by
any means whatsoever, has the ability, authority or right to regulate,
restrain, control, direct or affect the conduct of persons present on any such
property.
PERMIT OR SUFFER: To allow, approve, consent to, acquiesce in, agree to, or
failure to prevent through inaction.
PERSON: Any natural individual, corporation, firm, partnership, limited
liability company, unincorporated association, or any other group, enterprise
or entity capable of owning, using or occupying property.
PROPERTY: Any parcel of real property, together with the area within one
hundred feet (100') of any property line of said parcel of property, and any
buildings, structures, improvements or fixtures located thereon or affixed
thereto.
PUBLIC NUISANCE:
   A.   The use by the owner(s) or occupant(s) of a parcel of real property
within the village, including buildings, structures, fixtures or improvements
thereon, in conjunction with the commission of three (3) or more nuisance
offenses within any given one year period; or
   B.   The use by guests or invitees of the owner(s) or occupant(s), with the
owner's or occupant's permission or sufferance, of a parcel of real property
within the village, including buildings, structures, fixtures or improvements
thereon, in conjunction with three (3) or more nuisance offenses within any
given one year period; or
   C.   Any combination of nuisance offenses committed by either the owner(s),
occupant(s) or their respective guest(s) or invitee(s), pursuant to subsection
A or B of this definition, which in combination total three (3) or more
nuisance offenses within any given one year period. (Ord. 07-27-89)
6-5C-2: PUBLIC NUISANCES PROHIBITED:
It shall be unlawful for any owner or occupant of a parcel of property in the
village to intentionally, knowingly, recklessly or negligently permit or suffer
such property in which such person has an ownership interest to become, to
exist or to be used as a public nuisance. (Ord. 07-27-89)
6-5C-3: PREABATEMENT PROCEDURE:
Upon determination by the chief of police or his designee that there is
reasonable cause to believe that more than one nuisance offense has occurred on
a parcel of property within any given one year period, the chief of police or
his designee shall take the following action:
   A.   Notify by certified mail, return receipt requested, or by personal
service, the owner(s) and the occupant(s) of the property in question that said
property may be in danger of becoming a public nuisance under the provisions of
this article which notice shall identify the property in question by either
street address, PIN number or legal description, describe the acts or conduct
which have occurred on the property that could serve as part of the basis for
determining that the property constitutes a public nuisance, and request that
the owner(s) and occupant(s) contact the chief of police or his designee within
fourteen (14) days of the date of the letter to discuss potential courses of
action which will prevent and deter the property in question from becoming a
public nuisance.
   B.   After providing the aforesaid written notice, the chief of police or
his designee shall use his/her best efforts in order to identify and agree with
the owner(s) and/or occupant(s) upon a course of conduct to prevent the
property from becoming a public nuisance under this article.
   C.   The preabatement procedures required in this section shall be deemed
satisfied if:
      1.   The owner(s) and/or occupant(s) fail to meet with the chief of
police or his designee within fourteen (14) days after the mailing of the
preabatement notice; or
      2.   After meeting with the chief of police or his designee, the owner(s)
and/or occupant(s) refuse to enter into an agreement with respect to a
preabatement course of conduct; or
      3.   The owner(s) and/or occupant(s), after agreeing to a preabatement
course of conduct, breach that agreement; or
      4.   A rent/lease permit or code compliance letter has been revoked after
notice and an administrative hearing as provided in article B of this chapter
because the property has been determined to be "public nuisance" as herein
defined, in conjunction with the aforesaid administrative hearing. (Ord. 07-27-
89)
6-5C-4: JUDICIAL ABATEMENT PROCEDURE:
After the satisfaction of the preabatement procedures set forth in section
6-5C-3 of this article, upon determination by the chief of police or his
designees shall so notify the village attorney to initiate judicial proceedings
in the circuit court to abate the public nuisance and to seek the penalties and
remedies provided with respect thereto by the provisions of this article. (Ord.
07-27-89)
6-5C-5: JUDICIAL ABATEMENT REMEDY:
In the event that the village establishes during the course of a judicial
action or administrative proceeding that property within the corporate limits
of the village constitutes a public nuisance, a court with appropriate
jurisdiction has the power and in its discretion may issue a restraining order,
a preliminary injunction, as well as a permanent injunction in order to abate
the public nuisance under such conditions as will do justice and enforce the
purposes of this article. Judicial abatement may include an order restraining
the owner(s) and occupant(s) from using the property for any purpose for a
period of up to one year, which order may further provide that the owner(s) and
occupant(s) shall have the right to use the property during the period of time
specified in the order if any of them shall post a bond or other security
payable to the circuit court and approved by the court in an amount between
five thousand dollars ($5,000.00) and ten thousand dollars ($10,000.00), which
bond or security shall be forfeited and paid over to the circuit court by the
obligor thereof in the event that any additional nuisance offense is committed
upon such property within the period of time set forth in the court order.
(Ord. 07-27-89)
6-5C-6: PENALTY:
In addition to the judicial abatement remedy set forth hereinabove, any person
who is an owner or occupant of any property determined in a judicial action, or
in an administrative proceeding under title 11, chapter 12 of this code, to
constitute a public nuisance who has intentionally, knowingly, recklessly or
negligently permitted or suffered such property to become, be used as or exist
as a public nuisance shall be fined not less than fifty dollars ($50.00) nor
more than fifty thousand dollars ($50,000.00) per day for each day on which
such property was used as or existed as a public nuisance. (Ord. 07-27-89)
CHAPTER 6
CONDOMINIUMS
SECTION:
6-6-1: Definitions
6-6-2: Applicability Of Provisions
6-6-3: Administration
6-6-4: Property Report
6-6-5: Requirements Of Developer
6-6-6: Examination Of Records
6-6-7: Prohibited Acts And Conditions
6-6-8: Provisions Are Additional, Compliance By Injunction
6-6-9: Penalties
6-6-1: DEFINITIONS:
For the purposes of this chapter, the following words and phrases shall have
the meanings respectively ascribed to them by this section:
BLANKET ENCUMBRANCE: A trust deed, mortgage, judgment or other lien or
encumbrance, including an option or contract to sell or a trust agreement
affecting a condominium including any lien or other encumbrance arising as a
result of the imposition of any tax assessment by a public authority.
BOARD OF MANAGERS: The board of managers provided and referred to in the
Illinois condominium property act 1 .
CLOSING OF THE SALE: The operation of transferring ownership of a condominium
unit to the purchaser from the developer.
COMMON ELEMENTS: All of the condominium except the condominium units. "Common
elements" also includes limited common elements.
CONDOMINIUM: A form of property established pursuant to the Illinois
condominium property act.
CONDOMINIUM PROJECT: The sale of or plan by a designer to sell, or the offering
for sale of residential condominium units in an existing building or building
to be constructed or under construction.
CONDOMINIUM UNIT OR UNIT: A separate, three-dimensional (3-D) area within the
condominium identified as such in the declaration and on the condominium plat,
and shall include all improvements contained within such area except those
excluded in the declaration.
CONVERSION, CONVERT: The offering for sale by a developer or his agent of a
condominium unit occupied or rented for any purpose by any person before
commencement of a condominium project, which includes such unit.
DECLARATION: The declaration referred to in the Illinois condominium property
act.
DEVELOPER: Any person who submits property legally or equitably owned by him to
the provisions of the Illinois condominium property act including any successor
to such developer's interest in the property; or any person who offers units
legally or equitably owned by him for sale in the ordinary course of his
business. "Developer" does not include a corporation owning and operating a
cooperative apartment building, unless more than six (6) units are to be sold
to persons other than current stockholders of the corporation.
OFFERING: Any inducement, solicitation, advertisement, publication or
announcement by a developer to any person or the general public to encourage a
person to purchase a condominium unit in a condominium or prospective
condominium.
PERSON: A natural individual, corporation, partnership, trustee or other legal
entity capable of holding title to real property.
PROPERTY REPORT: The property report required in accordance with section
6-6-4 of this chapter.
PROSPECTIVE PURCHASER: A person who visits the condominium project site for the
purpose of inspection for possible purchase, or who requests the property
report. (Ord. 78-33-84)
 
Notes
1 1. 765 ILCS 605/1 et seq.
6-6-2: APPLICABILITY OF PROVISIONS:
The provisions of this chapter shall apply to any existing building for which a
declaration of condominium and plat of survey have not been filed with the
county recorder or the county registrar of titles by the effective date hereof.
Any party claiming that a building is not subject to the provisions of this
chapter shall submit proof to the village manager that the declaration and plat
were recorded prior to the effective date hereof. Said submittal shall include,
but not be limited to, copies of said declaration and survey including the date
stamp and document identification number provided by the county recorder or
registrar of titles. (Ord. 78-33-84)
6-6-3: ADMINISTRATION:
   A.   The quality control department shall administer this chapter and may
adopt rules and regulations for the effective administration hereof. The
quality control department is not responsible for the validity and completeness
of any property reports required by this chapter, as originated by the
developer.
   B.   It shall be the duty and responsibility of the developer who intends to
offer a building for sale as a condominium to notify the department, in
writing, of his intention to do so.
   C.   Whenever a residential building is converted to condominium ownership,
a new certificate of occupancy shall be required for each condominium unit and
for the common areas. Upon receipt of the developer's notice of intent to
convert a residential building or part thereof to condominium ownership, the
department of community development and growth management shall schedule a code
compliance inspection of the condominium units and common elements. The
compliance inspection shall identify any code deficiencies with respect to this
title that are related to either the condominium units or the common elements.
The developer shall promptly correct all such deficiencies. No certificate of
occupancy shall be issued by the department of community development and growth
management for any condominium unit until all code deficiencies have been
corrected. (Ord. 78-33-84; 1985 Code; Ord. 08-04-15)
6-6-4: PROPERTY REPORT:
   A.   Contents: A property report shall contain the following:
      1.   A statement indicating the name and address of:
         a.   The developer and legal and beneficial owner, if different, of
the land and improvements, including all general partners of a partnership or
principal executive officers and directors of a corporation.
         b.   Interim and permanent mortgages or construction lenders secured
by a blanket encumbrance.
         c.   The principal sales and management agents, attorneys,
accountants, architects, engineers and contractors for the project.
      2.   A description of all property and improvements including the
following:
         a.   Map, plat or architect's drawing showing location and dimensions
of the condominium project and the land it occupies, together with all
improvements including recreational facilities, proposed construction and
present and planned location of streets and driveways.
         b.   The share of ownership of each unit in each common element. The
identity of owners of such condominium unit, including the percentage of former
renters who have purchased or contracted to purchase a condominium unit when
the property is a conversion, if known. If such units are owned in trust or by
nominees, the beneficiaries or principal shall be named, if known.
         c.   A description of all of the common elements in the project
including a description of all existing and proposed recreational facilities
and other such facilities within the project. Limited common elements, if any,
and their ownership shall also be included.
         d.   A description of the nature and ownership of all improvements
occupying the same zoning lot, but which are not part of the condominium.
         e.   Location, nature and ownership of easements, streets and
driveways on or contiguous to the condominium.
         f.   The identification of drawings, architectural plans, shop
drawings for mechanical systems and equipment, and other related documents
which accurately describe the completed buildings and recreational facilities,
mechanical systems and equipment, and access points for the proper maintenance
of all such facilities and equipment to the extent these documents exist, where
these documents are located and the time at which they may be inspected.
         g.   Projected initiation and completion dates for proposed
construction, renovation and conversion.
         h.   A description of limitations upon uses permitted in individual
condominium units as contained in the declaration and bylaws of the condominium
association and applicable zoning provisions. Such description shall state
whether, or under what conditions, the condominium units may be rented together
by the unit owner.
         i.   Statement as to whether a purchaser may purchase more than one
unit and under what circumstances or conditions.
         j.   Statement of legal ownership, listing all restrictions, notices,
lis pendens and encumbrances of record.
      3.   Method and timing of transfer of control of the condominium to the
board of managers, and the nature and extent of any interest retained by the
developer thereafter.
      4.   A statement disclosing the existence of penalties if the
construction, renovation, conversion or completion date is not met and the
additional costs to be imposed upon unit owners if such date is not met.
      5.   The nature and extent of any protection of a purchaser if the
developer defaults on blanket encumbrances.
      6.   A statement of any litigation which would affect the condominium or
the developer's ability to convey clear title.
      7.   A statement of the current taxes and estimated changes in the tax
assessment of the condominium units which buyers may encounter during the first
two (2) years.
      8.   Copies of the forms of sales documents applicable to the individual
units, including, but not limited to:
         a.   Basic purchase contract form being used by the developer.
         b.   Deeds of conveyance.
         c.   Deed of trust, mortgage and promissory note, if any.
      9.   Statement of sales prices, terms, options, conditions of sale of
each unsold unit, including estimated closing and settlement costs and transfer
taxes.
      10.   Statement of estimated monthly payments for each unit to be
itemized as to taxes, utilities, operating costs, assessments, parking,
recreational facilities and all other payments in the first year after the
projected date of assumption of control by the board of managers.
      11.   If financed by the developer, the proposed financing of each unit,
including percent of sales price required for down payment, duration of the
loan, interest rate, service charge, appraisal charge, closing charges and
total monthly payment.
      12.   A description of all appliances and personal property included with
each unit.
      13.   Documents:
         a.   Copies of the following documents:
            (1)   The declaration and plat. However, prior to the recordation
of the declaration, a preliminary declaration and plat may be supplied,
provided it is accompanied by a statement in type size and style equal to at
least ten (10) point bold face type, as follows:
"THE DESCRIPTION OF UNITS AND PERCENTAGE OF OWNERSHIP INTEREST IN COMMON
ELEMENTS HEREIN IS PRELIMINARY AND MAY BE CHANGED IN MATERIAL RESPECTS UPON THE
RECORDING OF THE DECLARATION OF PLAT."
            (2)   The articles of incorporation of charter of the condominium
association, if any.
         b.   The description of the following documents:
            (1)   Any leases of real or personal property in the condominium
expiring later than two (2) years after the first unit is offered for sale.
            (2)   Any management contract, employment contract, insurance
policy or other contract affecting the use, maintenance or access to all or
part of the condominium expiring later than two (2) years after the first unit
is offered for sale.
            (3)   The coverage and amounts of insurance policies applicable to
the condominium maintained by or on behalf of the developer.
      14.   A statement of management and expected management costs of the
condominium including:
         a.   Name of management agent, if any, and the services the agent will
perform.
         b.   Length of term of any management contract, its costs and the
circumstances, if any, under which the charges may be increased.
         c.   The conditions, if any, under which the contract may be cancelled
or terminated.
         d.   A statement stating the relationship between the developer and
the management firm and their respective corporate officers and controlling
interests, if any.
      15.   An estimated operating budget, including the basis on which each
item included in such operating budget was formulated, for the condominium,
projected for a period of one year from the expected date that control of the
condominium project passes to the board of managers. The operating budget shall
include at least the following:
         a.   Operating Costs:
            Utilities;
            Heating fuels;
            Janitorial services;
            Trash and garbage disposal;
            Landscaping and building maintenance;
            Security;
            Maintenance and operation of recreational and other facilities;
            Building insurance;
            Elevator maintenance;
            Sidewalk and street maintenance;
            Other operating costs.
         b.   Management Costs:
            Accounting and bookkeeping services;
            Legal services;
            Management fees.
         c.   Reserve Costs:
            Reserve for improvements;
            Reserve for unexpected repair work;
            Reserve for replacement and upkeep of common area and facilities;
            Reserve for taxes and special assessments.
            If no reserve is provided for any one or more of the costs listed
herein, the following statement must be inserted in the property report in a
type size and style equal to at least ten (10) point bold type:
"THE DEVELOPER HAS NOT PROVIDED A RESERVE FOR CERTAIN POSSIBLE FUTURE COSTS OF
THE CONDOMINIUM IN HIS BUDGET. ACCORDINGLY, IT MAY BE NECESSARY TO PROVIDE FOR
A SPECIAL ASSESSMENT TO ALL CONDOMINIUM UNIT OWNERS TO PAY FOR SUCH COSTS
SHOULD THEY OCCUR."
      16.   Provisions, if any, the developer had made to cover the proposed
operations and maintenance budget in the event an insufficient number of units
are sold.
      17.   If a conversion, a report from a qualified licensed engineer or
registered architect describing the condition and expected useful life of the
roof, foundation, external and supporting walls, mechanical, electrical,
plumbing, heating and structural elements, and all other common facilities,
together with an estimate of repair and replacement costs for those items
needing repair or replacement at current market prices. This report shall
include the approximate dates of installation of the facilities listed above
and the dates of the major repairs to such facilities. There shall be attached
to such report:
         a.   A statement of the developer that no notice of violations of the
building provisions of this Village Code pertaining to the condominium building
have been received by the owner or his predecessors for ten (10) years
preceding the property report and its latest amendment; or
         b.   A list of all notices of violations of the building provisions of
this Village Code received, together with a detailed statement of all
violations referred to in such notices, for the prior ten (10) years.
      18.   A statement of whether, and under what circumstances, the unit
owners are required to be a member of, support or participate financially in
recreational facilities, such as but not limited to health clubs, exercise
rooms, swimming pools, party rooms and golf putting greens. If any such
facility is not part of the common elements, the following warning shall be
included in capital letters, in a type size and style equal to at least ten
(10) point bold type:
"THE (HERE NAME FACILITIES) ARE NOT INCLUDED IN THE COMMON ELEMENTS. THESE
FACILITIES ARE AVAILABLE TO UNIT OWNER FOR (HERE DESCRIBE MONTHLY CHARGE AND
INITIATION FEE). UNIT OWNERS ARE/ARE NOT (AS APPLICABLE) REQUIRED TO
PARTICIPATE FINANCIALLY."
      19.   A description of the location, ownership and availability to unit
owners and the general public of accessory off-street parking associated with
the condominium. If all such parking facilities are not (a) part of the common
elements or (b) divided as individual parking space among and designated as
being part of the units, the following statement shall be included in a type
and size and equal to at least ten (10) point bold type:
"PARKING FACILITIES ASSOCIATED WITH THIS BUILDING ARE NOT OWNED BY THE UNIT
OWNERS AND MAY BE SUBJECT TO BEING DENIED TO OR TAKEN FROM UNIT OWNERS."
      20.   A statement if there are any restrictions upon the free sale,
transfer, conveyance, encumbrance or leasing of a unit as follows:
"THE SALE, LEASE OR TRANSFER OF YOUR UNIT IS RESTRICTED OR CONTROLLED."
Immediately following this statement, there shall appear a reference to the
documents, articles, paragraphs and pages in the property report where the
restriction, limitation or control on the sale, lease or transfer of units is
set forth or described in detail.
      21.   A statement on the first page, the following warning in capital
letters in a type size and style equal to at least ten (10) point bold type:
VILLAGE OF OAK LAWN LAW SPECIFICALLY PROHIBITS ANY REPRESENTATION TO THE EFFECT
THAT THE VILLAGE HAS PASSED UPON THE MERITS OF OR GIVEN APPROVAL TO MAKE OR
CAUSE TO BE MADE TO ANY PROSPECTIVE PURCHASER ANY REPRESENTATIONS WHICH DIFFER
FROM THE STATEMENTS IN THIS PROPERTY REPORT. ORAL REPRESENTATIONS CANNOT BE
RELIED UPON AS CORRECTLY STATING THE REPRESENTATIONS OF THE DEVELOPER AND ARE
NOT BINDING ON THE DEVELOPER. REFER TO THE PROPERTY REPORT FOR BINDING
REPRESENTATIONS.
      22.   The signature of the executive officer of the developer and
statement affirming that the report and any supplements, modifications and
amendments are true, full, complete and correct.
   B.   Amendments To Report: The developer shall amend the property report
from time to time when any material changes occur in any matter contained in
such reports. Amendments shall be made as soon as practicable after such change
occurs, or the developer has reason to know of such change. Amendments shall be
attached to reports subsequently distributed to prospective purchasers and
shall be immediately distributed to all persons who have purchased or agreed to
purchase condominium units.
No later than thirty (30) days prior to the recording of the declaration and
plat, the developer shall give notice of any material changes in said
declaration and plat, as described in the property report, to each person who
has executed a contract to purchase a unit. (Ord. 78-33-84)
6-6-5: REQUIREMENTS OF DEVELOPER:
   A.   Notice Of Intent To Convert:
      1.   No less than one hundred eighty (180) days prior to recording the
declaration submitting the property to the provisions of the Illinois
Condominium Property Act, a developer shall give notice of such intent to
record to all persons who are tenants of the building on the property on the
date notice is given, and to the Village Clerk.
      2.   Any person who was a tenant as of the date of the notice of intent
and whose tenancy expires other than for cause prior to the expiration of one
hundred eighty (180) days from the date on which a copy of the notice of intent
was received by the tenant shall have the right to an additional tenancy on the
same terms and conditions and for the same rental until the expiration of such
one hundred eighty (180) day period by the giving of written notice thereof to
the developer within thirty (30) days of the date upon which a copy of the
notice of intent was received by the tenant.
      3.   During the period of one hundred eighty (180) days following his
receipt of the notice of intent, any person who was both a tenant on the date
of the notice of intent and a current tenant shall have the right of first
refusal, if at all, by giving notice thereof to the developer prior to the
expiration of thirty (30) days from the giving of notice by the developer to
the tenant that a contract to purchase the unit has been executed. Each
contract for sale of a unit shall conspicuously disclose the existence of, and
shall be subject to, such right of first refusal. The statement in the deed
conveying the unit to a purchaser to the effect that the tenant of the unit
either waived or failed to exercise the right of first refusal or had no right
of first refusal with respect to the unit shall extinguish any legal or
equitable right or interest to the possession or acquisition of the unit which
the tenant may have or claim with respect to the unit arising out of the right
of first refusal provided for in this subsection. The foregoing provisions
shall not affect any claim which the tenant may have against the developer for
damages arising out of the right of first refusal provided herein, nor shall it
affect the penalties provided in Section
6-6-9 of this Chapter.
      4.   No occupied unit shall be shown to any purchaser or prospective
purchaser for thirty (30) days after notice of intent to record, as provided
herein, is given.
      5.   Any notice provided for in this subsection shall be given by a
written notice delivered in person or mailed, certified or registered mail,
return receipt requested, to the party who is being given the notice.
   B.   Property Report Submitted To Tenants:
      1.   No later than thirty (30) days after mailing or delivering the
notice of intent as provided in subsection A of this Section, a developer of a
condominium of more than six (6) units must have a property report available
for mailing and distribution and immediately serve a copy of the property
report upon all persons who are tenants of the building to be converted. Such
service may be by certified or registered mail, return receipt requested, or by
personal delivery, provided a signed receipt is obtained from each tenant so
served. Said property report shall also be available at said time for
examination by the Department. A developer may make a charge, not to exceed two
dollars ($2.00), for each report mailed or distributed pursuant to this
Chapter.
      2.   Not later than the offering for sale of the first unit, a developer
of a condominium of more than six (6) units must:
         a.   Make said property report available for distribution to each
prospective purchaser.
         b.   Make available for inspection by prospective purchasers, copies
of all documents that were filed or required to be filed in connection with the
condominium project with the Recorder of Deeds of Cook County.
         c.   Keep a receipt signed by each purchaser acknowledging that the
person entering a contract to purchase has received and has had an opportunity
to review the property report. Such receipts are to be kept on file in the
possession of the developer for a period of three (3) years from the date of
signature of the purchaser and such receipts are subject to the inspection of
the Department at any reasonable time.
      3.   The board of managers shall keep a copy of the latest property
report for seven (7) years following the date of the property report's initial
distribution. Upon reasonable notice, the property report shall be made
available for inspection by any prospective purchaser of a unit from a unit
owner. (Ords. 78-33-84, 79-36-81, 81-24-43, 88-5-19, 97-20-78)
6-6-6: EXAMINATION OF RECORDS:
No person shall fail to allow unit owners to inspect the financial books and
records of the condominium association within three (3) business days of the
time written request for examination of the records is received. (Ord. 78-33-
84)
6-6-7: PROHIBITED ACTS AND CONDITIONS:
   A.   Required Use Of Recreational Facilities: The developer may not require
nor (except as established by the board of managers following assumption of
control by unit purchasers) may the condominium bylaws require that a unit
owner be a member of or participate in recreational or similar facilities which
are not owned in fee by the unit owners or by an association in which they are
members individually or through the board of managers.
   B.   Misleading Inducements To Buy: No person shall intentionally mislead a
prospective purchaser with any act, inducement to buy or advertisement by:
      1.   Employing any statement or pictorial representation which is false
or misleading; or
      2.   Omitting any material statement or pictorial representation.
   C.   Discrimination: No person shall be denied the right to purchase or
lease a unit because of race, religion, sex, sexual preference, marital status
or national origin. (Ord. 78-33-84)
6-6-8: PROVISIONS ARE ADDITIONAL, COMPLIANCE BY INJUNCTION:
The rights, obligations and remedies set forth in this Chapter shall be
cumulative and in addition to any other available at law or inequity. The
Quality Control Department or any prospective purchaser, purchaser or owner of
a unit may seek compliance by injunction of any provision of this Chapter;
provided, however, that only the Building Department may enforce the provisions
of Section
6-6-9 of this Chapter. (Ord. 78-33-84; 1985 Code)
6-6-9: PENALTIES:
   A.   General: Any person found guilty of violating any of the provisions of
Section
6-6-5 of this Chapter, upon conviction thereof, shall be punished by a fine of
not less than one hundred dollars ($100.00) nor more than seven hundred fifty
dollars ($750.00). Each failure to comply with these provisions with respect to
each person shall be considered a separate offense. A separate and distinct
offense shall be regarded as committed each day on which such person shall
continue or permit any such violation.
   B.   Revocation Of License: In addition to such fines and penalties,
violation of any provision of this Chapter shall be cause for revocation of any
license issued to such violator or offending party by the Village. Nothing
herein shall be construed to preclude the revocation of any license for
violation of any other provisions of this Code. See Section
3-1-6 of this Code for license revocation hearing provisions. (Ord. 78-33-84;
1985 Code; Ord. 99-5-11)
CHAPTER 7
MOBILE HOMES AND MOBILE HOME PARKS
SECTION:
6-7-1: Definitions
6-7-2: Mobile Home Park License And Permit Provisions
6-7-2-1: License To Operate
6-7-2-2: Permit To Construct
6-7-2-3: Issuance Or Denial Of License Or Permit
6-7-2-4: Display Of Licenses And Permits
6-7-3: Location
6-7-4: Park Plan
6-7-5: Service Buildings And Accommodations
6-7-6: Accessory Structures And Improvements
6-7-7: Utilities
6-7-8: Community Dining Facilities
6-7-9: Duties Of Park Attendant And Licensee
6-7-10: Investigations By School Authorities
6-7-11: Park Office; Rules And Regulations Posted
6-7-12: Records Kept
6-7-13: Parking Restrictions
6-7-14: Applicability Of Other Regulations
6-7-15: Pine Tree Mobile Home Court Rules And Regulations
6-7-1: DEFINITIONS:
Whenever used in this Chapter, unless a different meaning appears from the
context, the following terms and phrases shall have the meanings herein
ascribed to them:
DEPENDENT UNIT: A mobile home which does not have bathroom or shower and toilet
facilities.
MOBILE HOME OR TRAILER COACH: Any coach, cabin, trailer, house car or other
vehicle or structure intended for or capable of human dwelling or sleeping
purposes, mounted upon wheels or supports, and/or capable of being moved by its
own power or transported by another vehicle; provided, that any structure
resting in whole or on permanent foundation, with wheels, tongue and hitch
permanently removed, shall not be construed as a trailer coach or mobile home.
MOBILE HOME PARK: Any park, court, camp, site, plot, parcel or tract of land
designed, maintained, intended or used for the purpose of supplying a location
or accommodations for more than two (2) mobile homes and shall include all
buildings used or intended for use as part of the equipment thereof whether or
not a charge is made for the use of the park and its facilities. "Mobile home
park" shall not include automobile or trailer or mobile home sales lots on
which unoccupied mobile homes are parked for purposes of inspection and sale.
NONDEPENDENT UNIT: A mobile home that has bath or shower and toilet facilities.
SPACE: A plot of ground in a mobile home park of not less than one thousand
(1,000) square feet of space designed for the location for only one automobile
and/or one mobile home.
UNIT: A mobile home unit. (1951 Code 12-11-1; Ords. 72, 72-14-18)
6-7-2: MOBILE HOME PARK LICENSE AND PERMIT PROVISIONS:
6-7-2-1: LICENSE TO OPERATE:
   A.   License Required: It shall be unlawful for any person to establish,
operate or maintain, or permit to be established, operated or maintained upon
any property owned, leased or controlled by him, a mobile home park within the
limits of the Village without having first secured a license for each such park
in accordance with the provisions of Section
3-1-1 of this Code.
   B.   Application for License:
      1.   The application for a mobile home park license or a renewal thereof
shall be made on forms furnished by the Village and shall include the
following:
         a.   The name and address of the owner in fee of the tract (if the fee
is vested in some person other than the applicant);
         b.   A duly certified statement by that person that the applicant is
authorized by him to construct or maintain the mobile home park and make the
application;
         c.   The names and addresses of the partners if it is a partnership,
the names and addresses of the officers if the applicant is a corporation, and
the present or last address of the applicant at the time of the filing of the
application; and
         d.   Such legal description of the premises upon which the mobile home
park is or will be located as will readily identify and definitely locate the
premises.
      2.   The application shall be accompanied by two (2) copies of the park
plan showing the following, either existing or as proposed:
         a.   The extent and area used for park purposes;
         b.   Roadways and driveways;
         c.   Location of units for mobile homes;
         d.   The proposed and existing facilities in the mobile home park for
water supply, sewage, garbage and waste disposal, fire protection and for a
sanitary community building which will include a description of toilets,
urinals, sinks, washbasins, slop sinks, showers, drains and laundry facilities,
the proposed alterations therein and the maintenance thereof.
         e.   Method and plan of sewage disposal;
         f.   Method and plan of garbage removal;
         g.   Plan for water supply;
         h.   Plan for electrical lighting of units and land upon which the
mobile home park is to be located;
         i.   If the existing or proposed camp is designed to serve
nondependent mobile home units, such plans shall clearly set forth the location
of all sewer and water pipes and connections;
         j.   The calendar months of the year in which applicant will operate
said mobile home park;
         k.   The plot plans of the trailer coach park, building plans and
specifications for existing buildings and facilities, and the plans and
specifications for new buildings and facilities or the proposed alterations in
existing facilities, all as may be required by the rules and regulations of the
Village under the provisions of this Chapter.
         l.   A statement of the fire fighting facilities, public or private,
which are available to the mobile home park.
      3.   An affidavit of the applicant as to the truth of the matters
contained in the application shall be attached thereto. Where a permit to
construct as well as an original license to operate is sought by the applicant,
request therefor shall be made in the same application. Each application shall
be accompanied by the appropriate fee 1 .
   C.   Inspections: No mobile home park license shall be issued until the
Chief of Police, Health and Sanitation Officer, Chief of the Fire Department,
and Director of Office of Quality Control, or their authorized agents, inspect
or cause to be inspected each application and the premises to determine whether
the applicant and the premises on which mobile homes will be located comply
with the regulations, ordinances and laws applicable thereto. No license shall
be removed without a reinspection of the premises. For the purpose of making
inspections and securing enforcement, such officials or their authorized agents
shall have the right and are hereby empowered to enter on any premises on which
a mobile home is located, or about to be located, and to inspect the same and
all accommodations connected therewith, at any reasonable time.
   D.   Transfer of Licenses: All licenses issued under this Chapter shall be
nontransferable without the written consent of the Village; provided, however,
that the Village may not withhold such consent where the provisions of this
Chapter have been met.
   E.   Suspension or Revocation of License, Hearing:
      1.   Notice of Suspension or Revocation: Any license granted hereunder
shall be subject to revocation or suspension by the Village; however, the
Village Clerk shall first serve or cause to be served upon the licensee a
written notice in which shall be specified the way or ways in which such
licensee has failed to comply with the ordinance or any rules or regulations
pertaining thereto. Said notice shall require the licensee to remove or abate
such nuisance, insanitary or objectionable condition, specified in such notice
within five (5) days or within a longer period of time as may be allowed by the
Village. If the licensee fails to comply with the terms and conditions of said
notice, within the time specified or such extended period of time, the Village
may revoke or suspend such license.
      2.   Public Hearing: A public hearing shall be conducted by the Village,
if written request therefor is made by the licensee, within five (5) days after
receipt of the Village notice. Any person refused a permit to construct or
alter a mobile home park or a license, or whose license is suspended or
revoked, shall have the right to a hearing before the Village Board. A written
notice of a request for such a hearing shall be served upon the Village within
thirty (30) days of such refusal of a permit to construct or alter, or refusal
of a license or suspension or revocation thereof. Written notice of such
decision shall be given, by mail, to the park operator or the applicant, as the
case may be, within five (5) days of such refusal, suspension or revocation.
The hearing shall be conducted by the Village Board under such rules and
regulations and procedures as it shall provide. (1951 Code 12-11-3, 12-11-4;
Ords. 72, 72-14-18, 81-12-19; 1985 Code)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Code.
6-7-2-2: PERMIT TO CONSTRUCT:
   A.   Permits Required, Application:
      1.   No person shall construct a mobile home park without first obtaining
a permit to do so from the Director of Office of Quality Control.
      2.   No change in any sanitary facilities, methods of water supply,
sewage, drainage, garbage or waste disposal, and no change in the plot plan
shall be made without first making a written application to the Village and
receiving a written permit therefrom. Such application shall be made in the way
and manner hereinbefore set forth; such change or changes shall comply with
such safety and sanitary code, codes, rules and regulations as are applicable
thereto. No application fee shall be required to accompany an application for a
permit to alter a mobile home park where such alteration involves only a
reduction in the number of trailer spaces to a number less than such trailer
coach park is currently licensed for. Such change or changes shall comply with
such safety and sanitary code, building codes, rules and regulations as are
applicable thereto. Such permit does not relieve the applicant from securing
building permits or from complying with any zoning or other ordinance
applicable thereto.
   B.   Inspections: If a permit to construct a mobile home park has been
issued, the applicant, upon completion thereof, shall notify the Village. The
Village shall then inspect the mobile home coach park and if completed in
accordance with the accepted application, the Village shall issue a license.
(1951 Code 12-11-3; Ords. 72, 72-14-18, 81-12-19)
6-7-2-3: ISSUANCE OR DENIAL OF LICENSE OR PERMIT:
Upon receipt for an application for a permit to construct a mobile home park or
an application for a license to operate and maintain the same, the Village
Clerk shall, if the park is or the proposed park will be, in conformity with
this Chapter and the rules and regulations adopted by the Village pursuant
hereto, issue a permit to construct or a license, as the case may be. If the
application for a permit to construct or a license is declined, the Village
Clerk shall give the reasons therefor in writing to the applicant; and if the
objections can be corrected, the applicant may amend his application and
resubmit it for approval. (1951 Code 12-11-3; Ords. 72, 72-14-18)
6-7-2-4: DISPLAY OF LICENSES AND PERMITS:
All permits to construct, all licenses to operate and all permits to make
alterations in trailer parks shall be prominently displayed in the office of
the mobile home park. (1951 Code 12-11-3; Ords. 72, 72-14-18)
6-7-3: LOCATION:
   A.   No mobile home or mobile home park shall be located in any fire
district.
   B.   No occupied mobile home within the limits of the Village shall be
located between the recognized setback line for the zoning district in which
such mobile home is located and the street or highway, and in no circumstances
shall the minimum setback be less than five feet (5') from the side lot lines
of the mobile home park, nor less than ten feet (10') from any building or
other mobile home or from the boundary line of the premises on which located,
nor less than ten feet (10') from a public street or alley. (1951 Code 12-11-5;
Ords. 72, 72-14-18)
6-7-4: PARK PLAN:
   A.   Surface Drainage: Every mobile home or mobile home park shall be
located on a well- drained area and the premises shall be properly graded so as
to prevent the accumulation of storm or other waters. No mobile home or mobile
home park shall be located in any area that is situated so that drainage from
any barnyard, outdoor toilet or other source of filth can be deposited in its
location.
   B.   Mobile Home Site Requirements: Each trailer coach shall be allotted a
site of not less than one thousand (1,000) square feet, shall be clearly
defined and shall have a width of not less than twenty feet (20'). Each
individual trailer site shall abut or face on a driveway or roadway of not less
than twenty four feet (24') in width, which driveway or roadway shall have
unobstructed access to a public highway or alley. All streets and driveways in
every trailer coach park must be graveled or paved, and must be maintained in a
passable and reasonably dust-proof condition at all times, and all streets and
driveways in every trailer coach park established after January 1, 1954 shall
have a minimum width of twenty feet (20'). All streets and driveways in parks
constructed after August 21, 1967 shall have a minimum of twenty four feet
(24'). Such streets, driveways or roadways shall be maintained in good
condition, having storm sewers or natural drainage, and be well illuminated
during the hours of darkness.
   C.   Location of Dependent Units: The park shall be so laid out that no
dependent unit shall be located farther than two hundred feet (200') from the
toilets and service buildings provided for herein, and walkways to such
buildings shall be graveled or paved and well lighted at night. (1951 Code 12-
11-6; Ords. 72, 69-5-10, 72-14-18)
6-7-5: SERVICE BUILDINGS AND ACCOMMODATIONS:
   A.   Every mobile home park designed to serve dependent units shall have
erected thereon suitable buildings for housing toilets, lavatories, showers,
slop sinks and laundry facilities as required by this Chapter; such buildings
to be known as service buildings. Service buildings shall be located not more
than two hundred feet (200') from any dependent unit space, nor closer than
fifteen feet (15') from any mobile home space. Such buildings shall be of
permanent construction and adequately lighted, screened and ventilated.
   B.   There shall be provided separate toilet rooms for each sex. Water flush
toilets shall be required and shall be provided for each sex in the ratio of
one toilet for each eight (8) dependent units or fraction thereof and shall
have separate compartments, properly separated from other water closets, and
shall be not less than three feet (3') wide and shall be enclosed with proper
partition. Every male toilet room shall also contain one urinal for each
sixteen (16) dependent units but in no case shall any male toilet be without
one urinal. Toilet rooms shall contain lavatories with hot and cold water in
the ratio of one lavatory to every two (2) or less water closets.
   C.   Separate bathing facilities for each sex shall be provided with one
shower enclosed in a compartment at least four feet (4') for each eight (8)
dependent units or fraction thereof. Each shower compartment shall be
supplemented by an individual dressing compartment of at least twelve (12)
square feet. The floor of such compartment shall be waterproof and elevated
three inches (3") above the floor of the shower stall or a six inch (6")
curbing provided, separating shower compartment from dressing room. Mats, grids
and walkways made of wood, cloth or other absorbent materials will not be
approved for use in bath sections of community service buildings.
   D.   Laundry facilities shall be provided in the ratio of one double tray
unit and one conventional type washing machine, or one automatic washing
machine, with electric outlet, for each eight (8) units. Sufficient drying
facilities shall be available. No laundry tray shall be located in a toilet
room or bathroom.
   E.   Slop sinks for disposal of liquid wastes originating at the units shall
be provided in a separate room of the service building in the ratio of one slop
sink for each sixteen (16) dependent units.
   F.   The above accommodations shall be based on the total park capacity
according to accepted plans.
   G.   Floors of toilets, showers and the laundry shall be of concrete tile or
similar material impervious to water and easily cleaned. Concrete curbings
extending at least six inches (6") above the floor shall be provided and the
floor sloped to adequate drains. (1951 Code 12-11-8; Ords. 72, 75, 69-5-10, 72-
14-18)
6-7-6: ACCESSORY STRUCTURES AND IMPROVEMENTS:
   A.   Temporary porches, canvas-roofed canopies and skirts shall be permitted
for trailer coaches, and auxiliary rooms may also be authorized by regulation
of the Village if constructed of fire-resistant materials of such
specifications as shall be required by such regulations.
   B.   No mobile home shall be improved with a permanent foundation or lean-to
other than screens and awnings which shall be of fire- resistant materials.
(1951 Code 12-11-6; 1969 Code 12-21; Ords. 72, 72-14-18)
6-7-7: UTILITIES:
   A.   Water Supply:
      1.   An adequate supply of pure water, furnished through a pipe
distribution system connected directly with the public water main with supply
faucets located not more than two hundred feet (200') from any dependent mobile
home, shall be furnished for drinking and domestic purposes in all parks. Each
independent mobile home site shall have a cold water tap at least four inches
(4") aboveground.
      2.   Individual water service connections provided for direct use of an
independent unit shall be so constructed that they will not be damaged by the
parking of such units. Such systems shall be adequate to provide twenty (20)
pounds' pressure per square inch and capable of furnishing a minimum of one
hundred twenty five (125) gallons per day per space.
      3.   No common drinking vessels shall be permitted, nor shall any
drinking water faucets be placed in any toilet room.
      4.   Every mobile home park serving dependent units shall provide an
abundant supply of hot water at all reasonable hours for bathing, washing and
laundry facilities.
   B.   Sewer Regulations:
      1.   All liquid waste from showers, toilets, laundries, faucets,
lavatories, etc., shall be discharged into a sewer system extended from and
connected with the public sewer system. No water or waste shall be allowed to
fall on the ground from a mobile home.
      2.   Every space designed to serve a nondependent unit shall be provided
with sewer connections which shall comply with all applicable plumbing
regulations. The sewer connection shall be provided with suitable fittings so
that watertight connections can be made. Such connections shall be so
constructed so that they can be closed when not connected and trapped in such a
manner as to be maintained in an odor-free condition.
      3.   All sanitary facilities in any unit which are not connected with a
public sewer system by approved pipe connections shall be sealed and their use
is hereby declared unlawful.
      4.   Each faucet shall be equipped with facilities for drainage of waste
and excess water.
   C.   Garbage Disposal:
      1.   A sufficient number of adequate flyproof and watertight containers
shall be supplied for the storage of garbage except where an adequate
incinerator is provided.
      2.   Garbage containers shall be emptied at least every three (3) days
and shall not be filled to overflowing or allowed to become foul smelling or a
breeding place for flies. Garbage and rubbish shall be disposed of in a manner
which creates neither a nuisance nor a menace to health and which is approved
by the Department.
      3.   Adequate insect and rodent-control measures shall be employed. All
buildings shall be fly and rodentproof and rodent harborages shall not be
permitted to exist in the park.
   D.   Electrical Facilities:
      1.   Electrical outlets for each individual trailer site shall be
provided and the installation shall be in compliance with all State and local
electrical codes and ordinances 1 . No connected electric extension cord shall
lie on the ground or be suspended less than seven feet (7') from the ground
above sidewalks or pathways.
      2.   Every mobile home space shall be furnished with an electric service
outlet. Such outlet shall be equipped with an externally operated switch or
fuse of not less than thirty (30) amperes' capacity, and a heavy duty outlet
receptacle. Electrical outlets shall be weatherproof and no power lines shall
be less than fifteen feet (15') above ground. The entire electrical system
shall meet the minimum requirements of the State and local electrical codes.
(1951 Code 12-11-7, 12-11-9; 1969 Code 12-19; Ords. 72, 75, 72-14-18)
 
Notes
1 1. See Title 6, Chapter 2 of this Municipal Code.
6-7-8: COMMUNITY DINING FACILITIES:
Any mobile home park providing community dining facilities shall comply with
State and local regulations applicable to eating establishments 1 . (1951 Code
12-11-9; Ords. 72, 72-14-18)
 
Notes
1 2. S.H.A. ch. 56 /2, and Title 8, Chapter 2 of this Municipal Code.
6-7-9: DUTIES OF PARK ATTENDANT AND LICENSEE:
Every trailer coach park shall be in charge of a responsible attendant or
caretaker at all times, whose duty it shall be to maintain the park, its
facilities and equipment in a clean, orderly and sanitary condition and be
answerable, with the licensee, for any violation of the provisions of this
Chapter.
It is hereby made the duty of the attendant or person in charge together with
the licensee, to:
   A.   Keep a register of all guests, to be open at all times to inspection by
State, Federal, Municipal or school district officers which shall show for all
guests:
      1.   Names and addresses.
      2.   Number of children of school age.
      3.   State of legal residence.
      4.   Dates of entrance and departure.
      5.   License numbers of all mobile homes and towing or other vehicle and
the state issuing such licenses.
      6.   Purpose of stay in park.
      7.   Place of last location and length of stay.
      8.   Place of employment of each occupant.
The register shall be signed by each adult occupant and said record shall be
kept available for inspection for six (6) years. Any person furnishing
misinformation for purposes of registration shall be deemed guilty of a
misdemeanor and punishable under the general statutes or ordinances for such
offense. The registration records shall be neatly and securely maintained, and
no registration records shall be destroyed until six (6) years have elapsed
following the date of registration. The register shall be available at all
times for inspection by law enforcement officers.
   B.   Maintain the park in a clean, orderly and sanitary condition at all
times and maintain a temperature of at least sixty eight degrees (68) F. in all
service buildings during the period from October 1 to May 1.
   C.   Insure that the provisions of this Chapter are complied with and
enforced and report promptly to the proper authorities any violations of this
Chapter or any other violations of law which may come to his attention.
   D.   Report to the Health and Sanitation Officer all cases of persons or
animals affected or suspected of being affected with any communicable disease.
   E.   Prohibit the lighting of open fires on the premises.
   F.   Assume full responsibility for maintaining in good repair and condition
all sanitary and safety appliances on said park, and promptly bring such action
as is necessary to prosecute or eject from said park any person or persons who
wilfully or maliciously damage such appliances, or any person or persons who
fail to comply with the regulations of this Chapter.
   G.   It shall be the duty of each licensee on February 1 and September 1 of
each year, to file with the school board or boards of the school district or
districts wherein the mobile home park is located, a report giving the names
and ages of all children of school age living in said park. (1951 Code 12-11-
10; 1969 Code 12-33; Ords. 72, 72-14-18, 83-17-42; 1985 Code)
6-7-10: INVESTIGATIONS BY SCHOOL AUTHORITIES:
The governing body of the school district or governing bodies of the school
districts in which a mobile home park is located, by and through its officers,
attendance officers and proper employees, may inspect and visit a mobile home
park for the purposes of examining the register with reference to children of
school age for the purpose of enforcing attendance of school children housed in
the park. When a mobile home park is located in two (2) or more school
districts, the school district boards of said districts, acting jointly, shall
be and are hereby authorized to proceed under the provisions of this Section.
(1951 Code 12-11-11; Ords. 72, 72-14-18)
6-7-11: PARK OFFICE, RULES AND REGULATIONS POSTED:
In every mobile home park there shall be located the office of the attendant or
person in charge of said park. A copy of the park license and of this Chapter
shall be posted therein and the park register shall at all times be kept in
said office. Health rules and regulations of the Village and any change or
changes made from time to time shall be posted by the management in a
protected, conspicuous place within the trailer coach park. (1951 Code 12-11-
10; Ords 72, 72-14-18)
6-7-12: RECORDS KEPT:
The Director of Office of Quality Control and Clerk shall keep a record of all
mobile home parks, which records shall show the names and addresses of all
mobile home parks, names and addresses of the licensees, number of trailer
coach lots in each park, source of water supply, system of sewage and garbage
disposal, and any other information required by this Chapter or deemed
essential by the Village. (1951 Code 12-11-3; Ords. 72, 72-14-18)
6-7-13: PARKING RESTRICTIONS:
   A.   It shall be unlawful, except as provided in this Chapter, for any
person to park any mobile home on any street, alley, highway or other public
place, or on any tract of land owned by any person, within the Village.
   B.   Emergency or temporary stopping or parking is permitted on any street,
alley or highway for not longer than one hour subject to any other and further
prohibitions, regulations, or limitations imposed by the traffic and parking
regulations or ordinances for that street, alley or highway.
   C.   No person shall park or occupy any mobile home on any premises which is
situated outside an approved mobile home park. The parking of only one
unoccupied mobile home in an accessory private garage building or in a rear
yard is permitted providing no living quarters shall be maintained, or any
business practiced, in said mobile home while such mobile home is so parked or
stored.
   D.   No mobile home unit shall be parked in a park outside of a designated
space. (1951 Code 12-11-2, 12-11-6; Ords. 72, 72-14-18, 81-12-19)
6-7-14: APPLICABILITY OF OTHER REGULATIONS:
All plumbing, electrical, building and other work on or at any park required to
be licensed under this Chapter shall be in accordance with the ordinances of
the Village and the regulations of the State Health Department. Licenses and
permits granted under this Chapter grant no right to erect or repair any
structure, to do any plumbing work or to do any electrical work. (1951 Code 12-
11-12; Ords. 72, 72-14-18)
6-7-15: PINE TREE MOBILE HOME COURT RULES AND REGULATIONS:
The following rules and regulations shall apply exclusively to the licensees of
the Pine Tree Mobile Home Court owned and operated by the Village:
   I.   Your monthly rent is due on the first day of each month. You may pay
your rent at the Village Hall, Monday through Friday, eight thirty o'clock (8:
30) A.M. to five o'clock (5:00) P.M. A late charge of ten dollars ($10.00) will
be imposed without notice to you if you do not pay your monthly rent within
five (5) days after the rent is due. The late charge may be waived for good
cause by the Village.
   II.   Electricity and gas are metered and billed directly to you by the
service companies.
   III.   Metal storage units are permitted if you obtain the written approval
of the Village. You must obtain the approval before you erect any metal unit.
You must keep your metal storage unit in good condition at all times.
   IV.   No fences of any type are permitted.
   V.   You may store anything under your mobile home unless there is aluminum,
steel or fiberglass skirting placed around your entire trailer. The skirting
must match your mobile home. Storage under you mobile home is limited to items
of fire-resistant materials.
   VI.   Your visitors must park their vehicles outside the mobile home park.
Vehicles of visitors who violate this rule shall be ticketed and towed away.
   VII.   No outdoor clothes lines or clothes drying is permitted. You may not
hang wash on the railings or stairways leading into your mobile home.
   VIII.   Use of the laundry room is for you and the other licensees only. The
following laundry room rules must be adhered to:
      A.   Hours are eight o'clock (8:00) A.M. to ten o'clock (10:00) P.M.,
seven (7) days per week.
      B.   Rules for proper use of the machines are posted on the laundry room
wall over the machines. You must adhere to these rules.
      C.   The washers fill automatically. You may not ever add additional
water to the washers.
      D.   The laundry room must be kept clean at all times. You must remove
all items which you have brought in with you.
      E.   The Village has installed a lock on the laundry room door for your
protection. You must use a key to gain entry to the laundry room and must use
the key in order to leave the laundry room. You must keep the door closed at
all times.
      F.   Laundry room keys are issued by the office of the Director of Public
Works of the Village.
      G.   The Director of Public Works will issue laundry room keys only to
licensees registered with his office.
      H.   Only registered licensees are permitted in the laundry room. Any
person in the laundry room who is not a registered licensee is subject to
immediate removal.
      I.   If you allow persons who are not registered licensees to use your
key to the laundry room, you will be required to return your key to the
Director of Public Works and will forfeit your privilege of using the laundry
room.
For your own safety, do not open the laundry room door for anyone trying to get
in without a key.
   IX.   You must regulate the noise level of your radio and television set
during the day so as to avoid alarming or disturbing other licensees. The noise
level of your radio and television set must be kept low after ten o'clock (10:
00) P.M. each day. Outdoor television antennas are permitted on your mobile
home if you have obtained the prior approval of the Village. Other antennas and
CB units are not allowed.
   X.   You are responsible for any destruction or vandalism by your children
or your visitors. Children and visitors are not permitted to alarm or disturb
other licensees or their visitors.
   XI.   Bicycle riding is not permitted within the trailer park.
   XII.   The Village is not responsible for your lost or stolen articles. The
Village will prosecute theft to the full extent of the law.
   XIII.   Gambling, public intoxication, drinking in public and public
indecency, use of drugs and possession are not permitted. Violators of this
rule will be prosecuted by the Village to the full extent of the law.
   XIV.   No mechanical repairing or overhauling of motor-operated vehicles or
other motor- operated apparatus is permitted in the trailer park.
   XV.   You may not plant hedges, landscaping or greenery without the prior
written approval of the Village. You are responsible for the maintenance and
upkeep of your yard and must keep your yard neat and clean at all times. Your
grass is to be cut as frequently as necessary to maintain a neat and clean
appearance of your lawn. You may not have a vegetable garden. You may not plant
vegetation or greenery in front of your trailer.
The Village will impose a service charge for its time and equipment if it must
clean up or cut the grass on your lot.
   XVI.   You may not sub-lease your mobile home or mobile home lot.
   XVII.   If you wish to sell your mobile home while it is located on the
trailer park lot, you must first obtain written permission from the Village.
The Village is not required to rent a trailer park lot to anyone who purchases
your mobile home. Therefore, before a purchaser of your mobile home takes
possession of the trailer park lot, he or she must have an interview with the
park manager and must be approved by the Village. In addition, the new
purchaser must sign a lease agreement with the Village and must pay the
required deposit.
   XVIII.    No animals or fowl shall be kept in the trailer park.
   XIX.   No boat, travel trailer or truck is permitted to be kept in the
mobile home park. You may park any automobile in the mobile home park, provided
that you park on the driveway or in a designated parking area, and not on the
street or on any lawn.
Inoperative, unlicensed or unused vehicles must be removed from the trailer
park. The Village shall impose a charge for towing and storing of any vehicle
which it removes from the trailer park as being in violation of this rule.
   XX.   The hot water in the laundry room is for use in the laundry room only;
no hot water may be taken from the laundry room.
   XXI.   The speed limit for automobiles in the trailer park is ten (10) miles
per hour.
   XXII.   No commercial activity of any kind whatsoever shall be permitted or
conducted on or from any trailer without prior written consent from the
Village.
   XXIII.   You must register with the park manager all your guests whose stay
exceeds seventy two (72) hours (3 days). You are responsible for the conduct of
your guests.
   XXIV.   You may not install an air conditioner on your mobile home without
prior written approval of the Village. In order to obtain approval of the
Village, you must submit a written note from your neighbor on the side of your
mobile home on which the air conditioner is to be installed, stating that your
neighbor has no objection to the noise factor of the air conditioning unit.
   XXV.   Persons who are not registered licensees of the mobile home court are
not permitted in the mail room. Persons found in the mail room in violation of
this rule shall be immediately removed from the mail room and are subject to
arrest for trespassing.
   XXVI.   These rules are intended for the benefit of all licensees and
therefore these rules will be strictly enforced. If you do not comply with any
of these rules, you are subject to eviction from the mobile home court in
accordance with the law.
If you observe anyone violating any of these rules, you are requested to notify
the Village immediately so that proper action may be taken. (Ords. 78-24-61,
82-23-82)
CHAPTER 8
SIGNS AND ADVERTISING STRUCTURES
SECTION:
6-8-1: Purpose
6-8-2: Definitions
6-8-3: Sign Permits
6-8-4: Construction Specifications
6-8-5: Annual Inspections, Fees
6-8-6: General Provisions
6-8-7: Removal Of Unsafe And Unlawful Signs
6-8-8: Signs Obstructing Public Ways And Property
6-8-9: Nonconforming Signs
6-8-10: Prohibited Signs
6-8-11:    Obsolete And Abandoned Signs
6-8-12:    Outdoor Illuminated Signs
6-8-13:    Ground Signs
6-8-14:    Pole Signs
6-8-15:    Wall Signs
6-8-16:    Roof Signs
6-8-17:    Projecting Signs
6-8-18:    Marquees
6-8-19:    Awnings And Canopies
6-8-20:    Transportation Shelters And Advertising Signs
6-8-21:    Temporary Signs
6-8-22:    Mobile Signs
6-8-23:    Exemptions From Provisions
6-8-24:    Violations And Penalties
6-8-25:    Automobile "For Sale" Signs
6-8-26:    Village Districts
6-8-27:    Public Clocks
6-8-28:    Animated Signs
6-8-29:    Window Signs
6-8-30:    Portable Sidewalk Signs
6-8-1: PURPOSE:
This chapter is adopted for the following purposes:
   A.   To preserve and promote the public health, safety, and welfare, through
reasonable, consistent, and non-discriminatory sign standards.
   B.   To preserve property values within the Village by regulating and
controlling the design, location, construction, and maintenance of all signs.
   C.   To advance the economy and the business and industry of the Village by
promoting the reasonable, orderly and effective display of all signs, and
encouraging better communication between signs and the public.
   D.   To enhance the physical appearance of site identification to be in
harmony with the visual character of the associated street corridor and to be
an integral party of the aesthetic of the site and architectural style of
associated buildings the Village by preserving the scenic and natural beauty of
the area.
   E.   To protect the general public, pedestrians, and motorists within the
Village by assuring the design, location, construction, and maintenance of
signs presents safe navigation through discernable information.
   F.   To ensure that signs are constructed, installed and maintained in a
safe and satisfactory manner, except to the extent expressly preempted by state
or federal law, and protect the public from unsafe signs.
   G.   To confirm that signs may cause harm to the public by creating
obstructions, providing distractions to motorist, displacing alternative land
uses, decreasing property values and aesthetics, and endangering the safety of
person and property.
   H.   To protect the public investment in streets and highways by reducing
sign or advertising distractions that may increase traffic accidents.
   I.   To establish the Village's substantial and compelling interest in
regulating signs in a manner as to reduce the effects and impacts signs have on
the public health, safety, and welfare.
   J.   To protect physical and mental well-being of the general public by
recognizing and encouraging a sense of aesthetic appreciation for the visual
environment. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-2: DEFINITIONS:
As used in this chapter, unless the context otherwise indicates, the following
terms and phrases shall have the meanings herein ascribed to them:
ABANDONED SIGN: A sign remaining on a site absent of a display permitted within
this Chapter for six (6) months or longer.
ADVERTISING SIGN: A commercial sign intended to aid, directly or indirectly, in
the sale, use, or promotion of a product, commodity, service, sales event,
activity, entertainment, or real or personal property.
ANIMATED SIGN: A sign which includes action, motion, or color changes, or the
optical
illusion of action, motion or color changes, including signs using electronic
ink, signs set in motion by movement of the atmosphere, or made up of a series
of sections that turn, including any type of screen using animated or scrolling
displays using light bulbs, neon light, light emitting diodes (LEDs) or LCD
screen(s) or any other type of video display.
APPROVED COMBUSTIBLE MATERIAL: Wood, or materials not more combustible than
wood, and approved plastics.
APPROVED PLASTICS: Only those slow burning plastics such as cellulose acetate
or methyl methacrylate or other plastics of no greater combustibility as now
approved by the Underwriters' Laboratories, Inc., for sign use (UL 48).
AUTOMATIC CHANGEABLE COPY SIGN: A sign which contains electronically displayed
stationary numerical or alphabetical characters which are regularly changed.
AUTOMOBILE DEALERSHIP SIGN: A sign which specifically serves to inform of the
presence of an automobile dealership.
AUTOMOBILE "FOR SALE" SIGNS: Any sign temporarily affixed to an automobile or
other motorized vehicle that directs attention to the fact that a certain
automobile or motorized vehicle is being offered for sale, excluding signs used
for such purposes by commercial car dealers or vendors within the Village. An
automobile "for sale" sign must contain a working telephone number by which the
seller can be contacted. Automobile "for sale" signs shall not be considered
"advertising signs" or "mobile signs" as defined in this chapter.
AWNING: Any secondary covering made of cloth, canvas woven acrylic, cotton or
polyester yarn, vinyl laminated to polyester fabric or metal with aluminum,
iron or steel, or wood frame attached to a building and projecting from a
building, when the same is so erected as to permit its being raised to a
position fiat against the building when not in use or in a permanent extended
rigid position.
AWNING SIGN: A fabric (or similar material) awning that is printed, stamped,
stitched or otherwise applied onto a protective awning, canopy, or other
fabric, plastic, or structural protective cover over a door, entrance, window,
or outdoor service area.
BANNERS: A sign of lightweight flexible fabric or similar material intended to
hang either with or without frames, mounted to a pole (other than a light pole
sign), building or other structure at one or more edges. National flags, state
or municipal flags, or the official flag of any institution or business shall
not be considered banners.
BILLBOARD: Any advertising sign or other commercial sign which directs
attention to a business, commodity, service, entertainment, or attraction sold,
offered or existing elsewhere that upon the same lot where such sign is
displayed.
BULLETIN BOARD SIGN: A changeable sign for displayed news and dates which
pertain to the business or organization whose land the sign sits on.
BUSINESS ENTRANCE IDENTIFICATION SIGN: A sign displayed near the entrance of a
business displaying the business's name, operating hours, and contact
information.
BUSINESS SIGN: A sign immediately in front of or on a business's building,
which makes the business's presence known to people passing on foot and in
vehicles.
CANOPY: Any structure, other than an awning, made of cloth or metal with metal
frames attached to a building, projecting from a building and carried by a
frame supported by the ground or sidewalk.
CANOPY SIGN: A freestanding canopy which is decorated in such a way to
represent a business on the same tract of property not more than one hundred
feet (100') away.
CHANGEABLE COPY SIGN: An automatic or changeable copy sign that changeable
text, characters, and/or illustrations that allows the sign's display to be
amended, updated or otherwise modified. Electronic message boards are a type of
changeable copy sign.
CIVIC EVENT SIGN: A temporary sign placed with the intention of drawing people
to a public event. The public event must be accessible to everyone.
CONFORMING SIGN: A sign which is legal and conforms to all standards set forth
by this code.
CONSTRUCTION SIGN: Temporary sign placed on a construction site which shows the
name of the construction project, the contractors, the architects, engineers
and/or developers, and the approximate date of completion.
DOUBLE FACED SIGN: A sign which shows the same information to viewers standing
on opposite sides of the sign while in line with the primary street or other
thoroughfare the sign sits along.
ERECT: To build, construct, attach, hang, place, suspend or affix, and includes
the painting of wall signs.
EXTERNALLY ILLUMINATED SIGN: A sign lit by light bulbs, whose primary
illumination is obtained through the use of bulbs which are not placed inside
the sign structure.
FACING OR SURFACE: The surface of the sign upon, against or through which the
message is displayed or illustrated on the sign.
FAMILY ANNOUNCEMENT SIGN: A sign which commemorates an event in the household
where the sign is displayed.
FREESTANDING SIGN: A sign which is standing free of any structural support from
other structures or buildings.
FOOT-CANDLE. A unit of emitted light on a surface stated in lumens per square
foot and measurable with an illuminance meter (light meter).
GRAND OPENING SIGN: A sign which commemorates the initial opening period of any
event.
GROUND SIGN: A freestanding sign mounted to the ground with one or more poles
and not a direct part of the building and may be completely or principally
supported by one or more posts or other support structure, which is not
attached to the principal building on the property.
GOVERNMENTAL SIGN: A sign built and intended for the purpose of fulfilling a
government mandated purpose or requirement.
HOLIDAY DECORATIONS: Signs which are erected for the purpose of commemorating a
widely recognized holiday.
HORIZONTAL PROJECTING SIGN: Any sign which is greater in width than in height.
ICONIC SIGN: A sign that has been designated by the Historic Preservation
Commission and/or Architecture Design and Review Commission as providing and
promoting the preservation of the Village's unique character, history, and
identity.
IDENTIFICATION SIGN: A sign immediately in front of or on a business's
building, which makes the business's presence known to people passing on foot
and in vehicles.
ILLEGAL SIGN: A sign constructed without the issuance of any permit.
ILLUMINATED SIGN: A sign which uses electric lighting to stay visible during
the nighttime hours.
INCOMBUSTIBLE MATERIAL: Any material which will not ignite at or below a
temperature of one thousand two hundred degrees Fahrenheit (1,200°F) and will
not continue to burn or glow at that temperature except as provided by
subsection 6-8-11D2 of this chapter.
INFLATABLE SIGNS: Any inflatable object, that is either: a) tethered or
otherwise suspended on top of a structure; or b) fastened to the ground.
INTERNALLY ILLUMINATED SIGN: A sign whose primary illumination is obtained
through the use of lights which are placed within the structure itself.
JOINT IDENTIFICATION SIGN: An identification sign which only identifies a
planned development, and does not identify or make references to the individual
uses and tenants of units within the development.
LIGHT POLE SIGN: A banner mounted on street lighting poles with two (2) edges
affixed to a mounting bracket. Two (2) are the most that are allowable on any
light pole. Must contain suitably sized holes to facilitate the passage of
heavy wind without damaging the banner.
MANUAL CHANGEABLE COPY SIGN: A sign which contains manually displayed
stationary numerical or alphabetical characters which are regularly changed.
MARQUEE: Any permanent wall or roof-like construction projecting from the wall
of a building above an entrance.
MARQUEE LIGHTING: Lighting which uses light bulbs, neon light, or light
emitting diodes (LEDs) to create stationary lighting or synthetic movement on a
sign or to attract attention to a sign. The use of such lighting shall be
restricted to wall signs and marquee signs.
MARQUEE SIGN: Any painted, printed or illuminated sign attached to a marquee.
MEMORIAL SIGN: A sign which commemorates an event or series of events in the
past which draw the viewer to reflect upon those events.
MOBILE SIGN: Any electrical or nonelectrical sign designed for use and display
out of doors which is mounted on a trailer with a locking device, or which is
otherwise capable of being easily transported or moved from place to place.
MONUMENT SIGN: A sign, other than a pole sign, with steel supports, placed upon
or supported by the ground independently of any other structure. A sign that is
typically mounted on a masonry base. As distinguished from a ground sign, the
sign base of any monument sign must be a minimum of seventy five percent (75%)
or more of the width of the sign face that is to be situated upon the base. A
sign base less than seventy-five percent (75%) of the width of the sign face is
considered a ground sign.
MOVING SIGN: A sign with a moving mechanical element, whose primary purpose is
to attract attention to the sign.
NAMEPLATE SIGN: A sign used to display the personal name of a person operating
a business above or near said business's main entrance.
NONCONFORMING SIGN: A sign which conformed to all the necessary and relevant
codes when it was built, but presently considered noncompliant or in conflict
due to changes to the code. Also includes signs which have fallen into a state
of disrepair.
OBSOLETE SIGN: A sign which remains unchanged while the physical unit it
represents has either undergone a change in ownership or is vacant.
OFF SITE IDENTIFICATION SIGN: A sign which directs attention to a product or
service sold, offered, created, furnished, or conducted at a location not
directly associated with the property or site at which the sign is located.
ON SITE INFORMATIONAL SIGN: A sign placed on an establishment's property for
the purpose of directing patrons around the property to points of relevance,
such as the entrance and exit.
OTHER ADVERTISING STRUCTURE: Any "marquee", "canopy" or "awning" as further
defined herein.
OUTDOOR ADVERTISING SIGN: A sign providing display space for general commercial
advertising not related to the premises on site at which the sign is located;
commonly referred to as "billboards".
PARAPET SIGN: A sign mounted horizontally against a building's parapet, above
roof level.
PENNANTS: Any lightweight plastic, fabric or other material, whether or not it
contains a message of any kind, suspended from a rope, wire, or string, usually
in series, designed to move in the wind.
PERMANENT SIGN: A sign which is intended to stay intact and relevant for the
foreseeable future, without extensive modification from its as built form.
POLE SIGN: Any sign supported by upright members such as steel columns or poles
with a sign attached thereto, which is a freestanding structure with separate
foundations set into the ground and which is permanent.
POLITICAL SIGNS: A sign which advertises a local, county, state or national
candidate or proposition upon which a ballot is to be cast or vote taken in an
election.
PORTABLE SIDEWALK SIGN: A freestanding sign designed to be easily moved from
place to place and often containing a changeable copy sign element.
PRIVATE SALE SIGN: A temporary sign placed with the intention of drawing
customers to a "garage sale", "yard sale", or similar event.
PRIVATE WARNING SIGN: A warning sign on private property, notifying its viewer
of conditions and/or criteria set by the property's owner.
PROJECTING SIGN: Any sign which is attached to a building or other structure
and extends beyond the line of the said building or structure or beyond the
surface of that portion of the building or structure to which it is attached.
All projecting signs shall be illuminated signs, as defined by this chapter.
PUBLIC CLOCK: A timekeeping device, either digital or with a face and hands,
easily viewable to the public and either placed alongside an identification
sign or decorated to serve as an identification sign as well as a clock.
REAL ESTATE SIGN: A temporary sign placed on property notifying its viewers
that said property is for sale. Includes any relevant contact information.
ROOF SIGN: A sign located on or attached to roof framing.
SCOREBOARD SIGN: A freestanding sign intended to display scores for athletic
events, and also to serve as an outlet for advertisers who sponsor those
athletic events.
SIGN: Includes every sign, billboard, ground sign, wall sign, roof sign,
illuminated sign, light pole sign, projecting sign, temporary sign, mobile
sign, pylon or pole sign, marquee, awning and canopy, and includes any
announcement, declaration demonstration, display illustration or insignia
showing words, figures or pictures.
Any object, device, display or structure, or part thereof, which is used to
advertise, identify, display, direct or attract attention by any means
including words, printed text, letters figures, designs, symbols, pictures,
fixtures, colors, motion, illumination or projected images for the purpose of
delivering a message.
STACKED SIGN: A sign which is physically placed on top of or next to another
sign of similar dimensions and type.
STRUCTURAL TRIM: The molding, battens, cappings, nailing strips, latticing and
platforms which are attached to the sign structure.
SUSPENDED SIGN: An overhead sign hanging over a retail unit lined corridor, and
notifying its viewers where certain units are a short distance before they are
visible immediately to the viewer's right or left.
TEMPORARY BANNER: A banner mounted with the intention of serving for only a
short (finite) period of time, and constructed of nonpermanent materials.
TEMPORARY SIGNS: A sign constructed and intended for a temporary period of
display and is neither permanently installed in the ground nor permanently
affixed to a building, structure, wall or perimeter fencing.
THEATER SIGN: A sign mounted to the front of a cinema or performing arts venue,
with the name of said venue displayed along with a changeable listing of
current shows or performances.
TRANSPORTATION SHELTER ADVERTISEMENTS: Privately owned permanent structures
erected on or adjoining principal rights of way on arterial streets, for the
purpose of providing shelter from the weather for public mass transportation
passengers and containing up to six (6) panels of backlighted advertising.
VEHICLE SIGN: A sign placed on the side of a vehicle for the purpose of
identifying goods or services provided by the vehicle's owner or driver or an
entity which has contracted with the vehicle's owners.
VERTICAL PROJECTING SIGN: Any sign which is greater in height than in width.
WALL SIGN: A sign attached against a wall of a building or other structure,
with the exposed face of the sign parallel to the plane of the wall, and
attached to the exterior front, rear or wall side of any building or structure.
WINDOW SIGN: A sign or display installed, painted upon or placed into, or onto
the interior of a window. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-3: SIGN PERMITS:
   A.   Permits Required: It shall be unlawful for any person to erect, repair,
alter, relocate or maintain within the Village any "sign" or "other advertising
structure" or "awning" or "canopy" for commercial or business use, as defined
in this chapter, without first obtaining a sign permit from the Department of
Community Development and Growth Management and making payment of the fee
required by subsection D of this section. All illuminated signs shall, in
addition, be subject to the provisions of the electrical code, and the permit
fees required thereunder.
   B.   Application For Permit: Applications for sign permits shall be
submitted to the Department of Community Development and Growth Management and
Architectural Review and Preservation Commission. Applications for sign permits
shall be made upon blanks provided by the Department of Community Development
and Growth Management or have attached thereto the following information:
      1.   Name, address and telephone number of the applicant.
      2.   Location of building, structure or lot to which or upon which the
sign or other advertising structure is to be attached or erected.
      3.   Position of the sign or other advertising structure in relation to
adjacent buildings, structures or property lines.
      4.   Two (2) blueprints or ink drawings of the plans, specifications, and
method of construction, and attachment to the building or in the ground.
      5.   Copy of stress sheets and calculations showing the structure is
designed for dead load and wind pressure in any direction in the amount
required by this chapter and all other laws and ordinances of the Village.
      6.   Name of person erecting structure.
      7.   Written consent of the owner including name, address and telephone
number of the owner of the building, structure or land to which or on which the
structure is to be erected.
      8.   An electrical permit required and issued for said sign.
      9.   Insurance policy or bond as required by subsection E of this
section.
      10.   Data and information covering sign or signs, of uniqueness of
structure or innovation of materials and design.
      11.   If external or internal illumination, a detailed lighting plan,
including a photometric plan is required. All lighting plans shall include
manufacturer specifications pertaining to light intensity.
   C.   The Approval Of Plans And Specifications: The application for a permit
for erection of a sign or other advertising structure in which electrical
wiring and connections are to be used shall be submitted to the chief
electrical inspector. Said inspector shall examine the plans and specifications
respecting all wiring and connections to determine if the same complies with
the electrical code of the Village. This action of the chief electrical
inspector shall be taken prior to submission of the application to the
Department of Community Development and Growth Management.
   D.   Fees: The following permit fees for such sign or other advertising
structure regulated by this chapter shall be as follows:
 
All signs requiring a permit other than   $ 1.00
those listed below, per sq. ft.
Canopy/awning:                             
   Without signage                     50.00
   With signage                        $1.00 per sq. ft. minimum of $50.00
Marquee, per 10 sq. ft. or fraction       $10.00
thereof of displaying surface
Mobile signs, per 10 sq. ft. or fraction  10.00
thereof of displaying surface
Temporary signs, per 25 sq. ft. or        35.00
fraction thereof
 
When a fee is assessed, in no event shall the minimum fee be less than fifty
dollars ($50.00).
   E.   Bond Or Insurance Required: Every applicant for a permit referred to
herein shall, before the permit is granted, file with the Department of
Community Development and Growth Management a continuing bond in the penal sum
of fifty thousand dollars ($50,000.00), executed by the applicant and a surety
company to be approved by the Village attorney and conditioned for the faithful
performance of the provisions of this chapter and all amendments hereto, and of
all laws and ordinances relating to signs and other advertising structures, and
which shall indemnify and save harmless the Village from any and all damages,
judgments, costs or expenses which the Village may incur or suffer by reason of
granting of said permit. Any person lawfully maintaining a sign or other
advertising structure regulated by this chapter at the time of the enactment
hereof shall, within thirty (30) days after said enactment, comply with all the
provisions set forth in this subsection. A public liability insurance policy
issued by an insurance company authorized to do business in the state
conforming to this subsection may be permitted in lieu of a bond, provided that
a noncancelable clause or notification of nonrenewal clause is contained
therein.
   F.   Issuance Of Permit, Expiration: It shall be    the duty of the
Department of Community Development and Growth Management, upon filing of an
application for a sign permit, to examine such plans and specifications and
other data and the premises upon which it is proposed to erect the sign or
other advertising structure and upon compliance with all the requirements of
this chapter and all other laws and ordinances of the Village, the sign permit
shall be issued within fourteen (14) working days. If the work authorized under
a sign permit has not been completed within six (6) months after date of
issuance, the said permit shall become null and void and all fees shall be
forfeited.
   G.   Revocation Of Permit: The Department of Community Development and
Growth Management is hereby authorized and empowered to revoke any permit
issued upon failure of the holder thereof to comply with any provision of this
chapter. See section
3-1-6 of this code for permit revocation hearing provisions.
6-8-4: CONSTRUCTION SPECIFICATIONS:
   A.   Steel Supports: All outdoor advertising signs shall be constructed on
steel supports when the total area of said sign exceeds one hundred (100)
square feet.
   B.   Wind Pressure Requirements:
      1.   For the purpose of determining wind pressures, all signs shall be
classified as either open or solid. Signs in which the projected area exposed
to wind consists of seventy percent (70%) or more of the gross area as
determined by the overall dimensions shall be classed as solid signs; those in
which the projected exposed area is derived from open letters, figures, strips
and structural framing members, the aggregate total area of which is less than
seventy percent (70%) of the gross area so determined, shall be classed as open
signs.
      2.   All signs shall be designed and constructed to withstand wind
pressures applied to the projected exposed area, allowing for wind in any
direction, in accordance with the following table:
 
Height From     Wind Pressure Pounds Per Square Foot
Ground To Top   Solid Signs Open Signs
Of Sign In Feet
   Less than 17          23
   30 - 49   22          31
   50 - 99   28          39
   100 - 499 33          46
 
   C.   Wire Members: No cable or wire uplifts or guys are permitted.
   D.   Smooth Surface: All signs or other advertising structures which are
located in the property lines, or within five feet (5') thereof, shall have a
smooth surface and no nails, tacks or wires shall be permitted to protrude
therefrom, except electrical reflectors and devices which may extend over the
top and in front of the advertising structures. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-5: ANNUAL INSPECTIONS, FEES:
The division of building, planning and zoning shall inspect annually, or at
such other times which it deems necessary, each sign or other advertising
structure regulated by this chapter for the purpose of ascertaining whether the
same is structurally safe, in need of repair, or verify compliance with
lighting requirements. The sign permittee shall pay to the Village a sum of one
dollar fifty cents ($1.50) multiplied by the number of square feet of area for
each unit so inspected; provided, however, that in no event shall the
inspection fee be less than thirty dollars ($30.00) nor more than fifty dollars
($50.00) per location or business license. No charge shall be made until sign
is actually inspected. No inspection fee other than the permit fee as required
in subsection
6-8-3D of this chapter will be charged during the calendar year in which the
sign or other advertising structure is erected. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-6: GENERAL PROVISIONS:
   A.   Information Required On Signs: Every sign or other advertising
structure hereafter erected shall have painted in a conspicuous place thereon,
in letters not less than one inch (1") in height, the month and year of
erection, the name and address of the erector, the permit number and the
voltage of any electrical apparatus used in the connection therewith. Upon
annual inspection, inspection tag shall be affixed.
   B.   Maintenance Required: The owner of any sign as defined and regulated by
this chapter shall be required to have properly painted at least twice every
five (5) years, all parts and supports of the said sign, unless the same are
galvanized or otherwise treated to prevent rust.
   C.   Illumination:
      1.   Gooseneck reflectors and lights shall be permitted, on a permanent
basis, on freestanding signs, ground signs, parapet   signs, roof signs and
wall signs; provided, however, the reflectors shall be provided with proper
lenses concentrating the illumination upon the area of the sign so as to
prevent the glare upon the street or adjacent property.
      2.   It shall be unlawful for any person to maintain any sign which
extends over public property which is wholly or partially illuminated by
floodlights or spotlights.
      3.   Indoor illuminated signs. It shall be prohibited to place marquee
lighting on any indoor illuminated sign or building structure, including but
not limited to windows, doors, parapet, exterior walls, support beams, roofs
and balconies. The use of such lighting shall be restricted to wall signs, and
marquee signs.
   D.   Maintenance: Any type of sign shall be considered in violation of this
subsection if there is a visible lack of aesthetic maintenance, this includes,
but is not limited to: peeling of paint, cracks or broken portion of signs,
rust, burned out lights (if illuminated) and information which is no longer
legible due to weathering.
   E.   Number And Placement Of Signs:
      1.   There shall not be more than one advertising sign for each six
hundred feet (600') of parallel street frontage, except back to back sign
structures.
      2.   There shall be no "V" shape arrangements of back to back signs.
      3.   More than one advertising sign may be erected within six hundred
feet (600') of another sign structure if the face of the signs show in opposite
directions. No such structure shall contain more than one advertising sign per
each facing.
      4.   Advertising signs shall not be arranged so as to block the view of
signs which advertise the business on the adjacent property.
      5.   Notwithstanding any other provision in this code in the C-l, C-2 and
M-l zoning districts there shall be no more than two (2) ground signs on any
corner lot and one ground sign on any interior lot except that double faced
signs as permitted or allowed shall be deemed to be one sign.
   F.   Notice And Fines: The Village may provide written notice of a violation
of this section to the sign owner, permittee, or owner of the property upon
which the sign sits each of whom shall be jointly and severally liable for any
such violations. Upon receiving a written notice of a violation of this
section, the sign owner, permittee, or the owner of property upon which the
sign sits shall have twenty one (21) days to cure the violation. If the
violation is not cured within twenty one (21) days from receipt of the notice
of violation then the owner and/or person or entity listed on the sign permit
application shall be subject to a fine of not less than twenty five dollars
($25.00) nor more than seven hundred fifty dollars ($750.00). Each day the
violation exists after the twenty one (21) day period to cure the violation
shall be considered a separate violation.
   G.   Illumination Standards: Any sign that is illuminated, shall only be
illuminated by steady, stationary, shielded, and shaded light sources directly
solely at the sign, or internal to it so that the light intensity or brightness
does not create either a nuisance to adjacent property or a traffic hazard for
motorist or pedestrians. In no instance shall the lighting intensity of any
sign whether resulting from internal   illumination or external illumination,
exceeding the following:
      1.   Illuminated signs shall produce no more than XXX-foot candles of
illumination, four feet (4’) from the sign.
      2.   The source of light for external illuminated signs shall be located,
shielded, and directed in a manner that the light source is not visible from a
public street or private residence.
Any pre-existing Illuminated signs shall be classified as "Non-Conforming,"
pursuant to the Section
6-8-9 of this Chapter. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-7: REMOVAL OF UNSAFE AND UNLAWFUL SIGNS:
If the division of building, planning and zoning or other inspector as
designated by the Department of Community Development and Growth Management
shall find that any sign or other advertising structure regulated herein is
unsafe or insecure, or is a menace to the public, or has been constructed or
erected or is being maintained in violation of the provisions of this chapter,
it shall give written notice to the permittee thereof. If the permittee fails
to remove or alter the structure so as to comply with the standards herein set
forth within ten (10) days after such notice, such a sign or other advertising
structure may be removed or altered to comply by the division of building,
planning and zoning at the expense of the permittee or owner of the property
upon which it is located. The Department of Community Development and Growth
Management shall refuse to issue a permit to any permittee or owner who refuses
to pay costs so assessed and division of building, planning and zoning may
cause any sign or other advertising structure which is in immediate peril to
persons or property to be removed summarily and without notice. In the event
that a fire has caused immediate peril to persons or property, then the fire
chief, or in his absence, the senior fire officer present, may order the
removal of the sign. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-8: SIGNS OBSTRUCTING PUBLIC WAYS AND PROPERTY:
   A.   Ingress And Egress: No sign shall be erected, relocated or maintained
so as to prevent free ingress to or egress from any door, window, fire escape
or fire hydrant. No sign of any kind shall be attached to a standpipe, fire
escape or fire hydrant or light pole.
   B.   Utility Wires: Signs shall be installed so that there shall be a
minimum clearance of three feet (3') between the sign and any overhead utility
wires. Where the wires pass over the sign, the wires shall be protected as
recommended by the utility company owning or maintaining the wires.
   C.   Traffic Obstructions: No sign or other advertising structure as
regulated by this chapter shall be erected at the intersection of any streets
in such a manner as to obstruct free and clear vision; or at any location
where, by reason of the position, shape or color, it may interfere with,
obstruct the view of or be confused with any authorized traffic sign, signal or
device; or which makes use of the words "Stop", "Drive-In", "Danger" or any
other word, phrase, symbol or character, in such manner as to interfere with,
mislead or confuse traffic.
   D.   Projections Into Public Rights Of Way: No permanent or temporary sign
or portion of a sign or its supports shall extend into or over the property
line into the public right of way, except as noted for wall signs and portable
sidewalk signs.
   E.   Nuisance: Any sign placed in the public right of way in violation of
this chapter shall be considered a nuisance and subject to abatement by the
Village. The person or entity responsible for the illegally placed signs may be
responsible for reimbursement to the Village for the Village's costs of removal
or abatement of the illegally placed signs in addition to being charged with
violating the provisions of this chapter and subject to any fines or costs.
(Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-9: NONCONFORMING SIGNS:
   A.   Any sign which was erected in conformance with the sign ordinance in
effect at the time of its erection but which currently fails to meet the size,
location, or other applicable requirements of this chapter, as amended, shall
be considered nonconforming and shall either be removed or brought into
compliance with this chapter's requirements within the period of time
prescribed herein dating from the effective date of this section.
   B.   All nonconforming signs shall be removed or brought into compliance
with this chapter's requirements in accordance with the abatement schedule set
forth below. In order to utilize the abatement schedule, the owner of record,
or his agent, shall make available the market value, as of [March 1, 2019], of
any sign or signs which have been deemed nonconforming by these regulations.
The market value of nonconforming signs shall be based on the original cost,
including cost of installation, of said sign less ten percent (10%) of the
original cost per year for each year said sign has been standing prior to the
effective date of this section.
   C.   The date of the erection of any nonconforming sign shall be established
by the presentation by the owner of record of the sign, or his agent, of a
certified copy of the corresponding sign permit on file with the Department of
Community Development and Growth Management. Any sign erected without the
issuance of a valid sign permit shall be deemed an illegal sign and shall be
removed immediately.
   D.   All required documentation concerning the market value and erection
date of any nonconforming sign shall be presented to the Department of
Community Development and Growth Management within ten (10) days of receipt of
notice of nonconformance. The Department of Community Development and Growth
Management shall determine to its satisfaction the validity of all presented
documentation. Any decision of the Department of Community Development and
Growth Management may be appealed to the Appeals and Review Board.
   E.   Abatement Schedule:
Adjusted Market Value Abatement Date
Adjusted Market Value    Abatement Date
Less than $500.00        June 1, 2019
$500.00 to $999.99       September 1, 2019
$1,000.00 to $1,499.99   December 1, 2019
$1,500.00 to $1,999.99   March 1 ,2020
$2,000.00 to $2,999.99   June 1, 2020
$3,000.00 to $3,999.99   September 1, 2020
$4,000.00 to $4,999.99   December 1, 2020
$5,000.00 to $7,499.99   March 1, 2021
$7,500.00 to $9,999.99   June 1, 2021
$10,000.00 to $12,499.99 September 1, 2021
$12,500.00 to $14,999.99 December 1, 2021
$15,000.00 to $19,999.99 March 1, 2022
$20,000.00 and over      June 1, 2022
 
   F.   Any sign that is in noncompliance with the regulations of this chapter
shall be removed prior to or upon the date designated for removal in the above
abatement schedule. If the owner of, or the person or persons responsible for
the sign fails to remove the nonconforming sign, the owner of the premises upon
which the sign is located shall be responsible for the removal of the sign and
the work shall be done within ninety (90) days following the abatement date.
The procedure for the removal of all nonconforming signs shall be as follows:
      1.   The Department of Community Development and Growth Management after
proper notification, may cause the removal of any nonconforming sign and shall,
at its discretion, charge the costs incurred against any of the following, each
of whom shall be jointly and severally liable for said charges; provided,
however, that any decision or determination of the Department of Community
Development and Growth Management may be appealed to the Appeals and Review
Board:
         a.   The permittee.
         b.   The owner of the sign.
         c.   The owner of the premises on which the sign is located.
         d.   The occupant of the premises on which the sign is located.
      2.   A sign removed by the Village shall be held not less than thirty
(30) days by the Village during which time it may be recovered by the owner
upon payment to the Village for the costs of removal and storage. If not
recovered prior to expiration of the thirty (30) day period, the sign and
supporting structures shall be declared abandoned and title thereto shall vest
in the Village and the cost of removal shall be billed to the owner of the
sign. (Ords. 9-13-41, 9-16-65)
6-8-10: PROHIBITED SIGNS:
   A.   Any sign not expressly permitted in this Chapter is prohibited.
   B.   No person or entity shall display any of the following signs within the
Village of Oak Lawn:
      1.   Signs by which color, location, or design resemble or conflict with
traffic control signs or signals.
      2.   Portable signs.
      3.   Commercial signs on a vehicle where said vehicle is parked adjacent
to or near the right-of-way for the purposes of identifying or calling
attention to the business, such as utilizing directional signage, and is not
used for daily operations or during the regular course of business, or is not
licensed, insured, or operational.
      4.   Billboards, off site identification signs, or outdoor advertising
signs not existing at the time of the adoption of this Chapter. Any replacement
or erection of a billboard, off site identification sign, or outdoor
advertising sign shall be prohibited.
      5.   Any signs or attention getting devices, that rotate, revolve, or any
visible moving parts or that gives the appearance of movement, including, but
not limited to, searchlights, spinners, streamers, banners, balloons, barrels,
and other similar devices, or ornamentation designed for purposes of attracting
attention, promoting, or advertising, unless otherwise provided for in this
Chapter.
      6.   Any sign or structure which constitutes a hazard to public health,
safety, or welfare, including dangerous construction or sight obstructions.
      7.   Any sign located in the public right-of-way, unless otherwise
provided for in this Chapter.
      8.   Pole signs. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-11: OBSOLETE AND ABANDONED SIGNS:
Any sign now or hereafter existing which no longer advertises a bona fide
business conducted, or a product sold on the premises where said sign is
located, or any sign that is located on property that has failed to maintain a
valid business license or that becomes vacant and unoccupied for a period of
six (6) months or more will be deemed to have been abandoned or obsolete and
shall be taken down and removed including the sign face, supporting structures,
and structural trim by the owner, agent, permittee, or person having the
beneficial use of the building or structure upon which such sign may be found
within thirty (30) days after written notification from the division of
building, planning and zoning.
Upon failure to comply with such notice within the time specified in such
order, the obsolete or abandoned sign shall be considered a nuisance and the
division of building, planning and zoning is hereby authorized to cause removal
of such sign, and any expense incident thereto shall be paid by the owner of
the building or structure to which such sign is attached. Where an owner fails
to pay such expenses, the Village may file a lien upon the property for the
purposes of collecting all reasonable costs including attorney fees associated
with removal of the sign. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-12: OUTDOOR ILLUMINATED SIGNS:
The provisions of this section shall govern outdoor illuminated signs.
   A.   Box Or Drum:
      1.   Design: The design shall be such as to afford ample strength and
rigidity, to render the box practically weatherproof, to enclose all terminals
and wiring and to provide drainage for each compartment.
      2.   Joints: Where joints occur in a metal box or drum they shall be the
lap type. The lap shall be sufficient for weatherproofing.
      3.   Openings: All openings in the box or drum shall be provided with
covers arranged to lock securely. Unless hinged, the covers shall be secured to
the sign box by a noncorrosive chain.
   B.   Construction Of Faces, Boxes And Backs:
      1.   Approved plastic may be used for sign faces but not as a supporting
means.
      2.   If metal is used in the construction of illuminated sign faces on
box type signs or letters, it shall not be less than no. 24 U.S. gauge.
Metal used in faces of signs other than box type shall be not less than no. 18
U.S. gauge.
Metal shall be used for construction of backs of flat signs and shall be not
less than no. 24 U.S. gauge.
      3.   Box or drum shall be galvanized steel and painted or aluminum or
stainless steel, and drainage should be provided by means of one or more weep
holes, each not larger than one inch (1") or less than three-eighths inch (3/
8") in diameter. There shall be at least two (2) weep holes per compartment but
in no case shall the weep holes be less than four feet (4') on center.
      4.   Metal faces of all illuminated signs more than two feet (2') wide
shall be reinforced on the inside with galvanized iron stiffening channels or
angles and also stayed or braced to the frame or opposite side, such stays or
braces to be so placed that all surface areas of ten (10) square feet are so
reinforced.
   C.   Framework:
      1.   All signs having an area of twelve (12) or more square feet of one
face shall have an approved structural framework of galvanized steel angles or
the equivalent in strength to be not less than specified in the following
table:
FRAMEWORK TABLE
 
Area Of Face      Projecting Signs Single Faced Signs
12 to 20 sq.ft.   1 ½ x 1 ½ x 3/11 ½ x 2 x 3/16
21 to 40 sq.ft.   2 x 2 x 3/16     1 ½ x 2 x 3/16
41 to 70 sq. ft.  2 x 2 x 1/4      2 x 2 x 3/16
Larger than above 2 ½ x 2 ½ x 1/42 x 2 x 1/4
 
      2.   Signs of ten (10) or more linear feet shall have an intermediate
brace for every five (5) linear feet. If frame is made of angle larger than one
and one-half inches by one and one-half inches by one-sixteenth inch (1 ½" x 1
½" x 1/16"), the next size smaller angle may be used as a brace.
      3.   All connections between framework members shall be made with
galvanized machine bolts not less than three-eighths inch (3/8") in diameter or
riveted. Gusset plates or equivalent shall be used in making these connections
where the area of the sign is greater than forty (40) square feet. Metal sign
faces shall be bolted, screwed or riveted to flange of drum with a maximum
distance of eight inches (8") on center. If riveted, suitable tinned or cherry
rivets shall be used. If bolted, brass or galvanized bolts not less than eight
thirty-seconds (8/32) shall be used. If screwed, suitable sheet metal screws
shall be used.
      4.   Signs built in more than one section shall have provision for
connecting sections during the course of erection so that the entire structural
framework will act as a completely fabricated and assembled unit. Each section
shall be so constructed that the interior is accessible for maintenance and
inspection.
   D.   Materials:
      1.   Glass: The glass in a box type sign shall be double strength or
plate glass. The area of any one piece of glass in each face of a sign over
public property shall not exceed eight (8) square feet where double strength
glass is used, nor eighteen (18) square feet where plate glass is used. Each
such piece must be supported by a separate frame.
      2.   Plastics: Approved plastics shall mean only those slow burning
plastics such as cellulose acetate or methyl methacrylate or other plastics of
no greater combustibility as now approved by the Underwriters' Laboratories,
Inc., for sign use (UL 48), and may be used on signs under the following
conditions:
         a.   The material shall not be subjected to a temperature higher than
one hundred forty nine degrees Fahrenheit (149°F) unless such material is
certified by the manufacturer to be special heat resistant material, in which
case the temperature shall not exceed one hundred eighty degrees Fahrenheit
(180°F).
         b.   The sign shall be so constructed that the plastic material will
not come in contact with gas tubes or other source of illumination and shall be
kept at least two inches (2") from the nearest incandescent lamp.
         c.   The wiring and current carrying parts, other than lampholders,
shall be enclosed in metal.
         d.   When plastic material is mounted in a metal frame, proper
provision shall be made for the difference in expansion between the plastic and
the frame. One-sixteenth inch (1/16") total clearance for each twelve inches
(12") or fraction thereof, of the dimension perpendicular to the mounted edge
should be provided in the mounting system.
         e.   When plastic is held in place by flanges, troughs, channels or
clips, these means of attachment shall overlap the plastic by not less than the
following:
 
Width Of Panels Attachment Size
Up to 12"       3/8"
12" to 24"      7/16"
24" to 36"      ½"
36" to 48"      9/16"
Over 48"        3/4"
 
Flanges, troughs or continuous channels shall be made of galvanized sheet
metal, copper or aluminum, not less than twenty (20) gauge U.S. standard in
thickness. Where clips are used for attachment, they shall be made of
noncorrosive spring type material such as phosphor bronze or equivalent, the
thickness of which shall be not less than thirty two one-hundredths inch
(0.32"). Each clip shall not be less than two inches (2") wide and clips,
bolts, screws or rivets shall be spaced on not less than seven inch (7")
centers around the entire periphery of the plastic.
         f.   When bolts, screws or rivets are used to fasten plastic material
to a metal frame, washers shall be placed under the heads of the bolts, screws
or rivets. These washers shall be of metal of a noncorrosive material and have
an outside diameter of not less than six (6) times the diameter of the screw
being used. Washers are to be not less than thirty two hundredths inch (0.32")
in thickness.
         g.   Undue pressure of fastening devices against the plastic material
should be avoided by the use of suitable gasket material, sprint tension or
adequate clearance to allow for expansion.
         h.   Removable plastic panels not fastened to the frame or supported
on all edges shall be supported on their longest dimension by troughs on two
(2) opposite edges. Where such removable panels are made in more than one
section, the adjacent ends shall be pointed together, where the adjacent ends
exceed twelve inches (12") in length, by fastenings directly supported to the
frame of the sign. Such fastenings to be not less than twelve inches (12") on
centers.
         i.   The plastic decorations, faces, panels and letter facings shall
be demonstrated to be of sufficient thickness or so formed or supported as to
withstand twenty five (25) pounds' uniform load per square foot without coming
in contact with gas tubes or any other source of illumination and without
coming within two inches (2") of an incandescent lamp.
         j.   Plastic sheets may not be used as a supporting means for current
carrying parts or sources of illumination.
         k.   Approved solid type or hollow plastic letters or characters may
be used provided they are not used as a raceway.
         l.   The structure of the sign on which the plastic is mounted or
installed shall be noncombustible.
   E.   Catwalks And Ladders:
      1.   Catwalks for maintenance purposes on signs and sign structures shall
be constructed of metal.
      2.   Ladders on sign structures shall be at least fifteen inches (15")
wide. Ladders on signs shall be metal and shall not be less than ten inches
(10") wide.
   F.   Moving Parts: Signs employing the use of moving parts for animated
action as well as public clocks and thermometers shall be so designed that the
driving mechanism and the supporting bearings are securely anchored to the main
steel structure of the sign, and the supports for the movable portions of the
display must be calculated to support its weight plus the maximum wind pressure
to which the movable portions may be subjected. Mechanical motions having an
arc of rotation of less than sixty degrees (60°) may have their electrical
connections made through approved flexible rubber covered leads. Electrical
connections for actions greater than sixty degrees (60°) rotation must be
accomplished through the use of slip rings.
   G.   Method Of Hanging:
      1.   Flat signs shall be anchored below roof line except that additional
support other than chain may be provided over parapet to roof.
      2.   Projecting signs shall be rigidly hung on beams.
      3.   Separate projecting signs so hung as to appear as one sign shall be
rigidly hung on beams.
   H.   Poles Or Posts:
      1.   Poles or posts for the support of signs shall be of metal and shall
conform to accepted standard engineering design and loads; provided sign is not
more than twelve feet (12') above sidewalk, width is not more than half of
length, and hanging irons do not exceed two feet (2').
When pipe is employed, its trade size shall be not smaller than four inches
(4") and when supported by earth shall be firmly set in concrete with means to
prevent the pole from turning.
      2.   A sketch showing the size of the post and details of hanging shall
be submitted with the application for permit signed and stamped by a state of
Illinois registered architect or structural engineer.
      3.   Calculation for the size of the post shall be based on a combined
torsional and bending movement for a wind pressure of thirty (30) pounds per
square foot and a maximum fiber stress of sixteen thousand (16,000) pounds per
square inch.
      4.   Splices in pipe support standards shall be so constructed that they
develop required strength at that point. Splices shall be made at a point not
less than eight feet (8') above the foundation.
   I.   Safety Provisions: Facilities for proper protection of the public must
be provided during the erection or maintenance of all signs.
   J.   Restrictions: It shall be prohibited to place marquee lighting on any
outdoor illuminated sign or building structure, including but not limited to
windows, doors, parapet, exterior walls, support beams, roofs and balconies.
The use of such lighting shall be restricted to wall signs and marquee signs.
   K.   Illumination: [Discuss how to determine LED brightness from ground and
to determine length of frequency for LED]. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-13: GROUND SIGNS:
The provisions of this section shall govern ground signs.
   A.   Materials: All ground signs for which a permit is required under this
chapter, shall have a surface or facing of incombustible material; provided,
however, that combustible trim and structural supports and bracing may be used,
and further that the surface or facing may be of approved combustible material
if the surface is no more than sixty four (64) square feet in area located no
closer to an existing building or property line than ten feet (10').
   B.   Letters And Characters To Be Secured: All letters, figures, characters
or representations in cutout or irregular form, maintained in conjunction with,
attached to, or superimposed upon any sign shall be safely and securely built
or attached to the sign structure and shall comply with all requirements in
subsections
6-8-4D and
6-8-8A and B of this chapter.
   C.   Height And Area: The height of a ground sign shall not exceed twenty
four feet (24') nor shall the area or surface of both sides of the sign exceed
three hundred (300) square feet including the structural trim.
   D.   Location: No part of any ground sign shall be nearer the street than
the property line established by law.
   E.   Maintenance: All ground signs and the property described in the sign
permit shall be maintained by the owner of the sign in a clean, sanitary and
inoffensive condition, and free and clear of all obnoxious substances, rubbish
and weeds. Failure to clean up the premises shall be sufficient cause for
revocation of any permit issued pursuant to this chapter. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-14: POLE SIGNS:
Pole signs shall be hereby prohibited. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-15: WALL SIGNS:
The provisions of this section shall govern wall signs.
   A.   Materials: All wall signs for which a permit is required under this
chapter shall have a surface or facing of incombustible material or approved
nonrusting metal, paint, plastic or vinyl; provided, however, that combustible
decorative trim may be used thereon; and further provided, that combustible
facing not to exceed sixty four (64) square feet may be used if said sign is
nonilluminated or if there is no contact of any kind with the combustible
signs' materials and any or all electrical equipment, conduit, boxes or
attachments. Paint shall only be allowed to be placed upon a wall of brick or
masonry based construction, and must be used in conjunction with a suitable
primer prior to the application of paint and a protective clear coat following
the application of paint.
   B.   Location:
      1.   Placement And Area: No wall sign shall cover wholly or partially any
wall opening nor project beyond the ends or top of the wall to which it is
attached, and any one wall sign shall not exceed an area of two hundred (200)
square feet, unless approved by the Department of Community Development and
Growth Management. The maximum sign area shall be 7.5 percent of the area of
the first two (2) stories of building elevation on which it is placed, or in
the case of a multi-tenant industrial building, each tenant may have a sign not
to exceed 7.5 percent of the area of its leased exterior storefront.
      2.   Projection Above Sidewalk And Setback Line: No wall sign shall be
permitted to extend more than twelve inches (12") beyond the building line, and
shall not be attached to a wall at a height of less than ten feet (10') above
the sidewalk or ground.
   C.   Structural Requirements:
      1.   Supports And Attachments: All wall signs shall be safely and
securely attached to the building wall by means of metal anchors, bolts or
expansion screws of not less than three-eighths inch (3/8") in diameter
imbedded in said wall at least five inches (5"); provided, however, that such
signs may rest in, or be bolted to strong, heavy, metal brackets or saddles set
not over six feet (6') apart, each of which shall be securely fixed to the wall
as hereinbefore provided. In no case shall any wall sign be secured with wire,
strips of wood or nails.
      2.   Wind Pressure Requirements: All wall signs shall conform to the
requirements of subsection 6-8-4B of this chapter.
   D.   Nonconformance Criteria: A wall sign shall be considered nonconforming
due to the following conditions:
      1.   A visible lack of aesthetic maintenance. This includes, but is not
limited to, peeling of paint, rust, burned out lights (if illuminated) and
information which is no longer legible due to weathering.
      2.   Failing structure integrity. This includes, but is not limited to,
broken anchors, sign paneling which is easily moved in windy conditions, and
sign paneling which sags or peels away from the wall.
      3.   Obstruction by nature. This includes, but is not limited to, the
overgrowth of weeds and other plants at the base of the sign to a point where
the bottom of the sign becomes obscured and/or unreadable.
      4.   Illumination. The use of marquee lighting shall be allowed on wall
signs.
      5.   Notwithstanding anything to the contrary contained in this Chapter,
the Illumination regulations of this Chapter shall not apply or be imposed upon
any currently existing wall sign following the passage of an ordinance
approving this Chapter. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-16: ROOF SIGNS:
The provisions of this section shall govern roof signs.
A structure may have a wall sign or a roof sign but not both.
   A.   Materials: Every roof sign, including the upright supports and braces
thereof, shall be constructed entirely of galvanized or stainless steel;
provided, however, that combustible structural trim may be used thereon.
   B.   Height And Area: No roof sign shall have a surface or facing exceeding
fifty (50) square feet per side or one hundred (100) square feet for all sides,
nor have its highest point extended more than seventeen feet (17') above the
roof level, or the allowable height of the zoning district.
   C.   Location: No roof sign shall be placed on the roof of any building or
structure in such manner as to prevent free passage from one part of said roof
to any other part thereof or interfere with openings in said roof and shall
comply with subsections 6-8-8A and B of this chapter.
   D.   Structural Requirements:
      1.   Bracing, Anchorage And Supports: Every roof sign shall be mounted so
that no portion of said roof sign shall project into the public right of way.
The sign shall be physically mounted by means of a steel beam and/or steel pole
with no more than two (2) such beams or poles being utilized and erected in
accordance with standard engineering calculations. A-frames or other multiple
use of angle irons, chains or other lattice work erection systems are hereby
prohibited.
      2.   Wind Pressure Requirements: All roof signs shall conform to the
requirements of subsection
6-8-4B of this chapter. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-17: PROJECTING SIGNS:
The provisions of this section shall govern projecting signs.
   A.   Construction Generally: Every projecting sign shall be sealed by a
structural engineer and shall be constructed of incombustible materials, shall
be illuminated and shall be two (2) faced.
   B.   Materials: The lettering or advertising designs to be illuminated may
be composed of glass or other transparent or semitransparent incombustible
material. Any glass forming a part of any sign shall be safety glass or plate
glass at least one-fourth inch (1/4") thick and in case any single piece or
pane of glass has an area exceeding three (3) square feet, it shall be wired
glass. One section, not exceeding three (3) square feet in area, constructed of
wire glass or safety glass, shall be permitted on each side of a sign.
C.   Height And Area: Projecting signs shall be limited in area to seventy five
(75) square feet per side and shall not exceed a height of the    zoning
district.
   D.   Location: Every projecting sign shall be placed at least ten feet (10')
above any sidewalk used by the public over which it is erected, and a distance
not greater than twelve inches (12") from the face of the wall to which it is
attached, measuring from the point of the sign nearest thereto. No sign or part
thereof shall extend onto public property or right of way.
   E.   Support Structures:
      1.   Projecting signs exceeding ten (10) square feet in area or fifty
(50) pounds in weight shall not be attached to nor supported by frame buildings
nor the wooden framework of a building. Said signs shall be rigidly hung on
beams.
      2.   No projecting sign shall be secured with wire, strips of wood or
nails, nor shall any projecting sign be hung or secured to any other sign.
   F.   Illumination:
      1.   The reflectors shall be provided with the proper lenses
concentrating the illumination upon the area of the sign and preventing glare
upon the street or adjacent property; and no floodlight or spotlight nor
reflectors of the gooseneck type shall be permitted on projecting signs.
      2.   Every nonconforming projecting sign over public property shall be
illuminated between sunset and one o'clock (1:00) A.M. every morning, Monday
through Saturday, on each side thereof, by at least five (5) watts per square
foot of sign surface, but in no case less than sixty (60) watts for each sign
surface.
      3.   The use of marquee lighting shall be restricted for use on
projecting signs.
   G.   Movable Parts: Any movable part of a projecting sign such as the cover
of a service opening shall be securely fastened by chains or hinges.
   H.   V-Shaped Signs Prohibited: V-shaped signs, consisting of two (2)
single-faced signs erected without a roof or ceiling, shall not be permitted.
(Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-18: MARQUEES:
The provisions of this section shall govern marquees.
   A.   Materials: All marquees, including the anchors, bolts, supports, rods
and braces thereof, shall be constructed of incombustible materials and shall
be designed by a structural engineer.
   B.   Location:
      1.   Height Above Sidewalk: No portion of a marquee shall be less than
ten feet (10') above grade or any public thoroughfare.
      2.   Setback From Property Line: No marquee or any part thereof shall be
permitted to extend beyond the property line into any dedicated public right of
way.
   C.   Area: Attraction board and other illuminated surfaces shall not exceed
five hundred (500) square feet in total, including all vertical surfaces.
   D.   Structural Requirements:
      1.   Supports: Marquees shall be supported solely by the building to
which they are attached, and no columns or posts shall be permitted as support
therefor.
      2.   Roof Live Load And Wind Pressure Requirements: The roof of any
marquee shall be designed and constructed to support a live load of not less
than one hundred (100) pounds per square foot. The wind pressure requirements
shall be those stated in subsection 6-8-4B of this chapter.
      3.   Anchorage To Wood Structures: No marquees shall be erected on any
building of wood frame construction unless attached to the masonry, concrete or
steel supports of the building.
   E.   Signs Attached To Marquee: Signs attached to, or hung from a marquee
shall be completely within the borderline of the marquee outer edge, and shall
in no instance be lower than ten feet (10') above a sidewalk or public
thoroughfare. No sign or advertising material shall exceed five feet (5') in
height exclusive of the name of the establishment exhibiting such marquee. No
advertising material shall be placed upon the roof of any marquee.
   F.   Roofs Of Marquees:
      1.   Drainage: The roofs of all marquees shall be leakproof and properly
guttered and connected to downspouts to a storm sewer.
      2.   Roof Use And Materials: The roofs of all marquees shall be used for
no other purpose than to form and constitute a roof and shall be of
incombustible material.
   G.   Illumination: It shall be allowed to place marquee lighting on marquee
signs.
   H.   Notwithstanding anything to the contrary contained in this Chapter, the
Illumination regulations of this Chapter shall not apply or be imposed upon any
currently existing marquee sign following the passage of an ordinance approving
this Chapter. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-19: AWNINGS AND CANOPIES:
The provisions of this section shall govern awnings and canopies for commercial
or public use.
   A.   Permits And Inspections: Permits for awnings and canopies shall be
required as provided in subsection
6-8-3A of this chapter. Awnings and canopies shall be subject to annual
inspection as required in section
6-8-5 of this chapter.
   B.   Materials:
      1.   Awnings: Awnings may be constructed of cloth or metal, no vinyl;
provided, however all frames and supports shall be of metal. The awning may not
be internally illuminated (no backlighting) although there may be lighting
directed downward.
      2.   Canopies: Canopies may be constructed of cloth or metal hood, no
vinyl; provided, however, all frames and supports shall be of metal. Canopies
may not be internally illuminated (no backlighting) although there may be
lighting directed downward.
   C.   Location:
      1.   Height Above Grade:
         a.   Awnings: All awnings shall be constructed and erected so that the
lowest portion thereof shall not be less than eight feet (8') above grade,
public thoroughfare, or any pedestrian walk.
         b.   Canopies: All canopies shall be constructed and erected so that
the lowest portion thereof shall be not less than nine feet (9') above grade,
public thoroughfare, or any pedestrian walk.
      2.   Setback From Property Line: No awning or canopy shall be permitted
to extend beyond the property line into public right of way.
   D.   Support Structures:
      1.   Awnings:
         a.   Every awning shall be securely attached to and supported by the
building.
         b.   Posts or columns shall not be permitted for awnings.
         c.   No awning shall be attached to the wood jambs, frames or other
wood members of a building (frame buildings excepted) when such building is
less than ten feet (10') from public property.
      2.   Canopies: The framework of all canopies shall be designed by a
structural engineer. All frames and supports shall be of metal and designed to
withstand a wind pressure as provided in subsection
6-8-4B of this chapter.
   E.   Advertising: No advertising shall be placed on any awning or canopy;
except, that the name of the owner and business, industry or pursuit conducted
within the premises may be painted or otherwise permanently placed on the front
and side portions thereof except as approved by the Department of Community
Development and Growth Management. The area of advertisement located on an
awning or canopy shall be limited and count toward the allowable wall signage.
(Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-20: TRANSPORTATION SHELTERS AND ADVERTIS- ING SIGNS:
Transportation shelters may only be erected with prior specific approval of the
Zoning and Planning Commission and the president and board of trustees. Public
hearings for variations of zoning are not required for the placement of the
transportation shelters on public rights of way or on privately owned property;
provided, that prior approval was given by said commission and board. All
transportation shelters and advertising thereon shall be covered by the clauses
and provisions of a specific contract which shall be executed by the
transportation shelter corporation, firm, person or association, and the
Village president and board of trustees. (Ords. 9-13-41, 19-16-66)
6-8-21: TEMPORARY SIGNS:
   A.   Construction: Temporary signs must be maintained in a structurally
safe, clean, unblemished, and presentable manner.
      1.   Materials: Temporary signs may be constructed of cloth, canvas,
light fabric, cardboard, wallboard, wood or other light materials, with or
without frames.
      2.   Support Structures: Except for pennants, banners, inflatable signs
and window signs, if any temporary sign is attached to a wall, such a temporary
sign shall be attached to the wall with wire or steel cables or similar safe
fastening, but in no case shall string, rope or wood slats or other uncertain
fastenings be used to support or anchor the sign to the wall.
      3.   Location: No temporary signs shall be located in or on the public
right of way, sidewalk or other public thoroughfare, nor shall such signs be
located in such a manner as to interfere with the flow of traffic or restrict
the visibility at intersections or driveways.
      4.   Inflatable Signs: Inflatable signs may be erected on top of
structures or on the ground. Inflatable signs erected on the ground shall not
be tethered or otherwise suspended above the ground, but shall be fastened
directly to the ground without any vertical clearance between the inflatable
sign and the ground.
   B.   Duration: Temporary signs shall be allowed for the specified periods
set forth below:
      1.   Inflatable Signs: No inflatable sign shall be permitted on the same
parcel for a period of more than seven (7) consecutive days in any year, nor
for more than a total of thirty five (35) days in any year, with a maximum
height of fifteen feet (15').
      2.   Pennants: No pennants shall be permitted.
      3.   Banners: Banners shall comply with the allowable number and area of
wall signs. All banners shall remain on the location where they are erected or
placed for a period of not more than sixty (60) days, and shall be removed
within five (5) days after the event to which they relate has ended.
      4.   Other Temporary Signs: All other temporary signs, except for
political signs and subject to any other exemptions found in section 6-8-22 of
this chapter, shall remain on the location where they are erected or placed for
a period of not more than sixty (60)days, and shall be removed within five (5)
days after the event to which they relate has ended.
   C.   Permitted Temporary Signs By Type And Zoning District: Temporary signs
shall be allowed in the Village in accordance with the following table:
PERMITTED TEMPORARY SIGNS BY TYPE AND ZONING DISTRICT
Y = Allowed without sign permit.
P = Allowed only with sign permit pursuant to section
6-8-3 of this chapter.
N = Not allowed.
 
                    Zoning Districts
Sign Type           Residential          Commercial           Office All Other
                    (R-1, R-2, R-3, PUD) (C-1, C-2, C-3, PUD)
Banners             Y1                   P                    P      P
Inflatable          Y1                   P                    P      P
Pennants            P1                   P                    P      P
All other temporary P1                   P2                   P2     P2
 
Notes:   1.   No commercial message allowed on sign, except for a commercial
message drawing attention to an activity legally offered on the premises.
2.   A temporary sign over 8 square feet in area shall obtain a permit and
comply with all sections of the sign code.
   D.   Permitted Number And Size Of Temporary Signs By Sign Type: The number
and size of temporary signs shall be allowed in accordance with the following
table:
PERMITTED NUMBER AND SIZE OF TEMPORARY SIGNS BY TYPE
n/a = Not applicable
 
Sign Type           Number Allowed            Maximum Sign Area 1
Banners             1 per wall; maximum 4 per n/a
                    building
Inflatable          1 per lot                 n/a
Pennants            n/a                       n/a
All other temporary 1 per lot                 8 square feet per sign face
 
(Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-22: MOBILE SIGNS:
   A.   Number Of Days Permitted: No mobile sign shall be permitted to stand at
any address within the Village for a period of more than thirty (30)
consecutive days in any year, nor more than a total of sixty (60) days in any
year. No mobile sign permit shall be issued for consecutive thirty (30) day
periods without at least a period of thirty (30) days intervening.
   B.   Public Right Of Way: No mobile sign shall be permitted to stand upon
any public right of way. All mobile signs shall be placed on private property
only.
   C.   Traffic And Line Of Sight Hazards: All mobile signs shall meet all
Village requirements concerning traffic hazards and line of sight hazards.
   D.   Lights: No mobile sign shall be permitted with lights that flash, blink
or vary in intensity.
   E.   Compliance With Code: All mobile signs shall comply with all applicable
code provisions of the Village, including, but not limited to, the electrical
code provisions where the mobile sign is electrical.
   F.   Name And Address Of Owner: Each mobile sign shall have clearly and
permanently stamped, painted or affixed thereon in a prominent and visible
location, the name and address of the mobile sign's owner.
   G.   Electrical Mobile Signs: When a mobile sign is electrical, the
following regulations, in addition to all other applicable regulations, shall
apply thereto:
      1.   The total length of cord between the source of power and the mobile
sign unit shall be no more than twelve feet (12') in length.
      2.   The source of power shall be a weatherproof receptacle, grounded
type.
      3.   There shall not be any other connector of any kind between the
mobile sign and the source of power.
      4.   In no case shall the cord be in such a position that it will be
walked over by pedestrian traffic or be driven over by any form of wheeled
vehicle.
      5.   No mobile sign shall be operated with any power source unless the
mobile sign unit has a UL label affixed thereto.
      6.   Temporary electrical permits shall be required for electrical mobile
signs.
   H.   Maintenance: All mobile signs shall be maintained in good working order
and in a structurally safe condition, and the immediate area surrounding said
mobile sign shall be kept free from litter and debris.
   I.   Permit: It shall be unlawful for any person to place a mobile sign
within the Village without first having obtained a permit therefor from the
Department of Community Development and Growth Management. The application for
said permit shall be on a form provided by the Department of Community
Development and Growth Management and contain such information as required by
the Department of Community Development and Growth Management.
   J.   Permit Fee: The mobile sign permit fee shall be as set forth in
subsection
6-8-3D of this chapter and shall be in addition to any other permit or
inspection fees required by this code.
   K.   Permit Period: All mobile sign permits shall be valid for a period not
to exceed thirty (30) days and shall set forth thereon the date of issuance and
expiration.
   L.   Penalty Provisions:
      1.   Any person who places a mobile sign upon property within the Village
without first having obtained and kept in full effect a permit as required
above, shall be required to procure a permit for such sign, and shall pay a fee
therefor equal to twice the fee which is otherwise charged hereunder.
      2.   Any person violating the provisions of this section shall be fined
not less than ten dollars ($10.00) nor more than five hundred dollars
($500.00). A separate and distinct offense shall be regarded as having been
committed each day upon which said person shall continue any such violation.
      3.   Any person convicted of violating any of the provisions of this
section, in addition to the penalties provided herein, shall have his permit
revoked.
      4.   When the person responsible for violating the provisions of this
section cannot reasonably be determined, then the person holding the permit to
place the mobile sign at such location shall be the person who is in violation
of this section. In the event that at the time of the violation there exists no
valid permit for the particular mobile sign in violation, then the owner of the
sign shall be the person who is in violation of this section. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-23: EXEMPTIONS FROM PROVISIONS:
   A.   The provisions and regulations of this chapter shall not apply to the
following signs; provided, however, said signs shall be subject to the
provisions of sections
6-8-6 and
6-8-7 of this chapter; and further provided, any exempted permanent or
temporary sign as stated herein exceeding the limitations in the exceptions
shall come under all applicable provisions of this chapter:
      1.   House numbers, house nameplates, and professional nameplates not
exceeding two (2) square feet in area.
      2.   Signs painted on the exterior surface of a building or structure;
provided, however, if said signs have raised borders, letters, characters,
decorations or lighting appliances, they shall be subject to the provisions of
section
6-8-14 of this chapter and all other applicable provisions of this chapter.
Should the painted sign cover more than twenty five percent (25%) of the
subject wall, the sign owner shall petition the Department of Community
Development and Growth Management and upon their approval apply and pay for a
sign permit for said sign.
      3.   Traffic or other municipal signs, legal notices, railroad crossing
signs and danger signs. These signs are defined as governmental signs in
section
6-8-2 of this chapter.
      4.   Holiday decorations, signs or other material displayed in connection
with civic, patriotic or religious holidays.
      5.   Official flags, pennants, or insignia of any governmental or
nonprofit organization.
      6.   A sign advertising the sale or lease of a lot or space on which the
sign is placed. Such signs shall be subject to the public right of way location
restriction set forth in subsection
6-8-20A3 of this chapter, and shall not exceed eight (8) square feet per sign
face in area. Only one such sign may be placed on the lot or premises to which
it pertains, except that two (2) such signs may be placed on double frontage
lots. Such sign shall be removed within five (5) days after the buyer and
seller are mutually bound to conclude/consummate the sale or lease of the lot
or space to which the sign pertains.
      7.   A construction sign identifying the parties involved in the
construction to occur or occurring on the lot or premises on which the sign is
placed. Such signs shall be subject to the public right of way location
restriction set forth in subsection 6-8-20A3 of this chapter and shall not
exceed eight (8) square feet per sign face in area. Such signs may identify the
owner's name, the architect for the project, the contractor or contractors for
the project, the financing arrangements for the project, and/or the purpose for
which the project is intended. No products or services may be advertised on
construction signs. One construction sign may be placed on each part of the lot
or on each face of the building which borders or is oriented to a public
street. A construction sign shall be removed within five (5) days after the
final occupancy certificate is issued relative to the project.
      8.   Political signs displayed in accordance with an official election or
signs erected on behalf of candidates for public office. Such signs shall be
subject to the public right of way location restriction set forth in subsection
6-8-20A3 of this chapter and shall not exceed eight (8) square feet per sign
face in area.
      9.   Historical signs/iconic signs, as defined by the Oak Lawn historical
preservation commission and the office of quality control. [Listed provided by
Village]
      10.   Exempt Lighting. Any and all lighting used for temporary
emergencies to ensure public safety, lighting used for municipal construction,
lighting utilized for flags, lighting for radio, communication, and navigation
towers, and temporary exterior lighting approved by the Village.
   B.   No permit shall be required for normal painting or maintenance. (Ord.
9-13-41)
6-8-24: VIOLATIONS AND PENAL- TIES:
   A.   Any person found to be in violation of any of the provisions of this
chapter shall be subject to a civil penalty of not less than twenty five
dollars ($25.00) nor more than seven hundred fifty dollars ($750.00). Each day
such violation is committed, or permitted to continue, shall constitute a
separate offense and shall be punishable as such hereunder.
   B.   All violations of this chapter shall be processed according to the
provisions of title 11, chapter 12, "Administrative Adjudication Of
Nonvehicular Regulations Violations", of this code.
   C.   The Department of Community Development and Growth Management or its
designee is authorized and directed to enforce the provisions of this chapter
and shall be responsible for interpreting this chapter's provisions. (Ord. 9-
13-41)
6-8-25: AUTOMOBILE "FOR SALE" SIGNS:
The provisions of this section shall govern automobile "for sale" signs.
   A.   Residents or property owners within the Village shall be allowed to
offer automobiles and other motorized vehicles for sale through the use of "for
sale" signs affixed to such vehicles in accordance with the provisions of this
section.
   B.   No more than a total of six (6) motorized vehicles, and no more than
one at a time, shall be permitted to be offered for sale by residents of any
one household or one property address per year.
   C.   Motorized vehicles bearing "for sale" signs shall be displayed on
private driveways only. No motorized vehicle bearing a "for sale" sign may be
displayed on commercial property within the Village without previous permission
of the property owner. Vehicles bearing such signs which are permitted to be
parked in commercial parking lots with the property owner's permission shall
not be displayed in such lots for a period exceeding twelve (12) hours in any
one day. Any such vehicle remaining on a commercial lot for a period in excess
of twelve (12) hours, or displayed on public property within the Village, may
be towed at the discretion of the property owner or the Village, at the expense
of the vehicle owner or of the person leaving the automobile in such location.
   D.   Such signs shall not block or obscure the vision of the driver of the
vehicle and shall not violate any provisions of the Illinois vehicle code.
   E.   All "for sale" signs shall be maintained by the owner of the sign in a
clean, sanitary and inoffensive condition.
   F.   It shall be unlawful for any person to violate the provisions of this
section. Any person violating the provisions of this section shall be subjected
to a fine not less than twenty five dollars ($25.00) and not to exceed one
hundred dollars ($100.00). (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-26: VILLAGE DISTRICTS:
In order to regulate and limit the use of signs on lot areas and buildings, the
Village is hereby divided into four (4) districts, to be used only for the
regulation of signs, as follows:
   A.   Residential: This shall include the districts outlined in section
4-4-1 of this code known as R-1 (single-family residence district), R-2
(limited multi-family residence district), R-3 (multi-family residence
district), PD R-l (planned single-family residence district), PD R-2 (planned
limited multi-family residence district), and PD R-3 (planned multi-family
residence district).
   B.   Commercial: This shall include the districts outlined in section
4-4-1 of this code known as C-1 (general business district), C-2 (general
service district), C-3 (regional shopping district), O (office district), and
PK (parking district).
   C.   Manufacturing: This shall include the districts outlined in section
4-4-1 of this code known as M-l (restricted manufacturing district).
   D.   Public: This shall include the districts outlined in section
4-4-1 of this code known as PL (public lands district). (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-27: PUBLIC CLOCKS:
   A.   As defined by section
6-8-25 of this chapter, public clocks shall be allowed in
"commercial", "manufacturing", and "public" districts. Public clocks shall not
be allowed in "residential" districts.
   B.   Public clocks shall consist of one of two (2) types:
      1.   Circular Clock: A clock which consists of a flat panel covering an
electric motor, which powers a minute and hour hand. A second hand is allowed
but not required. The panel covering the motor shall have suitable circular
markings to denote its function as a twelve (12) hour clock.
      2.   Digital Clock: A clock comprised of electronic lights arranged in
such a pattern as to form numbers which display the current time. This type of
clock shall operate on a standard twelve (12) hour length. Both hour and minute
times must be shown; a blinking colon between hour and minute times, used to
show the progressing seconds, is allowed but not required. A digital clock
shall have no physical moving parts.
   C.   Any public clock found to be displaying an inaccurate time shall be
considered nonconforming, and have its owner notified of its discrepancy and be
given a fifteen (15) day repair period to correct the problem and restore the
clock to displaying the accurate time. The benchmark standard for accurate
Central Standard Time used is the National Institute Of Standards And
Technology (NIST). Correct time shall be defined as being within seven (7)
minutes of the NIST Central Standard Time. If the clock is not set to the
correct time following this fifteen (15) day period, the Village shall impose a
fifty dollar ($50.00) fine on the owner of the sign per every seven (7) days
which the clock continues to display the incorrect time.
   D.   All public clock owners shall be granted automatic fifteen (15) day
repair periods starting on the Monday after the second Sunday in March, and the
Monday following the first Sunday in November. These automatic fifteen (15) day
repair periods shall be granted to allow for the readjustment of their clock's
time in compliance with daylight savings time. If the necessary adjustments are
not made within this automatic repair period, the fine policy defined in
subsection D of this section will apply. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-28: ANIMATED SIGNS:
   A.   Animated signs shall be allowed in "commercial" districts, as defined
in section
6-8-25 of this chapter.
   B.   All rules of section
6-8-11. "Illuminated Signs", of this chapter also apply to animated signs.
   C.   Animated signs shall not be constructed and/or programmed in such a
manner in which causes them to be distracting motorists or pedestrians, putting
them at risk. Any degree of distraction shall be determined at a Village of Oak
Lawn code enforcer's discretion.
   D.   Animated signs shall only be permitted as accessories to other signs.
The animated portion of the sign shall not take up more than thirty percent
(30%) of the total square footage on one face of the sign. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-29: WINDOW SIGNS:
   A.   Window signs shall be allowed in "commercial" and "public" districts,
as defined in section §
6-8-25 of this chapter.
   B.   Window signs shall consist of waterproof fade resistant material
attached to the inside of a window, or a physical display of items which
extends inward to a horizontal depth of three feet (3') measured from the
inside of the window.
   C.   In situations where it is desirable to completely blank out a window as
to prevent light from coming through, occupants of the building or unit shall
be allowed two (2) options:
      1.   The glass shall be replaced with a nontransparent flat panel which
has similar structural characteristics as glass and does not require painting
as a regular maintenance task. This panel shall be colored in such a way as to
blend in with the colors of the building itself. Such a panel will thereafter
not be considered a window sign.
      2.   The interior of the glass shall be used to display tasteful artwork
in such a way which may promote the business as well as promote artistic
expression. The artwork may only be composed on the interior of the glass after
it has been checked for cracks, and may only consist of those materials which
are waterproof and fade resistant.
   D.   A window sign shall be considered nonconforming under the following
criteria:
      1.   The items or material used for the window sign become faded or show
signs of water damage.
      2.   The window itself becomes broken or cracked.
      3.   Signs of obvious neglect begin to show. This includes, but is not
limited to, the collection of dust, dirt, or other debris in the area
immediately inside the window.
      4.   If the window sign is not being used to blank out the entire window,
the sign takes up more than forty percent (40%) of the total square footage of
glass on the front of the unit. This nonconformance regulation shall only be
applied to those signs which are applied directly to the window or sit within
six inches (6") of the interior edge of the pane of glass. Window displays
shall be exempt.
In addition, a window without a window sign which remains undecorated with a
nondecorative, strictly functional item or item(s) directly behind the glass
shall be considered a nonconforming window sign until the installation of
either blank out option defined.
   E.   Illumination: It shall be prohibited to place marquee lighting on any
window sign or building structure, including but not limited to windows, doors,
parapet, exterior walls, support beams, roofs and balconies. (Ord. 9-13-41)
6-8-30: PORTABLE SIDEWALK SIGNS:
   A.   Commercial Districts: Portable sidewalk signs shall be allowed in
"commercial" districts, as defined in section 6-8-25 of this chapter.
   B.   Size: Portable sidewalk signs shall be no greater than six (6) square
feet in area and not exceed four feet (4') in height.
   C.   Number: No more than one sign shall be permitted per business customer
entrance. The Village reserves the right to limit the number of portable
sidewalk sign permits issued to ensure the proper flow of pedestrian and
vehicular traffic, and otherwise to protect the public health, safety, and
welfare. In that event, permits shall be processed and issued on a first come,
first serve basis.
   D.   Display: All portable sidewalk signs shall be removed:
      1.   When the business is not open for customers.
      2.   When the national weather service issues a high wind warning, a
tornado or severe thunderstorm watch or warning.
      3.   From public sidewalks during an accumulation snowfall event.
   E.   Permitted Locations: All portable sidewalk signs shall be located:
      1.   At grade level.
      2.   In front of the business property.
      3.   No more than fifteen feet (15') from the customer entrance to the
building for the business being advertised.
      4.   A minimum of two feet (2') back from the curb adjacent to parking
spaces.
   F.   Prohibited Locations: No portable sidewalk sign shall be located:
      1.   In landscaped areas or the parkway.
      2.   In parking aisles or stalls.
      3.   On or anchored to fences, planters, other signs, vehicles, utility
facilities or any structure.
      4.   In streets or alleys.
      5.   Within the Illinois department of transportation right of way.
   G.   Right Of Way Placement: Signs may be placed on a Village owned
sidewalk:
      1.   Pursuant to the issuance of an annual Village of Oak Lawn portable
sidewalk sign permit.
      2.   Subject to all requirements for insurance and indemnification as
provided below.
   H.   Construction And Maintenance: Signs shall be:
      1.   Constructed of exterior grade, rigid, weather resistant and durable
materials,
      2.   Of sufficient weight and durability to ensure the sign remains in
place during normal weather conditions, and
      3.   Maintained in a professional manner free of chipping paint, cracks,
gouges, loss of letters, and similar defects.
   I.   Elements Prohibited On Portable Sidewalk Signs: The following shall be
prohibited on portable signs:
      1.   Any form of internal or external illumination, including flashing,
blinking, or rotating lights;
      2.   Animation;
      3.   Attachments, including, but not limited to, sandbags or other items
to hold a sign in place, balloons, ribbons, flags, speakers, other copy or
graphics.
   J.   Clearance For Pedestrians: A clear pedestrian access path shall be
maintained as follows:
      1.   Public Sidewalks: A minimum of four feet (4').
      2.   Private Walkways: A minimum of three feet (3').
   K.   Insurance: The applicant for a portable sidewalk sign permit shall
provide, at its sole cost and expense, and shall thereafter maintain in effect
during the entire period of the permit, insurance in the following manner:
      1.   Comprehensive general liability insurance, including owner's
protective liability insurance and contractual liability insurance covering
claims for personal injury and property damage with limits of at least one
million dollars ($1,000,000.00) per occurrence and two million dollars
($2,000,000.00) aggregate;
      2.   Prior to issuance of a portable sidewalk sign permit, the applicant
shall provide the Village with copies of the certificates of insurance for the
required policies for each type of insurance naming the Village of Oak Lawn as
an additional insured party.
   L.   Indemnification: The permittee shall indemnify, defend, protect, and
hold harmless the Village, its corporate authorities, officers, employees,
agents and volunteers from and against any and all claims, demands, losses,
damages, liabilities, fines, charges, penalties, administrative and judicial
proceedings and orders, judgments, remedial actions of any kind, all costs and
cleanup actions of any kind, and all costs and expenses incurred in connection
therewith, including, but not limited to, reasonable attorney fees, expert
witness fees and costs of defenses (collectively, the "losses") directly or
proximately resulting from permittee's acts or omissions, except to the extent
that the Village is the sole legal cause of said losses. (Ord. 9-13-41)
CHAPTER 9
DANGEROUS AND ABANDONED BUILDINGS OR STRUCTURES
SECTION:
6-9-1: Definitions
6-9-2: Nuisance Declared
6-9-3: Action by Village Officials
6-9-4: Authority to Close Building, Suspend or Revoke License
6-9-5: Costs to Owner
6-9-6: Occupying Unsanitary Building
6-9-7: Abatement
6-9-8: Court Proceedings
6-9-9: Alternative Actions
6-9-1: DEFINITIONS:
As used in this Chapter, the following words and terms shall have the meanings
prescribed herein:
ABANDONED BUILDING or STRUCTURE:
   A.   Any vacant building which is frequented by persons who are not lawful
occupants of such structure; or
   B.   Any vacant building which, by reason of lack of maintenance, or by
reason of the boarding up of its doors and windows, or other reasons, has a
substantial adverse effect on the value of the property in the immediate
neighborhood; or
   C.   A building or structure, the principal use of which has been abandoned,
and that no longer has any function or use; or
   D.   Any railroad trestle or siding no longer in use.
BUILDING or STRUCTURE: Includes anything constructed or erected, the use of
which requires permanent or temporary location on or in the ground including,
but not limited to, buildings of all types and use groups, advertising signs,
billboards, swimming pools, sheds, garages, fences or any other manmade
structure.
DANGEROUS BUILDING or STRUCTURE:
   A.   Any building or structure which is dangerous to the public health or
safety because of its construction or condition, or which may cause or aid in
the spread of disease or which may become a harborage for rodents or other
animals, or which may cause injury to the occupants thereof or of a neighboring
building or structure; or
   B.   Any building or structure which, because of faulty construction, age,
lack of proper repair or any other cause, constitutes or creates a fire hazard;
or
   C.   Any building or structure, which, by reason of faulty construction, age
or lack of repair is likely to collapse or fall, in whole or in part; or
   D.   Any building or structure which has been damaged by fire, wind or other
causes so as to have become dangerous to life, safety, morals or the general
health and welfare of the occupants or the people of the Village; or
   E.   Any building which has become or is so dilapidated, decayed, unsafe,
unsanitary or which so utterly fails to provide the amenities essential to
decent living that it is unfit for human habitation, or is likely to cause
sickness or disease so as to work injury to the health, morals, safety or
general welfare of those living therein; or
   F.   Any building or structure which has parts thereof which are so attached
that they may fall or injure members of the public or property; or
   G.   Any building or structure which because of its condition is unsafe,
unsanitary or dangerous to the health, morals, safety or general welfare of the
people of this Village; or
   H.   Any building or structure existing in violation of any provision of the
Building Code of this Village 1  or any provision of the Fire Prevention Code
or other ordinances of this Village 2 . (1951 Code 21-15-1-21-15-8; Ord. 77-6-
5)
 
Notes
1 1. See Chapter 1 of this Title.
2 2. See Title 7, Chapter 3 of this Code.
6-9-2: NUISANCE DECLARED:
Any dangerous or abandoned building or structure, as defined by Section
6-9-1 of this Chapter, within the Village is hereby declared to be a nuisance.
(1951 Code 21-15-1-21-15-8; Ord. 77-6-5)
6-9-3: ACTION BY VILLAGE OFFICIALS:
   A.   Notice: Whenever it shall come to the knowledge of the Director of
Office of Quality Control, the Chief Building Inspector or to the Health and
Sanitation Officer that a building or structure is in a dangerous or abandoned
condition, such officer shall affix a notice on the building or structure, in a
conspicuous place on the exterior wall thereof, informing all persons to keep
out of said building. Any person removing, defacing or disturbing such notice
as affixed shall be liable to the penalties provided for in this Code.
   B.   Evacuation of Building: Whenever it shall come to the knowledge of the
Director of Office of Quality Control, the Chief Building Inspector or to the
Health and Sanitation Officer that a dangerous building exists with structural
faults that may cause the imminent collapse of the said building, or if any
building is in such condition as to make it dangerous to the health, morals,
safety or general welfare of its occupants, or that other conditions exist of
an emergency nature which may cause the death or injury of the occupants
thereof, such officer shall order the immediate evacuation of the occupants
from any such building.
   C.   Emergency Repair, Vacation or Demolition: In cases where it reasonably
appears that there is immediate danger to the life or safety of any person,
unless a "dangerous building or structure", as defined herein, is immediately
repaired, vacated or demolished, the Director of Office of Quality Control
shall cause the immediate repair, vacation or demolition of such dangerous
building or structure. The costs of such emergency repair, vacation or
demolition shall be collected in the same manner as provided in Section
6-9-8 of this Chapter. (1951 Code 21-15-1-21-15-8; Ords. 77-6-5, 81-12-19; 1985
Code)
6-9-4: AUTHORITY TO CLOSE BUILDING, SUSPEND OR REVOKE LICENSE:
   A.   The Director of Office of Quality Control, the Chief Building Inspector
or Health and Sanitation Officer may order that no person shall be permitted to
enter, occupy or use a dangerous building or structure or any portion thereof
until the same shall have been made safe and habitable, and may post such order
in a conspicuous place on the exterior of such building or structure. No person
shall enter, occupy or use (except for the purpose of repairing and making
safe) any building or structure, or portion thereof, after the posting of such
notice until such building or structure or portion thereof shall have been made
safe and habitable.
   B.   If any building or structure or portion thereof which is dangerous or
abandoned shall be devoted to any purpose requiring a license, the President
may suspend such license pending the making of repairs and he may revoke such
license if the owner or person having an interest in such unsafe or abandoned
building or structure or portion thereof fails to make the same safe
immediately and at his own expense. (1951 Code 21-15-1-21-15-8; Ords. 77-6-5,
81-12-19; 1985 Code)
6-9-5: COSTS TO OWNER:
If work has been done and expense incurred by the Village in connection with
the repair or demolition of any dangerous building or structure as provided
herein, a bill for such expense shall be rendered by the Village to the owner,
lessee, occupant or agent of the property. The Village may refuse to issue a
permit for the reconstruction, alteration or repair of any such building or
portion thereof until the expenditures incurred by the Village on account of
such repairs or demolition have been repaid. (1951 Code 21-15-1-21-15-8; Ord.
77-6-5)
6-9-6: OCCUPYING UNSANITARY BUILDING:
It shall be unlawful to occupy for human habitation any building which is
declared by the Health and Sanitation Officer to be unfit for such habitation
by reason of defective sanitary conditions until such conditions have been
remedied and the premises approved by such Health and Sanitation Officer as fit
for occupancy. (1951 Code 21-15-1-21-15-8; Ord. 77-6-5; 1985 Code)
6-9-7: ABATEMENT:
   A.   The Director of Office of Quality Control, the Chief Building Inspector
or the Health and Sanitation Officer may, prior to the action of the Board of
Trustees authorized in Section
6-9-8 hereof, give written notice to the owner or owner's agent of a dangerous
or abandoned building or structure, as defined herein, to repair or demolish it
within fifteen (15) days. Where, upon diligent search, the identity or
whereabouts of the owner or owner's agent of any building or structure is not
ascertainable, notice mailed to the person or persons in whose name such real
estate was last assessed is sufficient notice under this Section.
   B.   If, at the end of no less than fifteen (15) days and no more than
thirty (30) days from the date of the aforesaid written notice, the owner or
owner's agent has failed to take positive action to put such building or
structure in a satisfactory condition or to demolish it, the Director of Office
of Quality Control shall notify the Village Manager of such failure, and the
Village Manager shall advise the President and Board of Trustees that a
dangerous or abandoned building or structure exists and that no action has been
taken by the owner or owner's agent after written notice to repair or demolish
it. (1951 Code 21-15-1-21-15-8; Ords. 77-6-5, 81-12-19; 1985 Code)
6-9-8: COURT PROCEEDINGS:
After notification to the President and Board of Trustees by the Village
Manager that no action is being taken to demolish or repair a dangerous or
abandoned building or structure, the President and Board of Trustees may
authorize the Village Attorney to apply to the Circuit Court of Cook County for
an order authorizing the demolition or repair of a dangerous or abandoned
building or structure, as defined herein, if the owners thereof, including the
lien holders of record after at least fifteen (15) days' written notice by mail
to do so, have failed to put such building or structure in a satisfactory
condition or to demolish it. It is not a defense to such cause of action that
the building is boarded up or otherwise enclosed. Where, upon diligent search,
the identity or whereabouts of the owner or owners of any such building or
structure, including the lien holders of record, is not ascertainable, notice
mailed to the person or persons in whose name such real estate was last
assessed is sufficient notice under this Section. The cost of such demolition
or repair incurred by the Village or by a lien holder of record is recoverable
from the owner or owners of such real estate and is a lien thereon, which lien
is superior to all prior existing liens and encumbrances, except taxes;
provided that, within sixty (60) days after such repair or demolition, the
Village or the lien holder of record who incurred such cost and expense shall
file notice of lien of such cost and expense incurred in the office of the
Recorder of Deeds of Cook County or in the office of the Registrar of Titles of
such County, if the real estate affected is registered under the Torrens
system. The notice must consist of a sworn statement setting out:
   A.   A description of the real estate sufficient for identification thereof;
   B.   The amount of money representing the cost and expense incurred; and
   C.   The date or dates when the cost and expense was incurred by the Village
or by the lien holder of record.
Upon payment of the cost and expense by the owner of or persons interested in
the property after notice of lien has been filed, the lien shall be released by
the Village or person in whose name the lien has been filed, and the release
may be filed of record as in the case of filing notice of lien. The lien may be
enforced by proceedings to foreclose as in case of mortgage or mechanic's lien.
Suit to foreclose this lien must be commenced within three (3) years after the
date of filing notice of lien. (1951 Code 21-15-1-21-5-8; Ord. 77-6-5)
6-9-9: ALTERNATIVE ACTIONS:
In addition to the other remedies provided for here- in, the Village may
proceed in accordance with the provisions of chapter 24, sections 11-31-1 or
11-31-2 of the Illinois Revised Statutes, or may pursue any other remedy
provided by law. (1951 Code 21-15-1-21-15-8; Ord. 77-6-5)
CHAPTER 10
MOVING BUILDINGS
SECTION:
6-10-1: Permit Requirements
6-10-2: Lights Required
6-10-3: Prohibited Acts and Conditions
6-10-4: Compliance With Other Provisions
6-10-1: PERMIT REQUIREMENTS:
   A.   Permit Required: No building shall be moved on or across any public
street, walk, alley or other public place within the Village by any person,
except upon a permit duly issued by the Village.
   B.   Fee 1 : Before the issuance of such permit, the applicant shall pay to
the Village a sum of money in payment for the occupancy of the street or
streets through which the said building is proposed to be moved, and said sum
to be determined by estimating the number of days which will be required to
move said building, on the assumption that the building will be moved five
hundred feet (500') per day, at the rate of fifty dollars ($50.00) per day.
   C.   Bond: In addition to the payment for street occupancy, there shall be
furnished a bond in the sum of five thousand dollars ($5,000.00) with good and
sufficient sureties to be approved by the President, conditioned, among other
things that said party will pay any and all damage that may occur to any public
street, alley or public place over which said building may be moved, and to
reimburse the Village and any public utility company for the cost of removing
and replacing all overhead wires, cables and appurtenances and for the making
of the necessary repairs thereto.
   D.   Liability Insurance: Before any person, to whom a permit has been
issued under this Chapter, shall enter upon the moving of any building
authorized to be moved by the permit, he shall obtain from some reliable
insurance company, policies of liability insurance indemnifying the Village and
public for any damage or injury resulting to any of the workmen or employees of
such person engaged in the moving of such building, and also indemnifying the
Village and public for any damages or injury suffered by any other person
resulting from the moving of such building.
   E.   Issuance of Permit: If, upon consideration of such application and such
inspection as the Director of Office of Quality Control may deem necessary, it
shall appear to the Director that the work proposed to be done is in accordance
with this Code and the ordinances of the Village, the Director, upon the
payment to the Village of the fees and bond herein provided for, shall grant
the permit sought for such purpose. (1951 Code 21-13-1, 21-13-5; Ord. 81-12-19;
1985 Code)
 
Notes
1 1. See Section
  3-1-1-7 of this Code for license fee.
6-10-2: LIGHTS REQUIRED:
On each and every night while a building remains on the public streets or
alleys of the Village, from dusk to sunrise, the person to whom the permit was
issued shall place and cause to be maintained at the center and each end of
such building, red lights, which lights shall be plainly visible during the
period above mentioned. (1951 Code 21-13-3)
6-10-3: PROHIBITED ACTS AND CONDITIONS:
   A.   Driving Stakes in Pavements or Railroad Crossings: It shall be unlawful
for any person to whom a permit has been issued under this Chapter, to drive
stakes in any pavement, public alley or at any railroad or railway crossing.
   B.   Obstructing Intersections, Railroad Crossings: No building which is
authorized to be moved in accordance with the terms of this Chapter shall be
permitted to stand or remain in the intersection of any streets, or any street
and public alley, or upon any railroad or railway crossing at any time during
the course of its moving. (1951 Code 21-13-2, 21-13-4)
6-10-4: COMPLIANCE WITH OTHER PROVISIONS:
No permit to move any building within the Village shall be granted unless the
construction and proposed use of such building on the proposed new site shall
conform in every way to requirements of this Title and of the Zoning Ordinance.
1  (1951 Code 21-13-1)
 
Notes
1 1. See Title 4 of this Municipal Code.
CHAPTER 11
FENCES
SECTION:
6-11-1: Definition
6-11-2: Permit Required; Fee
6-11-3: Design And Construction Regulations
6-11-4: Cost Of Construction
6-11-5: Fencing For Construction And Demolition Activity
6-11-6: Fence Variations
6-11-7: Penalty
6-11-1: DEFINITION:
A "fence" is hereby defined as a manmade structure forming a barrier which is
not a part of any building or structure, and is more than two feet (2') in
height over existing grade. (1985 Code)
6-11-2: PERMIT REQUIRED; FEE:
It shall be unlawful for any person to erect or construct any fence within the
village limits without having first obtained a permit from the director of the
office of quality control. The fee for said permit shall be thirty dollars
($30.00). (Ords. 87-9-32, 94-4-13, 05-23-86)
6-11-3: DESIGN AND CONSTRUCTION REGULATIONS:
   A.   General: Every fence shall be designed and constructed to resist a
horizontal wind pressure of not less than twice that required for buildings, as
set forth in the building code. Where the distance from the lot line to the
face of any fence is less than the height of the fence, such fence shall have
lateral supports on one side with structural braces extending to the top of the
fence.
Where the style of the fence is such that the fence posts and support pieces
between fence posts are located only on one side of the fence, the fence shall
be constructed such that the side of the fence with the fence posts and support
pieces faces the interior of the fence owner's property (facing away from
streets, alleys and neighboring properties).
   B.   Residential Requirements:
      1.   The maximum fence height in a residential use district shall not
exceed six feet (6') between buildings or in the rear yard behind the building.
All height dimensions shall be measured from the established grade to the
topmost section of the fence.
      2.   No fence shall be built, constructed or erected so as to extend past
the front yard building line as established by the zoning ordinance 1 ; nor
shall any fence be built past the side street yard on a corner lot as
established by the zoning ordinance. No fence shall be erected in any public
right of way.
No fence shall be built, constructed or erected in any easement except wherein
a release is given to the village prior thereto, absolving the village from all
liability and damages resulting from subsequent entry for the purpose of
repair, inspection, maintenance, installation or removal of utilities. Any
fence allowed or permitted in an easement shall be so constructed that the
fence and posts may be readily removable. The village shall in no way be held
liable for the replacement of any fence removed, or any other expense entailed
by the removal or reerection of any fence in said easement.
      3.   A gate or opening shall be provided in all fence enclosures.
      4.   Any suitable construction material may be used for a fence, except
chicken wire, square welded mesh wire, barbed wire, electrically charged wire,
temporary snow fence, or solid concrete block; or be topped with sharp edged
material, except that this shall not be construed to prohibit picket fences.
The entire length of the fence shall be constructed of the same or harmonious
materials.
   C.   Commercial, Office Or Manufacturing Zoning District Requirements:
      1.   The maximum height of any fence in any commercial, office or
manufacturing use district shall not exceed six feet (6') in height.
      2.   No fence shall be built, constructed or erected so as to extend past
the front yard building line as established by the zoning ordinance 2 , nor
shall any fence be built past the side street yard on a corner lot as
established by the zoning ordinance. No fence shall be erected in any public
right of way. Fence construction shall be such that it provides a clear and
unobstructed view from the building line to the property line. A clear and
unobstructed view shall be construed to mean that at least 90 percent of any
object on the opposite side of the fence shall be clearly visible at all times.
No fence shall be built, constructed or erected in any utility easement, except
wherein a release is given to the village prior thereto, absolving the village
from all liability and damages resulting from subsequent entry for the purpose
of repair, inspection, maintenance, installation or removal of utilities. Any
fence allowed or permitted in an easement shall be so constructed that the
fence and posts may be readily removable. The village shall in no way be held
liable for the replacement of any fence removed, or any other expense entailed
by the removal or reerection of any fence in said easement.
      3.   Any suitable construction material may be used for a fence, except
chicken wire, square welded mesh wire, electrically charged wire, or temporary
snow fence; or be topped with sharp edged material, except that this shall not
be construed to prohibit picket fences.
      4.   Barbed wire may be used in commercial, office or manufacturing
zoning districts, provided that such barbed wire can only be constructed above
a minimum height of seven feet (7') from the established grade, and further
provided that such barbed wire shall be turned to the inside of the property
where so erected, and that no barbed wire shall be permitted closer than five
feet (5') from any public street, sidewalk, alley, park or public right of way
or public place. The entire length of the fence shall be constructed of the
same or harmonious materials. (1951 Code; Ords. 21-16-2, 21-16-3; 1985 Code;
Ords. 89-21-64, 89-21-66; 21-14-70)
 
Notes
1 1. See title 4 of this code.
2 1. See title 4 of this code.
6-11-4: COST OF CONSTRUCTION:
In case of dispute over the cost of erecting a division fence by adjoining
landowners, such dispute shall be settled according to the provisions of 765
Illinois Compiled Statutes 130/0.01 et seq. (1969 Code 6-163)
6-11-5: FENCING FOR CONSTRUCTION AND DEMOLITION ACTIVITY:
   A.   When a permit is issued by the village authorizing the demolition of an
existing building or construction of a new building, then the applicant for
said permit shall, prior to commencing any work pursuant to said permit, erect
a chainlink fence on the subject property, of not less than six feet (6') in
height, around either:
      1.   The perimeter of the building being demolished;
      2.   The perimeter of the building under construction; or
      3.   The perimeter of the entire subdivision or work site in relation to
which the permit has been issued.
The fence shall be installed not more than seven (7) days prior to the
commencement of any construction or demolition work on the subject property,
pursuant to the permit issued by the village. Said fence shall remain in place
until such time as the village's department of quality control has confirmed
that the building, or addition thereto, is secure from unpermitted entry
thereto (i.e., all doors and windows have been installed and can be locked) or,
in the case of demolition activity, until such time as all demolition debris
has been removed and any hole left by said demolition has been properly filled.
   B.   Any gate in a fence installed pursuant to subsection A of this section
must be closed at all times except to allow ingress and egress to and from the
work site. Said gate must be locked at all times other than when construction
or demolition activity is taking place on the work site.
   C.   Fencing, as required by subsection A of this section, shall also be
required for any building construction or demolition project of the village,
the Oak Lawn park district, any school district or any other governmental
entity, whether or not a permit has been or is required to be issued by the
village relative to said construction or demolition project.
   D.   When the building/demolition permit applicant, general contractor or
property owner is notified of a violation of the provisions of this section,
said person so notified shall have two (2) hours from receipt of said notice to
correct the violation. If the violation is not corrected, the village shall
have the right, but not the obligation, to correct the violation. The costs and
fees associated with such corrective action shall be charged to the building/
demolition permit applicant, general contractor or property owner, as the case
may be. The village shall also have the right to issue an immediate "stop work"
order applicable to the work site until compliance is achieved and/or the
costs, fees and/or fines are paid.
   E.   Any person who violates any of the provisions of this section shall be
fined not less than one hundred dollars ($100.00) nor more than seven hundred
fifty dollars ($750.00). Each day such violation continues shall constitute a
separate and distinct offense.
   F.   In addition to the penalty set forth in subsection E of this section,
no certificate of occupancy shall be issued relative to the construction in
question until such time that all costs incurred by the village under
subsection D of this section, and all fines imposed pursuant to subsection E of
this section, have been paid in full. (Ords. 00-19-61, 07-21-60)
6-11-6: FENCE VARIATIONS:
   A.   The following actions may be taken administratively by the director of
quality control upon petition of a property owner:
      1.   To grant a variation to subsection 6-11-3B2 of this chapter to allow
a fence to be built to the side property line on the side yard of a corner lot;
and
      2.   To grant a variation to subsection 6-11-3B4 of this chapter to allow
fence material to be used on a property to match surrounding fence material or
to use different fence material in a rear yard from that used in a front yard.
Such administrative variations shall be granted if deemed to be reasonable
requests by petitioner. An appeal of any decision made by the director of
quality control under this provision may be made to the Appeals and Review
Board under the provisions of title 2, chapter 2 of this code.
   B.   A variation from the fence height limitations of this chapter may be
applied for and granted; provided, that a finding is made as to each of the
following conditions:
      1.   That the plight of the petitioner for the variation is caused by
unique circumstances; and
      2.   That the variation, if granted, will not alter the essential
character of the immediate surrounding area; and
      3.   That a fence, in full compliance with the maximum height limitations
of this chapter, would not adequately serve the same purposes as a fence of
increased height; and
      4.   That strict adherence to the fence height limitation requirements of
this chapter would cause particular hardship upon the petitioner for the
variation.
All requests for the aforementioned variation shall be heard by the Appeals and
Review Board pursuant to subsection
2-2-2C9 of this code.
   C.   A variation from the fence location limitations of this chapter may be
applied for and granted by the Appeals and Review Board; provided, that a
finding is made as to each of the following conditions:
      1.   That the plight of the petitioner for the variation is caused by
unique circumstances; and
      2.   That the variation, if granted, will not alter the essential
character of the immediate surrounding area; and
      3.   That strict adherence to the fence location limitation requirements
of this chapter would cause particular hardship upon the petitioner for the
variation. (Ords. 93-14-53, 98-20-92, 07-05-17, 19-16-65)
6-11-7: PENALTY:
Any person violating any provisions of this chapter shall be subject to a fine
of not less than ten dollars ($10.00) nor more than seven hundred fifty dollars
($750.00). (1985 Code; Ord. 99-5-11)
CHAPTER 12
SWIMMING POOLS
SECTION:
6-12-1: Permit Required, Compliance With Building Code
6-12-2: Approval Of Plans And Specifications
6-12-3: Fences Required
6-12-4: Electrical Requirements
6-12-5: Maintenance And Inspections
6-12-1: PERMIT REQUIRED, COMPLIANCE WITH BUILDING CODE:
   A.   A permanently constructed pool shall require a permit from the building
department and the construction of said pool shall conform with the building
code 1  of the village.
   B.   All pools, whether above or below ground, shall require an electrical
permit from the electrical inspection department. The fee for said permit shall
be as set forth in chapter 2, article A of this title.
   C.   All pools, whether above or below ground, shall require a swimming pool
inspection permit from the electrical inspection department. Said swimming pool
inspection permit shall not require a fee. (Ord. 84-13-34)
 
Notes
1 1. See chapter 1 of this title.
6-12-2: APPROVAL OF PLANS AND SPECIFICATIONS:
No swimming pools shall be constructed unless and until the plans and
specifications for the same are submitted to and approval by the director of
office of quality control of the village.
The plans and specifications for a swimming pool shall provide for an adequate
enclosure by proper fencing or other means as a safety measure. (1951 Code 21-
17-1; Ords. 170, 81-12-19)
6-12-3: FENCES REQUIRED:
   A.   Permanent Pools: All permanently constructed swimming pools shall be
protected by a fenced enclosure as required for residential use, with a height
of not less than six feet (6') and shall be equipped with a self-closing gate
and a latch four and one-half feet (41/2') over grade. For the purposes of this
section, permanent swimming pools shall be defined as inground pools and
swimming pools with metal or solid structural material frames.
   B.   Temporary Pools: All temporary, portable, sectional construction,
aboveground or nonpermanent pools shall be protected by a fenced enclosure as
required for residential use, with a height of not less than four feet (4') and
shall be equipped with a self-latching gate. For the purposes of this section,
temporary swimming pools shall be defined as those swimming pools that do not
have solid, rigid structures, including those with inflated or vinyl sides with
PVC or metal tubing.
   C.   Exception: Fences are not required for swimming pools below twenty four
inches (24") in height. (Ord. 10-23-52)
6-12-4: ELECTRICAL REQUIREMENTS:
The following electrical requirements shall apply to swimming pools:
   A.   Location Of Conductors: No electrical service conductors shall be
installed above the swimming pool or within an area surrounding the pool for a
distance of ten feet (10') horizontally measured from its perimeter, including
diving structures, observation stands, decks, towers or platforms.
   B.   Conduit: Galvanized rigid conduit shall extend from a pool outlet box
to a distance of fifteen feet (15') from the pool, and shall be pitched down to
provide drainage.
   C.   Outlet Boxes: Outlet boxes shall be cast type with threaded hubs.
   D.   Threaded Connections: Threaded connections shall be made at boxes and
couplings within fifteen feet (15') of any pool outlet box (pressure connectors
are not acceptable).
   E.   Conductors: Conductors shall be approved for wet locations.
   F.   Fixtures:
      1.   Fixtures shall be Underwriters Laboratory approved for submersible
use when installed within the pool.
      2.   Fixtures shall be approved for wet locations when installed closely
adjacent to the pool.
      3.   Each fixture shall be grounded by means of a grounding conductor
which shall extend from the source (cut out cabinet or distribution cabinet) to
each fixture. All rails and diving boards shall be grounded.
   G.   Submersible Lighting: Submersible lighting for swimming pools,
reflector pools and display fountains shall be grounded by the following:
      1.   The voltage shall not exceed twelve (12) volts.
      2.   Step down transformers shall be used in conjunction with the
lighting and shall be remotely located from the units they supply. Overcurrent
protection shall be provided for both the primary and secondary windings.
      3.   A main disconnect shall be installed on the primary feed to the
transformer, also the transformer shall be properly grounded. (Ords. 66-23-62,
84-13-34)
6-12-5: MAINTENANCE AND INSPECTIONS:
   A.   All swimming pools shall be maintained in a clean and sanitary
condition by either filtering or refilling at periodic intervals and all
equipment shall be maintained in a satisfactory operating condition. Any
swimming pool that does not have a filtration system shall be emptied weekly.
   B.   The water shall be maintained in a proper condition through the use of
an appropriate chemical treatment.
   C.   All pools shall be subject to inspection by the duly designated village
officials at such times as the village shall deem necessary and prudent. (Ord.
10-23-52)
CHAPTER 13
SOUND CONTROL
SECTION:
6-13-1: Definitions
6-13-2: Standards Adopted
6-13-3: Sound Control Regulations
6-13-1: DEFINITIONS:
The following definitions shall be used in applying the provisions of this
Chapter:
APPROVED SOUND LABORATORY: A sound laboratory which conforms to all ASTM
requirements for facilities, staffing and testing procedures using ASTM
standards and is approved by the Village.
DETACHED SINGLE-FAMILY DWELLING: Any single-family dwelling which is separated
from adjacent buildings by six feet (6') or more.
FLANKING SOUND: Sound which is transmitted into a dwelling unit from other
dwelling units or common areas within the building by paths other than directly
through the rated partition of floor- ceiling assembly except from the outside
exterior wall of the dwelling unit.
IMPACT INSULATION CLASS (IIC): A single figure rating for floor-ceiling
constructions which has been determined by tests conducted in an approved sound
laboratory according to ASTM E492-73T.
SOUND TRANSMISSION CLASS (STC): A single figure rating for floor-ceiling and
interior partition construction which has been determined by tests conducted in
an approved sound laboratory according to ASTM E90-70 and as defined by ASTM
E413-73. (Ord. 75-27-105)
6-13-2: STANDARDS ADOPTED:
The following standards are hereby adopted by reference, three (3) copies of
which shall be and remain on file in the office of the Clerk, and shall be used
in applying the provisions of this Chapter:
   A.   ASTM E 90-70, "Laboratory Measurement of Airborne Sound Transmission
Loss of Building Partitions".
   B.   ASTM E336-71, "Measurement of Airborne Sound Insulation in Buildings".
   C.   ASTM E413-73, "Determination of Sound Transmission Class".
   D.   ASTM E492-73T, "Laboratory Measurement of Impact Sound Transmission
Through Floor- Ceiling Assemblies Using the Tapping Machine".
   E.   ASTM E497-73T, "Installation of Fixed Partitions of Light Frame Type
for the Purpose of Conserving Their Sound Insulation Efficiency". (Ord. 75-27-
105; 1985 Code)
6-13-3: SOUND CONTROL REGULATIONS:
The following sound control provisions shall apply for partitions and floor-
ceiling assemblies between dwelling units:
   A.   General: In buildings where persons may be residents, permanently or
temporarily, airborne sound control for partition assemblies, and airborne and
impact sound control for floor-ceiling assemblies shall be provided, where
these assemblies separate dwelling units, efficiency dwelling units, light
housekeeping rooms, guest and patient rooms, or similar residential uses, from
each other and between all such residential units and other common use spaces,
such as interior public corridors, stairwells, lobbies, service areas, garages,
commercial areas and all building mechanical equipment rooms or systems.
   B.   Partitions: Partition and wall assemblies shall have a minimum sound
transmission class of not less than fifty (50) (STC-50). Partition and wall
assemblies which have been tested and rated by an approved sound testing
laboratory shall be considered as meeting the requirement of this Section
providing that the assembly is not compromised by penetrations or flanking
sound transmission (ASTM E 497-73T) in field construction.
Penetrations of partitions and walls shall be limited to the following and
shall be protected as provided.
      1.   Entrance doors from interior corridor, provided the door and/or jam
is equipped with a perimeter seal and the doorway has a sealed threshold. The
door shall be at least a one and three-quarter inch (13/4") solid core door.
      2.   Required electrical outlet boxes and bathroom cabinets, provided
they are installed in accordance with the recommendations of ASTM E497-73T or
are installed and separated (not back to back) in a manner that will not result
in lowering the required STC rating of the partition.
      3.   Ventilation/exhaust ducts in the corridor partition or common to
more than one residential unit in party walls, provided the ducts are lined,
insulated, located or specially designed to maintain the required STC partition
rating.
      4.   The installation of bathtubs against metal or wood framed separation
partitions is not permitted unless the water-resistant wallboard is extended
behind each tub as recommended in ASTM E497-73T.
      5.   All joints at the perimeter of separation partitions and at the
perimeter of penetration openings, which are not sealed, shall be sealed with a
nonhardening, permanently resilient acoustical caulking compound.
   C.   Floor-Ceiling Assemblies: Floor-ceiling assemblies shall have a minimum
sound transmission class not less than fifty (50) (STC-50) and shall have an
impact insulation class of not less than fifty (50) (IIC-50). Floor- ceiling
assemblies which have been tested and rated by an approved sound testing
laboratory shall be considered as meeting the requirements of this Section,
providing that the assembly is not compromised by penetrations or flanking
sound transmission (ASTM E497-73T).
      1.   Carpeting and pad are required as the finish floor covering in all
common interior corridors and hallways (stairwells are optional) and for the
floors in the individual residential units except for kitchen and bathroom
floors. Kitchens and bathrooms of residential units as well as care facility
floors may use carpeting if the carpeting is readily cleanable with normal
cleaning products or may be of tile if underlayed with an approved design
assembly which meets the required STC and IIC.
      2.   Penetrations of the ceiling, in an approved floor-ceiling assembly,
such as lighting fixtures shall be protected with insulation and the perimeter
of the opening shall be sealed with a nonhardening, permanently resilient
acoustical caulking compound.
   D.   Mechanical Penetrations: All conduits, ducts, vents and pipes within
the required partition or floor-ceiling assembly which may be in structural
contact with more than one residential unit shall be isolated from the
structure at support points by means of resilient mounts designed to provide
acoustical isolation. All openings through which conduits, ducts, vents and
pipes pass shall have the excess openings filled and sealed with a nonhardening
permanently resilient, acoustical caulking compound.
   E.   Approvals: All partition and floor-ceiling assembly ratings (STC and
IIC), sound control designs and various materials to be used to conform with
these requirements shall receive prior approval from the Director of Office of
Quality Control. (Ords. 75-27-105, 81-12-19)
CHAPTER 14
FLOOD CONTROL AND PROTECTION
SECTION:
6-14-1: Definitions
6-14-2: General Flood Plain Regulations
6-14-3: Flood Hazard and Damage Prevention
6-14-3-1: Scope, Compliance With Other Regulations
6-14-3-2: Maps and Profiles
6-14-3-3: Streams and Floodwater Runoff Channels and Basins
6-14-3-4: Highwater Elevations
6-14-3-5: Bench Mark System
6-14-3-6: Subdivision and Improvement Regulations
6-14-3-7: Zoning Regulations
6-14-3-8: Building Regulations
6-14-1: DEFINITIONS:
FLOOD BASE ELEVATION: That elevation of the highest flood of record, determined
by the Director of Office of Quality Control's record of the elevations of the
highest flood at locations indicated on the flood plain map of the Village on
file in the office of said Director. Flood base elevations at intermediate
locations shall be interpolated along the watercourse between the two (2)
nearest flood base elevations, one each upstream and downstream. The
controlling flood base elevation for any building site shall be the same as the
flood base elevation at the nearest point of the watercourse as measured on a
line perpendicular to the direction of the watercourse.
FLOOD PLAIN: That continuous land area adjacent to a watercourse, whose
elevation is equal to or below the flood base elevation, including any land
which is surrounded on more than one-half (1/2) its perimeter by land having an
elevation equal to or lower than the flood base elevation.
FLOOD RESERVOIR: A ponding area created for the purpose of impounding flood
waters and alleviating flood damage which might result from manmade fills.
(Ords. 67-16-37, 81-12-19)
6-14-2: GENERAL FLOOD PLAIN REGULATIONS:
   A.   Purpose, Applicability of Provisions: The flood plain shall be subject
to flood plain regulations as established by the Village President and Board of
Trustees and set forth in this Chapter, the Subdivision Title of this Code 1
or other applicable Village ordinances. Said regulations will guide development
in order to promote the public health, safety and welfare through flood
regulations; provided however, that the provisions of this Chapter shall not
apply to existing subdivisions or commercial and/or industrial developments,
pertaining to the minimum elevations of streets, lots and structure foundations
which have been constructed prior to November 22, 1966.
   B.   Building Elevation: No new or existing building or structure shall be
erected or moved within a flood plain unless the lowest floor including the
basement floor is at an elevation which is not less than twenty four inches
(24") above the flood base elevation for the site; provided however, that
basement floors may be erected below such elevation when the top of any
basement wall or the bottom of any opening therein is not less than twenty four
inches (24") above such elevation and the construction of such basement floors
and walls complies with the requirements of this Title.
   C.   Ground Elevation: The elevation of the ground for a minimum distance of
twenty five feet (25') immediately surrounding any building or structure
erected or moved within a flood plain shall be at an elevation which is not
less than twelve inches (12") above the flood base elevation for the site and
shall extend in width not less than twenty five feet (25') at or above said
elevation to a public street or access way.
   D.   Design of Public Ways: The elevation of the finished surface at the
center line of any new street constructed within a flood plain shall be not
less than twelve inches (12") above the flood base elevation for the area. The
design of such streets shall be such that the normal direction of course of
drainage or runoff through the area is not interrupted.
   E.   Alternative Methods of Flood Control: The Village President and Board
of Trustees may, upon the recommendation of the Director of Office of Quality
Control, authorize any alternate method of flood control and storm water
drainage other than that specified herein, provided such alternate method shall
be in harmony with the purposes and intent express ed in this Chapter and be as
effective as the measures provided herein.
   F.   Compliance with Provisions: Upon compliance with the requirements of
this Chapter, the Village President and Board of Trustees will, upon request of
the affected property owner, authorize the Director of Office of Quality
Control or the Village Clerk to issue a certificate of compliance. (Ords. 67-
16-37, 81-12-19)
 
Notes
1 1. See Title 5 of this Municipal Code.
6-14-3: FLOOD HAZARD AND DAMAGE PREVENTION:
6-14-3-1: SCOPE, COMPLIANCE WITH OTHER REGULATIONS:
   A.   This Section amends and supplements the following ordinances:
      1.   Oak Lawn Subdivision Control Ordinance (Title 5 of this Municipal
Code).
      2.   Comprehensive Amendment of the Oak Lawn Zoning Ordinance (Title 4 of
this Municipal Code).
      3.   Oak Lawn Building Code (Chapter 1 of this Title).
   B.   The provisions of this Section further regulate and restrict:
      1.   The subdivision, layout, and improvement of land, including
drainage, underground utilities and service facilities.
      2.   The excavating, filling and grading of lots and other parcels and
areas of flood plains, the storing of certain materials thereon, stream and
other floodwater runoff channels, and detention ponds and basins.
      3.   The location, construction and elevation of buildings and other
structures and parts and appurtenances thereof, and the drainage of parking and
other paved lots or areas.
   C.   Before starting any of the work regulated by this Section, an applicant
shall comply with the requirements set forth in the aforementioned ordinances
with respect to submission and approval of preliminary and final subdivision
plats, improvement plans, building and zoning permits, inspections, appeals and
similar matters, along with those set forth in this Chapter and as required by
State statute and regulations of any department of the State. (Ord. 68-28-91)
6-14-3-2: MAPS AND PROFILES:
In reviewing submittals for sewer permits, the Village will base its review on
the highwater elevations and limits of the one hundred (100) year flood as
established by the most recent and best available data. If the one hundred
(100) year flood information is not available, the review will be based on the
flood of record data. The data which will be used by the Village is listed
below in descending order of overall accuracy, completeness, and currentness.
When data of higher order becomes available, data of lower order will not be
used.
HUD Flood Insurance Studies (FIS), if certified by the Illinois Department of
Transportation, Division of Water Resources (IDOT-DWR).
IDOT-DWR Regulatory Floodplain Maps and Profiles.
Soil Conservation Service-Metropolitan Sanitary District of Greater Chicago
(SCS-MSDGC) Floodwater Management Plans, using the "without project" data shown
therein.
Other detailed one hundred (100) year flood studies, if certified by IDOT-DWR.
HUD Flood Hazard Boundary Maps (FHBM) and Approximate FIS Data.
USGS Maps of Floodprone Areas.
USGS-NIPC Hydrologic Investigation Atlases (Floods of Record).
When either HUD Flood Hazard Boundary Maps or the USGS Maps of Floodprone Areas
are used to determine the limits of the flood plain, then regulatory highwater
elevations shall be those of the flood of record. (Ords. 68-28-91, 79-35-78)
6-14-3-3: STREAMS AND FLOODWATER RUNOFF CHANNELS AND BASINS:
The maps and profiles enumerated in Section
6-14-3-2 above shall be used in establishing the following:
   A.   Location, Identifications: Locations of certain existing streams and
other floodwater runoff channels and detention ponds and basins and names or
other identifying designations thereof. Such locations shall be subject to ap
proved straightenings and relocations as provided for in this Section.
   B.   Floodway Widths: Widths or limits of flood ways on or along the streams
and channels established herein. A floodway consists of the deeper parts of the
total flood plain, including the trench or channel and adjoining parts, which
are reasonably required for the downstream movement of floodwater or flood
flows as distinguished from the shallower parts of the flood plain where the
speed of flow or current is lessened by the friction of bottom materials and
growth; while the function of the flood plain is essentially that of slow-
moving storage of flood water.
   C.   Areas of Waterway Openings: Minimum areas of waterway openings to be
provided whenever new culverts, sewers or bridges are built at various points
on the streams and channels are to be determined by using the watershed areas
and characteristics as shown on the maps and profiles in Section
6-14-3-2 hereof. (Ord. 68-28-91)
6-14-3-4:HIGHWATER ELEVATIONS:
   A.   Profiles Adopted: Profiles of floods for the various streams and
tributaries as shown on the maps and reports cited in Section
6-14-3-2 hereof as such other interpretive maps for specific areas which the
Director of Office of Quality Control has prepared or may prepare in the
future, accompanying and identified as a part of this Section, are incorporated
herein for the purpose of establishing highwater elevations on or along the
streams, channels and basins established in Section
6-14-3-2 above.
   B.   Definitions:
      High-water elevation: The highest level of floodwater observed during
past floods or that may reasonably be expected.
      Flood plain: Such land whose surface elevation is lower than the
highwater elevation established under subsection A above.
   C.   Datum: The highwater and other elevations shown on the profiles are in
feet above mean sea level datum at New York City, or, when so noted on the
profile, in feet above the stream bed of the stream or channel or above some
other described point or mark in or near the channel.
   D.   Applicable Elevation: For the purposes of these regulations, the
highwater elevation applicable to a building, building site or part of a lot,
parcel or other area of land shall be the elevation established for 1) the
adjoining or nearest stream or other floodwater runoff channel measured at
right angles to the general direction of flow in the stream or channel, or 2)
the adjoining or nearest flood water detention pond or basin, whichever is the
highest. Care shall be taken to interpret the map properly in the areas which
are subject to "sheet flow" instead of general inundation. In case of dispute
over the interpretation of the map elevations, the decision of the Director of
Office of Quality Control shall govern.
   E.   Higher Future Floods: The establishment by this Section of highwater
elevations for the purpose of regulating future development in order to lessen
or avoid hazards to persons and damage to property from floodwaters is not
intended nor shall it be construed to imply or assure owners, occupants or
prospective purchasers of property that no flood of higher elevation will ever
occur in the future. (Ords. 68-28-91, 81-12-19)
6-14-3-5: BENCH MARK SYSTEM:
There is hereby established a system of points or marks of known elevations
above the mean sea datum at New York City (U.S.G.S.) Datum from which
elevations shall hereafter be taken by engineers, surveyors, architects and
contractors when making topographical surveys and maps and setting grades and
elevations for buildings, pavements, drainage facilities and other structures
or work publicly built or regulated by this Village.
These bench marks shall be clearly shown on any pertinent drawings submitted to
the Village. It shall be the responsibility of the petitioner to establish and
certify these points unless already established by the Village. (Ord. 68-28-91)
6-14-3-6: SUBDIVISION AND IMPROVEMENT REGULATIONS:
   A.   Required Information: Accompanying the preliminary plans of each
proposed subdivision submitted for preliminary approval, there shall be
furnished the following information and data:
      1.   Contour Map: A (2' minimum) interval contour map of the land to be
subdivided or improved and such adjoining land whose topography may affect the
layout or drainage of the subdivision or improvement. On such map shall be
shown the following:
         a.   The flow lines of streams and other floodwater runoff channels,
their normal shorelines and the shoreline of the established highwater
elevations.
         b.   Normal shorelines of lakes, ponds, swamps and other detention
basins; the shoreline of highwater elevations established under Section
6-14-3-4; and inflow and outflow, if any.
         c.   Farm drains, inlets and outfalls.
         d.   Storm, sanitary and combined sewers and outfalls.
         e.   Septic tank systems and outlets, if any.
         f.   Seeps, springs, flowing and other wells.
      2.   Channel Profiles: Profile drawings of each stream, channel, pond and
basin showing elevations of the following:
         a.   The stream bed or flow line.
         b.   Channel banks, if any.
         c.   Waterway openings of existing culverts and bridges within and
near the tract.
         d.   Size and elevation of sewer and drain outlets into the stream,
channel or basin.
         e.   The highwater elevations established under Section
6-14-3-4 of this Chapter.
Where a limited number of highwater observations or determinations have been
established on a stream or channel, the subdivider's engineer will, in drawing
his profiles, extend or project the established highwater elevation upstream or
downstream from a single elevation approximately parallel to the stream bed or
flow line as surveyed by him.
If a subdivider believes that the established highwater elevation or profile
unreasonably restricts the layout of his proposed subdivision or its
development, or that an error has been made in its establishment, he may appeal
to the Appeals and Review Board for a review thereof, and for a recommendation
to the Village Board for an amendment to the elevations. No such amendment
shall be made until a duly advertised public hearing on the amendment has been
held on the proposed amendment before the Zoning and Planning Commission.
      3.   Comprehensive Drainage Plan: A comprehensive drainage plan, first in
preliminary form, designed to handle safely the flood water runoff following
the heavier rainstorms. The plans shall provide or be accompanied by maps or
other descriptive material showing the following:
         a.   The extent and area of each water shed tributary to the drainage
channels in the subdivision.
         b.   The street storm sewers and other storm drains to be built, the
basis of their design, the outfall and outlet locations and elevations and the
functioning of the drains during highwater conditions.
         c.   The parts of the proposed street storm sewer system along which
overhead sewers in buildings are required to prevent sewage backups in
basements during the heavier rainstorms.
         d.   The parts of the proposed street system where pavements are
planned to be depressed sufficiently to convey or temporarily store overflow
from storm sewers and over-the-curb runoff resulting from the heavier
rainstorms and the outlets for such overflow.
         e.   Existing streams and floodwater runoff channels to be maintained,
enlarged, altered otherwise and eliminated; and new channels to be constructed,
their locations, cross sections and profiles.
         f.   Existing culverts, storm sewers and bridges, drainage areas,
elevations and adequacy of waterway openings; and new culverts and bridges to
be built, their materials, elevations, waterway openings and basis of design.
         g.   Existing detention or retention ponds and basins to be
maintained, enlarged, altered otherwise, and new ponds or basins to be built
with dams, and their design.
   B.   Design of Improvements: The following rules shall govern the design of
the improvement with respect to the drainage of floodwater runoff.
      1.   Design: Streets, blocks, depths of lots, parks and other public
grounds shall be located and laid out in such a manner as to preserve and
utilize natural streams, channels and detention basins, including wherever
possible, the larger streams and flood plains within parks or other public
grounds.
      2.   Channel Straightening: Sharply meandering streams or channels may be
partly straightened and minor changes made in other channels, subject to
approval of the Village Board provided:
         a.   The floodway dimensions of the stream or channel are maintained;
         b.   The volume of floodwater storage outside the floodway is not
reduced; and
         c.   The changes and channels comply with other provisions of this
subsection.
      3.   Lesser Channel Locations: Generally acceptable locations of the
lesser floodwater runoff channels in the design of the subdivision include the
following:
         a.   In a depressed median of a double-roadway street or parkway,
provided the median is wide enough to permit flat side slopes along the
floodway.
         b.   Centered on the rear lot lines in a block, or entirely within the
rear yards of a single row of lots or parcels, provided in either case, the
lots are deep enough to permit flat side slopes along the floodway and a
building site above the established highwater elevation plus one foot (1') on
the front part of the lot.
In each of the foregoing cases, a floodway easement at least twenty feet (20')
wide shall be provided and shown on the plat. The easement shall permit
necessary public channel maintenance and improvement work and access of
equipment therefor but shall prohibit buildings and other obstructing
structures thereon by the owner. This easement shall be specifically designated
as a drainage and utility easement on the documents to be recorded.
      4.   Uppermost Reaches of Drainage Channels: The smaller uppermost
reaches of the drainage channels and swales forming the natural drainage system
near the watershed divide may be relocated or eliminated and incorporated into
the planned system of storm sewers and/or open channels upon a showing that the
system is adequate and can safely handle the floodwater runoff following the
heavier rainstorms.
      5.   Within-Block Drainage: A continuous easement shall be provided along
rear lot lines and drawn on the subdivision plat within which utility lines and
drainage may be located and maintained but on which no accessory buildings may
be located or built. The utility-drainage easement shall be at least ten feet
(10') wide, five feet (5') on each lot. This easement shall be specifically
designated as a drainage easement and utility easement on the documents to be
recorded.
      6.   Storm Sewer Outfalls: Storm sewer outfalls shall be so designed, by
reason of elevation of the invert, pumping or otherwise, that when the
receiving stream is in full flood, the storm sewer will continue to drain the
area it is designed to serve. If this is not possible, street grades shall have
to be such that the storm water will be able to flow continuously to the
receiving stream or outfall. Continuous positive street drainage shall be
provided if at all possible.
      7.   Streets Designed as Channels: Cross-sections and grades of streets
shall be designed to temporarily store and convey the overflow from storm
sewers and over-the-curb runoff and be provided with adequate outlets for safe
disposal of such overflow. The street cross-sections shall provide a lot grade
for each adjoining building a minimum of one and one-half feet (11/2') higher
than the crown of the pavement, and the minimum sill elevation of any opening
in the outer foundation walls of such buildings shall be at least one and one-
half (11/2) feet above the highest highwater as shown on the maps cited in
Section
6-14-3-2 of this Chapter.
      8.   Flood Plain Lots: Land lying wholly or partly in a flood plain may
be laid out and platted as building lots or parcels, provided a building site,
not lower than the applicable highwater elevation plus one foot (1'):
         a.   Exists on the front part of the lot or parcel; or
         b.   Will be built up during execution of the subdivision improvement
plan through general excavation and filling; or
         c.   Can be provided with excavation taken from within the area of the
lot or parcel when it is built upon.
      9.   Bridge and Culvert Waterway Openings: Waterway openings of bridges
and culverts hereafter built over streams or floodwater runoff channels shall
be designed with a waterway opening at least as large as necessary to
accommodate the drainage areas shown on the maps cited in Section
6-14-3-2 and established under Section
6-14-3-3 of this Chapter.
      10.   Retention Basin Dams: Dams constructed to form floodwater retention
ponds, basins or lakes shall be designed to maintain a permanent low-water pool
and a temporary floodwater storage pool and shall be provided with outlets
which may be set or regulated to detain or empty slowly the storage pool
following periods of runoff according to the capacity of the downstream channel
to carry the flow safely. The design and construction of retention ponds,
basins or lakes shall be such as to insure proper water circulation and to
prevent stagnation.
      11.   Sanitary Manholes: Sanitary manholes constructed within flood plain
areas must have rim elevations thirty inches (30") above the flood plain crest
or must be provided with watertight, lock-type covers. (Ords. 68-28-91, 81-36-
77, 93-9-24, 19-16-65, 19-16-66)
6-14-3-7: ZONING REGULATIONS:
   A.   Building On and Use of Flood Plains: Except as regulated by this
Chapter or other ordinances and State statutes, any building or structure may
be placed or erected on and storage or other use may be made of the flood plain
that is permitted by the regulations of the zoning district in which such flood
plain is located.
   B.   Preservation of Floodways and Detention Basins: All buildings, other
structures, inoperable motor vehicles and similar machinery, and fills of any
kind of materials hereafter made, placed or erected on the flood plain shall be
set back at least the distances required to preserve and maintain open and
unobstructed:
      1.   The widths of the floodways established for streams and other
floodwater runoff channels; and
      2.   The boundaries or limits of floodwater detention or retention ponds
and basins, as established in Section
6-14-3-3 of this Chapter and as otherwise regulated herein.
   C.   Open Storage of Materials:
      1.   Floatable Materials: Logs, tree stumps and branches, lumber, lumber
products and other floatable material may be placed, displayed or stored in the
open on flood plain land where permitted by the zoning district regulations
applicable to such land, and by State statutes; provided, the area so used is
enclosed by an open wire fence properly anchored to restrain such materials
from floating downstream during times of high water.
      2.   Other Materials: The open storage or display on flood plain land of
inoperable motor vehicles and similar machinery and other nonfloatable
materials and products, where permitted by the regulations of the zoning
district applicable to such land and by State statutes, shall be considered the
same as the placement of fill on the flood plain and shall be subject to the
regulations of this Chapter relating to making fills. (Ord. 68-28-91)
6-14-3-8: BUILDING REGULATIONS:
   A.   Wall Openings: A door sill, window sill, or the base of any other
opening in the outer foundation walls of a main building or structure shall be
constructed at an elevation not lower than the established highwater elevation
plus one and one-half feet (11/2'), unless surrounded by a watertight areaway
whose top is not lower than the aforesaid minimum elevation.
   B.   Outer Walls, Basement Floors and Areaways: The outer walls, basement
floor and areaways of a building which encloses open space that is below the
established highwater elevation plus one and one-half feet (11/2'), shall be
constructed of concrete masonry materials up to the minimum elevation, shall be
watertight and shall be designed to withstand the hydrostatic pressure caused
by floodwater standing at highwater elevation adjoining the building site.
   C.   Construction Regulations for Drainage: The following regulations shall
apply to all construction within the Village:
      1.   Overhead Sewers: Basement or low-level gravity building drain
connections to the street storm sewer or combined sewer are prohibited and
overhead sewers are required. Where basements are built, a sump and sump pump
shall be installed to lift low-level waste water to the overhead sewer leading
to the street sewer.
      2.   Roof and Foundation Drainage:
         a.   Drainage from roofs and paved parking or other areas smaller than
forty thousand (40,000) square feet and from foundation footing drains shall
not be connected directly into sewers. Sanitary sewer service connections and
extensions shall be permitted in accordance with current Metropolitan Sanitary
District regulations.
         b.   Drainage from roofs and paved parking or other areas larger than
forty thousand (40,000) square feet along any street shall be stored
temporarily on the premises in detention ponds on roofs or at ground level or
otherwise, which storage areas shall be equipped with outlets that may be set
or regulated to empty slowly the stored runoff water into storm sewers or
surface channels according to their ability to handle the flow safely.
         c.   Roof drainage through downspouts or otherwise shall not be
connected directly to any street or public sewer (storm, sanitary or combined)
but shall be directed away from the building onto the lawn or yard or into a
seepage well.
         d.   Foundation footing drainage shall not be disposed of in a street
or public sanitary or combined sewer, but may be disposed of in a seepage well
or pumped out away from the building onto the lawn or yard.
      3.   Sanitary Manholes: Sanitary manholes constructed within flood plain
areas must have rim elevations thirty inches (30") above the flood plain crest
or be provided with watertight, lock-type covers.
   D.   Building Site: Each main building or structure shall have or be
provided with a building site or yard that is not lower than the established
highwater elevation plus one foot (1') and extends outside the building walls
at least five feet (5'). The finished grade or surface of this protective yard
shall be sloped to drain away from the walls for a distance of at least five
feet (5') (Ord. 68-28-91)
ARTICLE A. DEVELOPMENT IN SPECIAL FLOOD HAZARD AREAS
SECTION:
6-14A-1: Purpose
6-14A-2: Definitions
6-14A-3: How To Use This Article
6-14A-4: Duties Of The Enforcement Officials
6-14A-5: Base Flood Elevation
6-14A-6: Occupation And Use Of Flood Prone Areas
6-14A-7: Occupation And Use Of Identified Floodways
6-14A-8: Occupation And Use Of SFHA Where Floodways Are Not Identified
6-14A-9: Permitting Requirements Applicable To All Flood Plain Areas
6-14A-10: Other Development Requirements
6-14A-11: Variances
6-14A-12: Disclaimer Of Liability
6-14A-13: Penalty
6-14A-14: Abrogation And Greater Restrictions
